export default {"HINDUISM":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"HINDUISM","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"ganesha-race","game":"match","en":{"title":"Ganesha and the race around the world","word":"Ganesha","means":"गणेश: the elephant-headed god of new beginnings","story":[{"head":"a mango for the winner","text":"On the mountain Kailash, Shiva and Parvati had two sons. Kartikeya was quick and strong. Ganesha had an elephant's head and a big, kind heart. One day a sage brought them a single golden mango, and both boys wanted it."},{"head":"a race around the world","text":"Shiva smiled. “Whoever goes all the way around the world first,” he said, “wins the mango.” Kartikeya jumped onto his peacock and zoomed into the sky, over oceans and forests and snowy hills."},{"head":"Ganesha walks around his parents","text":"Ganesha's ride was a little mouse, not very fast at all. So Ganesha sat and thought. Then he stood up, folded his hands, and walked slowly all the way around his mother and father, and bowed."},{"head":"my whole world","text":"“Why did you walk around us?” asked Parvati. “Because you two are my whole world,” said Ganesha. His parents laughed with joy and gave him the mango. Kartikeya landed later, windblown and tired, and found his brother already smiling."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Kartikeya","flew off on his peacock"],["Ganesha","walked around his parents"],["the mango","the prize for the winner"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Ganesha taking one careful step, and breathe out slowly, like the next step. four slow steps around the people you love.","carry":"my family is my whole world","grownups":{"source":"a well-loved family telling. In the Shiva Purana the prize is the chance to marry first; in many South Indian tellings it is a mango, and Kartikeya goes off to Palani hill. Families tell it many ways.","ask":"who are the people who make up your whole world?"}},"es":{"title":"Ganesha y la carrera alrededor del mundo","word":"Ganesha","means":"गणेश: el dios con cabeza de elefante de los nuevos comienzos","story":[{"head":"un mango para el ganador","text":"En la montaña Kailash, Shiva y Parvati tenían dos hijos. Kartikeya era rápido y fuerte. Ganesha tenía cabeza de elefante y un corazón grande y bueno. Un día un sabio les trajo un solo mango dorado, y los dos lo querían."},{"head":"una carrera alrededor del mundo","text":"Shiva sonrió. “Quien dé primero la vuelta a todo el mundo”, dijo, “se gana el mango”. Kartikeya saltó sobre su pavo real y salió volando por el cielo, sobre mares, bosques y montes nevados."},{"head":"Ganesha rodea a sus padres","text":"Ganesha montaba un ratoncito que no era nada rápido. Así que Ganesha se sentó a pensar. Luego se levantó, juntó las manos, caminó despacio alrededor de su mamá y su papá, y les hizo una reverencia."},{"head":"todo mi mundo","text":"“¿Por qué caminaste a nuestro alrededor?”, preguntó Parvati. “Porque ustedes dos son todo mi mundo”, dijo Ganesha. Sus padres rieron de alegría y le dieron el mango. Kartikeya llegó después, cansado y despeinado, y encontró a su hermano sonriendo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Kartikeya","voló en su pavo real"],["Ganesha","caminó alrededor de sus padres"],["el mango","el premio para el ganador"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Ganesha dando un paso con cuidado, y exhala despacio, como el siguiente paso. cuatro pasos lentos alrededor de quienes quieres.","carry":"mi familia es todo mi mundo","grownups":{"source":"un relato familiar muy querido. En el Shiva Purana el premio es casarse primero; en muchos relatos del sur de la India es un mango, y Kartikeya se va a la colina de Palani. Las familias lo cuentan de muchas maneras.","ask":"¿quiénes son las personas que forman todo tu mundo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Rudra Samhita, Kumara Khanda (the brothers' race)","work":"Shiva Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the mango (jnana-pazham) telling","work":"South Indian temple and family tradition (Palani)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"hanuman-leap","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Hanuman's leap across the sea","word":"Hanuman","means":"हनुमान: Rama's brave and loyal monkey friend","story":[{"head":"Sita is far away","text":"Rama's wife Sita had been carried far away to the island of Lanka, across the wide, wide sea. Rama's monkey friends sat on the shore, worried. Nobody could jump that far. Nobody, they thought."},{"head":"a reminder from Jambavan","text":"Wise old Jambavan, the bear, turned to Hanuman. “You are the son of the wind,” he said. “You have forgotten how strong you are.” Hanuman remembered. He began to grow, bigger and bigger, as tall as a hill."},{"head":"one huge leap","text":"Hanuman climbed Mount Mahendra, pressed his feet down hard, and sprang into the sky. He flew over the waves like the wind itself, his tail streaming behind him, with Rama in his heart."},{"head":"a mountain offers a rest","text":"Halfway across, a mountain named Mainaka rose up out of the sea. “Rest on me a while,” it said. Hanuman touched it kindly, said thank you, and kept going. At last he landed in Lanka, ready to find Sita."}],"game":{"items":[["Jambavan reminded Hanuman how strong he was.",true,"yes: wise Jambavan told him he had forgotten his own strength."],["Hanuman crossed the sea on a raft of logs.",false,"not in it: he leaped from Mount Mahendra and flew over the waves."],["a mountain rose from the sea to offer him a rest.",true,"yes: Mainaka rose up; Hanuman thanked it and kept going."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Hanuman growing taller, and breathe out slowly, like his long flight over the waves. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can do more than I think","grownups":{"source":"Valmiki's Ramayana: Jambavan's reminder ends the Kishkindha Kanda, and the leap opens the Sundara Kanda. Tulsidas tells it again in the Ramcharitmanas, and many families read the Sundara Kanda on its own.","ask":"when has someone reminded you that you could do something hard?"}},"es":{"title":"el gran salto de Hanuman sobre el mar","word":"Hanuman","means":"हनुमान: el valiente y fiel amigo mono de Rama","story":[{"head":"Sita está muy lejos","text":"Sita, la esposa de Rama, había sido llevada muy lejos, a la isla de Lanka, al otro lado del ancho mar. Los amigos monos de Rama estaban sentados en la orilla, preocupados. Nadie podía saltar tan lejos. Nadie, pensaban."},{"head":"Jambavan le recuerda algo","text":"El sabio y viejo Jambavan, el oso, miró a Hanuman. “Eres hijo del viento”, le dijo. “Se te olvidó lo fuerte que eres”. Hanuman se acordó. Empezó a crecer, más y más grande, alto como una colina."},{"head":"un salto enorme","text":"Hanuman subió al monte Mahendra, apretó los pies contra el suelo y saltó al cielo. Voló sobre las olas como el mismo viento, con la cola ondeando detrás y Rama en el corazón."},{"head":"una montaña le ofrece descanso","text":"A mitad del camino, una montaña llamada Mainaka salió del mar. “Descansa en mí un rato”, le dijo. Hanuman la tocó con cariño, le dio las gracias y siguió. Por fin llegó a Lanka, listo para encontrar a Sita."}],"game":{"items":[["Jambavan le recordó a Hanuman lo fuerte que era.",true,"sí: el sabio Jambavan le dijo que había olvidado su propia fuerza."],["Hanuman cruzó el mar en una balsa de troncos.",false,"no: saltó desde el monte Mahendra y voló sobre las olas."],["una montaña salió del mar para ofrecerle descanso.",true,"sí: Mainaka salió del agua; Hanuman le dio las gracias y siguió."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Hanuman creciendo, y exhala despacio, como su largo vuelo sobre las olas. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo hacer más de lo que creo","grownups":{"source":"el Ramayana de Valmiki: el recordatorio de Jambavan cierra el Kishkindha Kanda y el salto abre el Sundara Kanda. Tulsidas lo vuelve a contar en el Ramcharitmanas, y muchas familias leen el Sundara Kanda por sí solo.","ask":"¿cuándo alguien te recordó que podías hacer algo difícil?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Kishkindha Kanda, final sargas (Jambavan's speech); Sundara Kanda, sarga 1 (the leap, Mainaka)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sundar Kand","work":"Ramcharitmanas of Tulsidas","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"prahlad-holika","game":"match","en":{"title":"Prahlad and the bonfire of Holi","word":"Holi","means":"होली: the spring festival of colors and bonfires","story":[{"head":"a king who wanted all the praise","text":"Long ago a proud king named Hiranyakashipu wanted everyone to worship him alone. But his own little son, Prahlad, loved Vishnu. Morning and night, Prahlad sang Vishnu's name, and he would not stop."},{"head":"Prahlad keeps singing","text":"The king tried and tried to change Prahlad's mind. Prahlad stayed gentle and polite, and he stayed just the same. “Vishnu is everywhere,” he said quietly, “even right here with me.”"},{"head":"Holika's magic shawl","text":"The king's sister, Holika, had a magic shawl that fire could not burn. She had a plan. She would sit in a bonfire with Prahlad on her lap, wrapped safe inside her shawl."},{"head":"the fire does not touch Prahlad","text":"The fire was lit. Prahlad said Vishnu's name, calm and steady. A wind lifted the shawl off Holika and wrapped it around Prahlad. The flames could not touch him, and Holika's trick did not work. Prahlad walked out safe. Every year, the night before Holi, many Hindus light a bonfire to remember him."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Prahlad","kept singing Vishnu's name"],["Holika","had a shawl fire couldn't burn"],["the bonfire","is lit the night before Holi"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, calm and steady like Prahlad, and breathe out slowly, like the cool wind that carried the shawl. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can stay kind and still be myself","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana (book 7) tells of Prahlad's love for Vishnu. The bonfire and Holika come from later tellings and from Holika Dahan, the bonfire night before Holi. In some tellings Holika's protection is a blessing, not a shawl.","ask":"when is it hard to stay gentle and still be yourself?"}},"es":{"title":"Prahlad y la fogata de Holi","word":"Holi","means":"होली: la fiesta de primavera de los colores y las fogatas","story":[{"head":"un rey que quería todas las alabanzas","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, un rey orgulloso llamado Hiranyakashipu quería que todos lo adoraran solo a él. Pero su propio hijito, Prahlad, amaba a Vishnu. Mañana y noche, Prahlad cantaba el nombre de Vishnu, y no dejaba de hacerlo."},{"head":"Prahlad sigue cantando","text":"El rey intentó una y otra vez que Prahlad cambiara de idea. Prahlad seguía amable y educado, y seguía igual. “Vishnu está en todas partes”, decía en voz baja, “incluso aquí conmigo”."},{"head":"el chal mágico de Holika","text":"Holika, la hermana del rey, tenía un chal mágico que el fuego no podía quemar. Tenía un plan. Se sentaría en una fogata con Prahlad en el regazo, bien envuelta en su chal."},{"head":"el fuego no toca a Prahlad","text":"Encendieron el fuego. Prahlad dijo el nombre de Vishnu, tranquilo y firme. Un viento levantó el chal de Holika y envolvió con él a Prahlad. Las llamas no pudieron tocarlo, y el truco de Holika no funcionó. Prahlad salió sano y salvo. Cada año, la noche antes de Holi, muchos hindúes encienden una fogata para recordarlo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Prahlad","siguió cantando a Vishnu"],["Holika","tenía un chal que no se quemaba"],["la fogata","se enciende antes de Holi"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, tranquilo y firme como Prahlad, y exhala despacio, como el viento fresco que llevó el chal. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ser amable y seguir siendo yo","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana (libro 7) cuenta el amor de Prahlad por Vishnu. La fogata y Holika vienen de relatos posteriores y de Holika Dahan, la noche de la fogata antes de Holi. En algunos relatos la protección de Holika es una bendición, no un chal.","ask":"¿cuándo es difícil ser amable y seguir siendo tú?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 7, chapters 4 to 8 (Prahlad)","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Holika Dahan festival telling","work":"later Puranic and family tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":4,"key":"rama-diwali","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Rama comes home and the lamps are lit","word":"Diwali","means":"the festival of lights; the name means a row of lamps","story":[{"head":"fourteen years in the forest","text":"Prince Rama had lived in the forest for fourteen years, with his wife Sita and his brother Lakshmana. Back home in the city of Ayodhya, the people missed him every single day."},{"head":"sandals on the throne","text":"Rama's brother Bharata kept Rama's place waiting for him. He set Rama's wooden sandals on the throne and cared for the kingdom in Rama's name, counting the days until his brother came back."},{"head":"the flight home","text":"At last the long trouble in Lanka was over and Sita was safe. Rama, Sita, Lakshmana and their friend Hanuman flew home together in a flying chariot called the Pushpaka, over forests and rivers."},{"head":"a city full of lamps","text":"Ayodhya ran out to meet them. Many Hindus tell that the people lit little clay lamps, called diyas, in every window and along every road, so Rama could find his way home in the dark. Families still light diyas at Diwali."}],"game":{"items":[["Bharata put Rama's sandals on the throne.",true,"yes: he kept Rama's place and cared for the kingdom while he waited."],["Rama came home riding a big gray elephant.",false,"not in it: they flew home in a flying chariot called the Pushpaka."],["the people lit lamps along every road.",true,"yes: little clay lamps called diyas, so Rama could find his way home."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a little lamp being lit, and breathe out slowly, like its soft glow spreading. one lamp for each breath, four in all.","carry":"I can make someone feel welcome today","grownups":{"source":"Valmiki's Ramayana tells of the fourteen years in the forest, Bharata and the sandals (Ayodhya Kanda), and the return to Ayodhya (end of the Yuddha Kanda). The lamps come from festival tradition. Many Hindus tell other Diwali stories too, such as Lakshmi's or Krishna's.","ask":"what does it feel like when someone welcomes you home?"}},"es":{"title":"Rama vuelve a casa y se encienden las lámparas","word":"Diwali","means":"la fiesta de las luces; el nombre significa hilera de lámparas","story":[{"head":"catorce años en el bosque","text":"El príncipe Rama había vivido en el bosque catorce años, con su esposa Sita y su hermano Lakshmana. En casa, en la ciudad de Ayodhya, la gente lo extrañaba todos los días."},{"head":"unas sandalias en el trono","text":"Bharata, el hermano de Rama, le guardó su lugar. Puso las sandalias de madera de Rama en el trono y cuidó el reino en nombre de Rama, contando los días hasta que su hermano volviera."},{"head":"el vuelo de regreso","text":"Por fin terminaron los largos problemas en Lanka y Sita estaba a salvo. Rama, Sita, Lakshmana y su amigo Hanuman volvieron juntos en un carro volador llamado Pushpaka, sobre bosques y ríos."},{"head":"una ciudad llena de lámparas","text":"Ayodhya salió corriendo a recibirlos. Muchos hindúes cuentan que la gente encendió lamparitas de barro, llamadas diyas, en cada ventana y en cada camino, para que Rama encontrara el camino a casa en la oscuridad. Las familias todavía encienden diyas en Diwali."}],"game":{"items":[["Bharata puso las sandalias de Rama en el trono.",true,"sí: le guardó su lugar y cuidó el reino mientras esperaba."],["Rama volvió montado en un gran elefante gris.",false,"no: volvieron volando en un carro volador llamado Pushpaka."],["la gente encendió lámparas en cada camino.",true,"sí: lamparitas de barro llamadas diyas, para que Rama encontrara el camino."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una lamparita que se enciende, y exhala despacio, como su luz suave que se extiende. una lámpara por cada respiración, cuatro en total.","carry":"puedo hacer que alguien se sienta bienvenido hoy","grownups":{"source":"el Ramayana de Valmiki cuenta los catorce años en el bosque, Bharata y las sandalias (Ayodhya Kanda) y el regreso a Ayodhya (final del Yuddha Kanda). Las lámparas vienen de la tradición de la fiesta. Muchos hindúes cuentan también otras historias de Diwali, como la de Lakshmi o la de Krishna.","ask":"¿qué se siente cuando alguien te recibe en casa?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Ayodhya Kanda (the exile of fourteen years; Bharata and the sandals)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Yuddha Kanda, final sargas (the Pushpaka and the return)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"govardhan-hill","game":"match","en":{"title":"Krishna lifts Govardhan hill","word":"Govardhan","means":"the hill Krishna lifted to shelter his village","story":[{"head":"a thank-you for the hill","text":"In the village of Vraja, the cowherd families were getting ready a big offering for Indra, the god of rain. Young Krishna asked, “Why not thank Govardhan hill instead? It gives our cows grass and our families water.”"},{"head":"a great storm","text":"So the villagers made their offering to the hill. Indra was not pleased. He sent thunder, wind and heavy rain, more and more, until the fields were flooding and the cows stood shivering."},{"head":"a hill held up with one hand","text":"Krishna walked to Govardhan and lifted the whole hill, as easily as a child picks up a mushroom. He held it high with one hand, like a giant umbrella, and called, “Come underneath, everyone, cows and calves too!”"},{"head":"everyone safe and dry","text":"The whole village sheltered under the hill for seven days. Then the rain stopped. Indra understood and came to Krishna to say sorry. Many Hindus remember this with Govardhan Puja, piling up food like a little mountain."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Indra","sent the heavy rain"],["Krishna","lifted the hill with one hand"],["Govardhan","sheltered the whole village"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Krishna lifting the hill, and breathe out slowly, like the rain stopping at last. four slow breaths, safe and dry.","carry":"I'll make room for someone today","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana, book 10 (chapters 24 to 27), tells this story. Many families say Krishna held the hill on his little finger. Govardhan Puja and Annakut, around Diwali, remember it.","ask":"who is someone who shelters you when things get stormy?"}},"es":{"title":"Krishna levanta la colina Govardhan","word":"Govardhan","means":"la colina que Krishna levantó para proteger a su aldea","story":[{"head":"un agradecimiento para la colina","text":"En la aldea de Vraja, las familias de pastores preparaban una gran ofrenda para Indra, el dios de la lluvia. El joven Krishna preguntó: “¿Por qué no le damos las gracias a la colina Govardhan? Les da pasto a nuestras vacas y agua a nuestras familias”."},{"head":"una gran tormenta","text":"Así que los aldeanos hicieron su ofrenda a la colina. A Indra no le gustó. Mandó truenos, viento y lluvia fuerte, más y más, hasta que los campos se inundaron y las vacas temblaban."},{"head":"una colina sostenida con una mano","text":"Krishna fue hasta Govardhan y levantó la colina entera, tan fácil como un niño levanta un hongo. La sostuvo en alto con una mano, como un paraguas gigante, y llamó: “¡Vengan todos aquí abajo, también las vacas y los terneros!”"},{"head":"todos a salvo y secos","text":"Toda la aldea se refugió bajo la colina durante siete días. Luego paró la lluvia. Indra entendió y vino a pedirle perdón a Krishna. Muchos hindúes lo recuerdan con Govardhan Puja, amontonando comida como una pequeña montaña."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Indra","mandó la lluvia fuerte"],["Krishna","levantó la colina con una mano"],["Govardhan","protegió a toda la aldea"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Krishna levantando la colina, y exhala despacio, como la lluvia que por fin para. cuatro respiraciones lentas, a salvo y secos.","carry":"hoy le haré un lugar a alguien","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana, libro 10 (capítulos 24 a 27), cuenta esta historia. Muchas familias dicen que Krishna sostuvo la colina con el dedo meñique. Govardhan Puja y Annakut, cerca de Diwali, la recuerdan.","ask":"¿quién te protege cuando las cosas se ponen difíciles?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 10, chapters 24 to 27","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"dhruva-star","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Dhruva and the pole star","word":"Dhruva","means":"the name means steady; the star that stays in its place","story":[{"head":"no room on the king's lap","text":"Little Prince Dhruva ran to sit on his father's lap. But the king's other wife, Suruchi, said there was no room for him there. Dhruva walked away with tears on his cheeks."},{"head":"his mother's advice","text":"His mother, Suniti, hugged him close. “Don't be angry,” she said softly. “Turn to Vishnu. He welcomes everyone.” So Dhruva set off for the forest, small and very brave."},{"head":"Narada the wandering sage","text":"On the way he met Narada, a wandering sage who carries a veena. Narada saw how determined the boy was, and taught him how to sit very still and think of Vishnu with a quiet heart."},{"head":"a star that never moves","text":"Dhruva sat so still, for so long, that Vishnu came to him. Dhruva's sadness melted away. Vishnu gave him a place in the sky that would never move: the pole star, steady all night, still showing travelers the way north."}],"game":{"items":[["Dhruva's mother told him to turn to Vishnu.",true,"yes: Suniti hugged him and said Vishnu welcomes everyone."],["Dhruva became the moon.",false,"not in it: he became the pole star, the star that stays in its place."],["Narada met Dhruva on his way to the forest.",true,"yes: the wandering sage taught him to sit still and think of Vishnu."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly and breathe out slowly, steady as the pole star. four slow breaths, still and bright in a dark sky.","carry":"I'll find room for someone who feels left out","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana (book 4, chapters 8 and 9) and the Vishnu Purana (book 1) tell Dhruva's story. In the texts Dhruva goes home afterward and rules for a long time before taking his place in the sky.","ask":"what helps you feel better when you feel left out?"}},"es":{"title":"Dhruva y la estrella polar","word":"Dhruva","means":"el nombre significa firme; la estrella que no se mueve","story":[{"head":"no hay lugar en las piernas del rey","text":"El pequeño príncipe Dhruva corrió a sentarse en las piernas de su papá. Pero la otra esposa del rey, Suruchi, le dijo que ahí no había lugar para él. Dhruva se fue con lágrimas en las mejillas."},{"head":"el consejo de su mamá","text":"Su mamá, Suniti, lo abrazó fuerte. “No te enojes”, le dijo con dulzura. “Busca a Vishnu. Él recibe a todos”. Así que Dhruva se fue al bosque, pequeño y muy valiente."},{"head":"Narada, el sabio viajero","text":"En el camino se encontró con Narada, un sabio viajero que lleva una vina. Narada vio lo decidido que estaba el niño y le enseñó a sentarse muy quieto y pensar en Vishnu con el corazón tranquilo."},{"head":"una estrella que nunca se mueve","text":"Dhruva se quedó tan quieto, por tanto tiempo, que Vishnu vino a verlo. La tristeza de Dhruva se deshizo. Vishnu le dio un lugar en el cielo que nunca se movería: la estrella polar, firme toda la noche, que todavía les muestra el norte a los viajeros."}],"game":{"items":[["la mamá de Dhruva le dijo que buscara a Vishnu.",true,"sí: Suniti lo abrazó y le dijo que Vishnu recibe a todos."],["Dhruva se convirtió en la luna.",false,"no: se convirtió en la estrella polar, la estrella que no se mueve."],["Narada se encontró con Dhruva camino al bosque.",true,"sí: el sabio viajero le enseñó a quedarse quieto y pensar en Vishnu."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio y exhala despacio, firme como la estrella polar. cuatro respiraciones lentas, quietas y brillantes en el cielo oscuro.","carry":"le haré lugar a alguien que se siente excluido","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana (libro 4, capítulos 8 y 9) y el Vishnu Purana (libro 1) cuentan la historia de Dhruva. En los textos, Dhruva vuelve a casa y reina mucho tiempo antes de ocupar su lugar en el cielo.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a sentirte mejor cuando te sientes excluido?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 4, chapters 8 and 9","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"book 1, chapters 11 and 12","work":"Vishnu Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":7,"key":"krishna-butter","game":"match","en":{"title":"baby Krishna and the butter","word":"Damodara","means":"a name of Krishna: the one with a rope around his tummy","story":[{"head":"Yashoda churns the butter","text":"In the village of Gokul, Krishna's mother Yashoda was churning milk into butter, singing about her little boy as she worked. Baby Krishna toddled over, hungry, and climbed into her lap for his milk."},{"head":"a broken pot of yogurt","text":"When the milk on the stove started to boil over, Yashoda hurried away. Krishna pouted. He cracked a clay pot of yogurt with a stone and slipped off to eat fresh butter. He shared it with the monkeys too!"},{"head":"a rope too short","text":"Yashoda came back, frowning and smiling at the same time. She decided to tie him to a big wooden mortar. But the rope was two fingers too short. She added more rope. Still two fingers too short!"},{"head":"only love can tie Krishna","text":"Yashoda tried and tried until she was hot and tired. When Krishna saw his mother so tired, he let the rope go around him at last. The story says nothing can hold Krishna except love."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Yashoda","churned milk into butter"],["the monkeys","got butter from Krishna"],["the rope","was always two fingers short"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the churning going round, and breathe out slowly, like soft butter rising to the top. four slow, gentle breaths.","carry":"I'll share a treat with someone today","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana, book 10, chapter 9, tells how Yashoda tried to tie little Krishna and the rope was always too short. His name Damodara comes from this story, and many Vaishnava families remember it in the month of Kartik.","ask":"why do you think only love could tie Krishna?"}},"es":{"title":"el bebé Krishna y la mantequilla","word":"Damodara","means":"un nombre de Krishna: el que tiene una cuerda en la pancita","story":[{"head":"Yashoda bate la mantequilla","text":"En la aldea de Gokul, Yashoda, la mamá de Krishna, batía leche para hacer mantequilla y cantaba sobre su niño mientras trabajaba. El bebé Krishna llegó caminando, con hambre, y se subió a su regazo a tomar leche."},{"head":"una olla de yogur rota","text":"Cuando la leche en el fuego empezó a derramarse, Yashoda salió corriendo. Krishna hizo pucheros. Rompió una olla de barro con yogur usando una piedra y se escapó a comer mantequilla fresca. ¡También la compartió con los monos!"},{"head":"una cuerda demasiado corta","text":"Yashoda volvió, enojada y sonriente a la vez. Decidió atarlo a un gran mortero de madera. Pero a la cuerda le faltaban dos dedos. Le agregó más cuerda. ¡Otra vez le faltaban dos dedos!"},{"head":"solo el amor puede atar a Krishna","text":"Yashoda lo intentó una y otra vez hasta quedar acalorada y cansada. Cuando Krishna vio a su mamá tan cansada, por fin dejó que la cuerda lo rodeara. La historia dice que nada puede sujetar a Krishna, excepto el amor."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Yashoda","batía leche para mantequilla"],["los monos","recibieron mantequilla"],["la cuerda","siempre era dos dedos corta"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como la leche que da vueltas al batirla, y exhala despacio, como la mantequilla suave que sube. cuatro respiraciones lentas y suaves.","carry":"hoy compartiré algo rico con alguien","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana, libro 10, capítulo 9, cuenta cómo Yashoda intentó atar al pequeño Krishna y la cuerda siempre era corta. De esta historia viene su nombre Damodara, y muchas familias vaishnavas la recuerdan en el mes de Kartik.","ask":"¿por qué crees que solo el amor podía atar a Krishna?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 10, chapter 9 (Damodara lila)","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"yashoda-universe","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Yashoda looks inside Krishna's mouth","word":"Yashoda","means":"Krishna's mother, who raised him in the village of Gokul","story":[{"head":"a report about mud","text":"One day Krishna's brother Balarama and their friends came running to Yashoda. “Krishna ate mud!” they told her. Yashoda put her hands on her hips and looked at her little son."},{"head":"open your mouth","text":"“Did you eat mud?” she asked. “No, Mother,” said Krishna. “They're just teasing. Look for yourself.” And he opened his mouth wide, wide, wide."},{"head":"the whole universe inside","text":"Yashoda peered in and gasped. Inside she saw the sky and the stars, the sun and the moon, mountains, oceans and islands. She saw the whole universe, and even her own village, and herself, looking in!"},{"head":"a hug and a forgetting","text":"Yashoda stood amazed. Then, the story says, Krishna gently let her forget what she had seen, so she could simply be his mother again. She scooped him up, laughing, and hugged him close."}],"game":{"items":[["Krishna's friends said he had eaten mud.",true,"yes: Balarama and the friends came running to tell Yashoda."],["Yashoda saw stars and oceans inside his mouth.",true,"yes: she saw the whole universe, even her village and herself."],["Yashoda sent Krishna to bed without supper.",false,"not in it: she forgot what she saw and hugged him close."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, as wide as the sky Yashoda saw, and breathe out slowly, as soft as her hug. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll look for something amazing in an ordinary day","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana, book 10, chapter 8, tells this story. In the text Krishna lets Yashoda forget through his maya, his power of wonder, so her love as a mother can go on.","ask":"if you could look inside something small and see something huge, what would you hope to see?"}},"es":{"title":"Yashoda mira dentro de la boca de Krishna","word":"Yashoda","means":"la mamá de Krishna, que lo crio en la aldea de Gokul","story":[{"head":"una noticia sobre el lodo","text":"Un día, Balarama, el hermano de Krishna, y sus amigos llegaron corriendo a ver a Yashoda. “¡Krishna comió lodo!”, le dijeron. Yashoda se puso las manos en la cintura y miró a su hijito."},{"head":"abre la boca","text":"“¿Comiste lodo?”, le preguntó. “No, mamá”, dijo Krishna. “Solo me están molestando. Mira tú misma”. Y abrió la boca grande, grande, grande."},{"head":"todo el universo adentro","text":"Yashoda se asomó y se quedó sin aliento. Adentro vio el cielo y las estrellas, el sol y la luna, montañas, mares e islas. Vio todo el universo, ¡y hasta su propia aldea, y a ella misma, mirando!"},{"head":"un abrazo y un olvido","text":"Yashoda se quedó asombrada. Entonces, dice la historia, Krishna dejó con suavidad que olvidara lo que había visto, para que pudiera ser simplemente su mamá otra vez. Ella lo levantó riendo y lo abrazó fuerte."}],"game":{"items":[["los amigos de Krishna dijeron que había comido lodo.",true,"sí: Balarama y los amigos llegaron corriendo a contarle a Yashoda."],["Yashoda vio estrellas y mares dentro de su boca.",true,"sí: vio todo el universo, hasta su aldea y a ella misma."],["Yashoda mandó a Krishna a la cama sin cenar.",false,"no: olvidó lo que vio y lo abrazó fuerte."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, tan amplio como el cielo que vio Yashoda, y exhala despacio, tan suave como su abrazo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"buscaré algo asombroso en un día normal","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana, libro 10, capítulo 8, cuenta esta historia. En el texto, Krishna deja que Yashoda olvide por medio de su maya, su poder de asombro, para que su amor de madre pueda seguir.","ask":"si pudieras mirar dentro de algo pequeño y ver algo enorme, ¿qué te gustaría ver?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 10, chapter 8 (the universe in Krishna's mouth)","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":9,"key":"sudama-rice","game":"match","en":{"title":"Sudama's handful of rice","word":"Sudama","means":"Krishna's childhood friend, poor in money and rich in love","story":[{"head":"two friends at school","text":"When they were boys, Krishna and Sudama studied together with the same teacher, sharing chores and jokes. Years later, Krishna lived in a palace in Dwaraka, and Sudama lived in a tiny hut with hardly any food."},{"head":"a gift tied in a cloth","text":"Sudama's wife said, “Go and visit your old friend.” But what gift could he bring? She found a little flattened rice, and Sudama tied it up in a corner of his old cloth."},{"head":"Krishna runs to hug him","text":"When Krishna saw Sudama at the door, he jumped up and hugged him. He washed his friend's tired feet and gave him his own seat. Sudama felt too shy to show his little gift."},{"head":"the handful of rice","text":"Krishna found the bundle and laughed with delight. He ate a handful of the rice as if it were a feast. Sudama walked home without asking for anything, and found his hut had become a fine house, full of good things."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Sudama","brought a little flattened rice"],["Krishna","ate it as if it were a feast"],["Sudama's hut","became a fine house"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a hug from an old friend, and breathe out slowly, like the long walk home. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll give a small gift from the heart today","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana, book 10, chapters 80 and 81. The text calls him a brahmin friend of Krishna; the name Sudama comes from later tellings, and in South India many know him as Kuchela.","ask":"what small gift could you give a friend that would mean a lot?"}},"es":{"title":"el puñado de arroz de Sudama","word":"Sudama","means":"el amigo de infancia de Krishna, pobre en dinero y rico en amor","story":[{"head":"dos amigos en la escuela","text":"Cuando eran niños, Krishna y Sudama estudiaban juntos con el mismo maestro, compartiendo tareas y bromas. Años después, Krishna vivía en un palacio en Dwaraka, y Sudama vivía en una choza pequeñita, casi sin comida."},{"head":"un regalo atado en un paño","text":"La esposa de Sudama le dijo: “Ve a visitar a tu viejo amigo”. Pero ¿qué regalo podía llevar? Ella encontró un poco de arroz aplastado, y Sudama lo ató en una esquina de su paño viejo."},{"head":"Krishna corre a abrazarlo","text":"Cuando Krishna vio a Sudama en la puerta, se levantó de un salto y lo abrazó. Le lavó los pies cansados a su amigo y le dio su propio asiento. Sudama sentía demasiada vergüenza para mostrar su regalito."},{"head":"el puñado de arroz","text":"Krishna encontró el paquetito y se rio de alegría. Se comió un puñado de arroz como si fuera un banquete. Sudama volvió a casa sin pedir nada, y encontró que su choza se había vuelto una casa hermosa, llena de cosas buenas."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Sudama","llevó un poco de arroz aplastado"],["Krishna","lo comió como un banquete"],["la choza de Sudama","se volvió una casa hermosa"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el abrazo de un viejo amigo, y exhala despacio, como el largo camino a casa. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy daré un regalito de corazón","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana, libro 10, capítulos 80 y 81. El texto lo llama un amigo brahmán de Krishna; el nombre Sudama viene de relatos posteriores, y en el sur de la India muchos lo conocen como Kuchela.","ask":"¿qué regalito podrías darle a un amigo que significaría mucho?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 10, chapters 80 and 81","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"shabari-berries","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Shabari's berries","word":"Shabari","means":"a woman of the forest who waited years to welcome Rama","story":[{"head":"Shabari waits by the path","text":"Deep in the forest lived an old woman named Shabari. Her teachers had told her that one day Prince Rama would come by. So every morning she swept the path clean and waited."},{"head":"picking the sweetest berries","text":"Every day she picked wild berries for Rama. She tasted each one and kept only the sweetest, so Rama would get nothing sour. Days became years. Shabari's hair turned white, and still she waited."},{"head":"Rama comes at last","text":"One day Rama and his brother Lakshmana really did come walking down the path. Shabari's heart leaped. She washed their feet, gave them a place to sit, and offered her basket of tasted berries."},{"head":"the sweetest gift","text":"Rama ate every berry with a smile. He didn't mind at all that she had tasted them first. In the story, what Rama saw was her love, and that mattered more to him than any fruit."}],"game":{"items":[["Shabari tasted each berry to keep the sweetest.",true,"yes: she kept only the sweet ones, so Rama would get nothing sour."],["Rama said the berries were too sour to eat.",false,"not in it: he ate every berry with a smile and saw only her love."],["Shabari swept the path while she waited.",true,"yes: every morning she swept the path clean and waited for Rama."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Shabari waiting by the path, and breathe out slowly, like her smile when Rama came. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll give my best to someone today","grownups":{"source":"Valmiki's Ramayana (near the end of the Aranya Kanda) tells of Shabari welcoming Rama with forest fruits. The tasted berries come from later devotional tellings and family storytelling.","ask":"what is something you made or picked especially for someone you love?"}},"es":{"title":"las moras de Shabari","word":"Shabari","means":"una mujer del bosque que esperó años para recibir a Rama","story":[{"head":"Shabari espera junto al camino","text":"En lo profundo del bosque vivía una anciana llamada Shabari. Sus maestros le habían dicho que algún día el príncipe Rama pasaría por ahí. Así que cada mañana barría el camino y esperaba."},{"head":"las moras más dulces","text":"Cada día recogía moras silvestres para Rama. Probaba cada una y guardaba solo las más dulces, para que a Rama no le tocara ninguna agria. Los días se volvieron años. El cabello de Shabari se puso blanco, y ella seguía esperando."},{"head":"por fin llega Rama","text":"Un día, Rama y su hermano Lakshmana de verdad llegaron caminando por el sendero. El corazón de Shabari dio un salto. Les lavó los pies, les dio dónde sentarse y les ofreció su canasta de moras probadas."},{"head":"el regalo más dulce","text":"Rama se comió cada mora con una sonrisa. No le importó nada que ella las hubiera probado primero. En la historia, lo que Rama vio fue su amor, y eso le importaba más que cualquier fruta."}],"game":{"items":[["Shabari probaba cada mora para guardar las más dulces.",true,"sí: guardaba solo las dulces, para que a Rama no le tocara ninguna agria."],["Rama dijo que las moras estaban muy agrias.",false,"no: se comió cada mora con una sonrisa y solo vio su amor."],["Shabari barría el camino mientras esperaba.",true,"sí: cada mañana barría el camino y esperaba a Rama."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Shabari esperando junto al camino, y exhala despacio, como su sonrisa cuando llegó Rama. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le daré lo mejor de mí a alguien","grownups":{"source":"el Ramayana de Valmiki (cerca del final del Aranya Kanda) cuenta cómo Shabari recibió a Rama con frutas del bosque. Las moras probadas vienen de relatos devocionales posteriores y de las familias.","ask":"¿qué hiciste o escogiste especialmente para alguien que quieres?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Aranya Kanda, final sargas (Shabari)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"squirrel-bridge","game":"match","en":{"title":"the squirrel who helped build the bridge","word":"seva","means":"helping others with love, without asking for anything back","story":[{"head":"a bridge across the sea","text":"Rama needed to cross the sea to bring Sita home. So the monkey army began to build a bridge, carrying huge rocks and whole trees and dropping them into the waves, splash after splash."},{"head":"a tiny helper","text":"On the beach, a little squirrel watched. “I want to help too,” she thought. She rolled in the sand, ran onto the bridge, and shook the sand off into the cracks between the rocks. Then back she ran for more."},{"head":"a monkey laughs","text":"A big monkey laughed. “Little one, what good is a bit of sand?” But the squirrel kept going, back and forth, all day long, filling every gap she could find."},{"head":"stripes from Rama's fingers","text":"Rama saw her. He picked her up gently and stroked her back with his fingers. “Every bit of help counts,” he said. Many families say that is why the little squirrels of India have stripes on their backs."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the monkeys","carried rocks and trees"],["the squirrel","shook sand into the gaps"],["Rama","stroked her back gently"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the squirrel rolling in the sand, and breathe out slowly, like the sand falling into place. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll do one small helpful thing today","grownups":{"source":"Valmiki's Ramayana (Yuddha Kanda) tells of the bridge built by Nala and the monkey army. The squirrel is not in Valmiki; it comes from later tellings in several Indian languages and from family storytelling.","ask":"what is a small way you helped today that someone might not have noticed?"}},"es":{"title":"la ardilla que ayudó a construir el puente","word":"seva","means":"ayudar a otros con amor, sin pedir nada a cambio","story":[{"head":"un puente sobre el mar","text":"Rama necesitaba cruzar el mar para traer a Sita a casa. Así que el ejército de monos empezó a construir un puente, cargando rocas enormes y árboles enteros y dejándolos caer en las olas, ¡plaf, plaf!"},{"head":"una ayudante pequeñita","text":"En la playa, una ardillita miraba. “Yo también quiero ayudar”, pensó. Se revolcó en la arena, corrió al puente y se sacudió la arena en las grietas entre las rocas. Luego volvió corriendo por más."},{"head":"un mono se ríe","text":"Un mono grande se rio. “Pequeña, ¿de qué sirve un poquito de arena?”. Pero la ardilla siguió, de ida y de vuelta, todo el día, llenando cada hueco que encontraba."},{"head":"rayas de los dedos de Rama","text":"Rama la vio. La levantó con cuidado y le acarició la espalda con los dedos. “Toda ayuda cuenta”, dijo. Muchas familias dicen que por eso las ardillitas de la India tienen rayas en la espalda."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["los monos","cargaron rocas y árboles"],["la ardilla","echó arena en los huecos"],["Rama","le acarició la espalda"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como la ardilla revolcándose en la arena, y exhala despacio, como la arena cayendo en su lugar. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy haré una pequeña cosa para ayudar","grownups":{"source":"el Ramayana de Valmiki (Yuddha Kanda) cuenta cómo Nala y el ejército de monos construyeron el puente. La ardilla no está en Valmiki; viene de relatos posteriores en varias lenguas de la India y de las familias.","ask":"¿de qué manera pequeña ayudaste hoy sin que nadie lo notara?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Yuddha Kanda (Nala builds the bridge, Setu)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the squirrel (popular telling)","work":"regional and family tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"ganesha-writes","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Ganesha writes the Mahabharata","word":"Mahabharata","means":"a very long Hindu epic, the great story of the Bharata family","story":[{"head":"a story too big to write alone","text":"The sage Vyasa had a huge story in his mind: the Mahabharata, full of heroes, families, battles of the heart and wise words. It was so long that he needed someone very fast to write it down as he spoke."},{"head":"Ganesha's one rule","text":"Ganesha, the god of wisdom, said yes, with one rule: “Once I start writing, you must not stop speaking.” Vyasa smiled and added a rule of his own: “Then you must understand every verse before you write it.”"},{"head":"a pen breaks","text":"So they began. Vyasa spoke and Ganesha wrote. Whenever Vyasa needed a moment to think, he said a tricky verse, and Ganesha paused to work it out. One day, many tell, Ganesha's pen broke!"},{"head":"a tusk for a pen","text":"Ganesha did not stop. He snapped off the tip of one of his own tusks and kept writing with it. That is why, in many pictures, Ganesha has one whole tusk and one broken one."}],"game":{"items":[["Ganesha said Vyasa must not stop speaking.",true,"yes: that was Ganesha's rule, and Vyasa added one of his own."],["Vyasa wrote the whole story down himself.",false,"not in it: Vyasa spoke, and Ganesha did the writing."],["Ganesha used his broken tusk as a pen.",true,"yes: when his pen broke, he kept writing with his tusk."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Vyasa thinking of the next verse, and breathe out slowly, like Ganesha's pen moving across the page. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll finish something I started","grownups":{"source":"the opening of the Mahabharata (Adi Parva) in many printed versions tells of Ganesha as Vyasa's scribe; some scholarly editions leave this passage out. The broken tusk as a pen comes from later tellings.","ask":"what would you want to write down so it's never forgotten?"}},"es":{"title":"Ganesha escribe el Mahabharata","word":"Mahabharata","means":"un poema épico hindú muy largo, la gran historia de la familia Bharata","story":[{"head":"una historia muy grande","text":"El sabio Vyasa tenía en la mente una historia enorme: el Mahabharata, lleno de héroes, familias, luchas del corazón y palabras sabias. Era tan larga que necesitaba a alguien muy rápido que la escribiera mientras él hablaba."},{"head":"la regla de Ganesha","text":"Ganesha, el dios de la sabiduría, dijo que sí, con una regla: “Una vez que empiece a escribir, no puedes dejar de hablar”. Vyasa sonrió y puso una regla propia: “Entonces debes entender cada verso antes de escribirlo”."},{"head":"se rompe la pluma","text":"Así empezaron. Vyasa hablaba y Ganesha escribía. Cuando Vyasa necesitaba un momento para pensar, decía un verso difícil, y Ganesha se detenía a descifrarlo. Un día, cuentan muchos, ¡la pluma de Ganesha se rompió!"},{"head":"un colmillo como pluma","text":"Ganesha no se detuvo. Se quitó la punta de uno de sus colmillos y siguió escribiendo con ella. Por eso, en muchas imágenes, Ganesha tiene un colmillo entero y uno roto."}],"game":{"items":[["Ganesha dijo que Vyasa no podía dejar de hablar.",true,"sí: esa era la regla de Ganesha, y Vyasa puso una propia."],["Vyasa escribió él mismo toda la historia.",false,"no: Vyasa hablaba y Ganesha escribía."],["Ganesha usó su colmillo roto como pluma.",true,"sí: cuando se le rompió la pluma, siguió escribiendo con su colmillo."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Vyasa pensando el siguiente verso, y exhala despacio, como la pluma de Ganesha moviéndose por la página. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"terminaré algo que empecé","grownups":{"source":"el comienzo del Mahabharata (Adi Parva), en muchas ediciones impresas, cuenta que Ganesha fue el escriba de Vyasa; algunas ediciones académicas omiten este pasaje. El colmillo roto como pluma viene de relatos posteriores.","ask":"¿qué te gustaría escribir para que nunca se olvide?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Adi Parva, chapter 1 (Ganesha as scribe; vulgate versions)","work":"Mahabharata","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"baby-hanuman-sun","game":"match","en":{"title":"baby Hanuman and the sun","word":"Surya","means":"the sun, and the god of the sun","story":[{"head":"a big orange fruit","text":"When Hanuman was a baby, he woke up very hungry. He looked out and saw the rising sun, round and orange and glowing. “What a delicious fruit!” he thought, and his tummy rumbled."},{"head":"a leap toward the sun","text":"Baby Hanuman, son of Vayu the wind, jumped up into the sky, higher and higher, reaching for the sun with his little hands. The gods in the sky could hardly believe their eyes."},{"head":"a bump on the chin","text":"Indra, king of the gods, stopped the baby before he reached the sun. Hanuman tumbled back down to earth and bumped his chin. Some say that is how he got his name, from hanu, an old word for jaw."},{"head":"blessings for Hanuman","text":"Vayu the wind was so sad that he stopped blowing, and everything grew still. So the gods came, comforted Vayu, helped Hanuman feel well again, and each gave the baby a blessing. The wind blew again, and Hanuman grew up strong and wise."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["baby Hanuman","thought the sun was a fruit"],["Vayu","stopped the wind for a while"],["the gods","gave Hanuman blessings"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the wind filling the sky, and breathe out slowly, like the wind blowing gently again. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll comfort someone who feels sad","grownups":{"source":"Valmiki's Ramayana tells it twice: Jambavan recalls it near the end of the Kishkindha Kanda, and the Uttara Kanda tells it again. In the text Indra strikes with his thunderbolt; families tell it gently, as here.","ask":"what is something you wanted so much you reached for it like baby Hanuman?"}},"es":{"title":"el bebé Hanuman y el sol","word":"Surya","means":"el sol, y el dios del sol","story":[{"head":"una gran fruta anaranjada","text":"Cuando Hanuman era bebé, se despertó con mucha hambre. Miró afuera y vio el sol que salía, redondo, anaranjado y brillante. “¡Qué fruta tan rica!”, pensó, y le sonó la pancita."},{"head":"un salto hacia el sol","text":"El bebé Hanuman, hijo de Vayu, el viento, saltó al cielo, cada vez más alto, estirando sus manitas hacia el sol. Los dioses del cielo casi no podían creer lo que veían."},{"head":"un golpecito en la barbilla","text":"Indra, el rey de los dioses, detuvo al bebé antes de que llegara al sol. Hanuman cayó rodando a la tierra y se golpeó la barbilla. Algunos dicen que de ahí viene su nombre, de hanu, una palabra antigua para mandíbula."},{"head":"bendiciones para Hanuman","text":"Vayu, el viento, se puso tan triste que dejó de soplar, y todo se quedó quieto. Entonces vinieron los dioses, consolaron a Vayu, ayudaron a Hanuman a sentirse bien otra vez, y cada uno le dio una bendición al bebé. El viento volvió a soplar, y Hanuman creció fuerte y sabio."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el bebé Hanuman","creyó que el sol era una fruta"],["Vayu","dejó de soplar un rato"],["los dioses","le dieron bendiciones"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el viento que llena el cielo, y exhala despacio, como el viento que vuelve a soplar suave. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"consolaré a alguien que esté triste","grownups":{"source":"el Ramayana de Valmiki lo cuenta dos veces: Jambavan lo recuerda cerca del final del Kishkindha Kanda, y el Uttara Kanda lo vuelve a contar. En el texto Indra lo golpea con su rayo; las familias lo cuentan con suavidad, como aquí.","ask":"¿qué has querido tanto que lo buscaste como el bebé Hanuman?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Kishkindha Kanda, final sargas (Jambavan's account); Uttara Kanda (Hanuman's childhood)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"ganga-comes-down","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Ganga comes down through Shiva's hair","word":"Ganga","means":"गंगा: the river Ganges, honored by many Hindus as a goddess","story":[{"head":"a river in the sky","text":"Long ago, the story says, the river Ganga flowed only in the heavens. On earth, a king named Bhagiratha wished she would come down, so her water could bring peace to his ancestors, who had died long before."},{"head":"Bhagiratha's long prayer","text":"Bhagiratha prayed for a long, long time. At last Ganga agreed to come. But she was so strong and so fast that if she fell straight onto the earth, the ground would crack."},{"head":"caught in Shiva's hair","text":"So Bhagiratha asked Shiva for help. Shiva stood on the mountain and let the mighty river fall onto his head. Ganga tumbled into his long, tangled hair and wandered around and around inside it."},{"head":"Ganga flows gently down","text":"Then Shiva let her out gently, a little at a time. Ganga flowed down from the mountains and across the land, following Bhagiratha, who led the way, all the way to the sea."}],"game":{"items":[["Shiva caught Ganga in his long hair.",true,"yes: she tumbled into his tangled hair and came out gently."],["Bhagiratha asked the ocean to carry Ganga.",false,"not in it: he asked Shiva to catch her as she fell."],["Ganga followed Bhagiratha across the land.",true,"yes: he led the way, and she flowed all the way to the sea."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the river falling softly into Shiva's hair, and breathe out slowly, like it flowing gently to the sea. four slow breaths.","carry":"when I have big feelings, I'll let them out gently","grownups":{"source":"Valmiki's Ramayana (Bala Kanda) and the Bhagavata Purana (book 9) tell how Bhagiratha brought Ganga to earth. In Hindi a huge, patient effort is still called a Bhagirath prayas, a Bhagiratha effort.","ask":"when have you had to be gentle with something strong, like a big feeling?"}},"es":{"title":"Ganga baja por el cabello de Shiva","word":"Ganga","means":"गंगा: el río Ganges, honrado por muchos hindúes como diosa","story":[{"head":"un río en el cielo","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, dice la historia, el río Ganga corría solo en los cielos. En la tierra, un rey llamado Bhagiratha deseaba que bajara, para que su agua diera paz a sus antepasados, que habían muerto mucho tiempo atrás."},{"head":"la larga oración de Bhagiratha","text":"Bhagiratha oró durante mucho, mucho tiempo. Por fin Ganga aceptó venir. Pero era tan fuerte y tan rápida que, si caía de golpe sobre la tierra, el suelo se partiría."},{"head":"atrapada en el cabello de Shiva","text":"Entonces Bhagiratha le pidió ayuda a Shiva. Shiva se paró en la montaña y dejó que el gran río le cayera sobre la cabeza. Ganga cayó en su cabello largo y enredado, y dio vueltas y vueltas adentro."},{"head":"Ganga baja con suavidad","text":"Después Shiva la dejó salir con suavidad, poco a poco. Ganga bajó de las montañas y cruzó la tierra, siguiendo a Bhagiratha, que iba adelante, hasta llegar al mar."}],"game":{"items":[["Shiva atrapó a Ganga en su cabello largo.",true,"sí: cayó en su cabello enredado y salió con suavidad."],["Bhagiratha le pidió al océano que llevara a Ganga.",false,"no: le pidió a Shiva que la atrapara al caer."],["Ganga siguió a Bhagiratha por la tierra.",true,"sí: él iba adelante, y ella corrió hasta el mar."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el río que cae suave en el cabello de Shiva, y exhala despacio, como el agua que corre tranquila hasta el mar. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"cuando sienta algo muy fuerte, lo dejaré salir con calma","grownups":{"source":"el Ramayana de Valmiki (Bala Kanda) y el Bhagavata Purana (libro 9) cuentan cómo Bhagiratha trajo a Ganga a la tierra. En hindi, un esfuerzo enorme y paciente todavía se llama Bhagirath prayas, un esfuerzo de Bhagiratha.","ask":"¿cuándo tuviste que tratar con suavidad algo muy fuerte, como un sentimiento grande?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Bala Kanda, sargas 38 to 44 (Sagara and Bhagiratha)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"book 9, chapter 9","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":15,"key":"arjuna-bird-eye","game":"match","en":{"title":"Arjuna and the bird's eye","word":"guru","means":"a teacher, like Drona, who taught the young princes","story":[{"head":"a wooden bird in a tree","text":"The teacher Drona taught archery to the young princes. One day he set a wooden bird high up in a tree. “Today's test,” he said, “is to hit the bird's eye.”"},{"head":"what do you see?","text":"He called the eldest prince, Yudhishthira. “Aim, and tell me what you see.” Yudhishthira said, “I see the tree, the bird, you, and my brothers.” Drona said kindly, “Put down your bow.”"},{"head":"everyone sees too much","text":"One by one, the other princes tried. Each one saw the leaves, the sky, the tree, the people watching. Drona asked each of them to step aside."},{"head":"Arjuna sees only the eye","text":"Then came Arjuna. “What do you see?” asked Drona. “Only the bird's eye,” said Arjuna. “Shoot,” said Drona. Arjuna's arrow flew straight to the mark. He had given his whole attention to one thing."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Drona","set the test in the tree"],["Yudhishthira","saw the tree and his brothers"],["Arjuna","saw only the bird's eye"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly and breathe out slowly, the way Arjuna looked at just one thing. four slow breaths, each one with all your attention.","carry":"I'll give my whole attention to one thing today","grownups":{"source":"a well-loved story of the princes' training from the Mahabharata's Adi Parva, found in many printed versions. In the older text Arjuna sees only the bird's head; families usually say its eye.","ask":"what helps you pay attention to just one thing?"}},"es":{"title":"Arjuna y el ojo del pájaro","word":"guru","means":"un maestro, como Drona, que enseñaba a los jóvenes príncipes","story":[{"head":"un pájaro de madera en un árbol","text":"El maestro Drona enseñaba tiro con arco a los jóvenes príncipes. Un día puso un pájaro de madera en lo alto de un árbol. “La prueba de hoy”, dijo, “es darle al ojo del pájaro”."},{"head":"¿qué ves?","text":"Llamó al príncipe mayor, Yudhishthira. “Apunta y dime qué ves”. Yudhishthira dijo: “Veo el árbol, el pájaro, a ti y a mis hermanos”. Drona le dijo con amabilidad: “Baja tu arco”."},{"head":"todos ven demasiado","text":"Uno por uno, los otros príncipes lo intentaron. Cada uno veía las hojas, el cielo, el árbol, a la gente mirando. Drona les pidió a todos que se hicieran a un lado."},{"head":"Arjuna solo ve el ojo","text":"Entonces llegó Arjuna. “¿Qué ves?”, preguntó Drona. “Solo el ojo del pájaro”, dijo Arjuna. “Dispara”, dijo Drona. La flecha de Arjuna voló directo al blanco. Había puesto toda su atención en una sola cosa."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Drona","puso la prueba en el árbol"],["Yudhishthira","vio el árbol y a sus hermanos"],["Arjuna","solo vio el ojo del pájaro"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio y exhala despacio, como Arjuna mirando una sola cosa. cuatro respiraciones lentas, cada una con toda tu atención.","carry":"hoy pondré toda mi atención en una sola cosa","grownups":{"source":"una historia muy querida del entrenamiento de los príncipes, del Adi Parva del Mahabharata, presente en muchas ediciones impresas. En el texto antiguo Arjuna solo ve la cabeza del pájaro; las familias suelen decir el ojo.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a poner atención en una sola cosa?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Adi Parva, Sambhava Parva (Drona's test of the princes)","work":"Mahabharata","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"churning-ocean","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the churning of the ocean of milk","word":"Lakshmi","means":"लक्ष्मी: the goddess of good fortune, light and plenty","story":[{"head":"a mountain and a serpent rope","text":"Long ago, the gods and the asuras wanted amrita, a drink that would keep them strong forever. It lay deep in the ocean of milk. So they used Mount Mandara as a giant stick to stir the ocean, and the great serpent Vasuki as the rope."},{"head":"a turtle holds the mountain","text":"As they pulled, back and forth, the mountain began to sink into the ocean. So Vishnu became a giant turtle, Kurma, and held the mountain up on his back. Round and round the churning went."},{"head":"Shiva's blue throat","text":"First a dark poison bubbled up, and nobody knew what to do. Shiva kindly drank it to keep the world safe, and held it in his throat, which turned blue. That is why he is called Nilakantha, the blue-throated one."},{"head":"Lakshmi rises from the waves","text":"Then wonderful things came up: a wish-giving cow, a snow-white horse, and Lakshmi, glowing and holding a lotus. Last of all came the amrita. Many Hindus welcome Lakshmi into their homes at Diwali."}],"game":{"items":[["a giant turtle held the mountain up.",true,"yes: Vishnu became Kurma the turtle and held it on his back."],["the rope for the churning was made of flowers.",false,"not in it: the rope was the great serpent Vasuki."],["Lakshmi rose from the ocean holding a lotus.",true,"yes: she came up glowing, among the wonderful things from the waves."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the ocean swirling one way, and breathe out slowly, like it swirling back. four slow breaths, round and round.","carry":"I'll work together with someone on something hard","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana (book 8, chapters 5 to 8) and the Vishnu Purana (book 1) tell of the churning of the ocean; the Mahabharata tells it too. The tellings differ on which treasures rose and in what order.","ask":"what good thing has come out of working hard together with someone?"}},"es":{"title":"el batido del océano de leche","word":"Lakshmi","means":"लक्ष्मी: la diosa de la buena fortuna, la luz y la abundancia","story":[{"head":"una montaña y una serpiente","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, los dioses y los asuras querían amrita, una bebida que los mantendría fuertes para siempre. Estaba en lo profundo del océano de leche. Así que usaron el monte Mandara como un palo gigante para batir el océano, y a la gran serpiente Vasuki como cuerda."},{"head":"una tortuga sostiene la montaña","text":"Mientras jalaban, de un lado a otro, la montaña empezó a hundirse en el océano. Entonces Vishnu se convirtió en una tortuga gigante, Kurma, y sostuvo la montaña sobre su espalda. El batido siguió dando vueltas y vueltas."},{"head":"la garganta azul de Shiva","text":"Primero salió burbujeando un veneno oscuro, y nadie sabía qué hacer. Shiva, con bondad, se lo tomó para proteger al mundo, y lo guardó en la garganta, que se puso azul. Por eso lo llaman Nilakantha, el de la garganta azul."},{"head":"Lakshmi sale de las olas","text":"Luego salieron cosas maravillosas: una vaca que concede deseos, un caballo blanco como la nieve, y Lakshmi, brillante y con una flor de loto. Al final salió la amrita. Muchos hindúes reciben a Lakshmi en sus casas en Diwali."}],"game":{"items":[["una tortuga gigante sostuvo la montaña.",true,"sí: Vishnu se convirtió en Kurma, la tortuga, y la sostuvo en su espalda."],["la cuerda para batir estaba hecha de flores.",false,"no: la cuerda era la gran serpiente Vasuki."],["Lakshmi salió del océano con una flor de loto.",true,"sí: salió brillando, entre las cosas maravillosas de las olas."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el océano que gira hacia un lado, y exhala despacio, como el océano que gira de vuelta. cuatro respiraciones lentas, vuelta y vuelta.","carry":"trabajaré junto con alguien en algo difícil","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana (libro 8, capítulos 5 a 8) y el Vishnu Purana (libro 1) cuentan el batido del océano; el Mahabharata también lo cuenta. Los relatos varían en qué tesoros salieron y en qué orden.","ask":"¿qué cosa buena ha salido de trabajar duro junto con alguien?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 8, chapters 5 to 8 (samudra manthan)","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"book 1, chapter 9","work":"Vishnu Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Adi Parva (Astika Parva, the churning)","work":"Mahabharata","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"krishna-kaliya","game":"match","en":{"title":"Krishna and the serpent Kaliya","word":"Yamuna","means":"यमुना: the river where Krishna played, flowing through Vraja","story":[{"head":"a river gone sour","text":"In Vraja, a great many-headed serpent named Kaliya had come to live in a pool of the Yamuna river. His breath made the water sour and unsafe, and the cows and children could no longer drink there."},{"head":"Krishna jumps in","text":"Krishna climbed a kadamba tree by the water, tightened his sash, and jumped in with a great splash. Kaliya rose up out of the water, hissing and swaying, to see who had come."},{"head":"a dance on the serpent's heads","text":"Krishna sprang up onto Kaliya's heads. There he danced, light and quick, stepping from one head to the next, like a drummer keeping time, until the great serpent grew tired and still."},{"head":"Kaliya's family asks for kindness","text":"Kaliya's wives came and folded their hands. “Please be kind to him,” they asked. Krishna stopped dancing. He told Kaliya to take his family and go live in the ocean, where he would be at home. The Yamuna ran clear again."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Kaliya","made the river water sour"],["Krishna","danced on the serpent's heads"],["Kaliya's wives","asked Krishna to be kind"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the river water turning clear, and breathe out slowly, like Krishna's light, quick steps. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll help keep a place clean and safe","grownups":{"source":"the Bhagavata Purana, book 10, chapters 16 and 17, tells of Krishna and Kaliya. In the text Krishna ends with mercy, sending Kaliya to live in the sea. Many pictures show Krishna dancing on the serpent's hoods.","ask":"how can we help keep the rivers and places we love clean?"}},"es":{"title":"Krishna y la serpiente Kaliya","word":"Yamuna","means":"यमुना: el río donde jugaba Krishna, que pasa por Vraja","story":[{"head":"un río que se puso agrio","text":"En Vraja, una gran serpiente de muchas cabezas llamada Kaliya se había ido a vivir a una poza del río Yamuna. Su aliento volvía el agua agria y peligrosa, y las vacas y los niños ya no podían beber ahí."},{"head":"Krishna se lanza al agua","text":"Krishna se subió a un árbol de kadamba junto al agua, se apretó la faja y se lanzó con un gran chapuzón. Kaliya salió del agua, silbando y meciéndose, para ver quién había llegado."},{"head":"un baile sobre las cabezas","text":"Krishna saltó sobre las cabezas de Kaliya. Ahí bailó, ligero y rápido, pasando de una cabeza a otra, como un tamborilero que lleva el ritmo, hasta que la gran serpiente se cansó y se quedó quieta."},{"head":"la familia de Kaliya pide bondad","text":"Las esposas de Kaliya llegaron y juntaron las manos. “Por favor, sé bueno con él”, le pidieron. Krishna dejó de bailar. Le dijo a Kaliya que se llevara a su familia a vivir al océano, donde estaría en su casa. El Yamuna volvió a correr limpio."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Kaliya","volvió agria el agua del río"],["Krishna","bailó sobre las cabezas"],["las esposas de Kaliya","pidieron bondad a Krishna"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el agua del río que se vuelve limpia, y exhala despacio, como los pasos ligeros y rápidos de Krishna. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"ayudaré a mantener un lugar limpio y seguro","grownups":{"source":"el Bhagavata Purana, libro 10, capítulos 16 y 17, cuenta la historia de Krishna y Kaliya. En el texto Krishna termina con misericordia, mandando a Kaliya a vivir al mar. Muchas imágenes muestran a Krishna bailando sobre las cabezas de la serpiente.","ask":"¿cómo podemos ayudar a mantener limpios los ríos y lugares que queremos?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"book 10, chapters 16 and 17","work":"Bhagavata Purana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"raksha-bandhan","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Draupadi's strip of silk and Raksha Bandhan","word":"rakhi","means":"राखी: a bright thread tied on a brother's wrist at Raksha Bandhan","story":[{"head":"Krishna hurts his finger","text":"One day Krishna hurt his finger. Everyone around him hurried off to look for a bandage, a cloth, anything at all to wrap it with. People ran this way and that."},{"head":"Draupadi tears her sari","text":"But Draupadi, his dear friend, did not run off. Without stopping to think, she tore a strip from the edge of her beautiful silk sari and tied it gently around his finger."},{"head":"a promise in return","text":"Krishna looked at the little strip of silk. He was so touched that he promised Draupadi he would be there to help her whenever she needed him. The story says he kept his promise."},{"head":"a thread on the wrist","text":"Many Hindus remember this story at Raksha Bandhan. A sister ties a rakhi, a bright thread, on her brother's wrist, and he promises to look out for her. In many families, cousins and close friends tie rakhis too."}],"game":{"items":[["Draupadi tore a strip from her sari.",true,"yes: she tied it gently around Krishna's hurt finger."],["Draupadi gave Krishna a bag of gold.",false,"not in it: she gave a strip of her silk sari, and that was enough."],["Krishna promised to help Draupadi.",true,"yes: he promised to be there whenever she needed him, and kept his word."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a thread being wrapped around a wrist, and breathe out slowly, like the knot being tied. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll look out for someone in my family today","grownups":{"source":"a well-loved family telling, not found in the Mahabharata itself, which tells of Krishna and Draupadi's friendship. Other Raksha Bandhan stories are told too, such as Indra and his wife Shachi.","ask":"who looks out for you, and who do you look out for?"}},"es":{"title":"la tira de seda de Draupadi y Raksha Bandhan","word":"rakhi","means":"राखी: un hilo de colores que se ata en la muñeca en Raksha Bandhan","story":[{"head":"Krishna se lastima un dedo","text":"Un día Krishna se lastimó un dedo. Todos a su alrededor salieron corriendo a buscar una venda, un trapo, cualquier cosa para envolverlo. La gente corría de aquí para allá."},{"head":"Draupadi rasga su sari","text":"Pero Draupadi, su querida amiga, no salió corriendo. Sin pararse a pensar, rasgó una tira del borde de su hermoso sari de seda y se la ató con cuidado alrededor del dedo."},{"head":"una promesa a cambio","text":"Krishna miró la tirita de seda. Se conmovió tanto que le prometió a Draupadi que estaría ahí para ayudarla siempre que lo necesitara. La historia dice que cumplió su promesa."},{"head":"un hilo en la muñeca","text":"Muchos hindúes recuerdan esta historia en Raksha Bandhan. Una hermana le ata una rakhi, un hilo de colores, en la muñeca a su hermano, y él promete cuidarla. En muchas familias, los primos y los amigos cercanos también se atan rakhis."}],"game":{"items":[["Draupadi rasgó una tira de su sari.",true,"sí: se la ató con cuidado en el dedo lastimado de Krishna."],["Draupadi le dio a Krishna una bolsa de oro.",false,"no: le dio una tira de su sari de seda, y eso bastó."],["Krishna prometió ayudar a Draupadi.",true,"sí: prometió estar ahí siempre que lo necesitara, y cumplió."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un hilo que se enrolla en la muñeca, y exhala despacio, como el nudo que se ata. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy cuidaré a alguien de mi familia","grownups":{"source":"un relato familiar muy querido, que no está en el Mahabharata mismo, aunque este cuenta la amistad de Krishna y Draupadi. También se cuentan otras historias de Raksha Bandhan, como la de Indra y su esposa Shachi.","ask":"¿quién te cuida a ti, y a quién cuidas tú?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Krishna and Draupadi (popular telling)","work":"family and festival tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Krishna and Draupadi's friendship (Sabha Parva and elsewhere)","work":"Mahabharata","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"saraswati-music","game":"match","en":{"title":"Saraswati brings music to the world","word":"Saraswati","means":"सरस्वती: the goddess of learning, music and words","story":[{"head":"a world without sound","text":"Many families tell that when Brahma made the world, it was full of mountains, rivers, trees and animals, but it was very, very quiet. No songs. No words. No music anywhere at all."},{"head":"Saraswati appears","text":"So Saraswati appeared, dressed in white and seated on a white lotus, with a veena, a stringed instrument, in her hands. A white swan glided beside her on the water."},{"head":"the first notes of music","text":"She touched the strings of her veena, and music rang out. The rivers began to murmur, the birds began to sing, the wind hummed in the trees, and people found words to speak to one another."},{"head":"books at her feet","text":"On Vasant Panchami, the spring festival of Saraswati, many Hindu children place their books, pens and instruments near her picture and ask for her blessing to learn. Some little ones write their very first letters that day."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Brahma","made a quiet world"],["Saraswati","brought music and words"],["the veena","the instrument she played"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a veena string being plucked, and breathe out slowly, like the note fading away. four slow breaths, soft as music.","carry":"I'll learn one new thing today and enjoy it","grownups":{"source":"the Rig Veda praises Sarasvati as a river and as a goddess of inspired speech. The story of the silent world is a well-loved telling that varies from family to family. Vasant Panchami is her festival in many places.","ask":"what is your favorite thing to learn, and why?"}},"es":{"title":"Saraswati trae la música al mundo","word":"Saraswati","means":"सरस्वती: la diosa del saber, la música y las palabras","story":[{"head":"un mundo sin sonido","text":"Muchas familias cuentan que, cuando Brahma hizo el mundo, estaba lleno de montañas, ríos, árboles y animales, pero era muy, muy silencioso. Sin canciones. Sin palabras. Sin música en ninguna parte."},{"head":"aparece Saraswati","text":"Entonces apareció Saraswati, vestida de blanco y sentada sobre un loto blanco, con una vina, un instrumento de cuerdas, en las manos. Un cisne blanco se deslizaba a su lado sobre el agua."},{"head":"las primeras notas de música","text":"Tocó las cuerdas de su vina, y sonó la música. Los ríos empezaron a murmurar, los pájaros a cantar, el viento a zumbar entre los árboles, y la gente encontró palabras para hablarse."},{"head":"libros a sus pies","text":"En Vasant Panchami, la fiesta de primavera de Saraswati, muchos niños hindúes ponen sus libros, lápices e instrumentos cerca de su imagen y le piden su bendición para aprender. Algunos pequeños escriben ese día sus primeras letras."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Brahma","hizo un mundo silencioso"],["Saraswati","trajo música y palabras"],["la vina","el instrumento que tocaba"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una cuerda de vina que se toca, y exhala despacio, como la nota que se apaga. cuatro respiraciones lentas, suaves como la música.","carry":"hoy aprenderé algo nuevo y lo disfrutaré","grownups":{"source":"el Rig Veda alaba a Sarasvati como río y como diosa de la palabra inspirada. La historia del mundo silencioso es un relato muy querido que cambia de familia en familia. Vasant Panchami es su fiesta en muchos lugares.","ask":"¿qué es lo que más te gusta aprender, y por qué?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"1.3.10-12 and other hymns to Sarasvati","work":"Rig Veda","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the silent world (popular telling); Vasant Panchami customs","work":"family and festival tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"kevat-boatman","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Rama and the boatman Kevat","word":"Kevat","means":"a boatman; in the Ramcharitmanas, the one who ferried Rama","story":[{"head":"a boatman by the Ganga","text":"Rama, Sita and Lakshmana came to the bank of the Ganga river. They needed to cross. A boatman named Kevat was there with his little boat, but he folded his arms and shook his head."},{"head":"a worry about magic feet","text":"“I've heard,” said Kevat, “that the dust from your feet once turned a stone into a woman. What if it turns my boat into a woman? How would I feed my family? First, let me wash your feet!”"},{"head":"Rama's feet are washed","text":"Rama laughed and said yes. Kevat filled a bowl with river water and washed Rama's feet with great care and great joy. His family gathered round, smiling. Only then did he row them across."},{"head":"a ring Kevat would not take","text":"On the far bank, Sita offered Kevat her ring to pay him. Kevat said no. “We are both boatmen,” he said. “I carried you across this river. One day, carry me across too.”"}],"game":{"items":[["Kevat washed Rama's feet before the crossing.",true,"yes: he washed them with river water, with great care and joy."],["Sita offered Kevat her ring.",true,"yes: she offered it to pay him, and he would not take it."],["Kevat asked for a bag of rice as his fare.",false,"not in it: he took no payment at all, only the joy of washing Rama's feet."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Kevat's oar dipping into the water, and breathe out slowly, like the boat gliding across. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll help someone without asking for anything back","grownups":{"source":"Tulsidas's Ramcharitmanas (Ayodhya Kand) tells of the boatman who washed Rama's feet. In Valmiki's Ramayana, Rama is welcomed at the river by Guha, king of the boat people. Families tell Kevat's words many ways.","ask":"why do you think Kevat didn't want to be paid?"}},"es":{"title":"Rama y el barquero Kevat","word":"Kevat","means":"un barquero; en el Ramcharitmanas, el que cruzó a Rama en su barca","story":[{"head":"un barquero junto al Ganga","text":"Rama, Sita y Lakshmana llegaron a la orilla del río Ganga. Necesitaban cruzar. Un barquero llamado Kevat estaba ahí con su barquita, pero se cruzó de brazos y negó con la cabeza."},{"head":"una duda sobre pies mágicos","text":"“He oído”, dijo Kevat, “que el polvo de tus pies una vez convirtió una piedra en mujer. ¿Y si convierte mi barca en mujer? ¿Cómo alimentaría a mi familia? ¡Primero déjame lavarte los pies!”"},{"head":"le lavan los pies a Rama","text":"Rama se rio y dijo que sí. Kevat llenó un tazón con agua del río y le lavó los pies a Rama con mucho cuidado y mucha alegría. Su familia se reunió alrededor, sonriendo. Solo entonces los cruzó remando."},{"head":"un anillo que Kevat no aceptó","text":"En la otra orilla, Sita le ofreció su anillo a Kevat para pagarle. Kevat dijo que no. “Los dos somos barqueros”, dijo. “Yo te crucé este río. Algún día, crúzame tú a mí también”."}],"game":{"items":[["Kevat le lavó los pies a Rama antes de cruzar.",true,"sí: se los lavó con agua del río, con mucho cuidado y alegría."],["Sita le ofreció su anillo a Kevat.",true,"sí: se lo ofreció para pagarle, y él no lo aceptó."],["Kevat pidió una bolsa de arroz como pago.",false,"no: no aceptó ningún pago, solo la alegría de lavarle los pies a Rama."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el remo de Kevat que entra en el agua, y exhala despacio, como la barca que se desliza. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"ayudaré a alguien sin pedir nada a cambio","grownups":{"source":"el Ramcharitmanas de Tulsidas (Ayodhya Kand) cuenta la historia del barquero que lavó los pies de Rama. En el Ramayana de Valmiki, a Rama lo recibe en el río Guha, rey de los barqueros. Las familias cuentan las palabras de Kevat de muchas maneras.","ask":"¿por qué crees que Kevat no quiso que le pagaran?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Ayodhya Kand, around dohas 99 to 102 (the boatman)","work":"Ramcharitmanas of Tulsidas","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Ayodhya Kanda (Guha at Shringaverapura)","work":"Valmiki Ramayana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"salt-in-water","game":"match","en":{"title":"the salt in the water","word":"namaste","means":"नमस्ते: a greeting with palms together; it means I bow to you","story":[{"head":"a lump of salt in water","text":"A wise teacher named Uddalaka had a son named Shvetaketu. One evening Uddalaka said, “Put this lump of salt in a cup of water, and bring it to me in the morning.”"},{"head":"where did the salt go?","text":"In the morning Uddalaka said, “Now bring me the salt.” Shvetaketu looked and looked. The water was clear. The salt was gone! He couldn't find even one tiny grain."},{"head":"a sip from top to bottom","text":"“Sip from the top,” said his father. “How is it?” “Salty.” “Sip from the middle.” “Salty.” “Sip from the bottom.” “Salty!” The salt was hidden, but it was everywhere in the water."},{"head":"you are that","text":"“Like the salt,” said Uddalaka, “the deepest truth is hidden in everything, and it is in you too. You are that, Shvetaketu.” Some Hindus explain namaste, palms together, as greeting that hidden truth in each other."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Uddalaka","the father who taught"],["Shvetaketu","tasted the salty water"],["the salt","hidden, but everywhere"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like salt melting into water, and breathe out slowly, like the water going still and clear. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll greet someone today and really mean it","grownups":{"source":"the Chandogya Upanishad (chapter 6, section 13) tells this story of Uddalaka Aruni and his son Shvetaketu, with the words tat tvam asi, you are that. Explaining namaste this way is a common modern teaching; the word itself means I bow to you.","ask":"what is something good you can't see, but you know is there?"}},"es":{"title":"la sal en el agua","word":"namaste","means":"नमस्ते: un saludo con las palmas juntas; significa me inclino ante ti","story":[{"head":"un trozo de sal en el agua","text":"Un maestro sabio llamado Uddalaka tenía un hijo llamado Shvetaketu. Una tarde, Uddalaka le dijo: “Pon este trozo de sal en una taza de agua, y tráemela en la mañana”."},{"head":"¿adónde se fue la sal?","text":"En la mañana, Uddalaka le dijo: “Ahora tráeme la sal”. Shvetaketu buscó y buscó. El agua estaba clara. ¡La sal había desaparecido! No encontraba ni un granito."},{"head":"un sorbo de arriba a abajo","text":"“Prueba de arriba”, dijo su papá. “¿Cómo está?”. “Salada”. “Prueba del medio”. “Salada”. “Prueba del fondo”. “¡Salada!”. La sal estaba escondida, pero estaba en toda el agua."},{"head":"tú eres eso","text":"“Como la sal”, dijo Uddalaka, “la verdad más profunda está escondida en todo, y también está en ti. Tú eres eso, Shvetaketu”. Algunos hindúes explican el namaste, con las palmas juntas, como saludar esa verdad escondida en el otro."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Uddalaka","el papá que enseñaba"],["Shvetaketu","probó el agua salada"],["la sal","escondida, pero en todas partes"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como la sal que se disuelve en el agua, y exhala despacio, como el agua que se queda quieta y clara. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy saludaré a alguien de verdad, con todo el corazón","grownups":{"source":"el Chandogya Upanishad (capítulo 6, sección 13) cuenta esta historia de Uddalaka Aruni y su hijo Shvetaketu, con las palabras tat tvam asi, tú eres eso. Explicar el namaste así es una enseñanza moderna común; la palabra misma significa me inclino ante ti.","ask":"¿qué cosa buena no puedes ver, pero sabes que está ahí?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"6.13 (salt in water; tat tvam asi)","work":"Chandogya Upanishad","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]},"CHRISTIANITY":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"CHRISTIANITY","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"creation","game":"match","en":{"title":"God makes the world","word":"creation","means":"everything God made, in the Bible's first story","story":[{"head":"darkness and the first light","text":"The Bible begins with a story. In the beginning, it says, God made the heavens and the earth. At first everything was dark and empty, with deep water everywhere. Then God said, “Let there be light: and there was light.” God called the light day, and the darkness night."},{"head":"sky, seas and green growing things","text":"Next God made the sky, stretched high above like a great roof. God gathered the waters into seas, and dry land appeared. On the land came grass, trees heavy with fruit, and plants full of seeds, ready to grow again and again."},{"head":"sun, moon, stars and animals","text":"God set the sun to shine by day, and the moon and stars to glow by night. Then the seas filled with fish, the air with birds, and the land with animals of every kind, from tiny creeping things to big cattle."},{"head":"people, and a day of rest","text":"Last of all God made people, man and woman, in God's own image, the Bible says, and gave them the earth to look after. God looked at everything and saw that it was very good. On the seventh day, God rested."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each one to the story. tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the light","God called it day"],["the sun and moon","shine by day and by night"],["the seventh day","God rested"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the first light spreading over the dark water. breathe out, soft as evening. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll find one beautiful thing outside and say thank you","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 1:1–2:3, the first pages of the Bible, which Christians share with Jews. Christians read it in different ways: some as an account of six days, others as a poem-like story about God as maker. Both find in it that the world is good.","ask":"If you could add one thing to the story's list of good things, what would it be?"}},"es":{"title":"Dios hace el mundo","word":"creación","means":"todo lo que Dios hizo, en el primer relato de la Biblia","story":[{"head":"la oscuridad y la primera luz","text":"La Biblia empieza con un relato. En el principio, dice, Dios hizo los cielos y la tierra. Al comienzo todo estaba oscuro y vacío, con agua profunda por todas partes. Entonces Dios dijo que hubiera luz, y hubo luz. A la luz la llamó día, y a la oscuridad, noche."},{"head":"cielo, mares y plantas que crecen","text":"Después Dios hizo el cielo, extendido muy alto como un gran techo. Juntó las aguas en mares, y apareció la tierra seca. En la tierra brotaron la hierba, árboles cargados de fruta y plantas llenas de semillas, listas para crecer una y otra vez."},{"head":"sol, luna, estrellas y animales","text":"Dios puso el sol para brillar de día, y la luna y las estrellas para alumbrar de noche. Luego los mares se llenaron de peces, el aire de aves y la tierra de animales de toda clase, desde bichitos que se arrastran hasta grandes vacas."},{"head":"las personas y un día de descanso","text":"Al final Dios hizo a las personas, hombre y mujer, a su propia imagen, dice la Biblia, y les dio la tierra para cuidarla. Dios miró todo lo que había hecho y vio que era muy bueno. El séptimo día, Dios descansó."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada uno con el relato. toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la luz","Dios la llamó día"],["el sol y la luna","brillan de día y de noche"],["el séptimo día","Dios descansó"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la primera luz que se extiende sobre el agua oscura. suelta el aire, suave como la tarde. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"buscaré algo bonito afuera y daré las gracias","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 1:1–2:3, las primeras páginas de la Biblia, que los cristianos comparten con los judíos. Los cristianos lo leen de distintas maneras: unos como un relato de seis días, otros como un relato casi poético sobre Dios creador. Todos encuentran en él que el mundo es bueno.","ask":"Si pudieras añadir algo a la lista de cosas buenas del relato, ¿qué sería?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 1:3","work":"The Bible (Genesis)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"Let there be light: and there was light."},{"ref":"Genesis 1:1–2:3","work":"The Bible (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Genesis 2:15","work":"The Bible (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"noah-rainbow","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Noah's ark and the rainbow","word":"covenant","means":"a promise that binds two together, like God's promise to Noah","story":[{"head":"Noah builds a big boat","text":"The Bible tells of a time when the world was full of wrongdoing, and a great flood was coming. But Noah tried to live rightly and walked with God. God told him to build a huge wooden boat, called an ark, and Noah got to work."},{"head":"two by two, the animals come","text":"Into the ark went Noah, his wife, their three sons and their sons' wives. Then came the animals, two by two: birds, cattle and little creeping things. When everyone was inside, God shut the door, and they were safe and dry."},{"head":"rain, and a dove with a leaf","text":"The rain fell for forty days and forty nights, and the ark floated on the water. At last the rain stopped. Noah sent out a dove, and one evening it flew back with a fresh olive leaf in its beak. Dry land was near!"},{"head":"a rainbow in the clouds","text":"Everyone stepped out onto dry ground, and Noah gave thanks to God. God made a promise, a covenant, never to flood the whole earth again, and set a rainbow in the clouds as its sign. When the rainbow shines, the story says, God remembers that promise."}],"game":{"items":[["Noah built a big wooden boat called an ark.",true,"yes: God told Noah to build the ark, and he did."],["Noah sent out a parrot to look for land.",false,"not in it: Noah sent a dove, and it came back with an olive leaf."],["God set a rainbow in the clouds as a sign of a promise.",true,"yes: the rainbow is the sign of God's promise never to flood the whole earth again."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the dove gliding over the water. breathe out, like the rain stopping. four slow breaths, and picture a rainbow.","carry":"I'll keep one promise today, big or small","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 6–9 in the Bible, a story Christians share with Jews. This gentle telling leaves out the harm the flood did; the Bible tells it more fully, and families can choose when to read that part together.","ask":"What's a promise someone kept for you, and how did it feel?"}},"es":{"title":"el arca de Noé y el arcoíris","word":"alianza","means":"una promesa que une a dos, como la promesa de Dios a Noé","story":[{"head":"Noé construye un barco enorme","text":"La Biblia cuenta de un tiempo en que el mundo estaba lleno de maldad, y venía un gran diluvio. Pero Noé trataba de vivir con rectitud y caminaba con Dios. Dios le pidió construir un enorme barco de madera, llamado arca, y Noé se puso a trabajar."},{"head":"de dos en dos, llegan los animales","text":"Al arca entraron Noé, su esposa, sus tres hijos y las esposas de sus hijos. Luego llegaron los animales, de dos en dos: aves, ganado y bichitos que se arrastran. Cuando todos estuvieron dentro, Dios cerró la puerta, y quedaron a salvo y secos."},{"head":"lluvia, y una paloma con una hoja","text":"Llovió cuarenta días y cuarenta noches, y el arca flotaba sobre el agua. Por fin dejó de llover. Noé soltó una paloma, y una tarde volvió con una hoja fresca de olivo en el pico. ¡La tierra seca estaba cerca!"},{"head":"un arcoíris en las nubes","text":"Todos bajaron a tierra seca, y Noé le dio gracias a Dios. Dios hizo una promesa, una alianza: nunca más inundaría toda la tierra. Y puso un arcoíris en las nubes como señal. Cuando brilla el arcoíris, dice el relato, Dios recuerda esa promesa."}],"game":{"items":[["Noé construyó un gran barco de madera llamado arca.",true,"sí: Dios le pidió a Noé construir el arca, y él lo hizo."],["Noé soltó un loro para buscar tierra.",false,"no está: Noé soltó una paloma, y volvió con una hoja de olivo."],["Dios puso un arcoíris en las nubes como señal de una promesa.",true,"sí: el arcoíris es la señal de la promesa de Dios de no volver a inundar toda la tierra."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la paloma que planea sobre el agua. suelta el aire, como la lluvia que se detiene. cuatro respiraciones lentas, e imagina un arcoíris.","carry":"hoy cumpliré una promesa, grande o pequeña","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 6–9 en la Biblia, un relato que los cristianos comparten con los judíos. Esta versión suave deja fuera el daño que causó el diluvio; la Biblia lo cuenta con más detalle, y cada familia puede elegir cuándo leer esa parte juntos.","ask":"¿Qué promesa te cumplió alguien, y cómo te sentiste?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 6:9–9:17","work":"The Bible (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"david-shepherd","game":"match","en":{"title":"David the shepherd boy","word":"shepherd","means":"someone who looks after sheep, day and night","story":[{"head":"the youngest son with the sheep","text":"In Bethlehem lived a man named Jesse, who had eight sons. The youngest, David, spent his days out in the fields keeping the family's sheep. He led them to grass and water and kept them together, and the Bible says he could play the harp."},{"head":"Samuel comes looking for a king","text":"One day the prophet Samuel came to Jesse's house. God had sent him to anoint a new king by pouring special oil on his head. Jesse brought his sons to Samuel one by one, tall and strong. Each time, Samuel said this was not the one."},{"head":"God looks at the heart","text":"When Samuel saw the eldest, he thought he looked just like a king. But God told Samuel that people look at the outside, and God looks at the heart. Were there any more sons? They fetched David from the sheep, and Samuel anointed him."},{"head":"a song about a shepherd","text":"The Bible keeps many songs, called psalms. One of them is called a psalm of David, and it begins, “The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want.” It pictures God caring for people the way David cared for his sheep: green grass, still water, and never alone."}],"game":{"prompt":"who's who? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["David","the youngest, who kept the sheep"],["Samuel","came to anoint a new king"],["Psalm 23","the Lord is my shepherd"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like sheep lying down in green grass. breathe out, like still water. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll look at someone's heart, not just how they look","grownups":{"source":"1 Samuel 16 tells how Samuel anointed David. Psalm 23, which the Bible calls a psalm of David, is one of the Bible's songs, prayed by Jews and Christians alike.","ask":"Who looks after you the way a shepherd looks after sheep?"}},"es":{"title":"David, el pastorcito","word":"pastor","means":"alguien que cuida ovejas, de día y de noche","story":[{"head":"el hijo menor con las ovejas","text":"En Belén vivía un hombre llamado Isaí, que tenía ocho hijos. El menor, David, pasaba los días en el campo cuidando las ovejas de la familia. Las llevaba a la hierba y al agua y las mantenía juntas, y la Biblia dice que sabía tocar el arpa."},{"head":"Samuel busca a un rey","text":"Un día el profeta Samuel llegó a la casa de Isaí. Dios lo había enviado a ungir a un nuevo rey, derramando un aceite especial sobre su cabeza. Isaí le presentó a sus hijos uno por uno, altos y fuertes. Cada vez, Samuel decía que no era ese."},{"head":"Dios mira el corazón","text":"Cuando Samuel vio al mayor, pensó que parecía un rey. Pero Dios le dijo a Samuel que las personas miran lo de afuera, y Dios mira el corazón. ¿Había más hijos? Trajeron a David de donde estaban las ovejas, y Samuel lo ungió."},{"head":"una canción sobre un pastor","text":"La Biblia guarda muchas canciones, llamadas salmos. Una se llama salmo de David, y empieza diciendo que el Señor es mi pastor y nada me faltará. Pinta a Dios cuidando a las personas como David cuidaba a sus ovejas: hierba verde, agua tranquila, y nunca solos."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién es quién? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["David","el menor, que cuidaba ovejas"],["Samuel","vino a ungir a un nuevo rey"],["el Salmo 23","el Señor es mi pastor"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como ovejas echadas en la hierba verde. suelta el aire, como agua tranquila. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"miraré el corazón de alguien, no solo su apariencia","grownups":{"source":"1 Samuel 16 cuenta cómo Samuel ungió a David. El Salmo 23, que la Biblia llama salmo de David, es una de las canciones de la Biblia, que rezan tanto judíos como cristianos.","ask":"¿Quién te cuida como un pastor cuida a sus ovejas?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"1 Samuel 16:1–13, 16:18","work":"The Bible (1 Samuel)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"1 Samuel 17:12","work":"The Bible (1 Samuel)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Psalm 23:1","work":"The Bible (Psalms)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"The LORD is my shepherd; I shall not want."}]},{"day":4,"key":"jonah-fish","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Jonah and the great fish","word":"mercy","means":"kindness that gives someone a second chance","story":[{"head":"Jonah sails the other way","text":"God asked a man named Jonah to go to the great city of Nineveh and tell the people to stop doing wrong. Jonah didn't want to go. So he went down to the sea and got on a ship sailing the opposite way."},{"head":"a storm, and a great fish","text":"A wild storm blew up, and the sailors were frightened. Jonah told them the storm had come because of him, and asked them to put him into the sea. They did, and the waves grew calm. God sent a great fish, and it swallowed Jonah whole."},{"head":"Jonah prays inside the fish","text":"For three days and three nights Jonah was inside the fish, and there he prayed to God. Then the fish brought him up onto dry land. God asked Jonah a second time to go to Nineveh, and this time Jonah went."},{"head":"a second chance for a whole city","text":"Jonah gave God's message. The people listened, from the king down. They were sorry and changed their ways, and God showed them mercy. The story ends with God reminding Jonah how much God cares for all the people of that big city, and even its animals."}],"game":{"items":[["Jonah got on a ship going the other way.",true,"yes: he didn't want to go to Nineveh, so he sailed the opposite way."],["Jonah rode to Nineveh on a camel.",false,"not in it: the story doesn't say how he traveled, only that the second time, he went."],["Jonah prayed inside the great fish.",true,"yes: three days and three nights, and then the fish brought him to dry land."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a wave rising. breathe out, like the sea going calm. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll give someone a second chance today","grownups":{"source":"The Book of Jonah, a short book of the Bible that Jews and Christians both read; Jesus mentions Jonah in Matthew 12:40. This telling leaves out the last chapter, where Jonah sulks under a plant and God gently asks him to care.","ask":"Have you ever not wanted to do something, then done it after all? What happened?"}},"es":{"title":"Jonás y el gran pez","word":"misericordia","means":"bondad que le da a alguien una segunda oportunidad","story":[{"head":"Jonás navega hacia el otro lado","text":"Dios le pidió a un hombre llamado Jonás que fuera a la gran ciudad de Nínive y les dijera a sus habitantes que dejaran de hacer el mal. Jonás no quería ir. Así que bajó al mar y subió a un barco que iba en dirección contraria."},{"head":"una tormenta y un gran pez","text":"Se levantó una tormenta feroz, y los marineros tenían miedo. Jonás les dijo que la tormenta había llegado por su culpa, y les pidió que lo pusieran en el mar. Lo hicieron, y las olas se calmaron. Dios envió un gran pez, que se tragó a Jonás entero."},{"head":"Jonás ora dentro del pez","text":"Durante tres días y tres noches Jonás estuvo dentro del pez, y allí oró a Dios. Entonces el pez lo dejó en tierra seca. Dios le pidió a Jonás por segunda vez que fuera a Nínive, y esta vez Jonás fue."},{"head":"otra oportunidad para toda una ciudad","text":"Jonás dio el mensaje de Dios. La gente escuchó, desde el rey para abajo. Se arrepintieron y cambiaron, y Dios tuvo misericordia de ellos. El relato termina con Dios recordándole a Jonás cuánto le importan todas las personas de esa gran ciudad, y hasta sus animales."}],"game":{"items":[["Jonás subió a un barco que iba hacia el otro lado.",true,"sí: no quería ir a Nínive, así que navegó en dirección contraria."],["Jonás llegó a Nínive montado en un camello.",false,"no está: el relato no dice cómo viajó, solo que la segunda vez sí fue."],["Jonás oró dentro del gran pez.",true,"sí: tres días y tres noches, y luego el pez lo dejó en tierra seca."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una ola que sube. suelta el aire, como el mar que se calma. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le daré a alguien una segunda oportunidad","grownups":{"source":"El libro de Jonás, un libro corto de la Biblia que leen judíos y cristianos; Jesús menciona a Jonás en Mateo 12:40. Esta versión deja fuera el último capítulo, donde Jonás se enoja bajo una planta y Dios le pide con suavidad que se preocupe por los demás.","ask":"¿Alguna vez no querías hacer algo y al final lo hiciste? ¿Qué pasó?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jonah 1–4","work":"The Bible (Jonah)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Matthew 12:40","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"daniel-lions","game":"match","en":{"title":"Daniel and the lions","word":"faithful","means":"steady and true, even when it's hard","story":[{"head":"Daniel prays by his window","text":"Daniel lived far from his home, in the kingdom of King Darius, who trusted him with important work. Three times a day Daniel knelt by his open window, facing toward Jerusalem, and prayed and gave thanks to God."},{"head":"a new rule from the king","text":"Some other leaders were jealous of Daniel. They asked the king to make a rule: for thirty days, nobody could pray to anyone except the king. The king agreed. Daniel heard about the rule, went home, and prayed by his window just as before."},{"head":"a night in the lions' den","text":"The rule said Daniel had to go into a den of lions. The king was very sad, because he liked Daniel. That night the king couldn't eat and couldn't sleep. Down in the den, Daniel spent the whole night among the lions."},{"head":"a glad king in the morning","text":"At first light the king hurried to the den and called out. Daniel answered that God had sent an angel to shut the lions' mouths, and he wasn't hurt at all. The glad king had Daniel lifted out, and told his whole kingdom to honor Daniel's God."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Daniel","prayed three times a day"],["the king","couldn't sleep all night"],["an angel","shut the lions' mouths"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the first morning light reaching the den. breathe out, calm as the quiet lions. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll keep doing one good thing, even when it's hard","grownups":{"source":"Daniel 6 in the Bible, a story Jews and Christians both tell. This telling leaves out what happened to the jealous leaders at the end of the chapter.","ask":"What's something good you keep doing even when it's hard?"}},"es":{"title":"Daniel y los leones","word":"fiel","means":"firme y leal, aunque sea difícil","story":[{"head":"Daniel ora junto a su ventana","text":"Daniel vivía lejos de su tierra, en el reino del rey Darío, que le confiaba trabajos importantes. Tres veces al día Daniel se arrodillaba junto a su ventana abierta, mirando hacia Jerusalén, y oraba y daba gracias a Dios."},{"head":"una nueva regla del rey","text":"Otros jefes tenían celos de Daniel. Le pidieron al rey que hiciera una regla: durante treinta días, nadie podía orar a nadie más que al rey. El rey aceptó. Daniel supo de la regla, fue a su casa y oró junto a su ventana como siempre."},{"head":"una noche en el foso de los leones","text":"La regla decía que Daniel tenía que entrar en un foso de leones. El rey se puso muy triste, porque Daniel le caía bien. Esa noche el rey no pudo comer ni dormir. Abajo en el foso, Daniel pasó toda la noche entre los leones."},{"head":"un rey feliz por la mañana","text":"Al amanecer el rey corrió al foso y llamó. Daniel respondió que Dios había enviado a un ángel a cerrar la boca de los leones, y que no le había pasado nada. El rey, feliz, mandó sacar a Daniel y pidió a todo su reino honrar al Dios de Daniel."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Daniel","oraba tres veces al día"],["el rey","no pudo dormir en toda la noche"],["un ángel","cerró la boca de los leones"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la primera luz de la mañana que llega al foso. suelta el aire, tranquilo como los leones callados. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"seguiré haciendo algo bueno, aunque sea difícil","grownups":{"source":"Daniel 6 en la Biblia, un relato que cuentan judíos y cristianos. Esta versión deja fuera lo que les pasó a los jefes celosos al final del capítulo.","ask":"¿Qué cosa buena sigues haciendo aunque sea difícil?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Daniel 6","work":"The Bible (Daniel)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"christmas-manger","game":"truth","en":{"title":"a baby in a manger","word":"manger","means":"a feeding box for animals, where baby Jesus was laid","story":[{"head":"the long trip to Bethlehem","text":"The emperor of Rome ordered everyone to be counted, each in their own town. So Joseph traveled with Mary to Bethlehem, the town of King David's family. Mary was soon to have a baby, and Christians believe this baby was God's own Son."},{"head":"no room, so a manger","text":"While they were in Bethlehem, Mary's baby was born. There was no room for them in the inn, so she wrapped him snugly in cloths and laid him in a manger, the box animals eat from. They named him Jesus."},{"head":"angels sing to the shepherds","text":"In the fields nearby, shepherds were watching their sheep at night. An angel came, shining with God's light, and told them not to be afraid: good news of great joy had come. Then a great crowd of angels praised God: “Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men.”"},{"head":"the shepherds find the baby","text":"The shepherds hurried to Bethlehem and found Mary, Joseph, and the baby lying in the manger, just as the angel had said. They told everyone what they had heard, and went back praising God. Mary kept all these things in her heart."}],"game":{"items":[["Baby Jesus was laid in a manger.",true,"yes: there was no room in the inn, so Mary laid him in the animals' feeding box."],["Mary rode to Bethlehem on a donkey.",false,"not in it: Luke doesn't say how they traveled. The donkey comes from later tellings and plays."],["Angels came to shepherds watching sheep at night.",true,"yes: an angel brought good news, and then many angels praised God."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a quiet night under the stars. breathe out, soft as a sleeping baby. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll share some good news with someone today","grownups":{"source":"Luke 2:1–20 in the Bible. Christians celebrate it at Christmas: many churches on December 25, and some Orthodox churches on January 7, by an older calendar. The ox and donkey of the nativity scene come from later tradition, not from Luke.","ask":"If you had been one of the shepherds, who would you have told first?"}},"es":{"title":"un bebé en un pesebre","word":"pesebre","means":"un comedero para animales, donde pusieron al niño Jesús","story":[{"head":"el largo viaje a Belén","text":"El emperador de Roma ordenó que contaran a todos, cada uno en su propio pueblo. Así que José viajó con María a Belén, el pueblo de la familia del rey David. María estaba por tener un bebé, y los cristianos creen que ese bebé era el propio Hijo de Dios."},{"head":"no hay lugar, así que un pesebre","text":"Mientras estaban en Belén, nació el bebé de María. No había lugar para ellos en el mesón, así que ella lo envolvió bien en pañales y lo acostó en un pesebre, la caja donde comen los animales. Le pusieron por nombre Jesús."},{"head":"los ángeles cantan a los pastores","text":"En los campos cercanos, unos pastores cuidaban sus ovejas de noche. Llegó un ángel, brillando con la luz de Dios, y les dijo que no tuvieran miedo: había llegado una noticia de gran alegría. Luego una multitud de ángeles alabó a Dios, cantando gloria a Dios en las alturas y paz en la tierra."},{"head":"los pastores encuentran al bebé","text":"Los pastores fueron deprisa a Belén y encontraron a María, a José y al bebé acostado en el pesebre, tal como había dicho el ángel. Contaron a todos lo que habían oído, y volvieron alabando a Dios. María guardaba todas estas cosas en su corazón."}],"game":{"items":[["Al niño Jesús lo acostaron en un pesebre.",true,"sí: no había lugar en el mesón, así que María lo acostó en el comedero de los animales."],["María viajó a Belén montada en un burro.",false,"no está: Lucas no dice cómo viajaron. El burro viene de relatos y obras de teatro posteriores."],["Unos ángeles visitaron a pastores que cuidaban ovejas de noche.",true,"sí: un ángel trajo buenas noticias, y luego muchos ángeles alabaron a Dios."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una noche tranquila bajo las estrellas. suelta el aire, suave como un bebé dormido. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le contaré una buena noticia a alguien","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 2:1–20 en la Biblia. Los cristianos lo celebran en Navidad: muchas iglesias el 25 de diciembre, y algunas iglesias ortodoxas el 7 de enero, según un calendario más antiguo. El buey y el burro del nacimiento vienen de una tradición posterior, no de Lucas.","ask":"Si hubieras sido uno de los pastores, ¿a quién le habrías contado primero?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 2:1–20","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 2:14","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men."}]},{"day":7,"key":"wise-men-star","game":"match","en":{"title":"the wise men and the star","word":"Epiphany","means":"a church feast that remembers the wise men's visit","story":[{"head":"a new star in the east","text":"Far to the east, some wise men watched the night sky. One night they saw a new star, and they believed it meant a great king had been born. So they set off on a long journey to find him and honor him."},{"head":"asking the way in Jerusalem","text":"In Jerusalem they asked where the newborn king could be found. King Herod heard and was troubled. His teachers searched the old writings and said the child would be born in Bethlehem. So Herod sent the wise men there, and asked them to come back and tell him."},{"head":"following the star to the house","text":"As they went, the star went ahead of them and stopped over the place where the child was. The Bible says they were filled with great joy. They went into the house and saw the child Jesus with his mother, Mary."},{"head":"three gifts, and a different road","text":"The wise men knelt and gave him gifts of gold, frankincense and myrrh. Frankincense is a sweet-smelling resin, and myrrh is a precious spice. Then a dream told them not to go back to Herod, so they went home by another road."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the wise men","came from the east"],["the star","stopped over the house"],["frankincense","a sweet-smelling resin"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like looking up at one bright star. breathe out, like a long road home. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll give someone the gift of my time today","grownups":{"source":"Matthew 2:1–12 in the Bible. Matthew doesn't say how many wise men came or call them kings; the number three comes from the three gifts, and their names from later tradition. Western churches remember them at Epiphany, January 6.","ask":"If you could bring a gift to someone you love, what would it be?"}},"es":{"title":"los magos y la estrella","word":"Epifanía","means":"una fiesta de la Iglesia que recuerda la visita de los magos","story":[{"head":"una estrella nueva en el oriente","text":"Lejos, en el oriente, unos sabios observaban el cielo de noche. Una noche vieron una estrella nueva, y creyeron que significaba que había nacido un gran rey. Así que emprendieron un largo viaje para encontrarlo y honrarlo."},{"head":"preguntando el camino en Jerusalén","text":"En Jerusalén preguntaron dónde estaba el rey recién nacido. El rey Herodes lo supo y se inquietó. Sus maestros buscaron en los escritos antiguos y dijeron que el niño nacería en Belén. Herodes envió allá a los magos, y les pidió que volvieran a contarle."},{"head":"siguiendo la estrella hasta la casa","text":"Mientras caminaban, la estrella iba delante de ellos y se detuvo sobre el lugar donde estaba el niño. La Biblia dice que se llenaron de una alegría enorme. Entraron en la casa y vieron al niño Jesús con su madre, María."},{"head":"tres regalos y otro camino","text":"Los magos se arrodillaron y le dieron regalos de oro, incienso y mirra. El incienso es una resina de olor dulce, y la mirra es una especia preciosa. Luego un sueño les avisó que no volvieran con Herodes, así que regresaron a casa por otro camino."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["los magos","vinieron del oriente"],["la estrella","se detuvo sobre la casa"],["el incienso","una resina de olor dulce"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como mirando una estrella brillante. suelta el aire, como un largo camino a casa. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le regalaré mi tiempo a alguien","grownups":{"source":"Mateo 2:1–12 en la Biblia. Mateo no dice cuántos magos vinieron ni los llama reyes; el número tres viene de los tres regalos, y sus nombres de una tradición posterior. Las iglesias de Occidente los recuerdan en la Epifanía, el 6 de enero, el Día de Reyes.","ask":"Si pudieras llevarle un regalo a alguien que quieres, ¿qué sería?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Matthew 2:1–12","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"calming-storm","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Jesus calms the storm","word":"calm","means":"quiet and still, with nothing rushing or churning","story":[{"head":"evening in the boat","text":"One evening, Jesus asked his friends, the disciples, to cross to the other side of the lake with him. They set off in a boat, and other little boats sailed along with them. Jesus was tired, and he lay down in the back."},{"head":"a storm while Jesus sleeps","text":"A great windstorm came up. Waves splashed into the boat until it was filling with water. And Jesus? He was asleep on a cushion in the back. His friends woke him and asked if he didn't care that they were in danger."},{"head":"peace, be still","text":"Jesus got up and spoke to the wind and the sea: “Peace, be still.” The wind stopped blowing, and there was a great calm. Then Jesus asked his friends why they had been so afraid, and whether they had faith."},{"head":"a great calm on the lake","text":"His friends were amazed. Who is this, they asked one another, that even the wind and the sea do what he says? For Christians, the story is a reminder that Jesus is with them when life feels stormy."}],"game":{"items":[["Jesus was asleep on a cushion when the storm came.",true,"yes: he was sleeping in the back of the boat while the waves splashed in."],["Jesus told his friends to row faster.",false,"not in it: Jesus spoke to the wind and the sea, and they went still."],["After Jesus spoke, there was a great calm.",true,"yes: the wind stopped and the lake went quiet."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a boat rocking gently. breathe out, peace, be still. four slow breaths, as the lake goes calm.","carry":"when I feel stormy inside, I'll take one slow breath","grownups":{"source":"Mark 4:35–41 in the Bible; Matthew 8 and Luke 8 tell it too. Jesus's words “Peace, be still” are from the King James Version.","ask":"What helps you feel calm when things feel stormy inside?"}},"es":{"title":"Jesús calma la tormenta","word":"calma","means":"quietud y paz, sin nada que corra ni se agite","story":[{"head":"de tarde en la barca","text":"Una tarde, Jesús les pidió a sus amigos, los discípulos, que cruzaran con él al otro lado del lago. Salieron en una barca, y otras barquitas iban con ellos. Jesús estaba cansado, y se acostó en la parte de atrás."},{"head":"una tormenta mientras Jesús duerme","text":"Se levantó un gran viento. Las olas entraban en la barca, y se estaba llenando de agua. ¿Y Jesús? Dormía sobre un cojín en la parte de atrás. Sus amigos lo despertaron y le preguntaron si no le importaba que estuvieran en peligro."},{"head":"silencio, quédate quieto","text":"Jesús se levantó y le habló al viento y al mar, pidiéndoles silencio y quietud. El viento dejó de soplar, y hubo una gran calma. Luego Jesús les preguntó a sus amigos por qué habían tenido tanto miedo, y si tenían fe."},{"head":"una gran calma en el lago","text":"Sus amigos estaban asombrados. ¿Quién es este, se preguntaban, que hasta el viento y el mar le obedecen? Para los cristianos, el relato les recuerda que Jesús está con ellos cuando la vida se siente como una tormenta."}],"game":{"items":[["Jesús dormía sobre un cojín cuando llegó la tormenta.",true,"sí: dormía en la parte de atrás de la barca mientras entraban las olas."],["Jesús les dijo a sus amigos que remaran más rápido.",false,"no está: Jesús le habló al viento y al mar, y se quedaron quietos."],["Cuando Jesús habló, hubo una gran calma.",true,"sí: el viento se detuvo y el lago quedó en silencio."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una barca que se mece suave. suelta el aire: silencio, quietud. cuatro respiraciones lentas, mientras el lago se calma.","carry":"si me siento como una tormenta, respiraré despacio","grownups":{"source":"Marcos 4:35–41 en la Biblia; Mateo 8 y Lucas 8 también lo cuentan. Las palabras de Jesús al viento se resumen aquí sin cita textual.","ask":"¿Qué te ayuda a sentir calma cuando por dentro sientes una tormenta?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Mark 4:35–41","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Mark)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Mark 4:39","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Mark)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"Peace, be still."}]},{"day":9,"key":"loaves-fishes","game":"match","en":{"title":"a boy's lunch for a crowd","word":"miracle","means":"a wonder that shows God's power, in the Bible's stories","story":[{"head":"a hungry crowd on a hillside","text":"A big crowd followed Jesus up a hillside by the Sea of Galilee. They had seen him heal sick people, and they wanted to be near him. As the day went on, Jesus looked out and saw how many had come."},{"head":"five loaves and two small fish","text":"Jesus asked his friend Philip where they could buy bread for so many. Philip said even lots of money wouldn't be enough. Then Andrew spoke up: a boy here has five barley loaves and two small fish. But what is that among so many?"},{"head":"Jesus gives thanks and shares","text":"Jesus asked everyone to sit down on the soft green grass. He took the boy's loaves, gave thanks to God, and passed them out, and the fish too. Everyone ate as much as they wanted. Thousands of people, all full!"},{"head":"twelve baskets left over","text":"Afterward Jesus asked his friends to gather up the leftovers, so nothing would be wasted. They filled twelve baskets with pieces of bread. And it all began with one boy who shared what he had."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the boy","had five loaves and two fish"],["Andrew","told Jesus about the boy"],["Jesus","gave thanks and shared"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like sitting down on soft green grass. breathe out, like bread passed from hand to hand. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll share something of mine today, even something small","grownups":{"source":"John 6:1–14 in the Bible. Matthew, Mark and Luke tell the feeding of the crowd too; John is the one who mentions the boy and his barley loaves.","ask":"What's something small you have that you could share with someone?"}},"es":{"title":"el almuerzo de un niño para una multitud","word":"milagro","means":"una maravilla que muestra el poder de Dios, en los relatos bíblicos","story":[{"head":"una multitud con hambre en una colina","text":"Una gran multitud siguió a Jesús a una colina junto al mar de Galilea. Lo habían visto sanar a enfermos, y querían estar cerca de él. Mientras pasaba el día, Jesús miró a su alrededor y vio cuánta gente había venido."},{"head":"cinco panes y dos pescaditos","text":"Jesús le preguntó a su amigo Felipe dónde podían comprar pan para tantos. Felipe dijo que ni con mucho dinero alcanzaría. Entonces Andrés dijo: aquí hay un niño que tiene cinco panes de cebada y dos pescaditos. Pero ¿qué es eso para tanta gente?"},{"head":"Jesús da gracias y comparte","text":"Jesús pidió a todos que se sentaran en la hierba verde y suave. Tomó los panes del niño, dio gracias a Dios y los repartió, y también los pescados. Todos comieron cuanto quisieron. ¡Miles de personas, todas satisfechas!"},{"head":"doce canastas de sobra","text":"Después Jesús pidió a sus amigos que recogieran lo que sobró, para que nada se desperdiciara. Llenaron doce canastas con pedazos de pan. Y todo empezó con un niño que compartió lo que tenía."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el niño","tenía cinco panes y dos peces"],["Andrés","le habló a Jesús del niño"],["Jesús","dio gracias y compartió"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como al sentarte en la hierba verde y suave. suelta el aire, como pan que pasa de mano en mano. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy compartiré algo mío, aunque sea pequeño","grownups":{"source":"Juan 6:1–14 en la Biblia. Mateo, Marcos y Lucas también cuentan cómo Jesús alimentó a la multitud; Juan es quien menciona al niño y sus panes de cebada.","ask":"¿Qué cosa pequeña tienes que podrías compartir con alguien?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"John 6:1–14","work":"The Bible (Gospel of John)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"jesus-children","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Jesus welcomes the children","word":"blessing","means":"a kind wish for good, asked from God for someone","story":[{"head":"families bring their children","text":"People were bringing their children to Jesus. They wanted him to touch them and bless them. Little ones, bigger ones, even babies in their parents' arms, all coming close through the crowd."},{"head":"the disciples say not now","text":"But Jesus's friends, the disciples, told the families off and tried to send them away. The Bible doesn't say why. Maybe they thought Jesus was too busy, or that children could wait."},{"head":"Jesus says let them come","text":"When Jesus saw it, he was not pleased with his friends. He told them to let the little children come to him and not to stop them, because God's kingdom belongs to people like these children."},{"head":"held close and blessed","text":"Then Jesus took the children up in his arms, laid his hands on them, and blessed them. The children his friends had tried to send away were now held close."}],"game":{"items":[["Families brought children to Jesus to be blessed.",true,"yes: they wanted Jesus to touch them and bless them."],["The disciples tried to send the children away.",true,"yes: and Jesus told them to let the children come."],["Jesus gave each child a present.",false,"not in it: he took them in his arms and blessed them."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like being lifted up in a warm hug. breathe out, safe and held. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll make room for someone younger today","grownups":{"source":"Mark 10:13–16 in the Bible; Matthew 19 and Luke 18 tell it too. Churches differ on baptism: Catholic, Orthodox and some Protestant churches baptize babies, while Baptists and others wait until a child can choose.","ask":"When have you felt really welcomed by someone? What did they do?"}},"es":{"title":"Jesús recibe a los niños","word":"bendición","means":"un deseo de bien, pedido a Dios para alguien","story":[{"head":"las familias traen a sus hijos","text":"La gente llevaba a sus niños a Jesús. Querían que él los tocara y los bendijera. Pequeños, más grandes y hasta bebés en brazos de sus padres, todos acercándose entre la multitud."},{"head":"los discípulos dicen ahora no","text":"Pero los amigos de Jesús, los discípulos, regañaron a las familias y trataron de mandarlas lejos. La Biblia no dice por qué. Quizás pensaron que Jesús estaba muy ocupado, o que los niños podían esperar."},{"head":"Jesús dice que los dejen venir","text":"Cuando Jesús lo vio, no le gustó lo que hacían sus amigos. Les dijo que dejaran a los niños venir a él y que no se lo impidieran, porque el reino de Dios es de los que son como estos niños."},{"head":"abrazados y bendecidos","text":"Entonces Jesús tomó a los niños en sus brazos, puso sus manos sobre ellos y los bendijo. Los niños que sus amigos habían querido mandar lejos ahora estaban en sus brazos."}],"game":{"items":[["Las familias llevaron a sus niños a Jesús para que los bendijera.",true,"sí: querían que Jesús los tocara y los bendijera."],["Los discípulos trataron de mandar lejos a los niños.",true,"sí: y Jesús les dijo que dejaran venir a los niños."],["Jesús le dio un regalo a cada niño.",false,"no está: los tomó en sus brazos y los bendijo."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como cuando te levantan en un abrazo cálido. suelta el aire, seguro y protegido. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le haré lugar a alguien más pequeño","grownups":{"source":"Marcos 10:13–16 en la Biblia; Mateo 19 y Lucas 18 también lo cuentan. Las iglesias difieren sobre el bautismo: la católica, la ortodoxa y algunas protestantes bautizan a bebés, mientras que los bautistas y otros esperan a que el niño pueda elegir.","ask":"¿Cuándo te sentiste muy bien recibido por alguien? ¿Qué hizo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Mark 10:13–16","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Mark)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 18:15–17","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"good-samaritan","game":"match","en":{"title":"the good Samaritan","word":"neighbor","means":"anyone near you who needs kindness, the story says","story":[{"head":"who is my neighbor?","text":"A teacher of God's law asked Jesus what to do to have eternal life. Jesus asked what the law said. The man answered with two lines from the Torah: love God with all your heart, and love your neighbor as yourself. But who is my neighbor? he asked. Jesus answered with a story."},{"head":"hurt on the road to Jericho","text":"A man was walking down the road from Jerusalem to Jericho. Robbers stopped him, took his things, hurt him, and left him lying by the road. He couldn't get up on his own."},{"head":"two travelers pass by","text":"Soon a priest came down that road. He saw the man and passed by on the other side. Then a Levite, a helper at the temple, came along and did the same. The story doesn't say why they didn't stop."},{"head":"a Samaritan stops to help","text":"Then a man from Samaria came, from a people the hurt man's people often didn't get along with. He felt for him, bandaged his wounds, set him on his own animal, and paid an innkeeper to care for him. Who was the neighbor? The one who showed kindness. Jesus said, “Go, and do thou likewise.”"}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the priest","passed by on the other side"],["the Samaritan","stopped and bandaged him"],["the innkeeper","was paid to care for him"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like someone kneeling down to help. breathe out, gentle as a bandage. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll stop and help someone who needs it today","grownups":{"source":"Luke 10:25–37 in the Bible. The two commandments come from the Torah (Deuteronomy 6:5, Leviticus 19:18), scripture that Jesus and everyone in the story shared as Jews. The point is who stops to help, not who belongs where. “Do thou likewise” means “do the same.”","ask":"Who could be your neighbor this week, even someone you don't know well?"}},"es":{"title":"el buen samaritano","word":"prójimo","means":"cualquiera cerca de ti que necesita bondad, dice el relato","story":[{"head":"¿quién es mi prójimo?","text":"Un maestro de la ley de Dios le preguntó a Jesús qué hacer para tener la vida eterna. Jesús le preguntó qué decía la ley. El hombre respondió con dos frases de la Torá: ama a Dios con todo tu corazón, y ama a tu prójimo como a ti mismo. Pero ¿quién es mi prójimo?, preguntó. Jesús respondió con un relato."},{"head":"herido en el camino a Jericó","text":"Un hombre bajaba por el camino de Jerusalén a Jericó. Unos ladrones lo detuvieron, le quitaron sus cosas, lo lastimaron y lo dejaron tirado junto al camino. No podía levantarse solo."},{"head":"dos viajeros pasan de largo","text":"Pronto pasó por ese camino un sacerdote. Vio al hombre y pasó por el otro lado. Luego llegó un levita, un ayudante del templo, e hizo lo mismo. El relato no dice por qué no se detuvieron."},{"head":"un samaritano se detiene a ayudar","text":"Entonces llegó un hombre de Samaria, de un pueblo con el que el pueblo del herido a menudo no se llevaba bien. Sintió compasión, le vendó las heridas, lo subió a su propio animal y le pagó a un posadero para que lo cuidara. ¿Quién fue el prójimo? El que mostró bondad. Jesús dijo: ve y haz tú lo mismo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el sacerdote","pasó por el otro lado"],["el samaritano","se detuvo y le vendó las heridas"],["el posadero","recibió pago para cuidarlo"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como alguien que se arrodilla para ayudar. suelta el aire, suave como una venda. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy me detendré a ayudar a alguien que lo necesite","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 10:25–37 en la Biblia. Los dos mandamientos vienen de la Torá (Deuteronomio 6:5, Levítico 19:18), escritura que Jesús y todos en el relato compartían como judíos. Lo importante es quién se detiene a ayudar, no quién pertenece a dónde.","ask":"¿Quién podría ser tu prójimo esta semana, aunque no lo conozcas bien?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 10:25–37","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 10:37","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"Go, and do thou likewise."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 6:5; Leviticus 19:18","work":"The Bible (Torah)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"lost-sheep","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the lost sheep","word":"parable","means":"a short story Jesus told to teach something big","story":[{"head":"a hundred sheep, one missing","text":"Some people grumbled that Jesus spent time with people who had done wrong. So Jesus told them a story. Imagine, he said, a shepherd with a hundred sheep. One day he finds that one of them is missing."},{"head":"the shepherd goes searching","text":"What does the shepherd do? He leaves the ninety-nine in the open country and goes after the lost one. He keeps on looking, and looking, until he finds it."},{"head":"carried home on his shoulders","text":"When he finds the lost sheep, he is so happy. He lifts it up onto his shoulders, full of joy, and carries it all the way home."},{"head":"a party for one sheep","text":"Then he calls his friends and neighbors together: “Rejoice with me; for I have found my sheep which was lost.” Jesus said heaven is full of joy like that whenever one person who has wandered away comes back to God."}],"game":{"items":[["The shepherd had a hundred sheep.",true,"yes: a hundred, and one went missing."],["The shepherd scolded the lost sheep when he found it.",false,"not in it: he put it on his shoulders, full of joy, and carried it home."],["The shepherd called his friends to celebrate.",true,"yes: he asked them to rejoice with him because the lost sheep was found."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a shepherd lifting a sheep onto his shoulders. breathe out, like the long walk home. four slow breaths.","carry":"if someone is left out, I'll go and find them","grownups":{"source":"Luke 15:3–7 in the Bible; Matthew 18:12–14 tells it too. In Luke it's the first of three “lost” stories: the lost sheep, the lost coin, and the lost son.","ask":"Have you ever lost something and then found it? How did that feel?"}},"es":{"title":"la oveja perdida","word":"parábola","means":"un relato corto que Jesús contó para enseñar algo grande","story":[{"head":"cien ovejas, falta una","text":"Algunas personas se quejaban de que Jesús pasaba tiempo con gente que había hecho cosas malas. Así que Jesús les contó un relato. Imaginen, dijo, a un pastor con cien ovejas. Un día descubre que le falta una."},{"head":"el pastor sale a buscar","text":"¿Qué hace el pastor? Deja a las noventa y nueve en el campo y va tras la que se perdió. Sigue buscando, y buscando, hasta que la encuentra."},{"head":"llevada a casa sobre sus hombros","text":"Cuando encuentra a la oveja perdida, se pone muy feliz. La sube sobre sus hombros, lleno de alegría, y la lleva cargada todo el camino hasta su casa."},{"head":"una fiesta por una oveja","text":"Luego reúne a sus amigos y vecinos y les pide que se alegren con él, porque encontró a su oveja perdida. Jesús dijo que en el cielo hay una alegría así cada vez que una persona que se había alejado vuelve a Dios."}],"game":{"items":[["El pastor tenía cien ovejas.",true,"sí: cien, y una se perdió."],["El pastor regañó a la oveja perdida cuando la encontró.",false,"no está: la subió a sus hombros, lleno de alegría, y la llevó a casa."],["El pastor llamó a sus amigos para celebrar.",true,"sí: les pidió que se alegraran con él porque encontró a la oveja perdida."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un pastor que sube una oveja a sus hombros. suelta el aire, como el largo camino a casa. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"si alguien se queda fuera, iré a buscarlo","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 15:3–7 en la Biblia; Mateo 18:12–14 también lo cuenta. En Lucas es el primero de tres relatos de cosas perdidas: la oveja, la moneda y el hijo.","ask":"¿Alguna vez perdiste algo y luego lo encontraste? ¿Cómo te sentiste?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 15:1–7","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 15:6","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"Rejoice with me; for I have found my sheep which was lost."},{"ref":"Matthew 18:12–14","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"lost-son","game":"match","en":{"title":"the son who came home","word":"forgive","means":"to let go of a hurt and welcome someone back","story":[{"head":"the younger son leaves home","text":"Jesus told another story. A man had two sons. The younger one asked for his share of the family's money, packed up, and went off to a faraway country. There he spent it all, until nothing was left."},{"head":"hungry, far away, with the pigs","text":"Then food ran short in that land, and he was hungry. He took a job feeding pigs, and he was so hungry he wished he could eat the pigs' food. Then he thought of home. He would go back and tell his father he was sorry."},{"head":"his father runs to meet him","text":"While he was still a long way off, his father saw him and ran to him. He threw his arms around him and kissed him. The father called for the best robe, a ring for his finger and shoes for his feet. Time for a feast!"},{"head":"a feast, and a talk with the brother","text":"The older brother, coming in from the fields, felt it wasn't fair and wouldn't go in. So the father went out to him too. You are always with me, he said, and all I have is yours. But your brother was lost, and now he's found."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the younger son","fed pigs in a faraway land"],["the father","ran to meet his son"],["the older brother","came in from the fields"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a father running down the road. breathe out, like a big welcome-home hug. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll say sorry, or say it's okay, to someone today","grownups":{"source":"Luke 15:11–32 in the Bible, often called the parable of the prodigal son. Christians commonly read the father as a picture of God's welcome. The story ends without telling us whether the older brother went in.","ask":"Who do you think the story is most about: the younger son, the father, or the older brother?"}},"es":{"title":"el hijo que volvió a casa","word":"perdonar","means":"dejar ir una ofensa y recibir de nuevo a alguien","story":[{"head":"el hijo menor se va de casa","text":"Jesús contó otro relato. Un hombre tenía dos hijos. El menor pidió su parte del dinero de la familia, empacó y se fue a un país lejano. Allí lo gastó todo, hasta que no le quedó nada."},{"head":"con hambre, lejos, con los cerdos","text":"Luego faltó la comida en ese país, y él tenía hambre. Consiguió un trabajo cuidando cerdos, y tenía tanta hambre que quería comer lo que comían los cerdos. Entonces pensó en su casa. Volvería y le diría a su padre que lo sentía."},{"head":"su padre corre a su encuentro","text":"Cuando todavía estaba lejos, su padre lo vio y corrió hacia él. Lo abrazó y lo besó. El padre pidió el mejor manto, un anillo para su dedo y sandalias para sus pies. ¡Hora de hacer una fiesta!"},{"head":"una fiesta y una charla con el hermano","text":"El hermano mayor, que volvía del campo, sintió que no era justo y no quiso entrar. Así que el padre salió también a buscarlo. Tú siempre estás conmigo, le dijo, y todo lo mío es tuyo. Pero tu hermano estaba perdido, y ya lo encontramos."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el hijo menor","cuidó cerdos en un país lejano"],["el padre","corrió al encuentro de su hijo"],["el hermano mayor","volvía del campo"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un padre que corre por el camino. suelta el aire, como un gran abrazo de bienvenida. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy pediré perdón, o diré que ya está bien","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 15:11–32 en la Biblia, a menudo llamada la parábola del hijo pródigo. Los cristianos suelen ver en el padre una imagen de la bienvenida de Dios. El relato termina sin decirnos si el hermano mayor entró.","ask":"¿De quién crees que trata más el relato: del hijo menor, del padre o del hermano mayor?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 15:11–32","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"zacchaeus","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Zacchaeus climbs a tree","word":"tax collector","means":"someone who gathered money for the rulers of the land","story":[{"head":"a short man in a big crowd","text":"In the town of Jericho lived a rich man named Zacchaeus. He was a chief tax collector, and people didn't think well of him. When Jesus came through town, Zacchaeus wanted to see him. But he was short, and he couldn't see over the crowd."},{"head":"up into a sycamore tree","text":"So Zacchaeus ran on ahead and climbed up a sycamore tree beside the road. Picture a grown man perched in the branches, peering down through the leaves, waiting for Jesus to pass by."},{"head":"Jesus calls him by name","text":"When Jesus reached the spot, he looked up and called Zacchaeus by name. He told him to hurry down, because today he would stay at Zacchaeus's house. Zacchaeus scrambled down and welcomed him with joy, though some in the crowd grumbled."},{"head":"Zacchaeus makes things right","text":"Zacchaeus stood up and said he would give half of what he owned to the poor. And if he had cheated anyone, he would pay them back four times over. Jesus said he had come to look for and save people who were lost."}],"game":{"items":[["Zacchaeus climbed a tree to see Jesus.",true,"yes: he was short, so he climbed a sycamore tree by the road."],["Jesus walked past without noticing Zacchaeus.",false,"not in it: Jesus looked up, called him by name, and went to his house."],["Zacchaeus promised to give half of what he owned to the poor.",true,"yes: and to pay back four times over anyone he had cheated."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like climbing up into a leafy tree. breathe out, like hopping down to say hello. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll call someone by name and make them feel welcome","grownups":{"source":"Luke 19:1–10 in the Bible. Tax collectors then gathered money for Rome, and people often suspected them of taking extra for themselves.","ask":"Why do you think Jesus chose to visit Zacchaeus's house?"}},"es":{"title":"Zaqueo se sube a un árbol","word":"recaudador","means":"alguien que cobraba impuestos para los gobernantes","story":[{"head":"un hombre bajito entre mucha gente","text":"En la ciudad de Jericó vivía un hombre rico llamado Zaqueo. Era jefe de los cobradores de impuestos, y la gente no pensaba bien de él. Cuando Jesús pasó por el pueblo, Zaqueo quería verlo. Pero era bajito, y no podía ver por encima de la multitud."},{"head":"arriba en un sicómoro","text":"Así que Zaqueo corrió adelante y se subió a un sicómoro junto al camino. Imagina a un hombre adulto sentado en las ramas, asomándose entre las hojas, esperando que pasara Jesús."},{"head":"Jesús lo llama por su nombre","text":"Cuando Jesús llegó al lugar, miró hacia arriba y llamó a Zaqueo por su nombre. Le dijo que bajara rápido, porque hoy se quedaría en su casa. Zaqueo bajó deprisa y lo recibió con alegría, aunque algunos en la multitud murmuraban."},{"head":"Zaqueo arregla las cosas","text":"Zaqueo se puso de pie y dijo que daría la mitad de lo que tenía a los pobres. Y si le había cobrado de más a alguien, se lo devolvería cuatro veces. Jesús dijo que había venido a buscar y salvar a los que estaban perdidos."}],"game":{"items":[["Zaqueo se subió a un árbol para ver a Jesús.",true,"sí: era bajito, así que se subió a un sicómoro junto al camino."],["Jesús pasó de largo sin ver a Zaqueo.",false,"no está: Jesús miró hacia arriba, lo llamó por su nombre y fue a su casa."],["Zaqueo prometió dar la mitad de lo que tenía a los pobres.",true,"sí: y devolver cuatro veces a quien le hubiera cobrado de más."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como al subir a un árbol frondoso. suelta el aire, como al bajar de un salto para saludar. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"llamaré a alguien por su nombre y lo haré sentir bienvenido","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 19:1–10 en la Biblia. Los cobradores de impuestos de entonces recogían dinero para Roma, y la gente a menudo sospechaba que se quedaban con más de lo debido.","ask":"¿Por qué crees que Jesús eligió visitar la casa de Zaqueo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 19:1–10","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":15,"key":"house-on-rock","game":"match","en":{"title":"the house on the rock","word":"foundation","means":"the strong ground a house is built on","story":[{"head":"a house built on rock","text":"At the end of a long teaching on a hillside, Jesus told a story about two builders. The first builder dug down deep and set his house on solid rock."},{"head":"a house built on sand","text":"The second builder built his house right on the sand. It was quicker, and from the outside the two houses might have looked just the same."},{"head":"rain, floods and wind","text":"Then the rain poured down, the rivers rose, and the wind blew and beat against both houses. Splash, whoosh, went the storm, on and on."},{"head":"one house stands firm","text":"The house on the rock stood firm. The house on the sand fell down with a great fall. Jesus said that people who hear his words and do them are like the builder who chose the rock."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each one to what happened.","pairs":[["the house on the rock","stood firm in the storm"],["the house on the sand","fell down"],["hearing and doing","like building on rock"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like feet planted on solid rock. breathe out, letting the wind go by. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll do one kind thing I already know is right","grownups":{"source":"Matthew 7:24–27, at the end of the Sermon on the Mount; Luke 6:47–49 tells it too. In Matthew, Jesus calls the builders wise and foolish; this telling keeps the picture and leaves out the labels.","ask":"What's one thing you know is right that you can do this week?"}},"es":{"title":"la casa sobre la roca","word":"cimiento","means":"la base firme sobre la que se construye una casa","story":[{"head":"una casa sobre la roca","text":"Al final de una larga enseñanza en una colina, Jesús contó un relato sobre dos constructores. El primero cavó hondo y puso su casa sobre roca firme."},{"head":"una casa sobre la arena","text":"El segundo constructor hizo su casa directamente sobre la arena. Era más rápido, y por fuera las dos casas tal vez se veían iguales."},{"head":"lluvia, crecidas y viento","text":"Entonces cayó la lluvia, crecieron los ríos, y el viento sopló y golpeó contra las dos casas. Chap, fuuu, sonaba la tormenta, sin parar."},{"head":"una casa sigue en pie","text":"La casa sobre la roca se quedó firme. La casa sobre la arena se cayó, y fue grande su caída. Jesús dijo que quienes oyen sus palabras y las ponen en práctica son como el constructor que eligió la roca."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada uno con lo que pasó.","pairs":[["la casa sobre la roca","se quedó firme en la tormenta"],["la casa sobre la arena","se cayó"],["oír y hacer","como construir sobre roca"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como con los pies plantados en roca firme. suelta el aire, dejando pasar el viento. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"haré algo bueno que ya sé que es correcto","grownups":{"source":"Mateo 7:24–27, al final del Sermón del Monte; Lucas 6:47–49 también lo cuenta. En Mateo, Jesús llama a los constructores prudente e insensato; esta versión guarda la imagen y deja fuera esas etiquetas.","ask":"¿Qué cosa sabes que es correcta y puedes hacer esta semana?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Matthew 7:24–27","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 6:47–49","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"mustard-seed","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the tiny mustard seed","word":"kingdom","means":"where God's ways of love and kindness are lived","story":[{"head":"a tiny seed in someone's hand","text":"Jesus once asked his listeners what God's kingdom is like. Then he answered with a picture: a mustard seed. The story says it is smaller than all the seeds in the earth, a speck you could lose in your palm."},{"head":"planted in the ground","text":"Someone takes that little seed and plants it in the ground. At first it doesn't look like much at all, and nobody can see anything happening."},{"head":"it grows great branches","text":"But the seed grows, and grows, and grows. It becomes bigger than all the other garden plants, and it puts out great branches."},{"head":"birds rest in its shade","text":"Birds come and rest in its shade. Christians often read the story to mean that God's kingdom starts small, like a little kindness, and grows bigger than anyone expects."}],"game":{"items":[["The mustard seed is tiny when it's planted.",true,"yes: the story says it's smaller than all the seeds in the earth."],["The seed grows great branches where birds rest.",true,"yes: birds come and rest in its shade."],["A farmer waters the seed every day.",false,"not in it: the story only says it's planted and grows."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a seed waking up in the soil. breathe out, like branches spreading wide. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll plant one small kindness today and see what grows","grownups":{"source":"Mark 4:30–32 in the Bible; Matthew 13:31–32 and Luke 13:18–19 tell it too. In Matthew and Luke it grows into a tree where birds make nests.","ask":"What's a small thing someone did for you that turned out to matter a lot?"}},"es":{"title":"la diminuta semilla de mostaza","word":"reino","means":"donde se viven los caminos de amor y bondad de Dios","story":[{"head":"una semilla diminuta en la mano","text":"Una vez Jesús preguntó a quienes lo escuchaban a qué se parece el reino de Dios. Luego respondió con una imagen: una semilla de mostaza. El relato dice que es más pequeña que todas las semillas de la tierra, una motita que podrías perder en la palma."},{"head":"sembrada en la tierra","text":"Alguien toma esa semillita y la siembra en la tierra. Al principio no parece gran cosa, y nadie puede ver que esté pasando nada."},{"head":"le crecen grandes ramas","text":"Pero la semilla crece, y crece, y crece. Se hace más grande que todas las demás plantas del huerto, y echa grandes ramas."},{"head":"los pájaros descansan a su sombra","text":"Los pájaros vienen y descansan a su sombra. Los cristianos a menudo leen el relato como que el reino de Dios empieza pequeño, como una bondad pequeñita, y crece más de lo que nadie espera."}],"game":{"items":[["La semilla de mostaza es diminuta cuando la siembran.",true,"sí: el relato dice que es más pequeña que todas las semillas de la tierra."],["A la semilla le crecen grandes ramas donde descansan los pájaros.",true,"sí: los pájaros vienen y descansan a su sombra."],["Un agricultor riega la semilla todos los días.",false,"no está: el relato solo dice que la siembran y crece."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una semilla que despierta en la tierra. suelta el aire, como ramas que se extienden. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy sembraré una pequeña bondad y veré qué crece","grownups":{"source":"Marcos 4:30–32 en la Biblia; Mateo 13:31–32 y Lucas 13:18–19 también lo cuentan. En Mateo y Lucas se convierte en un árbol donde los pájaros hacen nidos.","ask":"¿Qué cosa pequeña hizo alguien por ti que resultó muy importante?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Mark 4:30–32","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Mark)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Matthew 13:31–32; Luke 13:18–19","work":"The Bible (Gospels)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"widows-coins","game":"match","en":{"title":"the widow's two little coins","word":"offering","means":"a gift given to God, at church or at the temple","story":[{"head":"the offering box at the temple","text":"One day Jesus sat down near the offering box at the temple in Jerusalem. People came by to drop in their gifts, and Jesus watched them, with his friends close by."},{"head":"rich people give a lot","text":"Many rich people came and put in a lot of money. You can almost hear it: clink, clink, clink, coin after coin falling into the box."},{"head":"a widow gives two tiny coins","text":"Then a poor widow came, a woman whose husband had died. She dropped in two tiny copper coins, worth hardly anything at all."},{"head":"Jesus says she gave the most","text":"Jesus called his friends over. He told them this poor widow had put in more than all the others. They gave out of all they had to spare. She had very little, and she gave everything she had."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Jesus","sat near the offering box"],["the rich people","put in a lot of money"],["the widow","gave two tiny coins"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like holding two small coins in your hand. breathe out, like letting them go. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll give something small today, with my whole heart","grownups":{"source":"Mark 12:41–44 and Luke 21:1–4 in the Bible. The coins were tiny copper coins; the King James Version calls them “mites.”","ask":"What could you give that costs no money but means a lot?"}},"es":{"title":"las dos moneditas de la viuda","word":"ofrenda","means":"un regalo dado a Dios, en la iglesia o en el templo","story":[{"head":"la caja de ofrendas del templo","text":"Un día Jesús se sentó cerca de la caja de ofrendas del templo de Jerusalén. La gente pasaba a echar sus regalos, y Jesús los miraba, con sus amigos cerca."},{"head":"los ricos dan mucho","text":"Muchos ricos llegaron y echaron mucho dinero. Casi se puede oír: clin, clin, clin, moneda tras moneda cayendo en la caja."},{"head":"una viuda da dos moneditas","text":"Entonces llegó una viuda pobre, una mujer cuyo esposo había muerto. Echó dos moneditas de cobre, que casi no valían nada."},{"head":"Jesús dice que ella dio más","text":"Jesús llamó a sus amigos. Les dijo que esta viuda pobre había echado más que todos los demás. Ellos dieron de lo que les sobraba. Ella tenía muy poco, y dio todo lo que tenía."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Jesús","se sentó cerca de la caja"],["los ricos","echaron mucho dinero"],["la viuda","dio dos moneditas"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como sosteniendo dos moneditas en la mano. suelta el aire, como al dejarlas ir. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy daré algo pequeño, con todo mi corazón","grownups":{"source":"Marcos 12:41–44 y Lucas 21:1–4 en la Biblia. Eran moneditas de cobre muy pequeñas; algunas Biblias en español las llaman blancas.","ask":"¿Qué podrías dar que no cueste dinero pero signifique mucho?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Mark 12:41–44","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Mark)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 21:1–4","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"mary-martha","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Mary and Martha","word":"listen","means":"to give someone your full attention","story":[{"head":"Jesus visits two sisters","text":"Jesus and his friends came to a village, and a woman named Martha welcomed him into her home. Martha had a sister named Mary, who was there too."},{"head":"Martha busy getting everything ready","text":"There was so much to do for the guests! Martha hurried about getting everything ready, serving and fetching and fixing. She was busy, and she was starting to feel worried."},{"head":"Mary sits and listens","text":"Mary sat down at Jesus's feet and listened to what he was teaching. Martha came to Jesus and asked: didn't he care that her sister had left her to do all the work? Couldn't he tell Mary to help?"},{"head":"Jesus speaks kindly to Martha","text":"Jesus answered gently, saying her name twice: Martha, Martha. You are worried and upset about many things, he said, but only one thing is needed. Mary has chosen the good part, and it won't be taken from her."}],"game":{"items":[["Martha welcomed Jesus into her home.",true,"yes: Martha opened her home, and her sister Mary was there too."],["Mary sat at Jesus's feet and listened.",true,"yes: she sat and listened to his teaching."],["Jesus helped Martha carry the dishes.",false,"not in it: Jesus spoke kindly to Martha and said Mary had chosen the good part."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like sitting down after a busy day. breathe out, like setting down a heavy tray. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll put things down and really listen to someone","grownups":{"source":"Luke 10:38–42 in the Bible. Christians honor both sisters, Martha's welcome and Mary's listening. John 11 and 12 tell more about them and their brother Lazarus.","ask":"When do you feel most like Martha, and when most like Mary?"}},"es":{"title":"Marta y María","word":"escuchar","means":"prestarle a alguien toda tu atención","story":[{"head":"Jesús visita a dos hermanas","text":"Jesús y sus amigos llegaron a una aldea, y una mujer llamada Marta lo recibió en su casa. Marta tenía una hermana llamada María, que también estaba allí."},{"head":"Marta ocupada preparando todo","text":"¡Había tanto que hacer para los invitados! Marta iba de un lado a otro preparando todo, sirviendo, trayendo y arreglando. Estaba muy ocupada, y empezaba a sentirse preocupada."},{"head":"María se sienta a escuchar","text":"María se sentó a los pies de Jesús y escuchaba lo que él enseñaba. Marta fue a Jesús y le preguntó: ¿no le importaba que su hermana la hubiera dejado sola con todo el trabajo? ¿No podía decirle a María que la ayudara?"},{"head":"Jesús le habla con cariño a Marta","text":"Jesús respondió con suavidad, diciendo su nombre dos veces: Marta, Marta. Estás preocupada e inquieta por muchas cosas, le dijo, pero solo una es necesaria. María eligió la buena parte, y nadie se la quitará."}],"game":{"items":[["Marta recibió a Jesús en su casa.",true,"sí: Marta abrió su casa, y su hermana María también estaba allí."],["María se sentó a los pies de Jesús y escuchó.",true,"sí: se sentó a escuchar su enseñanza."],["Jesús ayudó a Marta a llevar los platos.",false,"no está: Jesús le habló con cariño a Marta y dijo que María había elegido la buena parte."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como al sentarte después de un día ocupado. suelta el aire, como al dejar una bandeja pesada. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"dejaré lo que hago y escucharé de verdad a alguien","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 10:38–42 en la Biblia. Los cristianos honran a las dos hermanas, la acogida de Marta y la escucha de María. Juan 11 y 12 cuentan más sobre ellas y su hermano Lázaro.","ask":"¿Cuándo te sientes más como Marta, y cuándo más como María?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 10:38–42","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"John 11–12","work":"The Bible (Gospel of John)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"lords-prayer","game":"match","en":{"title":"Jesus teaches a prayer","word":"prayer","means":"talking with God, out loud or quietly inside","story":[{"head":"the friends watch Jesus pray","text":"Jesus would often go off by himself to pray. One day, when he had finished praying in a quiet place, one of his friends came to him with a question."},{"head":"teach us to pray","text":"Lord, the friend said, teach us to pray. Jesus didn't give them a long speech. He gave them a short prayer they could say anywhere, by themselves or all together."},{"head":"our Father, hallowed be thy name","text":"In Matthew's Gospel it begins, “Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name.” Hallowed means honored as holy. It goes on to ask for God's ways to come on earth, for daily bread, for forgiveness, and for help to forgive others."},{"head":"a prayer churches share","text":"Christians call it the Lord's Prayer, or the Our Father. Catholic, Orthodox and Protestant Christians all pray it, in many languages. Some end it with a line of praise, “For thine is the kingdom,” and some don't."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each part to what it means.","pairs":[["hallowed","honored as holy"],["daily bread","what we need each day"],["the Lord's Prayer","the prayer Jesus taught"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a friend asking a question. breathe out, like a quiet answer. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll say thank you for my daily bread today","grownups":{"source":"Matthew 6:9–13 and Luke 11:1–4 in the Bible. Churches usually say Matthew's version. The closing line of praise appears in the King James text of Matthew but not in Luke, and churches differ on saying it.","ask":"What would you most like to say thank you for today?"}},"es":{"title":"Jesús enseña una oración","word":"oración","means":"hablar con Dios, en voz alta o en silencio por dentro","story":[{"head":"los amigos ven orar a Jesús","text":"Jesús solía apartarse a solas para orar. Un día, cuando terminó de orar en un lugar tranquilo, uno de sus amigos se le acercó con una pregunta."},{"head":"enséñanos a orar","text":"Señor, le dijo el amigo, enséñanos a orar. Jesús no les dio un largo discurso. Les dio una oración corta que podían decir en cualquier lugar, a solas o todos juntos."},{"head":"Padre nuestro, santificado sea tu nombre","text":"En el Evangelio de Mateo empieza llamando a Dios Padre nuestro, que está en el cielo, y pide que su nombre sea santificado, es decir, honrado como santo. Luego pide que los caminos de Dios lleguen a la tierra, el pan de cada día, perdón, y ayuda para perdonar a otros."},{"head":"una oración que comparten las iglesias","text":"Los cristianos la llaman el Padre Nuestro, o la oración del Señor. Católicos, ortodoxos y protestantes la rezan, en muchos idiomas. Algunos la terminan con una frase de alabanza, porque tuyo es el reino, y otros no."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada parte con lo que significa.","pairs":[["santificado","honrado como santo"],["el pan de cada día","lo que necesitamos cada día"],["el Padre Nuestro","la oración que enseñó Jesús"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un amigo que hace una pregunta. suelta el aire, como una respuesta tranquila. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy daré gracias por el pan de cada día","grownups":{"source":"Mateo 6:9–13 y Lucas 11:1–4 en la Biblia. Las iglesias suelen rezar la versión de Mateo. La frase final de alabanza aparece en algunas traducciones de Mateo pero no en Lucas, y las iglesias difieren en decirla.","ask":"¿Por qué te gustaría más dar gracias hoy?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Matthew 6:9","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"Our Father which art in heaven, Hallowed be thy name."},{"ref":"Matthew 6:13","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"For thine is the kingdom"},{"ref":"Luke 11:1–4","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 5:16","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"palm-sunday","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Jesus rides into Jerusalem","word":"hosanna","means":"a shout of praise that asks God to save","story":[{"head":"a young donkey for the ride","text":"As Jesus came near Jerusalem for the Passover feast, he sent two friends into a village to bring back a young donkey. They laid their coats on its back, and Jesus sat on it."},{"head":"coats and branches on the road","text":"A big crowd came out to meet him. People spread their coats on the road in front of him. Others cut branches from the trees, and John's Gospel says they carried palm branches."},{"head":"the crowd shouts hosanna","text":"The crowds in front and behind him shouted, “Hosanna to the son of David: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord.” The whole city was stirred up, asking: who is this?"},{"head":"palm branches in church","text":"Christians remember this day as Palm Sunday, one week before Easter. In some churches people carry palm branches or fold them into little crosses. It begins Holy Week, the days leading up to Easter."}],"game":{"items":[["Jesus rode into Jerusalem on a young donkey.",true,"yes: his friends brought a young donkey and laid their coats on it."],["People spread coats and branches on the road.",true,"yes: and John's Gospel says they carried palm branches."],["Jesus rode in on a big white horse.",false,"not in it: he rode a young donkey."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a palm branch lifting high. breathe out, like a coat laid gently on the road. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll cheer someone on today","grownups":{"source":"Matthew 21:1–11 in the Bible; Mark 11, Luke 19 and John 12 tell it too. Only John mentions palms. Western and Orthodox churches often keep Palm Sunday and Easter on different dates.","ask":"What does it feel like when a crowd cheers for you or someone you love?"}},"es":{"title":"Jesús entra en Jerusalén","word":"hosanna","means":"un grito de alabanza que le pide a Dios que salve","story":[{"head":"un burrito para el camino","text":"Cuando Jesús se acercaba a Jerusalén para la fiesta de la Pascua, envió a dos amigos a una aldea a traer un burrito. Pusieron sus mantos sobre el lomo del burrito, y Jesús se sentó encima."},{"head":"mantos y ramas en el camino","text":"Una gran multitud salió a recibirlo. La gente tendía sus mantos en el camino delante de él. Otros cortaban ramas de los árboles, y el Evangelio de Juan dice que llevaban ramas de palma."},{"head":"la multitud grita hosanna","text":"La gente que iba delante y detrás gritaba hosanna al hijo de David, y bendecía al que viene en el nombre del Señor. Toda la ciudad se agitó, preguntando: ¿quién es este?"},{"head":"ramos de palma en la iglesia","text":"Los cristianos recuerdan este día como el Domingo de Ramos, una semana antes de la Pascua. En algunas iglesias la gente lleva ramos de palma o los dobla en forma de crucecitas. Así empieza la Semana Santa, los días que llevan a la Pascua."}],"game":{"items":[["Jesús entró en Jerusalén montado en un burrito.",true,"sí: sus amigos trajeron un burrito y pusieron sus mantos encima."],["La gente tendió mantos y ramas en el camino.",true,"sí: y el Evangelio de Juan dice que llevaban ramas de palma."],["Jesús entró montado en un gran caballo blanco.",false,"no está: entró montado en un burrito."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una rama de palma que se alza. suelta el aire, como un manto tendido con cuidado en el camino. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le daré ánimo a alguien","grownups":{"source":"Mateo 21:1–11 en la Biblia; Marcos 11, Lucas 19 y Juan 12 también lo cuentan. Solo Juan menciona las palmas. Las iglesias de Occidente y las ortodoxas a menudo celebran el Domingo de Ramos y la Pascua en fechas distintas.","ask":"¿Qué se siente cuando una multitud te aplaude a ti o a alguien que quieres?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Matthew 21:1–11","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Matthew 21:9","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"Hosanna to the son of David: Blessed is he that cometh in the name of the Lord"},{"ref":"John 12:12–15","work":"The Bible (Gospel of John)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"easter-morning","game":"match","en":{"title":"Easter morning","word":"Easter","means":"the day Christians celebrate Jesus rising to new life","story":[{"head":"a last supper with friends","text":"A few days after Palm Sunday, Jesus shared a last supper with his friends. He broke bread and shared a cup with them, and asked them to do this to remember him. Christians still do, and call it communion, the Eucharist, or the Lord's Supper."},{"head":"Jesus dies on a cross","text":"Soon after, Jesus was arrested. The Roman rulers of the land put him to death on a cross, and he died. His friends and his mother were very sad. They laid his body in a tomb cut into rock, and a big stone was rolled across the door."},{"head":"the stone is rolled away","text":"Early on the third day, some women who loved Jesus came to the tomb with sweet spices. Mary Magdalene was one of them. But the big stone had been rolled away, and when they went inside, his body wasn't there."},{"head":"he is not here, but is risen","text":"Two men in shining clothes, angels, said to them, “He is not here, but is risen.” The women ran to tell the others. Christians believe Jesus rose to new life, and at Easter they celebrate with joy."}],"game":{"prompt":"match the Easter story. tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the last supper","bread and a cup with friends"],["the stone","was rolled away"],["the women","found the tomb empty"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the first light of Easter morning. breathe out, like a heavy stone rolling away. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll bring good news, or a smile, to someone today","grownups":{"source":"Luke 22–24 in the Bible; all four Gospels tell of the cross and the empty tomb. The Roman governor, Pontius Pilate, ordered the crucifixion. Western and Orthodox churches often celebrate Easter on different Sundays.","ask":"What do you think the women felt when they saw the stone rolled away?"}},"es":{"title":"la mañana de Pascua","word":"Pascua","means":"el día en que los cristianos celebran que Jesús resucitó","story":[{"head":"una última cena con sus amigos","text":"Unos días después del Domingo de Ramos, Jesús compartió una última cena con sus amigos. Partió el pan y compartió una copa con ellos, y les pidió que lo hicieran para recordarlo. Los cristianos todavía lo hacen, y lo llaman comunión, eucaristía o santa cena."},{"head":"Jesús muere en una cruz","text":"Poco después, arrestaron a Jesús. Los gobernantes romanos de esa tierra lo condenaron a morir en una cruz, y murió. Sus amigos y su madre estaban muy tristes. Pusieron su cuerpo en un sepulcro cavado en la roca, y una gran piedra cerró la entrada."},{"head":"la piedra está corrida","text":"Muy temprano el tercer día, unas mujeres que amaban a Jesús fueron al sepulcro con perfumes. María Magdalena era una de ellas. Pero la gran piedra estaba corrida, y cuando entraron, su cuerpo no estaba allí."},{"head":"no está aquí, ha resucitado","text":"Dos hombres con ropas brillantes, ángeles, les dijeron que él no estaba allí, sino que había resucitado. Las mujeres corrieron a contarlo a los demás. Los cristianos creen que Jesús resucitó a una vida nueva, y en Pascua lo celebran con alegría."}],"game":{"prompt":"une el relato de Pascua. toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la última cena","pan y una copa con amigos"],["la piedra","estaba corrida"],["las mujeres","encontraron el sepulcro vacío"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la primera luz de la mañana de Pascua. suelta el aire, como una piedra pesada que se corre. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le llevaré una buena noticia, o una sonrisa, a alguien","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 22–24 en la Biblia; los cuatro Evangelios cuentan la cruz y el sepulcro vacío. El gobernador romano, Poncio Pilato, ordenó la crucifixión. Las iglesias de Occidente y las ortodoxas a menudo celebran la Pascua en domingos distintos.","ask":"¿Qué crees que sintieron las mujeres al ver la piedra corrida?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 22:14–20","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 23:1–56","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 24:1–10","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Luke 24:6","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Luke)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"He is not here, but is risen"},{"ref":"John 19:25","work":"The Bible (Gospel of John)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]},"CATHOLIC":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"CATHOLIC","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"francis-birds","game":"match","en":{"title":"St. Francis and the birds","word":"St. Francis","means":"a saint from Assisi in Italy who loved God and every creature","story":[{"head":"a man who loved every creature","text":"Long ago in Italy lived a man named Francis. He gave away his fine clothes and wore a plain brown robe tied with a rope. He walked from town to town, telling people about God's love. He called the animals his brothers and sisters."},{"head":"trees full of birds","text":"One day, the old stories say, Francis was walking along a road with his friends. He looked up and saw trees full of birds: doves, crows and many more. He asked his friends to wait for him, and he hurried over to the birds."},{"head":"a talk for the birds","text":"The birds did not fly away. Francis spoke to them gently, calling them his little sisters. He told them to thank God for their feathers that keep them warm, for the wide sky to fly in, and for rivers and springs to drink from. They never plant or harvest, he said, and still God feeds them."},{"head":"wings spread wide","text":"The birds stretched their necks, opened their beaks and spread their wings, as if they were listening. When Francis made the sign of the cross over them, they rose up together, singing, and flew off into the sky. Francis walked on, full of joy."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Francis","spoke gently to the birds"],["the birds","spread their wings to listen"],["his friends","waited by the road"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a bird filling its wings with air. breathe out, like a bird floating down onto a branch. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can be gentle with an animal today","grownups":{"source":"This story comes from the Little Flowers of St. Francis, an old book of stories about him written down long after he lived. Thomas of Celano, who wrote an earlier life of Francis, tells it too.","ask":"If you could say thank you to an animal, which one would it be, and why?"}},"es":{"title":"San Francisco y los pájaros","word":"San Francisco","means":"un santo de Asís, en Italia, que amaba a Dios y a toda criatura","story":[{"head":"un hombre que amaba a toda criatura","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en Italia, vivía un hombre llamado Francisco. Regaló su ropa elegante y se vistió con un sencillo hábito café atado con una cuerda. Iba de pueblo en pueblo hablando del amor de Dios. A los animales los llamaba sus hermanos y hermanas."},{"head":"árboles llenos de pájaros","text":"Un día, cuentan las historias antiguas, Francisco caminaba por un camino con sus amigos. Miró hacia arriba y vio árboles llenos de pájaros: palomas, cuervos y muchos más. Les pidió a sus amigos que lo esperaran y se acercó rápido a los pájaros."},{"head":"una charla para los pájaros","text":"Los pájaros no se fueron volando. Francisco les habló con ternura y las llamó hermanitas aves. Les dijo que dieran gracias a Dios por sus plumas que los abrigan, por el cielo ancho para volar y por los ríos y fuentes para beber. Ellos nunca siembran ni cosechan, les dijo, y aun así Dios los alimenta."},{"head":"alas bien abiertas","text":"Los pájaros estiraron el cuello, abrieron el pico y extendieron las alas, como si estuvieran escuchando. Cuando Francisco hizo la señal de la cruz sobre ellos, se elevaron todos juntos, cantando, y se fueron volando por el cielo. Francisco siguió su camino, lleno de alegría."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Francisco","les habló con ternura"],["los pájaros","abrieron las alas para escuchar"],["sus amigos","esperaron en el camino"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un pájaro que llena sus alas de aire. suelta el aire, como un pájaro que baja flotando hasta una rama. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo tratar con cariño a un animal","grownups":{"source":"Esta historia viene de las Florecillas de San Francisco, un libro antiguo de relatos sobre él, escrito mucho después de su vida. Tomás de Celano, que escribió una vida de Francisco más temprana, también la cuenta.","ask":"Si pudieras darle las gracias a un animal, ¿a cuál sería y por qué?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"chapter 16 (the sermon to the birds)","work":"The Little Flowers of St. Francis (Fioretti)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"First Life, Book 1, chapter 21","work":"Thomas of Celano, First Life of St. Francis","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"therese-little-way","game":"truth","en":{"title":"St. Thérèse and the little way","word":"the little way","means":"doing small, everyday things with great love","story":[{"head":"a girl who wanted to be great","text":"In a town in France called Lisieux lived a young woman named Thérèse. She became a nun in a quiet convent, a house where sisters live and pray together. Thérèse wanted to do something great for God, like the heroes she read about."},{"head":"too small for big things","text":"But Thérèse felt very small. She could not sail across the sea or do mighty deeds. Then she had an idea. Maybe God did not need big things from her. Maybe God loved little things, done with lots of love."},{"head":"a smile for a hard-to-like sister","text":"In the convent there was one sister Thérèse found hard to like. So Thérèse tried something. Each time she met her, she gave her a warm smile and did kind little things for her. One day the sister asked Thérèse why she seemed to like her so much!"},{"head":"flowers scattered with love","text":"Thérèse called this her little way. She said she would scatter flowers for Jesus: every smile, every kind word, every small job done well. Catholics remember her as St. Thérèse, the Little Flower, and people still try her little way today."}],"game":{"items":[["Thérèse wanted to do something great for God.",true,"yes: she wanted to be great, and then she found her little way."],["Thérèse sailed across the sea to do mighty deeds.",false,"no: she felt too small for big deeds and stayed in her convent."],["Thérèse smiled at a sister she found hard to like.",true,"yes: and the sister asked why Thérèse seemed to like her so much."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like smelling a small flower. breathe out gently, like scattering petals along a path. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can do one small thing with great love","grownups":{"source":"Thérèse tells this in her own words in Story of a Soul, the book of her memories that her sisters published after her death. The sister she smiled at and the scattered flowers both come from it; we have paraphrased.","ask":"What is one small thing you could do with great love tomorrow?"}},"es":{"title":"Santa Teresita y el caminito","word":"el caminito","means":"hacer cosas pequeñas de cada día con mucho amor","story":[{"head":"una joven que quería ser grande","text":"En un pueblo de Francia llamado Lisieux vivía una joven llamada Teresa. Se hizo monja en un convento tranquilo, una casa donde las hermanas viven y rezan juntas. Teresa quería hacer algo grande por Dios, como los héroes de los libros que leía."},{"head":"demasiado pequeña para grandes cosas","text":"Pero Teresa se sentía muy pequeña. No podía cruzar el mar en barco ni hacer grandes hazañas. Entonces tuvo una idea. Tal vez Dios no necesitaba cosas grandes de ella. Tal vez a Dios le encantaban las cosas pequeñas, hechas con mucho amor."},{"head":"una sonrisa para una hermana difícil","text":"En el convento había una hermana a la que a Teresa le costaba querer. Así que Teresa probó algo. Cada vez que la veía, le regalaba una sonrisa cálida y hacía pequeños favores por ella. ¡Un día la hermana le preguntó por qué parecía quererla tanto!"},{"head":"flores esparcidas con amor","text":"Teresa llamó a esto su caminito. Dijo que esparciría flores para Jesús: cada sonrisa, cada palabra amable, cada pequeña tarea bien hecha. Los católicos la recuerdan como Santa Teresita, la Florecilla, y la gente todavía sigue su caminito hoy."}],"game":{"items":[["Teresa quería hacer algo grande por Dios.",true,"sí: quería ser grande, y entonces encontró su caminito."],["Teresa cruzó el mar en barco para hacer grandes hazañas.",false,"no: se sentía demasiado pequeña para grandes hazañas y se quedó en su convento."],["Teresa le sonreía a una hermana a la que le costaba querer.",true,"sí: y la hermana le preguntó por qué parecía quererla tanto."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si olieras una florecita. suelta el aire con suavidad, como si esparcieras pétalos en un camino. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo hacer una cosa pequeña con mucho amor","grownups":{"source":"Teresa lo cuenta con sus propias palabras en Historia de un alma, el libro de sus recuerdos que sus hermanas publicaron después de su muerte. La hermana a la que sonreía y las flores esparcidas vienen de ahí; lo contamos con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Qué cosa pequeña podrías hacer mañana con mucho amor?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Manuscript B (scattering flowers) and Manuscript C (the sister she found hard to like; the little way)","work":"St. Thérèse of Lisieux, Story of a Soul","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"annunciation","game":"match","en":{"title":"the angel visits Mary","word":"Annunciation","means":"the day the angel Gabriel told Mary she would have baby Jesus","story":[{"head":"a young woman in Nazareth","text":"In a small town called Nazareth lived a young woman named Mary. She was promised in marriage to a man named Joseph, from the family of King David. Mary's days were quiet and ordinary, until one special day."},{"head":"the angel's greeting","text":"God sent the angel Gabriel to Mary. The angel greeted her with words Catholics still say today: “Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with thee: blessed art thou among women.” Mary wondered what such a greeting could mean."},{"head":"a baby named Jesus","text":"Gabriel told her not to be afraid, because God was pleased with her. She would have a baby boy, and she would name him Jesus. He would be called the Son of the Most High. And her cousin Elizabeth was going to have a baby too!"},{"head":"a yes from Mary","text":"Mary listened. Then she gave her answer: she was the Lord's servant, and she was ready for God's plan. The angel left her, and soon Mary hurried off to the hill country to visit Elizabeth. Catholics call this day the Annunciation, which means the announcing."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Gabriel","brought Mary good news"],["Mary","said yes to God's plan"],["Elizabeth","was going to have a baby too"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Mary listening to the angel. breathe out softly, like a quiet yes. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can say yes when someone asks me for help","grownups":{"source":"Luke 1:26-39 in the Bible. The angel's greeting is quoted from the Douay-Rheims translation (Luke 1:28); the rest is paraphrased. The greeting is also where the Hail Mary prayer begins.","ask":"How do you think Mary felt when the angel came to her?"}},"es":{"title":"el ángel visita a María","word":"Anunciación","means":"el día en que el ángel Gabriel le dijo a María que tendría a Jesús","story":[{"head":"una joven en Nazaret","text":"En un pueblito llamado Nazaret vivía una joven llamada María. Estaba comprometida para casarse con un hombre llamado José, de la familia del rey David. Los días de María eran tranquilos y sencillos, hasta que llegó un día especial."},{"head":"el saludo del ángel","text":"Dios envió al ángel Gabriel a María. El ángel la saludó con palabras que los católicos todavía repiten hoy: le dijo que estaba llena de gracia, que el Señor estaba con ella y que era bendita entre las mujeres. María se preguntó qué querría decir ese saludo."},{"head":"un bebé llamado Jesús","text":"Gabriel le dijo que no tuviera miedo, porque Dios estaba contento con ella. Tendría un niño, y le pondría por nombre Jesús. Lo llamarían Hijo del Altísimo. ¡Y su prima Isabel también iba a tener un bebé!"},{"head":"el sí de María","text":"María escuchó. Luego dio su respuesta: ella era la servidora del Señor y estaba lista para el plan de Dios. El ángel se fue, y pronto María salió deprisa a la región de las montañas a visitar a Isabel. Los católicos llaman a este día la Anunciación, que quiere decir el anuncio."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Gabriel","le trajo a María buenas noticias"],["María","dijo que sí al plan de Dios"],["Isabel","también iba a tener un bebé"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como María escuchando al ángel. suelta el aire con suavidad, como un sí muy bajito. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo decir que sí cuando alguien me pide ayuda","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 1:26-39 en la Biblia, contado con nuestras palabras. El saludo del ángel (Lucas 1:28) es también el comienzo del Ave María.","ask":"¿Cómo crees que se sintió María cuando llegó el ángel?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 1:28","work":"The Gospel of Luke","translation":"Douay-Rheims (Challoner)","quoted":"Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with thee: blessed art thou among women."},{"ref":"Luke 1:26-39","work":"The Gospel of Luke","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":4,"key":"francis-wolf-gubbio","game":"truth","en":{"title":"St. Francis and the wolf of Gubbio","word":"Brother Wolf","means":"what Francis called the wolf, as if it were family","story":[{"head":"a hungry wolf near Gubbio","text":"The Little Flowers, an old book of stories about St. Francis, tells of a hill town in Italy called Gubbio. A big, hungry wolf lived nearby. It took the town's animals, and the people were so afraid that they hardly dared to go outside the town walls."},{"head":"a walk out to meet the wolf","text":"Francis felt sorry for the people, and for the wolf too. He walked out of the town to find it. When the wolf came running toward him, Francis made the sign of the cross and called out to it kindly, calling it Brother Wolf."},{"head":"a paw in his hand","text":"The wolf slowed down and lay at Francis's feet, gentle as a lamb. Francis promised that the people would feed it every day, so it would never be hungry again, if it promised to stop harming anyone. The wolf lifted its paw and put it in Francis's hand."},{"head":"a wolf with many friends","text":"After that, the wolf lived in Gubbio. It went from door to door, and the people gave it food. Nobody was afraid of it anymore, and even the dogs did not bark at it. The whole town had a new neighbor."}],"game":{"items":[["the wolf put its paw in Francis's hand.",true,"yes: that was its promise to live in peace."],["Francis built the wolf a little house with a red door.",false,"no: the wolf went from door to door, and the people fed it."],["the people of Gubbio fed the wolf.",true,"yes: they kept the promise Francis made for them."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Francis walking calmly toward the wolf. breathe out, like the wolf lying down in peace. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can make peace with someone I'm upset with","grownups":{"source":"This story is from the Little Flowers of St. Francis (chapter 21), an old book of stories about Francis gathered long after his life. No one can say how much happened just this way; Catholics treasure it as a picture of peace.","ask":"Who could you make peace with, and what would be your first step?"}},"es":{"title":"San Francisco y el lobo de Gubbio","word":"hermano lobo","means":"así llamaba Francisco al lobo, como si fuera de su familia","story":[{"head":"un lobo hambriento cerca de Gubbio","text":"Las Florecillas, un libro antiguo de relatos sobre San Francisco, cuentan de un pueblo de Italia, sobre una colina, llamado Gubbio. Cerca vivía un lobo grande y hambriento. Se llevaba los animales del pueblo, y la gente tenía tanto miedo que casi no se atrevía a salir de las murallas."},{"head":"al encuentro del lobo","text":"Francisco sintió compasión por la gente, y también por el lobo. Salió del pueblo a buscarlo. Cuando el lobo vino corriendo hacia él, Francisco hizo la señal de la cruz y le habló con cariño, llamándolo hermano lobo."},{"head":"una pata en su mano","text":"El lobo se detuvo y se echó a los pies de Francisco, manso como un cordero. Francisco le prometió que la gente le daría de comer cada día, para que nunca más tuviera hambre, si él prometía no hacerle daño a nadie. El lobo levantó la pata y la puso en la mano de Francisco."},{"head":"un lobo con muchos amigos","text":"Desde entonces, el lobo vivió en Gubbio. Iba de puerta en puerta, y la gente le daba comida. Ya nadie le tenía miedo, y ni siquiera los perros le ladraban. Todo el pueblo tenía un nuevo vecino."}],"game":{"items":[["el lobo puso la pata en la mano de Francisco.",true,"sí: esa fue su promesa de vivir en paz."],["Francisco le construyó al lobo una casita con puerta roja.",false,"no: el lobo iba de puerta en puerta, y la gente le daba de comer."],["la gente de Gubbio le daba de comer al lobo.",true,"sí: cumplieron la promesa que Francisco hizo por ellos."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como Francisco caminando tranquilo hacia el lobo. suelta el aire, como el lobo echándose en paz. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo hacer las paces con alguien con quien estoy enojado","grownups":{"source":"Esta historia viene de las Florecillas de San Francisco (capítulo 21), un libro antiguo de relatos reunidos mucho después de su vida. Nadie sabe cuánto pasó exactamente así; los católicos la aprecian como una imagen de la paz.","ask":"¿Con quién podrías hacer las paces, y cuál sería tu primer paso?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"chapter 21 (the wolf of Gubbio)","work":"The Little Flowers of St. Francis (Fioretti)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"christmas-bethlehem","game":"match","en":{"title":"Jesus is born in Bethlehem","word":"manger","means":"a box that holds hay for animals to eat","story":[{"head":"a trip to Bethlehem","text":"Caesar Augustus, the ruler of Rome, wanted all the people in his lands to be counted. So Joseph traveled with Mary from Nazareth to Bethlehem, the town of King David's family. Mary was going to have her baby very soon."},{"head":"no room at the inn","text":"While they were in Bethlehem, Mary's baby was born. She wrapped him snugly in cloths and laid him in a manger, the box where animals eat their hay, because there was no room for them in the inn."},{"head":"angels over the shepherds","text":"Out in the fields that night, shepherds were watching their sheep. An angel came to them, and God's light shone all around. The angel told them not to be afraid: there was good news of great joy. Then many angels praised God: “Glory to God in the highest; and on earth peace to men of good will.”"},{"head":"shepherds find the baby","text":"The shepherds hurried to Bethlehem. They found Mary and Joseph, and the baby lying in the manger, just as the angel had said. They went home praising God, and Mary kept all these things in her heart. Christians remember this night at Christmas."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the shepherds","hurried to find the baby"],["the angels","sang glory to God"],["Mary","laid her baby in a manger"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a quiet night in Bethlehem. breathe out gently, like Mary keeping it all in her heart. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can share good news with someone today","grownups":{"source":"Luke 2:1-20 in the Bible. The angels' song (Luke 2:14) is quoted from the Douay-Rheims translation; the rest is paraphrased. Luke mentions the manger and the inn, but not the ox and donkey that nativity scenes often show.","ask":"If you had been a shepherd that night, what would you have brought for the baby?"}},"es":{"title":"Jesús nace en Belén","word":"pesebre","means":"un cajón donde se pone el pasto para que coman los animales","story":[{"head":"un viaje a Belén","text":"César Augusto, el gobernante de Roma, quiso que contaran a toda la gente de sus tierras. Por eso José viajó con María desde Nazaret hasta Belén, el pueblo de la familia del rey David. A María le faltaba muy poco para tener a su bebé."},{"head":"no había lugar en la posada","text":"Mientras estaban en Belén, nació el bebé de María. Ella lo envolvió bien abrigado en pañales y lo acostó en un pesebre, el cajón donde comen los animales, porque no había lugar para ellos en la posada."},{"head":"ángeles sobre los pastores","text":"Esa noche, en el campo, unos pastores cuidaban sus ovejas. Un ángel se les apareció, y la luz de Dios brilló a su alrededor. El ángel les dijo que no tuvieran miedo: traía una buena noticia de gran alegría. Luego muchos ángeles alabaron a Dios, cantando gloria a Dios en las alturas y paz en la tierra."},{"head":"los pastores encuentran al bebé","text":"Los pastores fueron deprisa a Belén. Encontraron a María y a José, y al bebé acostado en el pesebre, tal como el ángel había dicho. Volvieron a casa alabando a Dios, y María guardaba todas estas cosas en su corazón. Los cristianos recuerdan esta noche en Navidad."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["los pastores","fueron deprisa a ver al bebé"],["los ángeles","cantaron gloria a Dios"],["María","acostó a su bebé en un pesebre"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una noche tranquila en Belén. suelta el aire con suavidad, como María guardando todo en su corazón. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo compartir una buena noticia con alguien","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 2:1-20 en la Biblia, contado con nuestras palabras. Lucas menciona el pesebre y la posada, pero no el buey y el burro que suelen aparecer en los nacimientos.","ask":"Si hubieras sido pastor esa noche, ¿qué le habrías llevado al bebé?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 2:14","work":"The Gospel of Luke","translation":"Douay-Rheims (Challoner)","quoted":"Glory to God in the highest; and on earth peace to men of good will."},{"ref":"Luke 2:1-20","work":"The Gospel of Luke","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"rosary-beads","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the beads of the Rosary","word":"Rosary","means":"a string of prayer beads, and the prayers Catholics say on them","story":[{"head":"a circle of beads","text":"Picture a string of beads in a grandmother's hand, with a small cross hanging from it. It is called a rosary. Catholics hold one bead at a time and say a prayer on each one, so the beads keep count while their minds stay on the prayer."},{"head":"the Our Father and the Hail Mary","text":"On a single bead they say the Our Father, the prayer Jesus taught his friends. On each bead in a group of ten they say the Hail Mary. It is built on the angel's greeting to Mary: “Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with thee.”"},{"head":"stories to think about","text":"While their fingers move along the beads, people think about stories from the lives of Jesus and Mary. These stories are called the mysteries. Some are joyful, like the angel's visit to Mary and baby Jesus born in Bethlehem."},{"head":"from Bethlehem to Easter morning","text":"Some are mysteries of light, from Jesus's teaching and miracles. Some are sorrowful, about the day he died. Some are glorious, like Easter morning. Praying the rosary is a little like turning slowly through a picture book of their lives, one bead at a time."}],"game":{"items":[["a rosary has a small cross hanging from it.",true,"yes: a string of beads with a small cross."],["people say the Our Father on every single bead.",false,"no: the Our Father on a single bead, and the Hail Mary on each bead in a group of ten."],["the mysteries are stories from the lives of Jesus and Mary.",true,"yes: joyful ones, ones of light, sorrowful ones and glorious ones."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a finger resting on one bead. breathe out, like sliding on to the next. four slow breaths, one bead at a time.","carry":"I can say thank you for one happy moment today","grownups":{"source":"The rosary's prayers and its four groups of mysteries are described in Pope John Paul II's letter Rosarium Virginis Mariae, which added the mysteries of light. The Hail Mary's opening is from Luke 1:28, quoted from the Douay-Rheims.","ask":"Which story from the life of Jesus or Mary would you most like to picture slowly?"}},"es":{"title":"las cuentas del Rosario","word":"Rosario","means":"un hilo de cuentas para rezar, y las oraciones que se rezan con él","story":[{"head":"un círculo de cuentas","text":"Imagina un hilo de cuentas en la mano de una abuela, con una crucecita colgando. Se llama rosario. Los católicos toman una cuenta a la vez y rezan una oración en cada una, así las cuentas llevan la cuenta mientras la mente sigue en la oración."},{"head":"el Padre Nuestro y el Ave María","text":"En una cuenta sola rezan el Padre Nuestro, la oración que Jesús enseñó a sus amigos. En cada cuenta de un grupo de diez rezan el Ave María, que nace del saludo del ángel a María: llena de gracia, el Señor está contigo."},{"head":"historias para pensar","text":"Mientras los dedos avanzan por las cuentas, la gente piensa en historias de la vida de Jesús y de María. Estas historias se llaman misterios. Algunos son gozosos, como la visita del ángel a María y el nacimiento del niño Jesús en Belén."},{"head":"de Belén a la mañana de Pascua","text":"Otros son misterios de luz, de las enseñanzas y milagros de Jesús. Otros son dolorosos, sobre el día en que murió. Otros son gloriosos, como la mañana de Pascua. Rezar el rosario es un poco como hojear despacio un libro de dibujos de sus vidas, una cuenta a la vez."}],"game":{"items":[["el rosario tiene una crucecita colgando.",true,"sí: un hilo de cuentas con una crucecita."],["en cada una de las cuentas se reza el Padre Nuestro.",false,"no: el Padre Nuestro en una cuenta sola, y el Ave María en cada cuenta de un grupo de diez."],["los misterios son historias de la vida de Jesús y de María.",true,"sí: gozosos, de luz, dolorosos y gloriosos."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un dedo que descansa en una cuenta. suelta el aire, como si pasaras a la siguiente. cuatro respiraciones lentas, una cuenta a la vez.","carry":"hoy puedo dar gracias por un momento feliz","grownups":{"source":"Las oraciones del rosario y sus cuatro grupos de misterios se describen en la carta Rosarium Virginis Mariae del papa Juan Pablo II, que añadió los misterios de luz. El Ave María comienza con el saludo del ángel en Lucas 1:28.","ask":"¿Qué historia de la vida de Jesús o de María te gustaría imaginar despacio?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 1:28","work":"The Gospel of Luke","translation":"Douay-Rheims (Challoner)","quoted":"Hail, full of grace, the Lord is with thee"},{"ref":"sections 19-38 (the mysteries) and 33-36 (the Our Father, the Hail Mary, the ten beads)","work":"Pope John Paul II, Rosarium Virginis Mariae (2002)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":7,"key":"greccio-crib","game":"match","en":{"title":"St. Francis and the Christmas crib","word":"Greccio","means":"the small Italian town where Francis set up a Christmas manger","story":[{"head":"an idea for Christmas","text":"Thomas of Celano, who wrote about St. Francis's life, tells this story. One Christmas, Francis wanted people to see with their own eyes how poor and small baby Jesus was, lying in a manger in Bethlehem. So he asked his friend John, from the town of Greccio, to help him."},{"head":"hay, an ox and a donkey","text":"John got everything ready. In a place near Greccio they set out a manger filled with hay, and they led in a real ox and a real donkey. It looked like a little piece of Bethlehem, right there in Italy."},{"head":"candles in the night","text":"On Christmas Eve, people came from all around carrying candles and torches. The night was as bright as day. The friars sang, the woods rang with their songs, and the people were full of joy."},{"head":"a story beside the manger","text":"Mass was celebrated over the manger. Francis stood beside it and told the people about the baby of Bethlehem, in a voice full of tenderness. Today many families set up a Christmas crib at home, and some remember Francis when they do."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Francis","wanted people to see Bethlehem"],["John of Greccio","got the manger ready"],["the people","came with candles and torches"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a candle flame standing still. breathe out so gently the flame would barely flicker. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can help get something ready for my family","grownups":{"source":"Thomas of Celano tells this in his First Life of St. Francis, written soon after Francis died; St. Bonaventure tells it again in his Major Life. Many people trace the custom of the Christmas crib back to Greccio.","ask":"If you made a Christmas scene, what would you put in it?"}},"es":{"title":"San Francisco y el nacimiento de Greccio","word":"Greccio","means":"el pueblito de Italia donde Francisco preparó un pesebre de Navidad","story":[{"head":"una idea para Navidad","text":"Tomás de Celano, que escribió sobre la vida de San Francisco, cuenta esta historia. Una Navidad, Francisco quiso que la gente viera con sus propios ojos qué pobre y pequeño era el niño Jesús, acostado en un pesebre en Belén. Así que le pidió ayuda a su amigo Juan, del pueblo de Greccio."},{"head":"paja, un buey y un burro","text":"Juan preparó todo. En un lugar cerca de Greccio pusieron un pesebre lleno de paja, y trajeron un buey de verdad y un burro de verdad. Parecía un pedacito de Belén, allí mismo en Italia."},{"head":"velas en la noche","text":"En Nochebuena, llegó gente de todas partes con velas y antorchas. La noche quedó tan clara como el día. Los frailes cantaban, el bosque resonaba con sus cantos, y la gente estaba llena de alegría."},{"head":"una historia junto al pesebre","text":"Se celebró la misa sobre el pesebre. Francisco, de pie a su lado, le habló a la gente del niño de Belén con una voz llena de ternura. Hoy muchas familias ponen un nacimiento en casa, y algunas se acuerdan de Francisco al hacerlo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Francisco","quiso que vieran Belén"],["Juan de Greccio","preparó el pesebre"],["la gente","llegó con velas y antorchas"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la llama quieta de una vela. suelta el aire tan suave que la llama apenas se mueva. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ayudar a preparar algo para mi familia","grownups":{"source":"Tomás de Celano lo cuenta en su Vida primera de San Francisco, escrita poco después de su muerte; San Buenaventura lo vuelve a contar en su Leyenda mayor. Mucha gente relaciona la costumbre del nacimiento con Greccio.","ask":"Si armaras un nacimiento, ¿qué pondrías en él?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"First Life, Book 1, chapter 30","work":"Thomas of Celano, First Life of St. Francis","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Major Life, chapter 10","work":"St. Bonaventure, Major Life of St. Francis","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"nicholas-gold","game":"truth","en":{"title":"St. Nicholas and the three bags of gold","word":"St. Nicholas","means":"a kind bishop who, the old stories say, gave gifts in secret","story":[{"head":"a young man named Nicholas","text":"Long ago, in a land by the sea that is now Turkey, lived a young man named Nicholas. The old stories say his parents left him money, and he wanted to use it to help people. Later he became a bishop, a leader who cares for a church family."},{"head":"a father with no money","text":"Nearby lived a father with three daughters. They were so poor that the girls could not get married, because in those days a bride's family needed money to start the new home. The father did not know what to do."},{"head":"gold through the window","text":"One night, Nicholas wrapped some gold in a cloth and tossed it through the family's window. Then he hurried away. Later he did it again, and the next daughter could marry too. Nobody knew who the secret helper was."},{"head":"please don't tell","text":"The third time, the father ran after him and saw that it was Nicholas. He thanked him again and again. Nicholas asked him to keep it a secret. Today, on St. Nicholas's feast day, children in some countries find small surprises in their shoes."}],"game":{"items":[["Nicholas tossed gold through the family's window.",true,"yes: at night, and then he hurried away."],["Nicholas left a note signed with his name.",false,"no: he wanted his gifts to stay a secret."],["the father ran after Nicholas the third time.",true,"yes: and Nicholas asked him to keep it a secret."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Nicholas tiptoeing up to the window. breathe out quietly, so no one hears. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can do something kind without telling anyone","grownups":{"source":"Little is known for certain about Nicholas, a bishop of Myra in today's Turkey. This story is told in early lives of Nicholas and in The Golden Legend, a medieval book of saints' stories. Tellings differ in small details.","ask":"Why do you think Nicholas wanted to keep his gifts a secret?"}},"es":{"title":"San Nicolás y las tres bolsas de oro","word":"San Nicolás","means":"un obispo bondadoso que, según las historias, daba regalos en secreto","story":[{"head":"un joven llamado Nicolás","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en una tierra junto al mar que hoy es Turquía, vivía un joven llamado Nicolás. Las historias antiguas cuentan que sus padres le dejaron dinero, y él quería usarlo para ayudar a otros. Más tarde fue obispo, un pastor que cuida a una familia de la Iglesia."},{"head":"un padre sin dinero","text":"Cerca vivía un padre con tres hijas. Eran tan pobres que las muchachas no podían casarse, porque en aquel tiempo la familia de la novia necesitaba dinero para empezar el nuevo hogar. El padre no sabía qué hacer."},{"head":"oro por la ventana","text":"Una noche, Nicolás envolvió un poco de oro en un paño y lo lanzó por la ventana de la familia. Luego se fue deprisa. Más tarde lo hizo otra vez, y la siguiente hija también pudo casarse. Nadie sabía quién era el ayudante secreto."},{"head":"por favor, no lo cuentes","text":"La tercera vez, el padre corrió tras él y vio que era Nicolás. Le dio las gracias una y otra vez. Nicolás le pidió que lo guardara en secreto. Hoy, en la fiesta de San Nicolás, los niños de algunos países encuentran pequeñas sorpresas en sus zapatos."}],"game":{"items":[["Nicolás lanzó oro por la ventana de la familia.",true,"sí: de noche, y luego se fue deprisa."],["Nicolás dejó una nota firmada con su nombre.",false,"no: quería que sus regalos fueran un secreto."],["la tercera vez, el padre corrió tras Nicolás.",true,"sí: y Nicolás le pidió que lo guardara en secreto."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como Nicolás acercándose de puntitas a la ventana. suelta el aire en silencio, para que nadie te oiga. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo hacer algo bueno sin contárselo a nadie","grownups":{"source":"Se sabe poco con certeza sobre Nicolás, obispo de Mira, en la actual Turquía. Esta historia aparece en vidas antiguas de Nicolás y en la Leyenda dorada, un libro medieval de vidas de santos. Los relatos cambian en pequeños detalles.","ask":"¿Por qué crees que Nicolás quería que sus regalos fueran un secreto?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"St. Nicholas (December 6)","work":"Jacobus de Voragine, The Golden Legend","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the dowry story","work":"Michael the Archimandrite, Life of St. Nicholas","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":9,"key":"martin-cloak","game":"match","en":{"title":"St. Martin shares his cloak","word":"cloak","means":"a big warm coat with no sleeves, worn over the shoulders","story":[{"head":"a cold day at the city gate","text":"Martin was a young soldier in the Roman army. One winter, when it was bitterly cold, he came to the gate of a city called Amiens. There sat a poor man with hardly any clothes, shivering, and people kept passing him by."},{"head":"a cloak cut in two","text":"Martin had already given away his other things. All he had left was his soldier's cloak. So he took his sword and cut the cloak in two. He gave one half to the poor man and wrapped himself in the other half."},{"head":"some laughed, some wondered","text":"Some people nearby laughed, because Martin looked funny in half a cloak. But others felt sorry that they had not helped the man themselves, when they had more to give."},{"head":"a dream of Jesus in the half cloak","text":"That night Martin had a dream. He saw Jesus wearing the half cloak he had given away. Jesus told the angels around him that Martin had dressed him in it. Later, Martin left the army and became a bishop, and Catholics remember him as St. Martin of Tours."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Martin","cut his cloak in two"],["the poor man","shivered at the city gate"],["the dream","showed Jesus in the half cloak"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like wrapping a warm cloak around you. breathe out, like sharing half with someone who is cold. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can share something warm with someone who needs it","grownups":{"source":"Sulpicius Severus, who knew Martin, tells this in his Life of St. Martin (chapter 3). The dream echoes Matthew 25:40, where Jesus says that what is done for the least of his brothers and sisters is done for him.","ask":"Who might be feeling cold or left out, and how could we share with them?"}},"es":{"title":"San Martín comparte su capa","word":"capa","means":"un abrigo grande sin mangas que se lleva sobre los hombros","story":[{"head":"un día frío en la puerta de la ciudad","text":"Martín era un joven soldado del ejército romano. Un invierno, cuando hacía un frío terrible, llegó a la puerta de una ciudad llamada Amiens. Allí estaba sentado un hombre pobre casi sin ropa, temblando, y la gente pasaba de largo."},{"head":"una capa cortada en dos","text":"Martín ya había regalado sus otras cosas. Solo le quedaba su capa de soldado. Así que tomó su espada y cortó la capa en dos. Le dio una mitad al hombre pobre y se envolvió en la otra mitad."},{"head":"unos se rieron, otros pensaron","text":"Algunas personas que estaban cerca se rieron, porque Martín se veía chistoso con media capa. Pero otras sintieron pena por no haber ayudado ellas mismas al hombre, cuando tenían más para dar."},{"head":"un sueño: Jesús con la media capa","text":"Esa noche Martín tuvo un sueño. Vio a Jesús vestido con la media capa que él había regalado. Jesús les dijo a los ángeles que lo rodeaban que Martín lo había vestido con ella. Más tarde, Martín dejó el ejército y fue obispo, y los católicos lo recuerdan como San Martín de Tours."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Martín","cortó su capa en dos"],["el hombre pobre","temblaba en la puerta"],["el sueño","mostró a Jesús con la media capa"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si te envolvieras en una capa calientita. suelta el aire, como si compartieras la mitad con alguien que tiene frío. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo compartir algo calientito con alguien que lo necesita","grownups":{"source":"Sulpicio Severo, que conoció a Martín, lo cuenta en su Vida de San Martín (capítulo 3). El sueño recuerda Mateo 25:40, donde Jesús dice que lo que se hace por el más pequeño de sus hermanos se hace por él.","ask":"¿Quién podría sentirse con frío o dejado de lado, y cómo podríamos compartir con esa persona?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"chapter 3","work":"Sulpicius Severus, Life of St. Martin","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Matthew 25:40","work":"The Gospel of Matthew","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"guadalupe-roses","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Juan Diego and the roses of Guadalupe","word":"tilma","means":"a simple cloak woven from plant fibers, like Juan Diego's","story":[{"head":"singing on Tepeyac hill","text":"An old account from Mexico tells of a man named Juan Diego. Early one December morning, he was walking past a hill called Tepeyac when he heard birds singing more beautifully than ever. At the top of the hill stood a lady, her clothes shining like the sun."},{"head":"a message for the bishop","text":"The lady spoke to him kindly in his own language, Nahuatl. She told him she was Mary, the mother of God. She asked him to tell the bishop to build a church there. Juan Diego went, but the bishop wanted a sign."},{"head":"roses on a cold hilltop","text":"Then Juan Diego's uncle fell sick, and Juan Diego was worried. The lady told him not to worry, because his uncle would get well. She sent him to the hilltop, where roses were blooming in the cold. He gathered them in his tilma."},{"head":"a picture on the cloak","text":"At the bishop's house, Juan Diego opened his tilma. The roses tumbled to the floor, and there on the cloth was a picture of the lady. The bishop believed, and a church was built. Catholics honor Our Lady of Guadalupe every December."}],"game":{"items":[["Juan Diego heard birds singing on the hill.",true,"yes: beautiful singing, and then he saw the lady."],["the lady gave Juan Diego a golden key for the bishop.",false,"no: she sent him roses, and a picture appeared on his tilma."],["roses were blooming on the cold hilltop.",true,"yes: and Juan Diego gathered them in his tilma."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like smelling roses on a cold hilltop. breathe out, like opening a cloak full of flowers. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can be kind to someone who is worried","grownups":{"source":"This comes from the Nican Mopohua, an account written in Nahuatl, the language of Juan Diego's people. Catholics honor Our Lady of Guadalupe each December, and Juan Diego is a saint. Historians still discuss how and when the account took shape.","ask":"When you feel worried, like Juan Diego did, who helps you feel better?"}},"es":{"title":"Juan Diego y las rosas de Guadalupe","word":"tilma","means":"una capa sencilla tejida con fibras de planta, como la de Juan Diego","story":[{"head":"cantos en el cerro del Tepeyac","text":"Un relato antiguo de México cuenta de un hombre llamado Juan Diego. Una mañana temprano de diciembre, pasaba junto a un cerro llamado Tepeyac cuando oyó pájaros que cantaban más bonito que nunca. En lo alto del cerro estaba una señora, con la ropa brillante como el sol."},{"head":"un mensaje para el obispo","text":"La señora le habló con cariño en su propia lengua, el náhuatl. Le dijo que era María, la madre de Dios. Le pidió que le dijera al obispo que construyera allí una iglesia. Juan Diego fue, pero el obispo quería una señal."},{"head":"rosas en un cerro frío","text":"Entonces el tío de Juan Diego se enfermó, y Juan Diego estaba preocupado. La señora le dijo que no se preocupara, porque su tío se iba a sanar. Lo mandó a lo alto del cerro, donde había rosas floreciendo en el frío. Él las juntó en su tilma."},{"head":"una imagen en la tilma","text":"En la casa del obispo, Juan Diego abrió su tilma. Las rosas cayeron al suelo, y allí, en la tela, estaba la imagen de la señora. El obispo creyó, y se construyó una iglesia. Los católicos honran a la Virgen de Guadalupe cada diciembre."}],"game":{"items":[["Juan Diego oyó pájaros cantando en el cerro.",true,"sí: un canto precioso, y luego vio a la señora."],["la señora le dio a Juan Diego una llave de oro para el obispo.",false,"no: lo mandó por rosas, y apareció una imagen en su tilma."],["había rosas floreciendo en lo alto del cerro frío.",true,"sí: y Juan Diego las juntó en su tilma."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si olieras rosas en un cerro frío. suelta el aire, como si abrieras una tilma llena de flores. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ser amable con alguien que está preocupado","grownups":{"source":"Viene del Nican Mopohua, un relato escrito en náhuatl, la lengua del pueblo de Juan Diego. Los católicos honran a la Virgen de Guadalupe cada diciembre, y Juan Diego es santo. Los historiadores aún estudian cómo y cuándo se formó el relato.","ask":"Cuando te sientes preocupado, como Juan Diego, ¿quién te ayuda a sentirte mejor?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the account of the apparitions at Tepeyac","work":"Nican Mopohua (in Luis Laso de la Vega, Huei tlamahuiçoltica)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"sign-of-the-cross","game":"match","en":{"title":"the Sign of the Cross","word":"Trinity","means":"one God: the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit","story":[{"head":"a hand that draws a cross","text":"Catholics often begin and end their prayers with a small movement called the Sign of the Cross. With the right hand they touch the forehead, then the chest, then the left shoulder and the right. Their hand draws a cross over themselves."},{"head":"three names, one God","text":"While they move their hand, they say the names of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Catholics believe in one God in three persons, called the Trinity. The words come from Jesus, who told his friends to baptize people in those three names."},{"head":"water at the church door","text":"At the door of many churches there is a small bowl of holy water. People dip their fingers in it and make the Sign of the Cross as they come in. It reminds them of their baptism, when water was poured over them."},{"head":"morning, meals and bedtime","text":"Some families make the Sign of the Cross when they wake up, before meals and at bedtime. Some parents trace a little cross on a child's forehead as a blessing. It is a prayer made with the hand, in just a moment."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the forehead","where the hand begins"],["holy water","a bowl by the church door"],["the Trinity","Father, Son and Holy Spirit"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a hand rising up to the forehead. breathe out, like it coming gently down. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can bless someone with a kind word today","grownups":{"source":"The Sign of the Cross is an old Christian practice. Its words come from Matthew 28:19, where Jesus speaks of baptizing in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Eastern Catholics and Orthodox Christians cross from the right shoulder to the left.","ask":"When in your day would you like to stop for a quiet moment?"}},"es":{"title":"la señal de la cruz","word":"Trinidad","means":"un solo Dios: el Padre, el Hijo y el Espíritu Santo","story":[{"head":"una mano que dibuja una cruz","text":"Los católicos suelen empezar y terminar sus oraciones con un pequeño gesto llamado la señal de la cruz. Con la mano derecha se tocan la frente, luego el pecho, luego el hombro izquierdo y el derecho. Su mano dibuja una cruz sobre sí mismos."},{"head":"tres nombres, un solo Dios","text":"Mientras mueven la mano, dicen los nombres del Padre, del Hijo y del Espíritu Santo. Los católicos creen en un solo Dios en tres personas, llamado la Trinidad. Las palabras vienen de Jesús, que pidió a sus amigos bautizar a la gente en esos tres nombres."},{"head":"agua en la puerta de la iglesia","text":"En la entrada de muchas iglesias hay una pequeña pila de agua bendita. La gente moja los dedos en ella y hace la señal de la cruz al entrar. Les recuerda su bautismo, cuando les echaron agua."},{"head":"mañana, comidas y hora de dormir","text":"Algunas familias hacen la señal de la cruz al despertar, antes de comer y a la hora de dormir. Algunos papás trazan una crucecita en la frente de su hijo como bendición. Es una oración hecha con la mano, en un momentito."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la frente","donde empieza la mano"],["el agua bendita","una pila junto a la puerta"],["la Trinidad","Padre, Hijo y Espíritu Santo"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una mano que sube hasta la frente. suelta el aire, como la mano que baja con suavidad. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo bendecir a alguien con una palabra amable","grownups":{"source":"La señal de la cruz es una práctica cristiana antigua. Sus palabras vienen de Mateo 28:19, donde Jesús habla de bautizar en el nombre del Padre, del Hijo y del Espíritu Santo. Los católicos orientales y los cristianos ortodoxos se persignan del hombro derecho al izquierdo.","ask":"¿En qué momento de tu día te gustaría parar un ratito en silencio?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Matthew 28:19","work":"The Gospel of Matthew","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"paragraphs 232-237 and 2157","work":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"patrick-shamrock","game":"truth","en":{"title":"St. Patrick and the shamrock","word":"shamrock","means":"a small clover plant with three leaves on one stem","story":[{"head":"a young shepherd far from home","text":"Long ago, Patrick grew up in Britain. As a young man he was taken away to Ireland, where he looked after sheep on the hills. Out there with the sheep, he prayed many times a day. After a long time, he found his way home."},{"head":"a dream calls him back","text":"Later, Patrick had a dream in which the people of Ireland called to him, asking him to come and walk among them again. So he did. He went back to Ireland to tell people about Jesus, and he stayed among them."},{"head":"a little green leaf","text":"People tell a story about him. When someone wondered how God could be one and also three, Patrick bent down and picked a shamrock, a small clover growing in the grass."},{"head":"three leaves, one stem","text":"He showed them its three leaves, all growing on one little stem. In the same way, Christians speak of one God: the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. Today, on St. Patrick's Day, many people wear something green, or a shamrock."}],"game":{"items":[["Patrick looked after sheep in Ireland as a young man.",true,"yes: and he prayed many times a day on the hills."],["Patrick picked a red rose to explain his idea.",false,"no: he picked a shamrock, a small green clover."],["the shamrock had three leaves on one stem.",true,"yes: a picture of one God: Father, Son and Holy Spirit."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the wind over green Irish hills. breathe out, like sheep resting in the grass. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can explain something kindly when someone asks","grownups":{"source":"Patrick tells of his life in his own Confession, written in Latin. The shamrock is a story people tell: it was written down much later, and Patrick's own writings don't mention it.","ask":"What is something hard to explain that you could show with a picture or an object?"}},"es":{"title":"San Patricio y el trébol","word":"trébol","means":"una plantita con tres hojas en un solo tallo","story":[{"head":"un joven pastor lejos de casa","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, Patricio creció en Britania. Siendo joven, se lo llevaron a Irlanda, donde cuidaba ovejas en los cerros. Allá, con las ovejas, rezaba muchas veces al día. Después de mucho tiempo, encontró el camino de regreso a casa."},{"head":"un sueño lo llama de vuelta","text":"Más tarde, Patricio tuvo un sueño en el que la gente de Irlanda lo llamaba y le pedía que volviera a caminar entre ellos. Y así lo hizo. Volvió a Irlanda para hablarle a la gente de Jesús, y se quedó con ellos."},{"head":"una hojita verde","text":"La gente cuenta una historia sobre él. Cuando alguien se preguntó cómo Dios podía ser uno y a la vez tres, Patricio se agachó y arrancó un trébol, una plantita que crecía en el pasto."},{"head":"tres hojas, un tallo","text":"Les mostró sus tres hojas, todas creciendo en un solo tallito. De la misma manera, los cristianos hablan de un solo Dios: el Padre, el Hijo y el Espíritu Santo. Hoy, en el día de San Patricio, mucha gente se viste de verde o lleva un trébol."}],"game":{"items":[["Patricio cuidaba ovejas en Irlanda cuando era joven.",true,"sí: y rezaba muchas veces al día en los cerros."],["Patricio arrancó una rosa roja para explicar su idea.",false,"no: arrancó un trébol, una plantita verde."],["el trébol tenía tres hojas en un solo tallo.",true,"sí: una imagen de un solo Dios: Padre, Hijo y Espíritu Santo."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el viento sobre los cerros verdes de Irlanda. suelta el aire, como las ovejas que descansan en el pasto. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo explicar algo con amabilidad cuando alguien me pregunta","grownups":{"source":"Patricio cuenta su vida en su propia Confesión, escrita en latín. Lo del trébol es una historia que la gente cuenta: se escribió mucho después, y los escritos de Patricio no la mencionan.","ask":"¿Qué cosa difícil de explicar podrías mostrar con un dibujo o un objeto?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"sections 1, 16 and 23","work":"St. Patrick, Confession (Confessio)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"tobias-raphael","game":"match","en":{"title":"the angel who walked with Tobias","word":"guardian angel","means":"an angel Catholics believe God gives to watch over each person","story":[{"head":"a long journey ahead","text":"The Book of Tobias, in the Catholic Bible, tells of a young man named Tobias. His father, who could no longer see, sent him on a long journey to collect money the family was owed. But Tobias did not know the way."},{"head":"a friendly traveler and a dog","text":"Outside, Tobias met a friendly young man who knew the road well and offered to go along. So off they went together, and the family dog followed behind them. Nobody knew that the traveler was really the angel Raphael."},{"head":"a big fish in the river","text":"One evening, by the river Tigris, a big fish leaped up out of the water near Tobias's feet. The traveler told him to catch it and keep parts of it, because they could be used as medicine."},{"head":"a surprise at home","text":"When they came home, the dog ran ahead, wagging its tail. Tobias put the fish medicine on his father's eyes, and his father could see again! Then the traveler said he was Raphael, an angel sent by God, and he was gone from their sight."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Tobias","went on a long journey"],["Raphael","walked beside him as a friend"],["the dog","ran home wagging its tail"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like walking beside a good friend. breathe out, like a dog wagging its tail at home. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can walk beside a friend who needs company","grownups":{"source":"The Book of Tobias (called Tobit in newer Bibles), chapters 5-12, paraphrased. Catholics read it with Matthew 18:10, where Jesus says children's angels always see the face of his Father, as a picture of guardian angels.","ask":"Who walks beside you and helps you when you don't know the way?"}},"es":{"title":"el ángel que caminó con Tobías","word":"ángel de la guarda","means":"un ángel que, según los católicos, Dios da para cuidar a cada persona","story":[{"head":"un largo viaje por delante","text":"El libro de Tobías, en la Biblia católica, cuenta de un joven llamado Tobías. Su padre, que ya no podía ver, lo mandó a un largo viaje para cobrar un dinero que le debían a la familia. Pero Tobías no conocía el camino."},{"head":"un viajero amable y un perro","text":"Afuera, Tobías conoció a un joven amable que conocía bien el camino y se ofreció a acompañarlo. Así que se fueron juntos, y el perro de la familia los seguía. Nadie sabía que el viajero era en realidad el ángel Rafael."},{"head":"un pez grande en el río","text":"Una tarde, junto al río Tigris, un pez grande saltó del agua cerca de los pies de Tobías. El viajero le dijo que lo atrapara y guardara algunas partes, porque servían como medicina."},{"head":"una sorpresa en casa","text":"Cuando llegaron a casa, el perro corrió adelante, moviendo la cola. Tobías le puso la medicina del pez en los ojos a su padre, ¡y su padre volvió a ver! Entonces el viajero dijo que era Rafael, un ángel enviado por Dios, y desapareció de su vista."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Tobías","hizo un largo viaje"],["Rafael","caminó a su lado como amigo"],["el perro","llegó a casa moviendo la cola"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si caminaras junto a un buen amigo. suelta el aire, como un perro que mueve la cola al llegar a casa. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo acompañar a un amigo que necesita compañía","grownups":{"source":"El libro de Tobías, capítulos 5 al 12, contado con nuestras palabras. Los católicos lo leen junto con Mateo 18:10, donde Jesús dice que los ángeles de los niños siempre ven el rostro de su Padre, como imagen de los ángeles de la guarda.","ask":"¿Quién camina a tu lado y te ayuda cuando no sabes el camino?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Tobias 5-12 (Douay-Rheims numbering; the dog in 6:1 and 11:9)","work":"The Book of Tobias (Tobit)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Matthew 18:10","work":"The Gospel of Matthew","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"paragraph 336","work":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"jesus-in-the-temple","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the boy Jesus in the temple","word":"temple","means":"the great house of prayer in Jerusalem","story":[{"head":"a family trip to Jerusalem","text":"Every year Mary and Joseph traveled to Jerusalem for the feast of Passover. When Jesus was twelve, he went with them. Jesus grew up in Nazareth, in the home of Joseph, the carpenter."},{"head":"where is Jesus?","text":"When the feast was over, the family started home with a big group of relatives and friends. After a whole day of walking, Mary and Joseph looked for Jesus. He wasn't anywhere in the group! They hurried back to Jerusalem to look for him."},{"head":"found among the teachers","text":"After three days they found him in the temple. He was sitting with the teachers, listening to them and asking them questions. Everyone who heard him was amazed at how well he understood."},{"head":"home again to Nazareth","text":"Mary told him they had been worried and had searched everywhere. Jesus said they should have known he would be in his Father's house. Then he went home to Nazareth with them, did what they asked, and grew in wisdom."}],"game":{"items":[["Jesus was found in the temple, asking the teachers questions.",true,"yes: and everyone was amazed at how well he understood."],["Jesus was found in a busy market, selling bread.",false,"no: he was in the temple, listening and asking questions."],["Mary and Joseph hurried back to Jerusalem to look for him.",true,"yes: they searched until they found him in the temple."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a family walking home together. breathe out, like Mary and Joseph's relief when they found him. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can ask a good question today and really listen","grownups":{"source":"Luke 2:41-52 in the Bible, paraphrased; Matthew 13:55 calls Jesus the carpenter's son. Catholics remember this as the Finding in the Temple, one of the joyful mysteries of the rosary, and honor Jesus, Mary and Joseph as the Holy Family.","ask":"What question would you like to ask a wise teacher?"}},"es":{"title":"el niño Jesús en el templo","word":"templo","means":"la gran casa de oración en Jerusalén","story":[{"head":"un viaje en familia a Jerusalén","text":"Cada año, María y José viajaban a Jerusalén para la fiesta de la Pascua. Cuando Jesús tenía doce años, fue con ellos. Jesús creció en Nazaret, en la casa de José, el carpintero."},{"head":"¿dónde está Jesús?","text":"Cuando terminó la fiesta, la familia emprendió el regreso con un grupo grande de parientes y amigos. Después de un día entero de camino, María y José buscaron a Jesús. ¡No estaba en el grupo! Volvieron deprisa a Jerusalén a buscarlo."},{"head":"entre los maestros","text":"Después de tres días lo encontraron en el templo. Estaba sentado con los maestros, escuchándolos y haciéndoles preguntas. Todos los que lo oían estaban asombrados de lo bien que entendía."},{"head":"de vuelta a Nazaret","text":"María le dijo que habían estado preocupados y que lo habían buscado por todas partes. Jesús dijo que debían saber que él estaría en la casa de su Padre. Luego volvió a Nazaret con ellos, les obedecía y crecía en sabiduría."}],"game":{"items":[["encontraron a Jesús en el templo, haciéndoles preguntas a los maestros.",true,"sí: y todos estaban asombrados de lo bien que entendía."],["encontraron a Jesús en un mercado, vendiendo pan.",false,"no: estaba en el templo, escuchando y haciendo preguntas."],["María y José volvieron deprisa a Jerusalén a buscarlo.",true,"sí: lo buscaron hasta encontrarlo en el templo."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una familia que camina junta de regreso a casa. suelta el aire, como el alivio de María y José al encontrarlo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo hacer una buena pregunta y escuchar de verdad","grownups":{"source":"Lucas 2:41-52 en la Biblia, contado con nuestras palabras; Mateo 13:55 llama a Jesús el hijo del carpintero. Los católicos lo recuerdan como el Niño perdido y hallado en el templo, un misterio gozoso del rosario, y honran a la Sagrada Familia.","ask":"¿Qué pregunta te gustaría hacerle a un maestro sabio?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 2:41-52","work":"The Gospel of Luke","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Matthew 13:55","work":"The Gospel of Matthew","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":15,"key":"canticle-creatures","game":"match","en":{"title":"Brother Sun and Sister Moon","word":"canticle","means":"a song of praise","story":[{"head":"a song from a sick friar","text":"Near the end of his life, St. Francis was sick, and his eyes hurt so much that bright light bothered him. Even so, he made up a song of praise to God for all of creation, in the everyday Italian of his hometown."},{"head":"Brother Sun, Sister Moon","text":"In his song, Francis praised God for Brother Sun, bright and beautiful, who brings the day. He praised God for Sister Moon and the stars, which God set in the sky, clear and precious and lovely."},{"head":"wind, water and fire","text":"He praised God for Brother Wind and the air and every kind of weather. He praised God for Sister Water, so useful and humble and pure. And for Brother Fire, who lights up the night, bright and playful and strong."},{"head":"Sister, Mother Earth","text":"He praised God for our Sister, Mother Earth, who feeds us and grows fruits and bright flowers and green plants. Francis called everything in the world brother or sister, as if all creation were one big family."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Brother Sun","brings the day"],["Sister Water","useful, humble and pure"],["Mother Earth","grows fruits and flowers"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Brother Wind blowing through the trees. breathe out, like Sister Water flowing on. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can say thank you for the sun or the rain","grownups":{"source":"Francis's Canticle of the Creatures, also called the Canticle of Brother Sun, written in his Umbrian Italian near the end of his life; paraphrased here. The song also has verses about forgiveness and about Sister Death, which we left out for young listeners.","ask":"If you could call one thing in nature your brother or sister, what would it be?"}},"es":{"title":"Hermano Sol y Hermana Luna","word":"cántico","means":"un canto de alabanza","story":[{"head":"un canto de un fraile enfermo","text":"Cerca del final de su vida, San Francisco estaba enfermo, y le dolían tanto los ojos que la luz fuerte le molestaba. Aun así, compuso un canto de alabanza a Dios por toda la creación, en el italiano sencillo de su tierra."},{"head":"Hermano Sol, Hermana Luna","text":"En su canto, Francisco alabó a Dios por el Hermano Sol, brillante y hermoso, que trae el día. Alabó a Dios por la Hermana Luna y las estrellas, que Dios puso en el cielo, claras, preciosas y bellas."},{"head":"viento, agua y fuego","text":"Alabó a Dios por el Hermano Viento, por el aire y por todo tipo de clima. Alabó a Dios por la Hermana Agua, tan útil, humilde y pura. Y por el Hermano Fuego, que alumbra la noche, alegre, brillante y fuerte."},{"head":"la Hermana Madre Tierra","text":"Alabó a Dios por nuestra Hermana, la Madre Tierra, que nos alimenta y da frutos, flores de colores y plantas verdes. Francisco llamaba hermano o hermana a todo lo que hay en el mundo, como si toda la creación fuera una gran familia."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el Hermano Sol","trae el día"],["la Hermana Agua","útil, humilde y pura"],["la Madre Tierra","da frutos y flores"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el Hermano Viento que sopla entre los árboles. suelta el aire, como la Hermana Agua que sigue corriendo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo dar gracias por el sol o por la lluvia","grownups":{"source":"El Cántico de las criaturas de Francisco, también llamado Cántico del Hermano Sol, escrito en su italiano de Umbría cerca del final de su vida; aquí contado con nuestras palabras. El canto también tiene estrofas sobre el perdón y sobre la Hermana Muerte, que dejamos fuera para los más pequeños.","ask":"Si pudieras llamar hermano o hermana a algo de la naturaleza, ¿qué sería?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Canticle of the Creatures (Canticle of Brother Sun)","work":"St. Francis of Assisi","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Assisi Compilation 83 (the circumstances of its writing)","work":"The Assisi Compilation (Legend of Perugia)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"mass-sign-of-peace","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the Mass and the sign of peace","word":"Mass","means":"the Catholic church service of readings, prayer and Communion","story":[{"head":"the church family gathers","text":"On Sundays, Catholic families gather at church for Mass. People stand and sing as the priest comes in. Everyone begins together with the Sign of the Cross, like a big family starting a meal."},{"head":"stories from the Bible","text":"First come readings from the Bible. People listen to stories and letters from long ago, and then to words from the life of Jesus, called the Gospel. Then the priest talks about what they heard, to help everyone understand it."},{"head":"bread and wine at the altar","text":"Next the priest stands at the altar, a special table. He prays over bread and wine, using the words Jesus said at his Last Supper with his friends. Catholics believe that the bread and wine become Jesus himself."},{"head":"peace be with you","text":"Before Communion, everyone says the Our Father together. Then comes the sign of peace. People turn to those around them, shake hands, smile or hug, and wish each other peace. Jesus had told his friends that he was leaving them his peace."}],"game":{"items":[["people share the sign of peace with those around them.",true,"yes: with a handshake, a smile or a hug."],["everyone at Mass claps three times before the readings.",false,"no: people stand, sing and then listen to readings from the Bible."],["the priest prays over bread and wine at the altar.",true,"yes: using the words Jesus said at his Last Supper."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a church growing quiet. breathe out, like a warm handshake that says peace. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can wish someone peace today","grownups":{"source":"The order of the Mass follows the Roman Missal. The sign of peace recalls John 14:27, where Jesus tells his friends he leaves them his peace. Catholics of different rites and places celebrate in somewhat different ways.","ask":"How could you share peace with someone at home this week?"}},"es":{"title":"la misa y el saludo de la paz","word":"misa","means":"la celebración católica con lecturas, oración y comunión","story":[{"head":"la familia de la Iglesia se reúne","text":"Los domingos, las familias católicas se reúnen en la iglesia para la misa. La gente se pone de pie y canta mientras entra el sacerdote. Todos empiezan juntos con la señal de la cruz, como una gran familia que empieza una comida."},{"head":"historias de la Biblia","text":"Primero vienen las lecturas de la Biblia. La gente escucha historias y cartas de hace mucho tiempo, y luego palabras de la vida de Jesús, llamadas el Evangelio. Después el sacerdote habla de lo que escucharon, para ayudar a todos a entenderlo."},{"head":"pan y vino en el altar","text":"Luego el sacerdote se pone frente al altar, una mesa especial. Reza sobre el pan y el vino con las palabras que Jesús dijo en su Última Cena con sus amigos. Los católicos creen que el pan y el vino se convierten en Jesús mismo."},{"head":"la paz esté contigo","text":"Antes de la comunión, todos rezan juntos el Padre Nuestro. Luego viene el saludo de la paz. La gente se voltea hacia quienes tiene cerca, se dan la mano, sonríen o se abrazan, y se desean la paz. Jesús les había dicho a sus amigos que les dejaba su paz."}],"game":{"items":[["la gente comparte el saludo de la paz con quienes tiene cerca.",true,"sí: dándose la mano, con una sonrisa o un abrazo."],["en la misa todos aplauden tres veces antes de las lecturas.",false,"no: la gente se pone de pie, canta y luego escucha lecturas de la Biblia."],["el sacerdote reza sobre el pan y el vino en el altar.",true,"sí: con las palabras que Jesús dijo en su Última Cena."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una iglesia que se queda en silencio. suelta el aire, como un apretón de manos cálido que dice paz. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo desearle paz a alguien","grownups":{"source":"El orden de la misa sigue el Misal Romano. El saludo de la paz recuerda Juan 14:27, donde Jesús les dice a sus amigos que les deja su paz. Los católicos de distintos ritos y lugares celebran de maneras algo diferentes.","ask":"¿Cómo podrías compartir la paz con alguien en casa esta semana?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Order of Mass","work":"The Roman Missal","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"John 14:27","work":"The Gospel of John","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"paragraphs 1345-1355 and 1376","work":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"first-communion","game":"match","en":{"title":"a First Communion day","word":"Communion","means":"receiving the holy bread Catholics believe is Jesus himself","story":[{"head":"the Last Supper","text":"On the night before he died, Jesus ate a last supper with his friends. He took bread, gave thanks, broke it and gave it to them, saying it was his body. He asked them to keep doing this to remember him."},{"head":"getting ready","text":"Catholic children prepare for their First Communion with classes about Jesus and the Mass. Before it, they go to Confession for the first time, to tell God they are sorry for wrong things and to hear that they are forgiven."},{"head":"a special day at church","text":"On the day, many children dress in their best clothes; in some places girls wear white dresses and veils. Family and friends come to Mass to celebrate with them. The church feels full of joy."},{"head":"Body of Christ, Amen","text":"When it is time, each child walks up to the priest. The priest holds up a small round host, the holy bread, and says, the Body of Christ. The child answers Amen, which means yes, it is so. Afterward, there is often a party!"}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the Last Supper","Jesus shared bread with friends"],["the host","the small round holy bread"],["Amen","yes, it is so"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like walking up the church aisle. breathe out, like a quiet Amen. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can say thank you before I eat today","grownups":{"source":"The Last Supper is told in Luke 22:19-20 and in the other Gospels; paraphrased here. Catholics believe the consecrated bread and wine are truly Jesus's body and blood. The age and customs for First Communion differ by country and parish.","ask":"What do you remember about a special day when your family celebrated you?"}},"es":{"title":"el día de la Primera Comunión","word":"Comunión","means":"recibir el pan santo que, según los católicos, es Jesús mismo","story":[{"head":"la Última Cena","text":"La noche antes de morir, Jesús cenó por última vez con sus amigos. Tomó el pan, dio gracias, lo partió y se lo dio, diciendo que era su cuerpo. Les pidió que siguieran haciéndolo para acordarse de él."},{"head":"preparándose","text":"Los niños católicos se preparan para su Primera Comunión con clases sobre Jesús y la misa. Antes, se confiesan por primera vez, para decirle a Dios que sienten lo que hicieron mal y escuchar que están perdonados."},{"head":"un día especial en la iglesia","text":"Ese día, muchos niños se ponen su mejor ropa; en algunos lugares las niñas llevan vestido blanco y velo. La familia y los amigos van a misa a celebrar con ellos. La iglesia se siente llena de alegría."},{"head":"el Cuerpo de Cristo, amén","text":"Cuando llega el momento, cada niño se acerca al sacerdote. El sacerdote levanta una hostia pequeña y redonda, el pan santo, y dice: el Cuerpo de Cristo. El niño responde amén, que quiere decir sí, así es. ¡Después, muchas veces hay fiesta!"}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la Última Cena","Jesús compartió el pan"],["la hostia","el pan santo pequeño y redondo"],["amén","sí, así es"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si caminaras por el pasillo de la iglesia. suelta el aire, como un amén muy bajito. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo dar gracias antes de comer","grownups":{"source":"La Última Cena se cuenta en Lucas 22:19-20 y en los otros Evangelios; aquí con nuestras palabras. Los católicos creen que el pan y el vino consagrados son de verdad el cuerpo y la sangre de Jesús. La edad y las costumbres de la Primera Comunión cambian según el país y la parroquia.","ask":"¿Qué recuerdas de un día especial en que tu familia te celebró?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Luke 22:19-20","work":"The Gospel of Luke","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"paragraphs 1244, 1376 and 1457","work":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"easter-morning","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Easter morning","word":"Easter","means":"the day Christians celebrate Jesus rising to new life","story":[{"head":"a sad Friday","text":"Many people followed Jesus, and the rulers of Rome were worried about him. The Roman governor, Pontius Pilate, sentenced him to die on a cross. Jesus died on a Friday. His friends laid his body in a tomb cut into the rock."},{"head":"the stone rolled away","text":"Very early on Sunday, while it was still dark, Mary Magdalene went to the tomb. She found that the big stone across the door had been rolled away, and the tomb was empty. She ran to tell Peter and another of Jesus's friends."},{"head":"a voice in the garden","text":"Mary stayed by the tomb, crying. Someone came near, and she thought he was the gardener. Then he said her name: Mary. At once she knew him. It was Jesus, alive!"},{"head":"good news for the friends","text":"Mary ran to the friends and told them she had seen the Lord. Christians call this day Easter, and they celebrate it with flowers, bright candles and songs of alleluia. Catholics call it the feast of feasts."}],"game":{"items":[["Mary Magdalene found the big stone rolled away.",true,"yes: early in the morning, and the tomb was empty."],["Mary knew it was Jesus when he gave her a basket of eggs.",false,"no: she knew him when he said her name."],["Mary ran to tell the friends she had seen Jesus.",true,"yes: she told them she had seen the Lord."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the first light of Easter morning. breathe out, like hearing someone gently say your name. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can say someone's name kindly today","grownups":{"source":"John 19-20 in the Bible, paraphrased. The Gospels tell Easter morning in slightly different ways, naming different women and angels. The Catechism of the Catholic Church calls Easter the feast of feasts.","ask":"What new beginning have you seen, like flowers coming back in spring?"}},"es":{"title":"la mañana de Pascua","word":"Pascua","means":"el día en que los cristianos celebran que Jesús volvió a la vida","story":[{"head":"un viernes triste","text":"Mucha gente seguía a Jesús, y los gobernantes de Roma estaban preocupados por él. El gobernador romano, Poncio Pilato, lo condenó a morir en una cruz. Jesús murió un viernes. Sus amigos pusieron su cuerpo en un sepulcro excavado en la roca."},{"head":"la piedra quitada","text":"Muy temprano el domingo, cuando todavía estaba oscuro, María Magdalena fue al sepulcro. Vio que la piedra grande de la entrada estaba quitada, y el sepulcro estaba vacío. Corrió a avisarles a Pedro y a otro de los amigos de Jesús."},{"head":"una voz en el huerto","text":"María se quedó junto al sepulcro, llorando. Alguien se acercó, y ella pensó que era el jardinero. Entonces él dijo su nombre: María. Enseguida lo reconoció. ¡Era Jesús, vivo!"},{"head":"buenas noticias para los amigos","text":"María corrió a decirles a los amigos que había visto al Señor. Los cristianos llaman a este día Pascua, y lo celebran con flores, velas encendidas y cantos de aleluya. Los católicos la llaman la fiesta de las fiestas."}],"game":{"items":[["María Magdalena vio que la piedra grande estaba quitada.",true,"sí: muy temprano, y el sepulcro estaba vacío."],["María reconoció a Jesús cuando él le dio una canasta de huevos.",false,"no: lo reconoció cuando él dijo su nombre."],["María corrió a decirles a los amigos que había visto a Jesús.",true,"sí: les dijo que había visto al Señor."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la primera luz de la mañana de Pascua. suelta el aire, como si alguien dijera tu nombre con cariño. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo decir el nombre de alguien con cariño","grownups":{"source":"Juan 19 y 20 en la Biblia, contado con nuestras palabras. Los Evangelios cuentan la mañana de Pascua de maneras algo distintas, con distintas mujeres y ángeles. El Catecismo de la Iglesia Católica llama a la Pascua la fiesta de las fiestas.","ask":"¿Qué nuevo comienzo has visto, como las flores que vuelven en primavera?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"John 19:16-42 and 20:1-18","work":"The Gospel of John","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"paragraph 1169","work":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"bernadette-lourdes","game":"match","en":{"title":"St. Bernadette and the lady at Lourdes","word":"Lourdes","means":"a town in France where Bernadette saw a lady by a spring","story":[{"head":"gathering sticks by the river","text":"In the town of Lourdes, in France, lived a poor girl named Bernadette. One cold day she went with her sister and a friend to gather firewood by the river, near a rocky cave called the grotto."},{"head":"a lady in white","text":"Bernadette heard a sound like a gust of wind. In the grotto she saw a young lady dressed in white, with a blue sash and a yellow rose on each foot. The lady held a rosary, and Bernadette began to pray hers."},{"head":"a spring under the mud","text":"The lady came again on other days. One day she asked Bernadette to drink from the spring. Bernadette saw only mud, but she dug with her hands, and soon clear water began to flow. It still flows today."},{"head":"the lady says her name","text":"Bernadette asked the lady her name. The lady answered in Bernadette's own language: I am the Immaculate Conception, a name Catholics give to Mary. Today people from many countries visit Lourdes to pray and wash in the water."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Bernadette","dug in the mud with her hands"],["the lady","had a yellow rose on each foot"],["the spring","began to flow with clear water"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like cool air in a rocky cave. breathe out, like clear water trickling from a spring. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can keep going when something looks hard at first","grownups":{"source":"Bernadette described what she saw in her own accounts, which the Church studied before approving devotion at Lourdes. The Immaculate Conception means Catholics believe Mary was kept free from sin from her very beginning. Bernadette later became a nun and is a saint.","ask":"When have you kept going, like Bernadette digging through the mud, and what happened?"}},"es":{"title":"Santa Bernardita y la señora de Lourdes","word":"Lourdes","means":"un pueblo de Francia donde Bernardita vio a una señora","story":[{"head":"juntando leña junto al río","text":"En el pueblo de Lourdes, en Francia, vivía una niña pobre llamada Bernardita. Un día frío fue con su hermana y una amiga a juntar leña junto al río, cerca de una cueva de rocas llamada la gruta."},{"head":"una señora vestida de blanco","text":"Bernardita oyó un ruido como una ráfaga de viento. En la gruta vio a una señora joven vestida de blanco, con un cinturón azul y una rosa amarilla en cada pie. La señora tenía un rosario, y Bernardita empezó a rezar el suyo."},{"head":"un manantial bajo el lodo","text":"La señora volvió otros días. Un día le pidió a Bernardita que bebiera del manantial. Bernardita solo veía lodo, pero escarbó con las manos, y pronto empezó a brotar agua clara. Todavía sigue brotando hoy."},{"head":"la señora dice su nombre","text":"Bernardita le preguntó a la señora su nombre. La señora le respondió en la lengua de Bernardita: Yo soy la Inmaculada Concepción, un nombre que los católicos dan a María. Hoy gente de muchos países visita Lourdes para rezar y lavarse en el agua."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Bernardita","escarbó el lodo con las manos"],["la señora","tenía rosas amarillas en los pies"],["el manantial","empezó a dar agua clara"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el aire fresco de una cueva de rocas. suelta el aire, como agua clara que brota de un manantial. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo seguir adelante aunque algo parezca difícil al principio","grownups":{"source":"Bernardita describió lo que vio en sus propios relatos, que la Iglesia estudió antes de aprobar la devoción en Lourdes. La Inmaculada Concepción quiere decir que los católicos creen que María fue preservada del pecado desde el primer momento. Bernardita después fue monja y es santa.","ask":"¿Cuándo has seguido adelante, como Bernardita escarbando en el lodo, y qué pasó?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"her written accounts of the apparitions","work":"St. Bernadette Soubirous, accounts of the apparitions at Lourdes","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"paragraph 491","work":"Catechism of the Catholic Church","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"anthony-lost-book","game":"truth","en":{"title":"St. Anthony and the missing book","word":"St. Anthony","means":"a saint many Catholics ask for help in finding lost things","story":[{"head":"a precious book of psalms","text":"Long ago, St. Anthony was a friar, a brother who lived and prayed with other brothers. In those days books were copied by hand and hard to come by. Anthony had a book of psalms, full of the notes he used for teaching, and it was precious to him."},{"head":"the book goes missing","text":"The old stories say that a young man living with the brothers grew tired of that life. He decided to leave, and he took Anthony's book with him without asking."},{"head":"a quiet prayer instead","text":"When Anthony found that his book was gone, he was sad. He did not get angry, and he did not chase after the young man. Instead, he prayed that the book would come back."},{"head":"the book comes home","text":"Soon the young man came back. He returned the book to Anthony and stayed with the brothers. Because of this story, many Catholics ask St. Anthony to help them when they lose something, from keys to toys. It is a popular custom, not a rule."}],"game":{"items":[["Anthony's missing book was a book of psalms.",true,"yes: full of the notes he used for teaching."],["Anthony chased the young man down the road.",false,"no: Anthony prayed, and the young man brought the book back."],["the young man came back and returned the book.",true,"yes: and he stayed with the brothers."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like opening a precious old book. breathe out, like turning a page. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can help someone find something they lost","grownups":{"source":"Anthony of Padua was a Franciscan friar and preacher. The story of the missing book of psalms is told in later lives of Anthony, and the old stories differ in details. Asking his help with lost things is a popular devotion, not Church teaching.","ask":"How did it feel when you found something special you had lost?"}},"es":{"title":"San Antonio y el libro perdido","word":"San Antonio","means":"un santo al que muchos católicos piden ayuda con cosas perdidas","story":[{"head":"un libro de salmos muy querido","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, San Antonio era fraile, un hermano que vivía y rezaba con otros hermanos. En esa época los libros se copiaban a mano y eran difíciles de conseguir. Antonio tenía un libro de salmos, lleno de las notas que usaba para enseñar, y lo quería mucho."},{"head":"el libro desaparece","text":"Las historias antiguas cuentan que un joven que vivía con los hermanos se cansó de esa vida. Decidió irse, y se llevó el libro de Antonio sin pedir permiso."},{"head":"una oración en vez de enojo","text":"Cuando Antonio vio que su libro no estaba, se puso triste. No se enojó, ni salió a perseguir al joven. En cambio, rezó para que el libro volviera."},{"head":"el libro vuelve a casa","text":"Al poco tiempo el joven regresó. Le devolvió el libro a Antonio y se quedó con los hermanos. Por esta historia, muchos católicos le piden ayuda a San Antonio cuando pierden algo, desde llaves hasta juguetes. Es una costumbre popular, no una regla."}],"game":{"items":[["el libro perdido de Antonio era un libro de salmos.",true,"sí: lleno de las notas que usaba para enseñar."],["Antonio persiguió al joven por el camino.",false,"no: Antonio rezó, y el joven regresó con el libro."],["el joven regresó y devolvió el libro.",true,"sí: y se quedó con los hermanos."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si abrieras un libro antiguo y querido. suelta el aire, como si pasaras una página. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ayudar a alguien a encontrar algo que perdió","grownups":{"source":"Antonio de Padua fue un fraile franciscano y predicador. La historia del libro de salmos perdido aparece en vidas de Antonio escritas más tarde, y los relatos cambian en los detalles. Pedirle ayuda con las cosas perdidas es una devoción popular, no una enseñanza de la Iglesia.","ask":"¿Cómo te sentiste cuando encontraste algo especial que habías perdido?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the story of the stolen psalter","work":"later legends of St. Anthony of Padua (Franciscan tradition)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"teresa-calcutta","game":"match","en":{"title":"St. Teresa of Calcutta","word":"charity","means":"love in action: helping others freely","story":[{"head":"a teacher in Calcutta","text":"A nun named Teresa worked as a teacher at a school in the city of Calcutta, in India. She had taken her name from St. Thérèse of Lisieux, the saint of the little way. Outside the school walls, she saw many people who were very poor."},{"head":"a call to the streets","text":"Teresa said she felt God calling her to live among the poorest people and help them. So she left the school. She put on a simple white sari with a blue border and went out into the streets of the city."},{"head":"care for the sick and alone","text":"She washed and cared for people who were sick and had no one to look after them. She taught children in the open air. Other women came to help her, and together they became the Missionaries of Charity."},{"head":"small acts, great love","text":"People called her Mother Teresa. Her sisters still care for poor and sick people in many countries. Like the Thérèse she was named for, she showed that small acts done with love matter. The Church named her a saint, St. Teresa of Calcutta."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Teresa","wore a white and blue sari"],["Missionaries of Charity","the sisters who joined her"],["Calcutta","the city in India where she lived"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a quiet street early in the morning. breathe out, like a gentle hand helping someone up. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can notice someone who needs help and help them","grownups":{"source":"From well-known accounts of her life, paraphrased. Mother Teresa founded the Missionaries of Charity in Calcutta, now called Kolkata. She chose her religious name in honor of St. Thérèse of Lisieux, and Pope Francis declared her a saint.","ask":"Who in our neighborhood might need a small act of love?"}},"es":{"title":"Santa Teresa de Calcuta","word":"caridad","means":"amor que se hace obra: ayudar a otros sin esperar nada","story":[{"head":"una maestra en Calcuta","text":"Una monja llamada Teresa trabajaba como maestra en una escuela de la ciudad de Calcuta, en la India. Había tomado su nombre de Santa Teresita de Lisieux, la del caminito. Fuera de los muros de la escuela, veía a mucha gente muy pobre."},{"head":"un llamado a las calles","text":"Teresa dijo que sentía que Dios la llamaba a vivir entre los más pobres y a ayudarlos. Así que dejó la escuela. Se puso un sencillo sari blanco con borde azul y salió a las calles de la ciudad."},{"head":"cuidar a los enfermos y solos","text":"Lavaba y cuidaba a personas enfermas que no tenían a nadie que las atendiera. Les daba clases a los niños al aire libre. Otras mujeres vinieron a ayudarla, y juntas formaron las Misioneras de la Caridad."},{"head":"pequeños actos, gran amor","text":"La gente la llamaba Madre Teresa. Sus hermanas todavía cuidan a personas pobres y enfermas en muchos países. Como la Teresita de quien tomó su nombre, mostró que los pequeños actos hechos con amor importan. La Iglesia la declaró santa: Santa Teresa de Calcuta."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Teresa","usaba un sari blanco y azul"],["las Misioneras de la Caridad","las hermanas que se le unieron"],["Calcuta","la ciudad de la India donde vivía"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una calle tranquila muy temprano. suelta el aire, como una mano suave que ayuda a alguien a levantarse. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo fijarme en quien necesita ayuda y ayudarle","grownups":{"source":"Basado en relatos conocidos de su vida, con nuestras palabras. La Madre Teresa fundó las Misioneras de la Caridad en Calcuta, hoy llamada Kolkata. Eligió su nombre religioso en honor a Santa Teresita de Lisieux, y el papa Francisco la declaró santa.","ask":"¿Quién en nuestro barrio podría necesitar un pequeño acto de amor?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"homily for the canonization of Mother Teresa of Calcutta","work":"Pope Francis, Holy See (Vatican)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"biographical note","work":"Missionaries of Charity / Holy See biography of St. Teresa of Calcutta","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]},"JUDAISM":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"JUDAISM","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"creation-seventh-day","game":"match","en":{"title":"the seven days and the day of rest","word":"Bereishit","means":"“in the beginning,” the first word of the Torah (בראשית)","story":[{"head":"light in the dark","text":"In the beginning, the story says, everything was dark, and there was only deep, wild water. Then God said, “Let there be light.” And there was light. God saw that the light was good. That was the first day."},{"head":"sky, sea and dry land","text":"Next God made the wide sky above, and gathered the seas together so dry land could appear. Grass and trees began to grow on the land, and the fruit had seeds tucked inside it."},{"head":"sun, stars, fish, birds and people","text":"God set the sun, the moon and the stars in the sky. The seas filled with fish, the air with birds, and the land with animals. Last of all, God made people, and blessed them."},{"head":"the seventh day, a day to rest","text":"On the seventh day God rested, and blessed that day and made it holy. Many Jewish families remember it every week. They call it Shabbat, a day for resting, sharing meals, singing and being together."}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes together? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the first day","light in the dark"],["the seas","filled with fish"],["the seventh day","a day of rest"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like light spreading across a dark sky. breathe out slowly, like the whole world resting on the seventh day. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will rest for a moment and notice something good","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 1:1–2:3, the opening of the Torah. The six days are in Genesis 1; the seventh day of rest is Genesis 2:1–3. The command to keep Shabbat is in Exodus 20:8–11.","ask":"what would you like to do on a day just for resting?"}},"es":{"title":"los siete días y el día de descanso","word":"Bereshit","means":"“en el principio”, la primera palabra de la Torá (בראשית)","story":[{"head":"luz en la oscuridad","text":"En el principio, dice la historia, todo estaba oscuro y solo había agua profunda y salvaje. Entonces Dios dijo: “Que haya luz”. Y hubo luz. Dios vio que la luz era buena. Ese fue el primer día."},{"head":"cielo, mar y tierra seca","text":"Después Dios hizo el ancho cielo arriba, y juntó los mares para que apareciera la tierra seca. En la tierra empezaron a crecer la hierba y los árboles, y las frutas tenían semillas guardadas adentro."},{"head":"sol, estrellas, peces, aves y personas","text":"Dios puso el sol, la luna y las estrellas en el cielo. Los mares se llenaron de peces, el aire de aves y la tierra de animales. Al final, Dios hizo a las personas y las bendijo."},{"head":"el séptimo día, un día para descansar","text":"El séptimo día Dios descansó, bendijo ese día y lo hizo santo. Muchas familias judías lo recuerdan cada semana. Lo llaman Shabat, un día para descansar, compartir la comida, cantar y estar juntos."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el primer día","luz en la oscuridad"],["los mares","se llenaron de peces"],["el séptimo día","un día de descanso"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como la luz que se extiende por un cielo oscuro. exhala despacio, como el mundo entero descansando el séptimo día. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a descansar un momento y fijarme en algo bueno","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 1:1–2:3, el comienzo de la Torá. Los seis días están en Génesis 1; el séptimo día de descanso, en Génesis 2:1–3. El mandato de guardar el Shabat está en Éxodo 20:8–11.","ask":"¿qué te gustaría hacer en un día solo para descansar?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 1:3","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":"JPS 1917","quoted":"Let there be light."},{"ref":"Genesis 1:1–2:3","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Exodus 20:8–11","work":"Torah (Exodus)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"noah-rainbow","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Noah’s ark and the rainbow","word":"keshet","means":"the Hebrew word for rainbow, and also for a bow (קשת)","story":[{"head":"a big boat for Noah","text":"Long ago, the story says, the world had grown full of unkindness. God told Noah, a good and gentle man, that a great flood was coming, and that he should build an ark: a huge wooden boat with rooms inside and a roof on top."},{"head":"two by two, the animals come","text":"Noah and his family gathered food for everyone. Then the animals came, two by two: big ones and tiny ones, animals that walk, birds that fly, and creatures that creep. When everyone was inside, the door was shut and the rain began."},{"head":"a dove with an olive leaf","text":"It rained and rained, and the ark floated safely on the water. At last the rain stopped. Noah sent out a dove, and one evening she came back with a fresh olive leaf in her beak. The land was drying!"},{"head":"a rainbow in the clouds","text":"Everyone stepped out onto the dry, green earth. God set a rainbow in the clouds as a sign of a promise: never again would a flood cover the whole earth. Many Jewish families say a short blessing when they see a rainbow."}],"game":{"items":[["the animals came into the ark two by two.",true,"yes: big ones and tiny ones, two by two."],["Noah sent out a parrot to look for land.",false,"not in it: Noah sent a dove, and she came back with an olive leaf."],["God set a rainbow in the clouds as a sign of a promise.",true,"yes: a promise that a flood would never again cover the whole earth."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the dove flying out over the quiet water. breathe out slowly, like a rainbow stretching across the sky. four slow breaths.","carry":"when I see a rainbow, I’ll remember a promise","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 6:9–9:17 in the Torah. The dove and the olive leaf are in Genesis 8:8–12; the rainbow and the promise are in Genesis 9:12–17. The blessing on seeing a rainbow is in the Talmud, Berakhot 59a.","ask":"what is a promise you made, and how did you keep it?"}},"es":{"title":"el arca de Noé y el arcoíris","word":"keshet","means":"la palabra hebrea para arcoíris, y también para arco (קשת)","story":[{"head":"un gran barco para Noé","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, dice la historia, el mundo se había llenado de falta de bondad. Dios le dijo a Noé, un hombre bueno y tranquilo, que venía un gran diluvio, y que construyera un arca: un enorme barco de madera con cuartos adentro y un techo arriba."},{"head":"de dos en dos, llegan los animales","text":"Noé y su familia juntaron comida para todos. Entonces llegaron los animales, de dos en dos: grandes y pequeñitos, animales que caminan, aves que vuelan y bichitos que se arrastran. Cuando todos estuvieron adentro, se cerró la puerta y empezó a llover."},{"head":"una paloma con una hoja de olivo","text":"Llovió y llovió, y el arca flotaba a salvo sobre el agua. Por fin dejó de llover. Noé soltó una paloma, y una tarde ella volvió con una hoja fresca de olivo en el pico. ¡La tierra se estaba secando!"},{"head":"un arcoíris en las nubes","text":"Todos salieron a la tierra seca y verde. Dios puso un arcoíris en las nubes como señal de una promesa: nunca más un diluvio cubriría toda la tierra. Muchas familias judías dicen una bendición corta cuando ven un arcoíris."}],"game":{"items":[["los animales entraron al arca de dos en dos.",true,"sí: grandes y pequeñitos, de dos en dos."],["Noé soltó un loro para buscar tierra.",false,"no está: Noé soltó una paloma, y ella volvió con una hoja de olivo."],["Dios puso un arcoíris en las nubes como señal de una promesa.",true,"sí: la promesa de que un diluvio nunca más cubriría toda la tierra."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como la paloma que vuela sobre el agua tranquila. exhala despacio, como un arcoíris que se extiende por el cielo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"cuando vea un arcoíris, me acordaré de una promesa","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 6:9–9:17 en la Torá. La paloma y la hoja de olivo están en Génesis 8:8–12; el arcoíris y la promesa, en Génesis 9:12–17. La bendición al ver un arcoíris está en el Talmud, Berajot 59a.","ask":"¿qué promesa hiciste alguna vez, y cómo la cumpliste?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 6:9–9:17","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Berakhot 59a","work":"Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"abraham-sarah-tent","game":"match","en":{"title":"Abraham and Sarah’s open tent","word":"hachnasat orchim","means":"welcoming guests, a mitzvah (הכנסת אורחים)","story":[{"head":"a hot day by the tent","text":"Abraham and Sarah lived in a tent near some big oak trees. One very hot afternoon, Abraham sat at the door of the tent, looking out at the road shimmering in the heat."},{"head":"three travelers on the road","text":"He looked up and saw three travelers standing nearby. Abraham did not wait for them to come to him. He ran to meet them, bowed low, and asked them to stop and rest for a while."},{"head":"water, bread and a shady tree","text":"He brought water so they could wash their dusty feet, and they rested under the tree. Sarah quickly baked fresh bread. Abraham brought more food and milk, and served the guests himself."},{"head":"good news for Sarah","text":"The visitors brought good news: Sarah and Abraham would soon have a baby boy. Sarah laughed when she heard it. In time their son was born, and they named him Isaac."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Abraham","ran to meet the travelers"],["Sarah","baked fresh bread"],["the travelers","brought good news"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like cool shade under a big tree. breathe out slowly, like a tired traveler finally sitting down to rest. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will make someone feel welcome","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 18:1–15 in the Torah; Isaac's birth is in Genesis 21:1–3. The rabbis later held up Abraham's open tent as a model for welcoming guests. Genesis calls the visitors men; Jewish tradition understands them as angels.","ask":"how could our family make a guest feel at home?"}},"es":{"title":"la tienda abierta de Abraham y Sara","word":"hajnasat orjim","means":"recibir a los invitados, una mitzvá (הכנסת אורחים)","story":[{"head":"un día caluroso junto a la tienda","text":"Abraham y Sara vivían en una tienda cerca de unos grandes robles. Una tarde de mucho calor, Abraham estaba sentado a la entrada de la tienda, mirando el camino que brillaba con el calor."},{"head":"tres viajeros en el camino","text":"Levantó la vista y vio a tres viajeros de pie cerca de allí. Abraham no esperó a que ellos vinieran. Corrió a recibirlos, se inclinó y les pidió que se detuvieran a descansar un rato."},{"head":"agua, pan y un árbol con sombra","text":"Les trajo agua para lavarse los pies llenos de polvo, y ellos descansaron bajo el árbol. Sara horneó pan fresco muy rápido. Abraham trajo más comida y leche, y él mismo atendió a los invitados."},{"head":"una buena noticia para Sara","text":"Los visitantes traían una buena noticia: Sara y Abraham pronto tendrían un bebé. Sara se rio cuando lo oyó. Con el tiempo nació su hijo, y le pusieron Isaac."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Abraham","corrió a recibir a los viajeros"],["Sara","horneó pan fresco"],["los viajeros","traían una buena noticia"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como la sombra fresca bajo un gran árbol. exhala despacio, como un viajero cansado que por fin se sienta a descansar. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a hacer que alguien se sienta bienvenido","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 18:1–15 en la Torá; el nacimiento de Isaac está en Génesis 21:1–3. Los rabinos tomaron la tienda abierta de Abraham como modelo para recibir invitados. Génesis llama a los visitantes hombres; la tradición judía los entiende como ángeles.","ask":"¿cómo podría nuestra familia hacer que un invitado se sienta en casa?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 18:1–15","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Genesis 21:1–3","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":4,"key":"rebecca-well","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Rebecca waters the camels","word":"chesed","means":"loving-kindness: doing more good than you have to (חסד)","story":[{"head":"a servant sets off with camels","text":"When Isaac grew up, his father Abraham sent a trusted servant on a long journey, to find a wife for Isaac among their relatives. The servant set off with ten camels, loaded with gifts."},{"head":"a quiet wish at the well","text":"In the evening he stopped by a well, where young women came to draw water. He asked God for a sign: let the right one offer a drink to me, and to my thirsty camels too."},{"head":"water for every camel","text":"Before he had even finished, Rebecca came with a jar on her shoulder. She gave him a drink, and then offered to draw water for his camels too. She hurried back and forth to the well until every camel had drunk."},{"head":"a new family","text":"The servant bowed and thanked God. Rebecca's family welcomed him into their home. Rebecca agreed to go with him, and she and Isaac married, and Isaac loved her."}],"game":{"items":[["Rebecca gave water to the servant and to his camels.",true,"yes: she drew water until every camel had drunk."],["the servant arrived riding an elephant.",false,"not in it: he came with ten camels, loaded with gifts."],["Rebecca agreed to go and marry Isaac.",true,"yes: she went with the servant, and she and Isaac married."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a cool jar filling up at the well. breathe out slowly, like a thirsty camel taking a long drink. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will help someone without being asked","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 24 in the Torah. The servant is not named in the chapter; later tellings often call him Eliezer. Rebecca does more than she is asked, which Jewish teaching calls chesed, loving-kindness.","ask":"when has someone been kind to you without being asked?"}},"es":{"title":"Rebeca les da agua a los camellos","word":"jésed","means":"bondad amorosa: hacer más bien del que te piden (חסד)","story":[{"head":"un sirviente sale con camellos","text":"Cuando Isaac creció, su padre Abraham envió a un sirviente de confianza en un largo viaje, para buscarle esposa a Isaac entre sus parientes. El sirviente salió con diez camellos cargados de regalos."},{"head":"un deseo en silencio junto al pozo","text":"Al atardecer se detuvo junto a un pozo, adonde las jóvenes venían a sacar agua. Le pidió a Dios una señal: que la indicada me ofrezca de beber a mí, y también a mis camellos sedientos."},{"head":"agua para todos los camellos","text":"Antes de que terminara de hablar, llegó Rebeca con un cántaro al hombro. Le dio de beber, y luego se ofreció a sacar agua también para sus camellos. Fue y vino al pozo, deprisa, hasta que todos los camellos bebieron."},{"head":"una nueva familia","text":"El sirviente se inclinó y le dio gracias a Dios. La familia de Rebeca lo recibió en su casa. Rebeca aceptó ir con él, y ella e Isaac se casaron, e Isaac la amó."}],"game":{"items":[["Rebeca les dio agua al sirviente y a sus camellos.",true,"sí: sacó agua hasta que todos los camellos bebieron."],["el sirviente llegó montado en un elefante.",false,"no está: llegó con diez camellos cargados de regalos."],["Rebeca aceptó ir a casarse con Isaac.",true,"sí: se fue con el sirviente, y ella e Isaac se casaron."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un cántaro fresco que se llena en el pozo. exhala despacio, como un camello sediento que bebe un largo trago. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a ayudar a alguien sin que me lo pida","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 24 en la Torá. El capítulo no dice el nombre del sirviente; relatos posteriores a menudo lo llaman Eliezer. Rebeca hace más de lo que le piden, lo que la enseñanza judía llama jésed, bondad amorosa.","ask":"¿cuándo alguien fue amable contigo sin que se lo pidieras?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 24","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"jacob-ladder","game":"match","en":{"title":"Jacob’s dream of the ladder","word":"Beth-el","means":"“house of God,” the name Jacob gave the place (בית אל)","story":[{"head":"a stone for a pillow","text":"Jacob was traveling far from home, all by himself. When the sun went down, he stopped for the night in an open place. He took a stone, put it under his head, and lay down to sleep."},{"head":"a ladder up to the sky","text":"Jacob had a wonderful dream. He saw a ladder standing on the ground, with its top reaching all the way up to the sky, and angels of God going up and coming down on it."},{"head":"a promise in the night","text":"In the dream, God stood beside him and made a promise: I am with you. I will watch over you wherever you go, and I will bring you back home again."},{"head":"the house of God","text":"Jacob woke up full of wonder. He said that God was in this place, and he had not known it. He set up his stone as a marker, and called the place Beth-el, the house of God."}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes together? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the stone","under Jacob’s head"],["the ladder","reached up to the sky"],["the angels","went up and came down"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like an angel climbing up the ladder. breathe out slowly, like an angel coming gently down. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will look for something wonderful in an ordinary place","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 28:10–22 in the Torah. Jacob is on his way to his uncle Laban's home. Jewish commentators have offered many readings of what the ladder and the angels mean.","ask":"where is a place that feels special to you, and why?"}},"es":{"title":"el sueño de Jacob y la escalera","word":"Betel","means":"“casa de Dios”, el nombre que Jacob le dio al lugar (בית אל)","story":[{"head":"una piedra como almohada","text":"Jacob viajaba lejos de casa, completamente solo. Cuando se puso el sol, se detuvo a pasar la noche en un lugar abierto. Tomó una piedra, la puso bajo su cabeza y se acostó a dormir."},{"head":"una escalera hasta el cielo","text":"Jacob tuvo un sueño maravilloso. Vio una escalera apoyada en el suelo, con la punta llegando hasta el cielo, y ángeles de Dios que subían y bajaban por ella."},{"head":"una promesa en la noche","text":"En el sueño, Dios estaba a su lado y le hizo una promesa: estoy contigo. Te cuidaré dondequiera que vayas, y te traeré de vuelta a casa."},{"head":"la casa de Dios","text":"Jacob despertó lleno de asombro. Dijo que Dios estaba en ese lugar, y él no lo sabía. Levantó su piedra como señal y llamó al lugar Betel, la casa de Dios."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la piedra","bajo la cabeza de Jacob"],["la escalera","llegaba hasta el cielo"],["los ángeles","subían y bajaban"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un ángel que sube por la escalera. exhala despacio, como un ángel que baja con suavidad. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a buscar algo maravilloso en un lugar común","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 28:10–22 en la Torá. Jacob va camino a la casa de su tío Labán. Los comentaristas judíos han ofrecido muchas lecturas sobre lo que significan la escalera y los ángeles.","ask":"¿qué lugar se siente especial para ti, y por qué?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 28:10–22","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"joseph-coat","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Joseph’s special coat","word":"forgive","means":"to let go of anger at someone who hurt you, and start again","story":[{"head":"a special coat for Joseph","text":"Jacob had twelve sons, and he loved Joseph very much. He gave Joseph a special coat, long and beautiful. Joseph also had dreams, and he told his brothers that one day they would bow down to him."},{"head":"jealous brothers","text":"His brothers felt jealous, and the jealousy grew and grew. One day, far from home, they did something very unkind. They sold Joseph to traders who were going to Egypt, and he was taken far from his family."},{"head":"Joseph stores grain in Egypt","text":"In Egypt, Joseph had hard times, but he did not give up. He explained Pharaoh's dreams: seven years of plenty, then seven years of hunger. Pharaoh put Joseph in charge, and he stored grain so people could eat."},{"head":"a hug and forgiveness","text":"When the hungry years came, Joseph's brothers traveled to Egypt for food. They did not know him, but he knew them. At last Joseph told them who he was. He cried, hugged them, and forgave them."}],"game":{"items":[["Joseph’s father gave him a special coat.",true,"yes: a long, beautiful coat from his father Jacob."],["Joseph stayed angry at his brothers forever.",false,"not in it: Joseph cried, hugged his brothers and forgave them."],["Joseph stored grain so people would have food.",true,"yes: he got ready for the seven hungry years."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a brother opening his arms for a hug. breathe out slowly, like old anger floating away. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will let go of something that made me upset","grownups":{"source":"Genesis 37 and 39–45 in the Torah. The Hebrew words for Joseph's coat are unclear: older English Bibles say a coat of many colors, others a long-sleeved or ornamented coat. Joseph forgives his brothers in Genesis 45 and again in 50:15–21.","ask":"is it hard or easy for you to forgive, and what helps?"}},"es":{"title":"la túnica especial de José","word":"perdonar","means":"dejar ir el enojo con quien te lastimó, y empezar de nuevo","story":[{"head":"una túnica especial para José","text":"Jacob tenía doce hijos, y quería mucho a José. Le dio una túnica especial, larga y hermosa. José también tenía sueños, y les contó a sus hermanos que un día ellos se inclinarían ante él."},{"head":"hermanos celosos","text":"Sus hermanos sintieron celos, y los celos crecieron y crecieron. Un día, lejos de casa, hicieron algo muy poco amable. Vendieron a José a unos comerciantes que iban a Egipto, y se lo llevaron lejos de su familia."},{"head":"José guarda grano en Egipto","text":"En Egipto, José pasó momentos difíciles, pero no se rindió. Explicó los sueños del faraón: siete años de abundancia y luego siete años de hambre. El faraón puso a José a cargo, y él guardó grano para que la gente pudiera comer."},{"head":"un abrazo y el perdón","text":"Cuando llegaron los años de hambre, los hermanos de José viajaron a Egipto a buscar comida. Ellos no lo reconocieron, pero él sí a ellos. Por fin José les dijo quién era. Lloró, los abrazó y los perdonó."}],"game":{"items":[["el padre de José le dio una túnica especial.",true,"sí: una túnica larga y hermosa de su padre Jacob."],["José se quedó enojado con sus hermanos para siempre.",false,"no está: José lloró, abrazó a sus hermanos y los perdonó."],["José guardó grano para que la gente tuviera comida.",true,"sí: se preparó para los siete años de hambre."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un hermano que abre los brazos para un abrazo. exhala despacio, como un enojo viejo que se va flotando. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a dejar ir algo que me hizo enojar","grownups":{"source":"Génesis 37 y 39–45 en la Torá. Las palabras hebreas para la túnica de José no son claras: biblias antiguas dicen una túnica de muchos colores, otras una túnica de mangas largas o adornada. José perdona a sus hermanos en Génesis 45 y otra vez en 50:15–21.","ask":"¿te cuesta perdonar o te resulta fácil? ¿qué te ayuda?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Genesis 37; 39–45; 50:15–21","work":"Torah (Genesis)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":7,"key":"baby-moses-basket","game":"match","en":{"title":"baby Moses in the basket","word":"Moshe","means":"Moses’ Hebrew name (משה); the story links it to “drew out”","story":[{"head":"a baby hidden at home","text":"The Hebrew people lived in Egypt, and Pharaoh, the king, had made a harsh rule against their baby boys. When a Hebrew mother had a baby boy, she kept him hidden at home for three months."},{"head":"a basket among the reeds","text":"When she could not hide him any longer, she made a little basket out of reeds and coated it so no water could get in. She laid her baby inside and set it among the tall reeds by the river."},{"head":"big sister Miriam keeps watch","text":"The baby's big sister stood a little way off, watching to see what would happen. Her name was Miriam. She waited and waited by the river, keeping an eye on the little basket."},{"head":"the princess finds the baby","text":"Pharaoh's daughter came down to the river, saw the basket and opened it. The baby was crying, and she felt sorry for him. Miriam ran up and offered to find a nurse, and brought the baby's own mother! The princess named him Moses."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the mother","made a basket of reeds"],["Miriam","watched from a little way off"],["the princess","named the baby Moses"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the river gently rocking a little basket. breathe out slowly, like Miriam waiting patiently by the reeds. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will look out for someone younger than me","grownups":{"source":"Exodus 2:1–10 in the Torah. The chapter calls her only “his sister”; Jewish tradition, with Numbers 26:59, names Moses' sister Miriam. Exodus 2:10 links the name Moses to drawing him out of the water.","ask":"who looks out for you, the way Miriam looked out for her brother?"}},"es":{"title":"el bebé Moisés en la canasta","word":"Moshé","means":"el nombre hebreo de Moisés (משה); la historia lo une a “sacar”","story":[{"head":"un bebé escondido en casa","text":"El pueblo hebreo vivía en Egipto, y el faraón, el rey, había dado una orden muy dura contra sus bebés varones. Cuando una madre hebrea tuvo un niño, lo mantuvo escondido en casa durante tres meses."},{"head":"una canasta entre los juncos","text":"Cuando ya no pudo esconderlo más, hizo una canastita de juncos y la cubrió para que no entrara el agua. Acostó a su bebé adentro y la puso entre los juncos altos a la orilla del río."},{"head":"la hermana Miriam vigila","text":"La hermana mayor del bebé se quedó un poco lejos, mirando qué iba a pasar. Se llamaba Miriam. Esperó y esperó junto al río, sin quitarle los ojos de encima a la canastita."},{"head":"la princesa encuentra al bebé","text":"La hija del faraón bajó al río, vio la canasta y la abrió. El bebé lloraba, y ella sintió compasión por él. Miriam corrió y se ofreció a buscar una nodriza, ¡y trajo a la propia mamá del bebé! La princesa lo llamó Moisés."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la mamá","hizo una canasta de juncos"],["Miriam","miraba desde un poco lejos"],["la princesa","le puso Moisés al bebé"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el río que mece suavemente una canastita. exhala despacio, como Miriam que espera con paciencia junto a los juncos. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a cuidar a alguien más pequeño que yo","grownups":{"source":"Éxodo 2:1–10 en la Torá. El capítulo la llama solo “su hermana”; la tradición judía, junto con Números 26:59, llama Miriam a la hermana de Moisés. Éxodo 2:10 une el nombre de Moisés a sacarlo del agua.","ask":"¿quién te cuida a ti, como Miriam cuidó a su hermano?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Exodus 2:1–10","work":"Torah (Exodus)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Numbers 26:59","work":"Torah (Numbers)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"passover-seder","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Passover: the night of questions","word":"matzah","means":"flat bread made with no time to rise, eaten at Passover (מצה)","story":[{"head":"hard times in Egypt","text":"Long ago, the story says, the Hebrew people were slaves in Egypt. They worked very hard making bricks, and they were not free. Moses went to Pharaoh again and again and said: let my people go."},{"head":"out of Egypt in a hurry","text":"After many hard days, Pharaoh finally let them go. They left in such a hurry that their bread dough had no time to rise. They baked it flat, and that flat bread is called matzah."},{"head":"the seder table","text":"Every spring, many Jewish families remember this story at Passover with a special meal called the seder. On the table are matzah, a dish of salt water, bitter herbs, and sweet charoset made from fruit and nuts."},{"head":"a night for questions","text":"At the seder, children are invited to ask questions. Often the youngest asks why this night is different from all other nights. Then everyone tells the story together, as if they had left Egypt themselves."}],"game":{"items":[["the people left Egypt in such a hurry that their bread had no time to rise.",true,"yes: they baked it flat, and that is matzah."],["at the seder, children are told to sit quietly and not ask anything.",false,"not in it: children are invited to ask questions."],["the seder table has bitter herbs and sweet charoset.",true,"yes: along with matzah and a dish of salt water."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a child getting ready to ask a big question. breathe out slowly, like a whole family telling the story together. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will ask a question about something I wonder about","grownups":{"source":"Exodus 1–15 in the Torah tells the story; the unrisen bread is Exodus 12:39. The seder and its questions come from the Mishnah (Pesachim 10) and the Haggadah, the book families read at the table. Seder customs differ between families and communities.","ask":"what is a question you would like to ask at our table?"}},"es":{"title":"Pésaj: la noche de las preguntas","word":"matzá","means":"pan plano hecho sin tiempo para levar, que se come en Pésaj (מצה)","story":[{"head":"tiempos difíciles en Egipto","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, dice la historia, el pueblo hebreo era esclavo en Egipto. Trabajaban muy duro haciendo ladrillos, y no eran libres. Moisés fue a ver al faraón una y otra vez y le dijo: deja ir a mi pueblo."},{"head":"salen de Egipto a toda prisa","text":"Después de muchos días difíciles, el faraón por fin los dejó ir. Salieron tan deprisa que la masa del pan no tuvo tiempo de levar. La hornearon plana, y ese pan plano se llama matzá."},{"head":"la mesa del séder","text":"Cada primavera, muchas familias judías recuerdan esta historia en Pésaj con una comida especial llamada séder. En la mesa hay matzá, un plato de agua con sal, hierbas amargas y jaroset dulce, hecho de frutas y nueces."},{"head":"una noche para preguntar","text":"En el séder, se invita a los niños a hacer preguntas. A menudo el más pequeño pregunta por qué esta noche es diferente de todas las demás noches. Entonces todos cuentan la historia juntos, como si ellos mismos hubieran salido de Egipto."}],"game":{"items":[["el pueblo salió de Egipto tan deprisa que el pan no tuvo tiempo de levar.",true,"sí: lo hornearon plano, y eso es la matzá."],["en el séder, a los niños se les pide que se queden callados y no pregunten nada.",false,"no está: se invita a los niños a hacer preguntas."],["en la mesa del séder hay hierbas amargas y jaroset dulce.",true,"sí: junto con la matzá y un plato de agua con sal."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un niño que se prepara para hacer una gran pregunta. exhala despacio, como una familia entera que cuenta la historia junta. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a preguntar sobre algo que me da curiosidad","grownups":{"source":"Éxodo 1–15 en la Torá cuenta la historia; el pan sin levar está en Éxodo 12:39. El séder y sus preguntas vienen de la Mishná (Pesajim 10) y de la Hagadá, el libro que las familias leen en la mesa. Las costumbres del séder varían entre familias y comunidades.","ask":"¿qué pregunta te gustaría hacer en nuestra mesa?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Exodus 1:14; 5:1; 12:39","work":"Torah (Exodus)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Pesachim 10:4–5","work":"Mishnah","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the Passover Haggadah","work":"Haggadah","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":9,"key":"miriam-song","game":"match","en":{"title":"Miriam’s song by the sea","word":"timbrel","means":"a small hand drum, like a tambourine (תוף, tof)","story":[{"head":"the sea in the way","text":"After the Hebrew people left Egypt, they walked until they came to the edge of the sea. Pharaoh had changed his mind, and his chariots were coming behind them. The people were worried. Where could they go?"},{"head":"a dry path through the water","text":"Moses held out his hand over the sea. All night a strong wind blew, and the waters parted. The people walked through on dry ground, with a wall of water on each side."},{"head":"a timbrel for Miriam","text":"On the far shore, everyone was safe. Then Miriam, the sister of Aaron and Moses, picked up her timbrel, a little hand drum. All the women followed her with their timbrels, dancing."},{"head":"everyone sings and dances","text":"Miriam sang, and the people sang a song of thanks to God. This song, the Song of the Sea, is still sung in synagogues today, and some families remember Miriam at the seder with a cup of water."}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes together? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the strong wind","parted the waters"],["Miriam","picked up her timbrel"],["the women","followed her, dancing"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a soft wind blowing over the sea. breathe out slowly, like the first beat of Miriam’s little drum. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will sing or hum something happy","grownups":{"source":"Exodus 14:21–15:21 in the Torah. Moses and the people sing in Exodus 15:1–18; Miriam leads the women with timbrels in 15:20–21. Miriam's cup at the seder is a newer custom that some families keep.","ask":"what is a song you sing when you feel thankful or happy?"}},"es":{"title":"la canción de Miriam junto al mar","word":"pandero","means":"un tambor pequeño de mano, como una pandereta (תוף, tof)","story":[{"head":"el mar en el camino","text":"Después de salir de Egipto, el pueblo hebreo caminó hasta llegar a la orilla del mar. El faraón había cambiado de idea, y sus carros venían detrás de ellos. La gente estaba preocupada. ¿Adónde podían ir?"},{"head":"un camino seco entre las aguas","text":"Moisés extendió la mano sobre el mar. Toda la noche sopló un viento fuerte, y las aguas se abrieron. El pueblo pasó por tierra seca, con una pared de agua a cada lado."},{"head":"el pandero de Miriam","text":"En la otra orilla, todos estaban a salvo. Entonces Miriam, la hermana de Aarón y de Moisés, tomó su pandero, un tamborcito de mano. Todas las mujeres la siguieron con sus panderos, bailando."},{"head":"todos cantan y bailan","text":"Miriam cantó, y el pueblo cantó una canción de gracias a Dios. Esta canción, el Canto del Mar, todavía se canta hoy en las sinagogas, y algunas familias recuerdan a Miriam en el séder con una copa de agua."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el viento fuerte","abrió las aguas"],["Miriam","tomó su pandero"],["las mujeres","la siguieron bailando"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un viento suave que sopla sobre el mar. exhala despacio, como el primer golpe del tamborcito de Miriam. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a cantar o tararear algo alegre","grownups":{"source":"Éxodo 14:21–15:21 en la Torá. Moisés y el pueblo cantan en Éxodo 15:1–18; Miriam guía a las mujeres con panderos en 15:20–21. La copa de Miriam en el séder es una costumbre más reciente que algunas familias guardan.","ask":"¿qué canción cantas cuando te sientes agradecido o feliz?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Exodus 14:21–15:21","work":"Torah (Exodus)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"shabbat-candles","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the Shabbat candles","word":"Shabbat","means":"the day of rest, from Friday sunset to Saturday night (שבת)","story":[{"head":"getting ready on Friday","text":"On Friday afternoon, many Jewish homes are busy. Someone sets the table, someone brings home a soft, braided challah bread, and everyone hurries to finish before the sun goes down."},{"head":"candles lit before sunset","text":"Just before sunset, someone in the family lights the Shabbat candles. Many families light two, and some light one more for each child. The little flames make the whole room glow."},{"head":"hands over the eyes, a blessing","text":"The person who lit them circles their hands around the flames, covers their eyes, and says a blessing. When they open their eyes, the family often says Shabbat shalom: have a peaceful Shabbat!"},{"head":"a day of rest begins","text":"The Torah says the seventh day is for rest. So for many families, Shabbat is a time with no rush, from Friday sunset until the stars come out on Saturday night: meals, songs, stories and naps."}],"game":{"items":[["the Shabbat candles are lit just before sunset on Friday.",true,"yes: just before the sun goes down."],["the family blows out the candles right after the blessing.",false,"not in it: the little flames keep glowing as Shabbat begins."],["Shabbat lasts from Friday sunset until the stars come out on Saturday night.",true,"yes: a time for meals, songs, stories and naps."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a little flame waking up on a candle. breathe out slowly, like soft light filling a quiet room. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will slow down and wish someone a peaceful day","grownups":{"source":"The command to remember the seventh day and rest is in Exodus 20:8–11 and Deuteronomy 5:12–15. Lighting candles for Shabbat is an old rabbinic practice discussed in the Talmud (Shabbat 23b and 25b). Customs differ between families.","ask":"what would make our Friday evening feel special and calm?"}},"es":{"title":"las velas de Shabat","word":"Shabat","means":"el día de descanso, del viernes al atardecer al sábado de noche (שבת)","story":[{"head":"preparativos del viernes","text":"El viernes por la tarde, muchas casas judías están llenas de movimiento. Alguien pone la mesa, alguien trae a casa una jalá, un pan suave y trenzado, y todos se apuran para terminar antes de que se ponga el sol."},{"head":"velas encendidas antes del atardecer","text":"Justo antes del atardecer, alguien de la familia enciende las velas de Shabat. Muchas familias encienden dos, y algunas encienden una más por cada hijo. Las llamitas hacen brillar todo el cuarto."},{"head":"manos sobre los ojos, una bendición","text":"Quien las encendió hace círculos con las manos alrededor de las llamas, se cubre los ojos y dice una bendición. Al abrir los ojos, la familia a menudo dice Shabat shalom: ¡que tengas un Shabat en paz!"},{"head":"comienza un día de descanso","text":"La Torá dice que el séptimo día es para descansar. Por eso, para muchas familias, el Shabat es un tiempo sin prisa, desde el viernes al atardecer hasta que salen las estrellas el sábado: comidas, canciones, cuentos y siestas."}],"game":{"items":[["las velas de Shabat se encienden justo antes del atardecer del viernes.",true,"sí: justo antes de que se ponga el sol."],["la familia apaga las velas justo después de la bendición.",false,"no está: las llamitas siguen brillando mientras comienza el Shabat."],["el Shabat dura desde el viernes al atardecer hasta que salen las estrellas el sábado.",true,"sí: un tiempo para comidas, canciones, cuentos y siestas."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una llamita que despierta en una vela. exhala despacio, como una luz suave que llena un cuarto tranquilo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy iré despacio y le desearé a alguien un día en paz","grownups":{"source":"El mandato de recordar el séptimo día y descansar está en Éxodo 20:8–11 y Deuteronomio 5:12–15. Encender velas para el Shabat es una antigua práctica rabínica que se trata en el Talmud (Shabat 23b y 25b). Las costumbres varían entre familias.","ask":"¿qué haría que nuestro viernes en la noche se sienta especial y tranquilo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Exodus 20:8–11; Deuteronomy 5:12–15","work":"Torah","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Shabbat 23b; 25b","work":"Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"ruth-naomi","game":"match","en":{"title":"Ruth stays with Naomi","word":"loyal","means":"sticking with someone in good times and in hard times","story":[{"head":"Naomi goes home to Bethlehem","text":"Naomi lived far from her home in Bethlehem. Her husband and her two sons had died, and she felt sad and alone. So she decided to go back home to Bethlehem."},{"head":"where you go, I will go","text":"Her daughter-in-law Ruth loved her very much. Naomi told her to stay in her own land, but Ruth said: where you go, I will go, and your people will be my people. So they walked to Bethlehem together."},{"head":"gathering grain in Boaz’s field","text":"It was barley harvest. Ruth went out to gather the grain the reapers left behind, so she and Naomi would have food. The field belonged to Boaz, a kind man, who told his workers to leave extra grain for her."},{"head":"a baby for Naomi to hold","text":"Boaz saw how loyal Ruth was to Naomi. In time, Ruth and Boaz married and had a baby boy named Obed. Naomi held the baby close, and she was not alone anymore. The story says Obed became King David's grandfather."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Ruth","gathered the leftover grain"],["Boaz","left extra grain for her"],["Naomi","held baby Obed close"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a warm wind over a field of barley. breathe out slowly, like two friends walking side by side. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will stick by a friend who needs me","grownups":{"source":"The Book of Ruth, in the Writings, the third part of the Tanakh. Ruth's promise to Naomi is Ruth 1:16–17; Boaz's kindness is in chapter 2; Obed and David are in 4:13–17. Many Jewish communities read Ruth on the festival of Shavuot.","ask":"who is someone you would stick with, no matter what?"}},"es":{"title":"Rut se queda con Noemí","word":"leal","means":"quedarse con alguien en los tiempos buenos y en los difíciles","story":[{"head":"Noemí vuelve a Belén","text":"Noemí vivía lejos de su hogar en Belén. Su esposo y sus dos hijos habían muerto, y ella se sentía triste y sola. Así que decidió volver a casa, a Belén."},{"head":"adonde tú vayas, iré yo","text":"Su nuera Rut la quería mucho. Noemí le dijo que se quedara en su propia tierra, pero Rut le dijo: adonde tú vayas, iré yo, y tu pueblo será mi pueblo. Así que caminaron juntas hasta Belén."},{"head":"recogiendo grano en el campo de Booz","text":"Era la cosecha de la cebada. Rut salió a recoger el grano que los segadores dejaban atrás, para que ella y Noemí tuvieran comida. El campo era de Booz, un hombre bondadoso, que les dijo a sus trabajadores que dejaran grano de más para ella."},{"head":"un bebé en los brazos de Noemí","text":"Booz vio lo leal que era Rut con Noemí. Con el tiempo, Rut y Booz se casaron y tuvieron un niño llamado Obed. Noemí abrazó al bebé, y ya no estaba sola. La historia dice que Obed fue el abuelo del rey David."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Rut","recogía el grano que quedaba"],["Booz","dejaba grano de más para ella"],["Noemí","abrazó al bebé Obed"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un viento tibio sobre un campo de cebada. exhala despacio, como dos amigas que caminan lado a lado. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a acompañar a un amigo que me necesite","grownups":{"source":"El libro de Rut, en los Escritos, la tercera parte del Tanaj. La promesa de Rut a Noemí está en Rut 1:16–17; la bondad de Booz, en el capítulo 2; Obed y David, en 4:13–17. Muchas comunidades judías leen Rut en la fiesta de Shavuot.","ask":"¿con quién te quedarías, pase lo que pase?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Ruth 1–4","work":"Tanakh (Writings)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"david-shepherd-harp","game":"truth","en":{"title":"David the shepherd and his harp","word":"psalm","means":"a song or poem to God; the Psalms are Tehillim (תהלים)","story":[{"head":"the youngest, out with the sheep","text":"David was the youngest of eight brothers in Bethlehem. While his older brothers were busy at home, David was out in the fields, taking care of his father's sheep."},{"head":"the prophet Samuel looks at the heart","text":"One day the prophet Samuel came to visit. He looked at David's older brothers, but God told him not to look at how tall or handsome someone is. God looks at the heart. So Samuel sent for David, the youngest."},{"head":"music for a troubled king","text":"King Saul was often troubled and low. His servants looked for someone who played the harp well, and they found David. Whenever the king felt heavy inside, David played, and Saul felt better."},{"head":"the songs called psalms","text":"David grew up to become king. Jewish tradition says he wrote many of the Psalms, songs of thanks, sadness and joy that are still sung and prayed today. One psalm calls God a shepherd who cares for his flock."}],"game":{"items":[["David took care of his father’s sheep.",true,"yes: he was out in the fields with the sheep."],["David played the drums for King Saul.",false,"not in it: David played the harp, and Saul felt better."],["Samuel was told to look at a person’s heart.",true,"yes: not at how tall or handsome someone is."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a sheep resting in soft green grass. breathe out slowly, like the gentle notes of David’s harp. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will look at someone’s heart, not their looks","grownups":{"source":"1 Samuel 16 in the Prophets: Samuel's visit is 16:1–13, and David plays for Saul in 16:14–23. David is the youngest of eight sons in 17:12. The instrument, the kinnor, is translated harp or lyre. Psalm 23 calls God a shepherd.","ask":"what music helps you feel better when you are sad?"}},"es":{"title":"David el pastor y su arpa","word":"salmo","means":"un canto o poema a Dios; los Salmos son Tehilim (תהלים)","story":[{"head":"el menor, con las ovejas","text":"David era el menor de ocho hermanos en Belén. Mientras sus hermanos mayores estaban ocupados en casa, David estaba en el campo, cuidando las ovejas de su padre."},{"head":"el profeta Samuel mira el corazón","text":"Un día el profeta Samuel vino de visita. Miró a los hermanos mayores de David, pero Dios le dijo que no mirara lo alto o lo guapo que es alguien. Dios mira el corazón. Así que Samuel mandó llamar a David, el menor."},{"head":"música para un rey preocupado","text":"El rey Saúl a menudo estaba preocupado y decaído. Sus sirvientes buscaron a alguien que tocara bien el arpa, y encontraron a David. Cada vez que el rey se sentía pesado por dentro, David tocaba, y Saúl se sentía mejor."},{"head":"los cantos llamados salmos","text":"David creció y llegó a ser rey. La tradición judía dice que él escribió muchos de los Salmos, cantos de gracias, de tristeza y de alegría que todavía se cantan y se rezan hoy. Un salmo llama a Dios un pastor que cuida a su rebaño."}],"game":{"items":[["David cuidaba las ovejas de su padre.",true,"sí: estaba en el campo con las ovejas."],["David tocaba el tambor para el rey Saúl.",false,"no está: David tocaba el arpa, y Saúl se sentía mejor."],["a Samuel le dijeron que mirara el corazón de las personas.",true,"sí: no lo alto o lo guapo que es alguien."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una oveja que descansa en la hierba verde y suave. exhala despacio, como las notas tranquilas del arpa de David. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a mirar el corazón de alguien, no su apariencia","grownups":{"source":"1 Samuel 16 en los Profetas: la visita de Samuel está en 16:1–13, y David toca para Saúl en 16:14–23. David es el menor de ocho hijos en 17:12. El instrumento, el kinor, se traduce como arpa o lira. El Salmo 23 llama a Dios un pastor.","ask":"¿qué música te ayuda a sentirte mejor cuando estás triste?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"1 Samuel 16:1–23; 17:12","work":"Tanakh (Prophets)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Psalm 23","work":"Tanakh (Writings)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"jonah-big-fish","game":"match","en":{"title":"Jonah and the big fish","word":"teshuvah","means":"turning back: saying sorry and changing your ways (תשובה)","story":[{"head":"a ship sailing the other way","text":"God asked Jonah to go to the big city of Nineveh and tell the people to change their ways. But Jonah did not want to go. He got on a ship sailing the opposite way."},{"head":"a storm on the sea","text":"Out at sea, a great storm blew, and the sailors were afraid. Jonah knew the storm was because of him, and told them to put him into the sea. When they did, the sea became calm."},{"head":"three days inside a big fish","text":"God sent a big fish to swallow Jonah. For three days and three nights, Jonah sat inside the fish, and there he prayed. Then the fish spat him out, safe, onto dry land."},{"head":"a whole city says sorry","text":"This time Jonah went to Nineveh. The people listened, said sorry and changed their ways, even the king. God forgave them. At the end, God asks Jonah: shouldn’t I care about this great city, its people, and its animals too?"}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes together? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the ship","sailed the opposite way"],["the big fish","swallowed Jonah"],["Nineveh","changed its ways"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a stormy sea growing calm. breathe out slowly, like Jonah stepping onto dry, safe sand. four slow breaths.","carry":"if I mess up today, I can say sorry and start again","grownups":{"source":"The Book of Jonah, among the Prophets. The fish is in Jonah 2:1 and 2:11 (1:17 and 2:10 in many English Bibles); God's last question is 4:11. Many Jewish communities read the whole book on the afternoon of Yom Kippur.","ask":"why do you think God cared about the people of Nineveh, and even their animals?"}},"es":{"title":"Jonás y el gran pez","word":"teshuvá","means":"volver: pedir perdón y cambiar tu manera de actuar (תשובה)","story":[{"head":"un barco hacia el otro lado","text":"Dios le pidió a Jonás que fuera a la gran ciudad de Nínive y le dijera a la gente que cambiara su manera de actuar. Pero Jonás no quería ir. Se subió a un barco que iba en dirección contraria."},{"head":"una tormenta en el mar","text":"En alta mar sopló una gran tormenta, y los marineros tenían miedo. Jonás sabía que la tormenta era por él, y les dijo que lo pusieran en el mar. Cuando lo hicieron, el mar se calmó."},{"head":"tres días dentro de un gran pez","text":"Dios envió un gran pez que se tragó a Jonás. Durante tres días y tres noches, Jonás estuvo dentro del pez, y allí rezó. Entonces el pez lo dejó, sano y salvo, en tierra seca."},{"head":"una ciudad entera pide perdón","text":"Esta vez Jonás fue a Nínive. La gente escuchó, pidió perdón y cambió su manera de actuar, hasta el rey. Dios los perdonó. Al final, Dios le pregunta a Jonás: ¿no debería importarme esta gran ciudad, su gente y también sus animales?"}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el barco","iba en dirección contraria"],["el gran pez","se tragó a Jonás"],["Nínive","cambió su manera de actuar"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un mar tormentoso que se calma. exhala despacio, como Jonás que pisa la arena seca y segura. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"si hoy me equivoco, puedo pedir perdón y empezar de nuevo","grownups":{"source":"El libro de Jonás, entre los Profetas. El pez está en Jonás 2:1 y 2:11 (1:17 y 2:10 en muchas biblias en español); la última pregunta de Dios está en 4:11. Muchas comunidades judías leen el libro entero en la tarde de Yom Kipur.","ask":"¿por qué crees que a Dios le importaba la gente de Nínive, y hasta sus animales?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jonah 1–4","work":"Tanakh (Prophets)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"esther-purim","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Queen Esther is brave","word":"Purim","means":"“lots,” as in drawing lots; the festival of Esther (פורים)","story":[{"head":"a crown for Esther","text":"In the land of Persia lived a young Jewish woman named Esther. She was raised by her cousin Mordecai. King Ahasuerus chose her to be his queen, but Esther did not tell anyone at the palace that she was Jewish."},{"head":"an angry minister’s plan","text":"The king's chief minister, Haman, became angry because Mordecai would not bow down to him. Haman made a plan to harm all the Jewish people in the land. Mordecai asked Esther to help."},{"head":"a brave visit to the king","text":"Nobody was allowed to go to the king without being called. Esther was afraid, but she asked her people to fast for her, and then she went in. The king held out his golden scepter and welcomed her."},{"head":"a happy Purim","text":"Esther told the king the truth about Haman's plan, and her people were saved. Today, at Purim, many Jewish families hear the story read aloud, wear costumes, make noise at Haman's name, and send treats to friends and gifts to people in need."}],"game":{"items":[["Esther was raised by her cousin Mordecai.",true,"yes: Mordecai raised her."],["Esther hid in the palace and never spoke to the king.",false,"not in it: she bravely went to the king and told him the truth."],["at Purim, many families wear costumes and send treats.",true,"yes: and they send gifts to people in need."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Esther gathering her courage. breathe out slowly, like the king holding out his golden scepter. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will be brave and tell the truth","grownups":{"source":"The Book of Esther, in the Writings. Purim's customs come from Esther 9:20–22: feasting, sending food to friends and gifts to the poor; the name comes from the lot (pur) in 9:24–26. Making noise at Haman's name during the reading is a later custom.","ask":"when have you needed to be brave to speak up?"}},"es":{"title":"la reina Ester es valiente","word":"Purim","means":"“suertes”, como al echar suertes; la fiesta de Ester (פורים)","story":[{"head":"una corona para Ester","text":"En la tierra de Persia vivía una joven judía llamada Ester. La había criado su primo Mardoqueo. El rey Asuero la eligió para ser su reina, pero Ester no le contó a nadie en el palacio que era judía."},{"head":"el plan de un ministro enojado","text":"El ministro principal del rey, Amán, se enojó porque Mardoqueo no se inclinaba ante él. Amán hizo un plan para hacerle daño a todo el pueblo judío del país. Mardoqueo le pidió ayuda a Ester."},{"head":"una visita valiente al rey","text":"Nadie podía presentarse ante el rey sin ser llamado. Ester tenía miedo, pero le pidió a su pueblo que ayunara por ella, y luego entró. El rey le extendió su cetro de oro y la recibió."},{"head":"un Purim feliz","text":"Ester le contó al rey la verdad sobre el plan de Amán, y su pueblo se salvó. Hoy, en Purim, muchas familias judías escuchan la historia en voz alta, se disfrazan, hacen ruido al oír el nombre de Amán, y mandan dulces a sus amigos y regalos a quienes lo necesitan."}],"game":{"items":[["a Ester la crio su primo Mardoqueo.",true,"sí: Mardoqueo la crio."],["Ester se escondió en el palacio y nunca le habló al rey.",false,"no está: fue con valentía a ver al rey y le dijo la verdad."],["en Purim, muchas familias se disfrazan y mandan dulces.",true,"sí: y mandan regalos a quienes lo necesitan."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Ester que junta su valor. exhala despacio, como el rey que extiende su cetro de oro. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a ser valiente y decir la verdad","grownups":{"source":"El libro de Ester, en los Escritos. Las costumbres de Purim vienen de Ester 9:20–22: banquetes, enviar comida a los amigos y regalos a los pobres; el nombre viene de la suerte (pur) en 9:24–26. Hacer ruido al oír el nombre de Amán durante la lectura es una costumbre posterior.","ask":"¿cuándo tuviste que ser valiente para hablar?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Esther 2:7–10; 3:1–6; 4:11–16; 5:1–2; 7:1–6; 9:20–26","work":"Tanakh (Writings)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":15,"key":"hanukkah-oil","game":"match","en":{"title":"Hanukkah and the little jar of oil","word":"Hanukkah","means":"“dedication”: giving the Temple back to its holy work (חנוכה)","story":[{"head":"the Temple taken over","text":"Long ago, the Temple in Jerusalem had been taken over by a foreign king, and it was no longer used for worshipping God. After a long, hard struggle, a small group called the Maccabees won it back."},{"head":"cleaning and fixing the Temple","text":"The Temple was dusty and broken. The people cleaned it, fixed it, and made it ready again. Hanukkah means dedication: they were giving the Temple back to its holy work."},{"head":"one little jar of oil","text":"They wanted to light the menorah, the Temple's golden lamp. But the Talmud says they found only one little jar of pure oil, sealed and untouched, with just enough to burn for one day."},{"head":"eight nights of light","text":"The Talmud says the oil burned for eight days. Today, many Jewish families light a Hanukkah menorah for eight nights, adding one more light each night, and set it where people outside can see it shine."}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes together? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the people","cleaned and fixed the Temple"],["the little jar","enough oil for one day"],["the oil","burned for eight days"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like one small flame. breathe out slowly, like a whole window full of Hanukkah lights. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will do one small kind thing, like one little light","grownups":{"source":"The little jar of oil is told in the Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 21b, which also says to place the lights where they can be seen outside. The Books of the Maccabees, not part of the Jewish Bible, tell the history of the Temple being won back and rededicated.","ask":"if we put a light in our window, what would you want it to say to people passing by?"}},"es":{"title":"Janucá y la pequeña jarra de aceite","word":"Janucá","means":"“dedicación”: devolver el Templo a su obra sagrada (חנוכה)","story":[{"head":"el Templo ocupado","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, un rey extranjero había ocupado el Templo de Jerusalén, y ya no se usaba para adorar a Dios. Después de una lucha larga y difícil, un grupo pequeño llamado los macabeos lo recuperó."},{"head":"limpiar y arreglar el Templo","text":"El Templo estaba lleno de polvo y roto. La gente lo limpió, lo arregló y lo dejó listo otra vez. Janucá significa dedicación: le estaban devolviendo al Templo su obra sagrada."},{"head":"una pequeña jarra de aceite","text":"Querían encender la menorá, la lámpara de oro del Templo. Pero el Talmud dice que encontraron solo una pequeña jarra de aceite puro, sellada y sin tocar, con lo justo para arder un día."},{"head":"ocho noches de luz","text":"El Talmud dice que el aceite ardió durante ocho días. Hoy, muchas familias judías encienden una menorá de Janucá durante ocho noches, con una luz más cada noche, y la ponen donde la gente de afuera pueda verla brillar."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la gente","limpió y arregló el Templo"],["la pequeña jarra","aceite para un solo día"],["el aceite","ardió durante ocho días"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una sola llamita. exhala despacio, como una ventana entera llena de luces de Janucá. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a hacer algo pequeño y amable, como una lucecita","grownups":{"source":"La pequeña jarra de aceite se cuenta en el Talmud de Babilonia, Shabat 21b, que también dice que las luces se pongan donde se vean desde afuera. Los libros de los Macabeos, que no forman parte de la Biblia judía, cuentan la historia de cómo el Templo fue recuperado y dedicado de nuevo.","ask":"si pusiéramos una luz en nuestra ventana, ¿qué te gustaría que les dijera a quienes pasan?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Shabbat 21b","work":"Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"1 Maccabees 4:36–59","work":"Books of the Maccabees","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"hillel-one-foot","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Hillel and the question on one foot","word":"Torah","means":"teaching: the Five Books of Moses, and all Jewish learning (תורה)","story":[{"head":"a visitor with a strange challenge","text":"Long ago in Jerusalem lived two teachers, Hillel and Shammai. One day a man who was not Jewish came with a strange challenge: teach me the whole Torah while I stand on one foot!"},{"head":"first, a visit to Shammai","text":"First he went to Shammai. Shammai was a builder, and he had a measuring stick in his hand. He thought the question was not serious, and he sent the man away with the stick."},{"head":"a kind answer from Hillel","text":"Then the man went to Hillel. Hillel was patient. He said: what is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbor. That is the whole Torah. The rest explains it."},{"head":"now go and learn","text":"Then Hillel added: now go and learn it. The man did go and learn, and the Talmud says he became Jewish. People still repeat Hillel’s gentle answer today."}],"game":{"items":[["the man asked to learn the whole Torah while standing on one foot.",true,"yes: that was his strange challenge."],["Hillel told the man to come back next year.",false,"not in it: Hillel answered him right away, kindly and patiently."],["Hillel said: what is hateful to you, do not do to your neighbor.",true,"yes: and then he said, now go and learn."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like someone balancing on one foot. breathe out slowly, like Hillel giving a gentle, patient answer. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I won’t do to others what I wouldn’t like","grownups":{"source":"The Babylonian Talmud, Shabbat 31a. The same page tells other stories of Hillel's patience beside Shammai's strictness. The Talmud honors both teachers; Jewish law generally follows the school of Hillel.","ask":"can you say the most important rule you know while standing on one foot?"}},"es":{"title":"Hilel y la pregunta en un pie","word":"Torá","means":"enseñanza: los Cinco Libros de Moisés, y todo el estudio judío (תורה)","story":[{"head":"un visitante con un reto extraño","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en Jerusalén, vivían dos maestros, Hilel y Shamai. Un día, un hombre que no era judío llegó con un reto extraño: ¡enséñame toda la Torá mientras me paro en un solo pie!"},{"head":"primero, una visita a Shamai","text":"Primero fue a ver a Shamai. Shamai era constructor, y tenía en la mano una vara de medir. Pensó que la pregunta no era en serio, y despidió al hombre con la vara."},{"head":"una respuesta amable de Hilel","text":"Entonces el hombre fue a ver a Hilel. Hilel era paciente. Le dijo: lo que no te gusta que te hagan, no se lo hagas a tu prójimo. Esa es toda la Torá. Lo demás lo explica."},{"head":"ahora ve y aprende","text":"Hilel añadió: ahora ve y apréndelo. El hombre fue y aprendió, y el Talmud dice que se hizo judío. La gente todavía repite hoy la respuesta amable de Hilel."}],"game":{"items":[["el hombre pidió aprender toda la Torá parado en un solo pie.",true,"sí: ese era su reto extraño."],["Hilel le dijo al hombre que volviera el año siguiente.",false,"no está: Hilel le respondió enseguida, con amabilidad y paciencia."],["Hilel dijo: lo que no te gusta que te hagan, no se lo hagas a tu prójimo.",true,"sí: y luego dijo, ahora ve y aprende."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como alguien que se equilibra en un pie. exhala despacio, como Hilel que da una respuesta amable y paciente. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy no voy a hacerle a otro lo que no me gustaría","grownups":{"source":"El Talmud de Babilonia, Shabat 31a. La misma página cuenta otras historias de la paciencia de Hilel junto a la severidad de Shamai. El Talmud honra a los dos maestros; la ley judía en general sigue a la escuela de Hilel.","ask":"¿puedes decir la regla más importante que conoces parado en un pie?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Shabbat 31a","work":"Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"honi-carob-tree","game":"match","en":{"title":"Honi and the carob tree","word":"carob","means":"a tree with long, sweet brown pods you can eat (חרוב)","story":[{"head":"a man planting a carob tree","text":"One day Honi was walking along a road and saw a man planting a carob tree. Honi asked him: how long until this tree gives fruit? The man said: seventy years."},{"head":"planting for the grandchildren","text":"Honi asked: do you think you will be here in seventy years to eat it? The man said: when I came into the world, I found carob trees. My grandparents planted them for me, so I am planting for my grandchildren."},{"head":"a nap of seventy years","text":"Honi sat down to eat some bread, and he fell asleep. Rocks grew up around him like a little cave, hiding him from sight, and he slept and slept, for seventy years!"},{"head":"a tree full of carobs","text":"When Honi woke up, he saw a man picking carobs from the tree. Honi asked: are you the one who planted it? The man said: no, I am his grandson. The tree had grown, and its fruit was for someone new."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the man","planted a carob tree"],["Honi","slept for seventy years"],["the grandson","picked the carobs"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a little seed going into the ground. breathe out slowly, like a tree growing tall over many years. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will do something good that someone else will enjoy later","grownups":{"source":"The Babylonian Talmud, Taanit 23a, tells this story of Honi, called the Circle-Drawer. In the Talmud the story goes on to a sadder ending, left out here. Some families tell it on Tu BiShvat, the new year for trees.","ask":"what could we start today that someone will enjoy after us?"}},"es":{"title":"Honi y el algarrobo","word":"algarrobo","means":"un árbol con vainas largas, dulces y marrones que se comen (חרוב)","story":[{"head":"un hombre planta un algarrobo","text":"Un día Honi iba caminando por un camino y vio a un hombre plantando un algarrobo. Honi le preguntó: ¿cuánto falta para que este árbol dé fruto? El hombre dijo: setenta años."},{"head":"plantar para los nietos","text":"Honi le preguntó: ¿crees que estarás aquí en setenta años para comerlo? El hombre dijo: cuando llegué al mundo, encontré algarrobos. Mis abuelos los plantaron para mí, así que yo planto para mis nietos."},{"head":"una siesta de setenta años","text":"Honi se sentó a comer un poco de pan, y se quedó dormido. Unas rocas crecieron a su alrededor como una cuevita, escondiéndolo de la vista, y durmió y durmió, ¡setenta años!"},{"head":"un árbol lleno de algarrobas","text":"Cuando Honi despertó, vio a un hombre recogiendo algarrobas del árbol. Honi le preguntó: ¿eres tú quien lo plantó? El hombre dijo: no, soy su nieto. El árbol había crecido, y su fruto era para alguien nuevo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el hombre","plantó un algarrobo"],["Honi","durmió setenta años"],["el nieto","recogió las algarrobas"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una semillita que entra en la tierra. exhala despacio, como un árbol que crece alto durante muchos años. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a hacer algo bueno que otra persona disfrutará después","grownups":{"source":"El Talmud de Babilonia, Taanit 23a, cuenta esta historia de Honi, llamado el que dibujaba círculos. En el Talmud la historia sigue hasta un final más triste, que aquí se deja fuera. Algunas familias la cuentan en Tu Bishvat, el año nuevo de los árboles.","ask":"¿qué podríamos empezar hoy que alguien disfrute después de nosotros?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Taanit 23a","work":"Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"rosh-hashanah-shofar","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the shofar on Rosh Hashanah","word":"shofar","means":"a ram’s horn, blown like a trumpet (שופר)","story":[{"head":"a new year in the fall","text":"In the fall, many Jewish families celebrate Rosh Hashanah, the Jewish New Year. The Torah calls it a day of blowing the horn. People gather in synagogue, and families share festive meals together."},{"head":"the sound of the shofar","text":"The horn is called a shofar, and it is often made from a ram's horn. In synagogue someone blows it, and it makes long notes, broken notes, and short quick notes, like a wake-up call."},{"head":"apples dipped in honey","text":"At home, many families dip apples in honey and wish each other a good and sweet new year. Some eat round challah bread, round like a year that goes around and around."},{"head":"starting fresh","text":"Rosh Hashanah begins ten days of thinking about the past year: saying sorry, making things right, and choosing how to grow. The shofar's sound is like a call to wake up and start fresh."}],"game":{"items":[["the shofar is often made from a ram’s horn.",true,"yes: and it makes long, broken and short notes."],["on Rosh Hashanah, families dip apples in chocolate.",false,"not in it: many families dip apples in honey, for a sweet new year."],["Rosh Hashanah begins ten days of thinking about the past year.",true,"yes: saying sorry, making things right, and starting fresh."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like someone filling up with air to blow the shofar. breathe out slowly, like one long, steady note. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will wish someone a sweet day","grownups":{"source":"The Torah names the day in Leviticus 23:23–25 and Numbers 29:1, a day of blowing the horn. The name Rosh Hashanah and the rules for the shofar come from the Mishnah, tractate Rosh Hashanah. Apples and honey are a family custom.","ask":"what is one thing you would like to do better in the new year?"}},"es":{"title":"el shofar en Rosh Hashaná","word":"shofar","means":"un cuerno de carnero que se toca como una trompeta (שופר)","story":[{"head":"un año nuevo en el otoño","text":"En el otoño, muchas familias judías celebran Rosh Hashaná, el Año Nuevo judío. La Torá lo llama un día de tocar el cuerno. La gente se reúne en la sinagoga, y las familias comparten comidas de fiesta."},{"head":"el sonido del shofar","text":"El cuerno se llama shofar, y a menudo está hecho de un cuerno de carnero. En la sinagoga alguien lo toca, y hace notas largas, notas cortadas y notas cortas y rápidas, como una llamada para despertar."},{"head":"manzanas con miel","text":"En casa, muchas familias mojan manzanas en miel y se desean un año nuevo bueno y dulce. Algunas comen jalá redonda, redonda como un año que da vueltas y vueltas."},{"head":"empezar de nuevo","text":"Rosh Hashaná comienza diez días para pensar en el año que pasó: pedir perdón, arreglar las cosas y elegir cómo crecer. El sonido del shofar es como una llamada a despertar y empezar de nuevo."}],"game":{"items":[["el shofar a menudo está hecho de un cuerno de carnero.",true,"sí: y hace notas largas, cortadas y cortas."],["en Rosh Hashaná, las familias mojan manzanas en chocolate.",false,"no está: muchas familias mojan manzanas en miel, para un año dulce."],["Rosh Hashaná comienza diez días para pensar en el año que pasó.",true,"sí: pedir perdón, arreglar las cosas y empezar de nuevo."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como alguien que se llena de aire para tocar el shofar. exhala despacio, como una nota larga y firme. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a desearle a alguien un día dulce","grownups":{"source":"La Torá nombra este día en Levítico 23:23–25 y Números 29:1, un día de tocar el cuerno. El nombre Rosh Hashaná y las reglas del shofar vienen de la Mishná, tratado Rosh Hashaná. Las manzanas con miel son una costumbre familiar.","ask":"¿qué te gustaría hacer mejor en el año nuevo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Leviticus 23:23–25; Numbers 29:1","work":"Torah","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Rosh Hashanah 3–4","work":"Mishnah","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"sukkot-sukkah","game":"match","en":{"title":"a hut called a sukkah","word":"sukkah","means":"a little hut with a leafy roof, used at Sukkot (סוכה)","story":[{"head":"building a little hut","text":"A few days after Yom Kippur comes the happy festival of Sukkot. Many Jewish families build a sukkah, a little hut, in the yard, on a balcony, or next to the synagogue."},{"head":"a roof with the stars showing","text":"The roof is made of branches or leaves, with spaces left open so you can see the stars at night. Children hang paper chains and fruit inside. For a week, families eat their meals in the sukkah."},{"head":"remembering the desert journey","text":"The Torah says the people lived in huts while they traveled through the desert after leaving Egypt. Sitting in the sukkah helps families remember that journey, and say thank you for the harvest."},{"head":"the lulav, the etrog and guests","text":"Families also hold the four kinds: a palm branch with myrtle and willow, called the lulav, and a lemony fruit called the etrog. They wave them in every direction. Many families invite guests to share the sukkah."}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes together? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the sukkah","a little hut"],["the roof","branches, with stars showing"],["the etrog","a lemony fruit"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like cool night air in the sukkah. breathe out slowly, like stars peeking through the leafy roof. four slow breaths.","carry":"tonight I will look up and say thank you for something","grownups":{"source":"Leviticus 23:33–43 in the Torah: dwelling in booths for seven days (23:42–43) and taking the four kinds (23:40). How to build a sukkah is discussed in the Mishnah and Talmud, tractate Sukkah. Customs vary between families.","ask":"who would you invite to eat with us in a sukkah, and why?"}},"es":{"title":"una cabaña llamada sucá","word":"sucá","means":"una cabañita con techo de hojas, que se usa en Sucot (סוכה)","story":[{"head":"construir una cabañita","text":"Unos días después de Yom Kipur llega la alegre fiesta de Sucot. Muchas familias judías construyen una sucá, una cabañita, en el patio, en un balcón o junto a la sinagoga."},{"head":"un techo con las estrellas a la vista","text":"El techo se hace de ramas o de hojas, con espacios abiertos para ver las estrellas de noche. Los niños cuelgan cadenas de papel y frutas adentro. Durante una semana, las familias comen en la sucá."},{"head":"recordar el viaje por el desierto","text":"La Torá dice que el pueblo vivió en cabañas mientras viajaba por el desierto después de salir de Egipto. Sentarse en la sucá ayuda a las familias a recordar ese viaje, y a dar gracias por la cosecha."},{"head":"el lulav, el etrog y los invitados","text":"Las familias también sostienen las cuatro especies: una rama de palmera con mirto y sauce, llamada lulav, y una fruta parecida al limón llamada etrog. Las agitan en todas las direcciones. Muchas familias invitan a otros a compartir la sucá."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la sucá","una cabañita"],["el techo","ramas, con estrellas a la vista"],["el etrog","una fruta parecida al limón"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el aire fresco de la noche en la sucá. exhala despacio, como las estrellas que asoman por el techo de hojas. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"esta noche voy a mirar al cielo y dar gracias por algo","grownups":{"source":"Levítico 23:33–43 en la Torá: vivir en cabañas durante siete días (23:42–43) y tomar las cuatro especies (23:40). Cómo construir una sucá se trata en la Mishná y el Talmud, tratado Sucá. Las costumbres varían entre familias.","ask":"¿a quién invitarías a comer con nosotros en una sucá, y por qué?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Leviticus 23:33–43","work":"Torah (Leviticus)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sukkah","work":"Mishnah and Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"shema-bedtime","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the Shema, morning and night","word":"Shema","means":"“listen” or “hear”: the first word of the Shema (שמע)","story":[{"head":"words that begin with listen","text":"In the Torah, Moses tells the people some special words. They begin with the Hebrew word Shema, which means listen, or hear: “Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one.”"},{"head":"when you lie down and get up","text":"The next verses say to love God with all your heart, to teach these words to your children, and to say them when you lie down and when you get up. So many Jewish people say the Shema morning and night."},{"head":"a mezuzah on the doorpost","text":"The verses also say to write the words on the doorposts of your house. So many Jewish families put a mezuzah by their doors: a little case with a tiny scroll inside, with the Shema written on it by hand."},{"head":"the Shema at bedtime","text":"At bedtime, many Jewish children say the Shema with a parent before sleep. Some cover their eyes with a hand while they say the first line, to help them pay close attention."}],"game":{"items":[["Shema means listen, or hear.",true,"yes: it is the first word of the verse."],["the mezuzah holds a tiny scroll with a drawing of a lion.",false,"not in it: the tiny scroll has the Shema written on it by hand."],["the verses say to say these words when you lie down and when you get up.",true,"yes: so many people say the Shema morning and night."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like getting cozy in bed at night. breathe out slowly, like listening to a quiet room. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will really listen when someone talks to me","grownups":{"source":"Deuteronomy 6:4–9 in the Torah; the quoted line is Deuteronomy 6:4 in the 1917 Jewish Publication Society translation. The mezuzah scroll holds Deuteronomy 6:4–9 and 11:13–21. Saying the Shema at bedtime is discussed in the Talmud, Berakhot 4b–5a.","ask":"what helps you feel calm and safe at bedtime?"}},"es":{"title":"el Shemá, de mañana y de noche","word":"Shemá","means":"“escucha” u “oye”: la primera palabra del Shemá (שמע)","story":[{"head":"palabras que empiezan con escucha","text":"En la Torá, Moisés le dice al pueblo unas palabras especiales. Empiezan con la palabra hebrea Shemá, que significa escucha, u oye: escucha, Israel, el Señor es nuestro Dios, el Señor es uno."},{"head":"al acostarte y al levantarte","text":"Los versículos siguientes dicen que hay que amar a Dios con todo el corazón, enseñar estas palabras a los hijos, y decirlas al acostarse y al levantarse. Por eso muchos judíos dicen el Shemá por la mañana y por la noche."},{"head":"una mezuzá en el marco de la puerta","text":"Los versículos también dicen que hay que escribir estas palabras en los marcos de las puertas de la casa. Por eso muchas familias judías ponen una mezuzá junto a sus puertas: una cajita con un pequeño pergamino adentro, con el Shemá escrito a mano."},{"head":"el Shemá a la hora de dormir","text":"A la hora de dormir, muchos niños judíos dicen el Shemá con su mamá o su papá antes de dormirse. Algunos se cubren los ojos con la mano mientras dicen la primera línea, para prestar mucha atención."}],"game":{"items":[["Shemá significa escucha, u oye.",true,"sí: es la primera palabra del versículo."],["la mezuzá guarda un pequeño pergamino con el dibujo de un león.",false,"no está: el pequeño pergamino tiene el Shemá escrito a mano."],["los versículos dicen que hay que decir estas palabras al acostarse y al levantarse.",true,"sí: por eso muchos dicen el Shemá por la mañana y por la noche."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como cuando te acomodas en la cama por la noche. exhala despacio, como cuando escuchas un cuarto en silencio. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a escuchar de verdad cuando alguien me hable","grownups":{"source":"Deuteronomio 6:4–9 en la Torá; la línea citada es Deuteronomio 6:4, aquí en una traducción propia al español (el inglés usa la traducción de la Jewish Publication Society de 1917). La mezuzá contiene Deuteronomio 6:4–9 y 11:13–21. Decir el Shemá antes de dormir se trata en el Talmud, Berajot 4b–5a.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a sentirte tranquilo y seguro a la hora de dormir?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Deuteronomy 6:4","work":"Torah (Deuteronomy)","translation":"JPS 1917","quoted":"Hear, O Israel: the LORD our God, the LORD is one."},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 6:4–9; 11:13–21","work":"Torah (Deuteronomy)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Berakhot 4b–5a; 13b","work":"Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"tzedakah-field-corners","game":"match","en":{"title":"the corners of the field","word":"tzedakah","means":"giving to people in need; from the Hebrew for fairness (צדקה)","story":[{"head":"a farmer’s field at harvest","text":"In the Torah there is a rule for farmers. When you harvest your field, don't cut the grain all the way to the corners, and don't go back to pick up what you dropped."},{"head":"the corners left for others","text":"The corners and the dropped stalks are left on purpose, for people who are poor and for strangers passing through. They can come and gather food for themselves, so they have enough too."},{"head":"just like Ruth in the field","text":"Remember Ruth? She gathered grain just like this, in Boaz's field. Today many families are not farmers, but the idea is the same: share what you have, so everyone has enough."},{"head":"a tzedakah box at home","text":"Many Jewish homes keep a tzedakah box. Children drop in coins, often before Shabbat begins. When the box is full, the family gives the money to help people who need it."}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes together? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the corners","left for people in need"],["Ruth","gathered grain in Boaz’s field"],["the tzedakah box","coins dropped in before Shabbat"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a farmer looking out over a golden field. breathe out slowly, like a coin dropping softly into a box. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I will share something so someone else has enough","grownups":{"source":"Leviticus 19:9–10 and 23:22 in the Torah give the rule of the field's corners (pe'ah) and fallen grain; Deuteronomy 24:19 adds the forgotten sheaf. Ruth 2 shows it in action. The tzedakah box is a long-standing home custom.","ask":"what could our family share with people who need it?"}},"es":{"title":"las esquinas del campo","word":"tzedaká","means":"dar a quienes lo necesitan; viene del hebreo para justicia (צדקה)","story":[{"head":"el campo de un agricultor en la cosecha","text":"En la Torá hay una regla para los agricultores. Cuando cosechas tu campo, no cortes el grano hasta las esquinas, y no vuelvas a recoger lo que se te cayó."},{"head":"las esquinas que se dejan para otros","text":"Las esquinas y las espigas caídas se dejan a propósito, para las personas pobres y para los forasteros que pasan. Ellos pueden venir a recoger comida, para que también tengan suficiente."},{"head":"igual que Rut en el campo","text":"¿Te acuerdas de Rut? Ella recogía grano justo así, en el campo de Booz. Hoy muchas familias no son agricultoras, pero la idea es la misma: compartir lo que tienes, para que todos tengan suficiente."},{"head":"una alcancía de tzedaká en casa","text":"Muchas casas judías tienen una alcancía de tzedaká. Los niños echan monedas, a menudo antes de que empiece el Shabat. Cuando la alcancía se llena, la familia da el dinero para ayudar a quienes lo necesitan."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["las esquinas","se dejan para quien lo necesita"],["Rut","recogía grano en el campo de Booz"],["la alcancía de tzedaká","monedas antes del Shabat"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un agricultor que mira su campo dorado. exhala despacio, como una moneda que cae suavemente en una alcancía. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy voy a compartir algo para que otro tenga suficiente","grownups":{"source":"Levítico 19:9–10 y 23:22 en la Torá dan la regla de las esquinas del campo (peá) y del grano caído; Deuteronomio 24:19 añade la gavilla olvidada. Rut 2 lo muestra en acción. La alcancía de tzedaká es una antigua costumbre del hogar.","ask":"¿qué podría compartir nuestra familia con quienes lo necesitan?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Leviticus 19:9–10; 23:22","work":"Torah (Leviticus)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Deuteronomy 24:19","work":"Torah (Deuteronomy)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Ruth 2","work":"Tanakh (Writings)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]},"ISLAM":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"ISLAM","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"crying-camel","game":"match","en":{"title":"the camel who cried","word":"rahma","means":"mercy: a deep, caring kindness (رحمة)","story":[{"head":"a hungry camel in a garden","text":"Long ago, in a garden of palm trees in Madina, there lived a camel. It belonged to a young man. The camel was hungry, and it was worn out from working too hard."},{"head":"the camel cries when he comes","text":"One day the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ came into the garden. A companion named Abdullah ibn Ja'far was with him, and he later told what happened. When the camel saw the Prophet ﷺ, it let out a long, soft cry, and tears ran from its eyes."},{"head":"a gentle hand behind its ears","text":"Abdullah reported that the Prophet ﷺ went over to the camel and gently stroked it behind its ears. The camel grew calm and quiet. Then the Prophet ﷺ asked whose camel it was, and a young man came and said it was his."},{"head":"a word for the young man","text":"The Prophet ﷺ reminded the young man to be mindful of God in caring for this animal that God had placed in his hands. He said the camel had complained to him that it was kept hungry and tired. Muslims tell this story to remember that animals feel too."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the camel","cried when it saw the Prophet ﷺ"],["the Prophet ﷺ","stroked it behind its ears"],["the young man","owned the camel"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the tired camel resting in the shade. breathe out softly, like a gentle hand stroking its ears. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll be gentle with an animal","grownups":{"source":"a hadith in Sunan Abi Dawud (2549), reported by the companion Abdullah ibn Ja'far. Hadith are reports of what the Prophet ﷺ said and did; this one is retold here in simple words, not quoted.","ask":"how can we tell when an animal is tired, hungry or sad?"}},"es":{"title":"el camello que lloraba","word":"rahma","means":"misericordia: una bondad profunda y cariñosa (رحمة)","story":[{"head":"un camello con hambre en un huerto","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en un huerto de palmeras en Medina, vivía un camello. Era de un joven. El camello tenía hambre y estaba agotado de tanto trabajar."},{"head":"el camello llora al verlo llegar","text":"Un día el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ entró en el huerto. Lo acompañaba un compañero llamado Abdullah ibn Ja'far, que después contó lo que pasó. Cuando el camello vio al Profeta ﷺ, soltó un llanto largo y suave, y le corrieron lágrimas de los ojos."},{"head":"una mano suave detrás de sus orejas","text":"Abdullah contó que el Profeta ﷺ se acercó al camello y lo acarició con suavidad detrás de las orejas. El camello se calmó y se quedó tranquilo. Luego el Profeta ﷺ preguntó de quién era el camello, y un joven vino y dijo que era suyo."},{"head":"unas palabras para el joven","text":"El Profeta ﷺ le recordó al joven que tuviera presente a Dios al cuidar de este animal que Dios había puesto en sus manos. Le dijo que el camello se le había quejado de que lo tenían con hambre y cansado. Los musulmanes cuentan esta historia para recordar que los animales también sienten."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el camello","lloró al ver al Profeta ﷺ"],["el Profeta ﷺ","lo acarició tras las orejas"],["el joven","era el dueño del camello"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el camello cansado que descansa a la sombra. suelta el aire con suavidad, como una mano que acaricia sus orejas. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy seré amable con un animal","grownups":{"source":"un hadiz de Sunan Abi Dawud (2549), narrado por el compañero Abdullah ibn Ja'far. Los hadices son relatos de lo que el Profeta ﷺ dijo e hizo; este se cuenta aquí con palabras sencillas, sin citarlo.","ask":"¿cómo podemos saber si un animal está cansado, con hambre o triste?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sunan Abi Dawud 2549","work":"Sunan Abi Dawud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"bismillah-bowl","game":"truth","en":{"title":"a boy at the shared bowl","word":"Bismillah","means":"in the name of God (بسم الله): words said to begin something","story":[{"head":"a boy named Umar","text":"There was once a boy named Umar ibn Abi Salama. He grew up in the home of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, who looked after him as part of the family. Years later, Umar told this story about himself."},{"head":"a hand that wandered everywhere","text":"At mealtimes, everyone ate together from one big shared bowl. Little Umar was hungry, and his hand went everywhere: this side of the bowl, that side, the middle, wherever something looked good."},{"head":"three things to remember","text":"Umar reported that the Prophet ﷺ spoke to him and told him three things: to say God's name before eating, to eat with his right hand, and to eat from the part of the bowl in front of him."},{"head":"from that day on","text":"Umar said that from then on, that was how he ate. Today many Muslim children say Bismillah, in the name of God, before a meal. And eating from your own side of the bowl leaves plenty for everyone else."}],"game":{"items":[["Umar's hand wandered all over the shared bowl.",true,"yes: he was hungry, and his hand went everywhere in the bowl."],["Umar was told to say God's name before eating.",true,"yes: that was the first of the three things."],["Umar was sent away from the meal.",false,"not in it: he was told three things, and he ate that way from then on."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like smelling warm food in a big shared bowl. breathe out slowly, and leave room for everyone. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can say Bismillah before my next meal","grownups":{"source":"a hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (5376) and Sahih Muslim (2022), told by Umar ibn Abi Salama about his own boyhood, retold here in simple words.","ask":"what could we say or do before a meal to remember where it came from?"}},"es":{"title":"un niño junto al plato compartido","word":"Bismillah","means":"en el nombre de Dios (بسم الله): palabras para empezar algo","story":[{"head":"un niño llamado Umar","text":"Había una vez un niño llamado Umar ibn Abi Salama. Creció en la casa del Profeta Muhammad ﷺ, que lo cuidaba como parte de la familia. Años después, Umar contó esta historia sobre sí mismo."},{"head":"una mano que iba a todas partes","text":"A la hora de comer, todos comían juntos de un gran plato compartido. El pequeño Umar tenía hambre, y su mano iba a todas partes: a este lado del plato, al otro, al centro, a donde algo se viera rico."},{"head":"tres cosas para recordar","text":"Umar contó que el Profeta ﷺ le habló y le dijo tres cosas: que dijera el nombre de Dios antes de comer, que comiera con la mano derecha y que comiera de la parte del plato que estaba frente a él."},{"head":"desde ese día","text":"Umar dijo que desde entonces comió así. Hoy muchos niños musulmanes dicen Bismillah, en el nombre de Dios, antes de comer. Y comer de tu lado del plato deja bastante para todos los demás."}],"game":{"items":[["la mano de Umar iba por todo el plato compartido.",true,"sí: tenía hambre, y su mano iba a todas partes del plato."],["a Umar le dijeron que dijera el nombre de Dios antes de comer.",true,"sí: esa fue la primera de las tres cosas."],["a Umar lo mandaron lejos de la comida.",false,"no está: le dijeron tres cosas, y desde entonces comió así."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como quien huele comida tibia en un gran plato compartido. suelta el aire despacio y deja lugar para todos. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo decir Bismillah antes de mi próxima comida","grownups":{"source":"un hadiz de Sahih al-Bujari (5376) y Sahih Muslim (2022), contado por Umar ibn Abi Salama sobre su propia niñez, relatado aquí con palabras sencillas.","ask":"¿qué podríamos decir o hacer antes de comer para recordar de dónde viene la comida?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 5376","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih Muslim 2022","work":"Sahih Muslim","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"ibrahim-night-sky","game":"match","en":{"title":"Ibrahim looks up at the sky","word":"Ibrahim","means":"the prophet called Abraham in English (إبراهيم)","story":[{"head":"a bright star at night","text":"The Qur'an says God showed Ibrahim (peace be upon him) the wonders of the heavens and the earth. One night, as darkness fell, Ibrahim saw a bright star. He said that this might be his Lord."},{"head":"the star goes down","text":"But the star sank lower and lower until it was gone. Ibrahim said he did not love things that set and disappear. Then the moon came up, round and glowing, and he said the same: this might be his Lord."},{"head":"the moon and the sun set too","text":"But the moon set too. In the morning the sun rose, shining bright over everything, and Ibrahim said this one was greater. Yet by evening, the sun went down as well."},{"head":"the One who made them all","text":"So Ibrahim turned his face to the One who made the heavens and the earth. Muslims tell this story to remember that God made the stars, the moon and the sun, and is greater than all of them."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each light in the sky.","pairs":[["the star","sank out of sight first"],["the moon","rose round and glowing"],["the sun","seemed greater than the others"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the sun rising in the morning. breathe out slowly, like a star sinking softly at night. four slow breaths.","carry":"tonight I'll look up at the moon or a star","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah al-An'am (6:75-79), retold in simple words. Muslim scholars read it in different ways: some say Ibrahim was searching, others that he was showing his people, step by step, that only the Maker of the sky is worthy of worship.","ask":"what in the sky fills you with wonder?"}},"es":{"title":"Ibrahim mira el cielo","word":"Ibrahim","means":"el profeta llamado Abraham en español (إبراهيم)","story":[{"head":"una estrella brillante de noche","text":"El Corán dice que Dios le mostró a Ibrahim (la paz sea con él) las maravillas de los cielos y la tierra. Una noche, cuando oscureció, Ibrahim vio una estrella brillante. Dijo que tal vez esa era su Señor."},{"head":"la estrella se oculta","text":"Pero la estrella bajó y bajó hasta desaparecer. Ibrahim dijo que no amaba lo que se pone y desaparece. Entonces salió la luna, redonda y brillante, y él dijo lo mismo: tal vez esa era su Señor."},{"head":"la luna y el sol también se ponen","text":"Pero la luna también se puso. En la mañana salió el sol, brillando sobre todo, e Ibrahim dijo que este era más grande. Pero al llegar la tarde, el sol también se ocultó."},{"head":"el que los hizo a todos","text":"Entonces Ibrahim volvió su rostro hacia Aquel que hizo los cielos y la tierra. Los musulmanes cuentan esta historia para recordar que Dios hizo las estrellas, la luna y el sol, y es más grande que todos ellos."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada luz del cielo.","pairs":[["la estrella","se ocultó primero"],["la luna","salió redonda y brillante"],["el sol","parecía más grande que los otros"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el sol que sale en la mañana. suelta el aire despacio, como una estrella que baja suave en la noche. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"esta noche miraré la luna o una estrella","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura al-An'am (6:75-79), contado con palabras sencillas. Los sabios musulmanes lo leen de distintas maneras: unos dicen que Ibrahim buscaba, otros que mostraba a su pueblo, paso a paso, que solo el Creador del cielo merece adoración.","ask":"¿qué hay en el cielo que te llena de asombro?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 6:75-79","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":4,"key":"building-the-kaaba","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Ibrahim and Ismail build the Kaaba","word":"Kaaba","means":"the cube-shaped House in Makkah that Muslims face to pray","story":[{"head":"a father and a son","text":"The Qur'an tells about Ibrahim (peace be upon him) and his son Ismail (peace be upon him). In a dry valley, the place Muslims call Makkah, the two of them had an important job: raising a House for the worship of God."},{"head":"stones, one by one","text":"A long report in Sahih al-Bukhari says Ismail brought the stones, and Ibrahim set them in place. Stone after stone, day by day, the walls grew higher."},{"head":"a stone to stand on","text":"When the walls grew too tall to reach, Ismail brought a big stone for his father to stand on. Ibrahim climbed up and kept building, while Ismail kept handing him stones. Father and son, side by side."},{"head":"please accept it from us","text":"As they raised the walls, the Qur'an says, they asked God together to accept their work. That House is the Kaaba. Muslims around the world face toward it when they pray, and pilgrims on Hajj walk around it together."}],"game":{"items":[["Ismail brought stones and Ibrahim set them in place.",true,"yes: father and son worked together, stone by stone."],["Ibrahim stood on a big stone when the walls grew tall.",true,"yes: Ismail brought him a stone to stand on."],["they painted the walls with gold when they finished.",false,"not in it: as they built, they asked God to accept their work."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like lifting a stone up high. breathe out slowly, like setting it down in its place. four slow breaths, one stone at a time.","carry":"I'll help someone in my family with a job today","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah al-Baqarah (2:127), and a long report from Ibn Abbas in Sahih al-Bukhari (3364) about the building, retold simply. Facing the Kaaba in prayer comes from 2:144.","ask":"what could we build or make together, side by side?"}},"es":{"title":"Ibrahim e Ismail construyen la Kaaba","word":"Kaaba","means":"la Casa en forma de cubo en La Meca, hacia donde rezan los musulmanes","story":[{"head":"un padre y un hijo","text":"El Corán habla de Ibrahim (la paz sea con él) y de su hijo Ismail (la paz sea con él). En un valle seco, el lugar que los musulmanes llaman La Meca, los dos tenían un trabajo importante: levantar una Casa para adorar a Dios."},{"head":"piedras, una por una","text":"Un largo relato de Sahih al-Bujari dice que Ismail traía las piedras e Ibrahim las colocaba en su lugar. Piedra tras piedra, día tras día, los muros crecían."},{"head":"una piedra para subirse","text":"Cuando los muros quedaron demasiado altos, Ismail trajo una piedra grande para que su padre se subiera. Ibrahim subió y siguió construyendo, mientras Ismail le seguía pasando piedras. Padre e hijo, lado a lado."},{"head":"acepta esto de nosotros","text":"Mientras levantaban los muros, dice el Corán, pidieron juntos a Dios que aceptara su trabajo. Esa Casa es la Kaaba. Los musulmanes de todo el mundo se vuelven hacia ella al rezar, y los peregrinos del Hajj caminan juntos a su alrededor."}],"game":{"items":[["Ismail traía piedras e Ibrahim las colocaba.",true,"sí: padre e hijo trabajaron juntos, piedra por piedra."],["Ibrahim se subió a una piedra grande cuando los muros crecieron.",true,"sí: Ismail le trajo una piedra para subirse."],["al terminar, pintaron los muros de oro.",false,"no está: mientras construían, pidieron a Dios que aceptara su trabajo."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como quien levanta una piedra bien alto. suelta el aire despacio, como quien la pone en su lugar. cuatro respiraciones lentas, una piedra a la vez.","carry":"hoy ayudaré a alguien de mi familia con una tarea","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura al-Baqara (2:127), y un largo relato de Ibn Abbas en Sahih al-Bujari (3364) sobre la construcción, contado con sencillez. Volverse hacia la Kaaba al rezar viene de 2:144.","ask":"¿qué podríamos construir o hacer juntos, lado a lado?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 2:127","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 3364","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Qur'an 2:144","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"nuh-ark","game":"match","en":{"title":"Nuh builds a great ship","word":"Nuh","means":"the prophet called Noah in English (نوح)","story":[{"head":"a ship of planks and nails","text":"The Qur'an tells about Nuh (peace be upon him). God told him to build a ship, and promised to watch over the work. So Nuh began building, with planks of wood and nails."},{"head":"some people laughed","text":"Each time some of his people walked past, they laughed at the ship. Nuh kept working, plank by plank, day after day, until the ship was ready."},{"head":"two of every kind","text":"Then the water came, pouring from the sky and rising from the ground. Nuh was told to bring on board a pair of every kind of animal, with his family and the people who believed. They boarded in the name of God."},{"head":"resting on the mountain","text":"The ship sailed on waves as tall as mountains. At last the sky cleared, the water sank back into the earth, and everything grew still. The ship came to rest on a mountain called al-Judi."}],"game":{"prompt":"match the pieces of the story.","pairs":[["two of every kind","came aboard the ship"],["planks and nails","what the ship was made of"],["al-Judi","where the ship came to rest"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a big wave lifting the ship. breathe out slowly, like the ship settling on the mountain. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll keep going on something hard, one plank at a time","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah Hud (11:36-44), with the planks and nails from Surah al-Qamar (54:13), retold simply. The Qur'an also tells of those who drowned, including one of Nuh's sons; this bedtime telling leaves that out.","ask":"what is something you kept working on, even when it was hard?"}},"es":{"title":"Nuh construye un gran barco","word":"Nuh","means":"el profeta llamado Noé en español (نوح)","story":[{"head":"un barco de tablas y clavos","text":"El Corán habla de Nuh (la paz sea con él). Dios le dijo que construyera un barco, y le prometió cuidar el trabajo. Así que Nuh empezó a construir, con tablas de madera y clavos."},{"head":"algunas personas se reían","text":"Cada vez que algunas personas de su pueblo pasaban, se reían del barco. Nuh siguió trabajando, tabla por tabla, día tras día, hasta que el barco estuvo listo."},{"head":"dos de cada especie","text":"Entonces llegó el agua, cayendo del cielo y brotando de la tierra. A Nuh le dijeron que subiera al barco una pareja de cada especie de animal, con su familia y la gente que creía. Subieron en el nombre de Dios."},{"head":"descansando en la montaña","text":"El barco navegó sobre olas altas como montañas. Por fin el cielo se despejó, el agua volvió a la tierra y todo quedó en calma. El barco se posó sobre un monte llamado al-Judi."}],"game":{"prompt":"une las partes de la historia.","pairs":[["dos de cada especie","subieron al barco"],["tablas y clavos","de lo que estaba hecho el barco"],["al-Judi","donde se posó el barco"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una gran ola que levanta el barco. suelta el aire despacio, como el barco que se posa en la montaña. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"seguiré con algo difícil, una tabla a la vez","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura Hud (11:36-44), con las tablas y clavos de la sura al-Qamar (54:13), contado con sencillez. El Corán también habla de quienes se ahogaron, entre ellos un hijo de Nuh; este relato para dormir lo deja fuera.","ask":"¿en qué seguiste trabajando aunque era difícil?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 11:36-44","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Qur'an 54:11-13","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"yunus-big-fish","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Yunus and the big fish","word":"Yunus","means":"the prophet called Jonah in English (يونس)","story":[{"head":"a ship loaded full","text":"The Qur'an tells about Yunus (peace be upon him). He left his people upset and went away. He boarded a ship that was loaded full. Out at sea, the people on board drew lots, and Yunus went into the water."},{"head":"inside the big fish","text":"A big fish swallowed Yunus. Inside the fish it was dark, very dark, and there was nobody else there. It was a long way from home."},{"head":"a call in the dark","text":"In the darkness, Yunus called out to God. He said there is no god but God, he praised God, and he said that he had done wrong. The Qur'an says God heard him and saved him from his sorrow."},{"head":"shade on the shore","text":"The fish set Yunus down on an open shore. He was weak and tired, so a gourd vine grew over him, with big leaves for shade. When he was strong again, Yunus was sent back to his people, and they believed."}],"game":{"items":[["a big fish swallowed Yunus.",true,"yes: and inside the fish it was very dark."],["Yunus called out to God from inside the fish.",true,"yes: he praised God and said he had done wrong, and God saved him."],["the fish carried Yunus to an island made of gold.",false,"not in it: the fish set him on an open shore, and a gourd vine grew to shade him."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Yunus resting in the cool shade of big green leaves. breathe out slowly, like waves rolling up the shore. four slow breaths.","carry":"when I make a mistake, I can say so and start again","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an: Surah al-Anbiya (21:87-88) and Surah as-Saffat (37:139-148), retold simply. The Qur'an calls him Dhu'n-Nun, the man of the fish. His words in the fish are a well-known prayer that many Muslims say in hard times.","ask":"when you feel worried, what helps you feel calm again?"}},"es":{"title":"Yunus y el gran pez","word":"Yunus","means":"el profeta llamado Jonás en español (يونس)","story":[{"head":"un barco cargado hasta arriba","text":"El Corán habla de Yunus (la paz sea con él). Se fue de su pueblo molesto y se marchó. Subió a un barco que iba cargado hasta arriba. En alta mar, la gente del barco echó suertes, y Yunus fue al agua."},{"head":"dentro del gran pez","text":"Un gran pez se tragó a Yunus. Dentro del pez estaba oscuro, muy oscuro, y no había nadie más. Estaba muy lejos de casa."},{"head":"una llamada en la oscuridad","text":"En la oscuridad, Yunus llamó a Dios. Dijo que no hay más dios que Dios, alabó a Dios y dijo que había hecho mal. El Corán dice que Dios lo escuchó y lo salvó de su tristeza."},{"head":"sombra en la orilla","text":"El pez dejó a Yunus en una playa abierta. Estaba débil y cansado, así que creció sobre él una planta de calabaza, con hojas grandes para darle sombra. Cuando recobró las fuerzas, Yunus fue enviado de vuelta a su pueblo, y ellos creyeron."}],"game":{"items":[["un gran pez se tragó a Yunus.",true,"sí: y dentro del pez estaba muy oscuro."],["Yunus llamó a Dios desde dentro del pez.",true,"sí: alabó a Dios y dijo que había hecho mal, y Dios lo salvó."],["el pez llevó a Yunus a una isla de oro.",false,"no está: el pez lo dejó en una playa abierta, y una planta de calabaza creció para darle sombra."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como Yunus descansando a la sombra fresca de grandes hojas verdes. suelta el aire despacio, como las olas que llegan a la orilla. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"cuando me equivoque, puedo decirlo y empezar de nuevo","grownups":{"source":"el Corán: sura al-Anbiya (21:87-88) y sura as-Saffat (37:139-148), contado con sencillez. El Corán lo llama Dhu'n-Nun, el del pez. Sus palabras dentro del pez son una oración conocida que muchos musulmanes dicen en tiempos difíciles.","ask":"cuando te sientes preocupado, ¿qué te ayuda a calmarte?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 21:87-88","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Qur'an 37:139-148","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":7,"key":"baby-musa-river","game":"match","en":{"title":"baby Musa floats on the river","word":"Musa","means":"the prophet called Moses in English (موسى)","story":[{"head":"a mother's worry","text":"The Qur'an tells about a mother in Pharaoh's land whose baby boy, Musa (peace be upon him), was not safe. God inspired her to feed him and, if she feared for him, to place him on the river. God promised to bring him back to her."},{"head":"a little box on the water","text":"So she laid her baby in a little box and set it on the river. Her heart ached. She asked Musa's sister to follow it, and the sister watched from a distance, without anyone noticing her."},{"head":"found by Pharaoh's family","text":"Pharaoh's family lifted the box out of the water. Pharaoh's wife looked at the baby and said he would be a joy to them both. She asked to keep him, and perhaps raise him as their son."},{"head":"home to his own mother","text":"But baby Musa would not feed from any of the nurses they brought. His sister stepped forward and said she knew a family who would care for him well. So Musa went home to his own mother, and her worry turned to comfort, as God had promised."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Musa's mother","set him on the river"],["Musa's sister","watched from a distance"],["Pharaoh's wife","wanted to keep the baby"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a little box floating on the river. breathe out slowly, like a mother's worry melting into comfort. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll look out for someone younger than me today","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah al-Qasas (28:7-13), with the little box from Surah Ta Ha (20:39), retold simply. The Qur'an calls it a tabut, a chest or box; many retellings say basket. Why the baby was in danger is left out here.","ask":"who watches over you, the way Musa's sister watched over him?"}},"es":{"title":"el bebé Musa flota en el río","word":"Musa","means":"el profeta llamado Moisés en español (موسى)","story":[{"head":"la preocupación de una madre","text":"El Corán habla de una madre en la tierra del Faraón cuyo bebé, Musa (la paz sea con él), no estaba a salvo. Dios le inspiró que lo alimentara y, si temía por él, que lo pusiera en el río. Dios le prometió devolvérselo."},{"head":"una cajita sobre el agua","text":"Así que acostó a su bebé en una cajita y la puso en el río. Le dolía el corazón. Le pidió a la hermana de Musa que la siguiera, y la hermana miraba desde lejos, sin que nadie la notara."},{"head":"lo encuentra la familia del Faraón","text":"La familia del Faraón sacó la cajita del agua. La esposa del Faraón miró al bebé y dijo que sería una alegría para los dos. Pidió quedarse con él, y tal vez criarlo como su hijo."},{"head":"de vuelta con su madre","text":"Pero el bebé Musa no quería comer de ninguna de las nodrizas que le traían. Su hermana se acercó y dijo que conocía una familia que lo cuidaría bien. Así Musa volvió con su propia madre, y su preocupación se volvió consuelo, como Dios había prometido."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la madre de Musa","lo puso en el río"],["la hermana de Musa","miraba desde lejos"],["la esposa del Faraón","quiso quedarse con el bebé"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una cajita que flota en el río. suelta el aire despacio, como la preocupación de una madre que se vuelve consuelo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy cuidaré a alguien más pequeño que yo","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura al-Qasas (28:7-13), con la cajita de la sura Ta Ha (20:39), contado con sencillez. El Corán la llama tabut, un cofre o caja; muchos relatos dicen canasta. Aquí no se cuenta por qué el bebé corría peligro.","ask":"¿quién te cuida, como la hermana de Musa lo cuidaba a él?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 28:7-13","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Qur'an 20:39","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"maryam-date-palm","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Maryam and the date palm","word":"Maryam","means":"Mary, the mother of Isa (Jesus), honored in the Qur'an","story":[{"head":"a quiet place far away","text":"The Qur'an tells about Maryam (peace be upon her), a woman devoted to God. She was going to have a baby, Isa (peace be upon him). When the time came near, she went away by herself to a far-off place."},{"head":"under the palm tree","text":"Maryam was tired and aching, and she sat down by the trunk of a date palm. She felt very sad and very alone."},{"head":"a voice and a little stream","text":"Then a voice called gently to her from below, telling her not to be sad. A little stream of water was flowing right beneath her. And if she shook the trunk of the palm, fresh ripe dates would fall for her."},{"head":"dates to eat, water to drink","text":"She was told to eat, to drink, and to be at peace. In the story, Maryam and her baby Isa were cared for: water to drink, sweet dates to eat, and a reason not to be sad."}],"game":{"items":[["Maryam rested by the trunk of a date palm.",true,"yes: she sat by the palm when she was tired and aching."],["a little stream flowed beneath her.",true,"yes: water to drink, right where she needed it."],["a bird brought Maryam a basket of bread.",false,"not in it: if she shook the palm, ripe dates would fall for her."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a cool little stream. breathe out slowly, like ripe dates dropping softly from the palm. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll bring a drink to someone who looks tired","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah Maryam (19:22-26), retold simply. Scholars differ on who called to her from below: some say the angel Jibril, others the newborn Isa. Surah 19 carries Maryam's name.","ask":"when you feel tired or sad, what small thing helps you feel cared for?"}},"es":{"title":"Maryam y la palmera de dátiles","word":"Maryam","means":"María, la madre de Isa (Jesús), honrada en el Corán","story":[{"head":"un lugar tranquilo y lejano","text":"El Corán habla de Maryam (la paz sea con ella), una mujer dedicada a Dios. Iba a tener un bebé, Isa (la paz sea con él). Cuando se acercó el momento, se fue sola a un lugar lejano."},{"head":"bajo la palmera","text":"Maryam estaba cansada y adolorida, y se sentó junto al tronco de una palmera de dátiles. Se sentía muy triste y muy sola."},{"head":"una voz y un arroyito","text":"Entonces una voz la llamó con suavidad desde abajo, diciéndole que no estuviera triste. Un arroyito de agua corría justo debajo de ella. Y si sacudía el tronco de la palmera, caerían para ella dátiles frescos y maduros."},{"head":"dátiles para comer, agua para beber","text":"Le dijeron que comiera, que bebiera y que estuviera en paz. En la historia, Maryam y su bebé Isa fueron cuidados: agua para beber, dátiles dulces para comer y una razón para no estar tristes."}],"game":{"items":[["Maryam descansó junto al tronco de una palmera.",true,"sí: se sentó junto a la palmera cuando estaba cansada y adolorida."],["un arroyito corría debajo de ella.",true,"sí: agua para beber, justo donde la necesitaba."],["un pájaro le trajo a Maryam una canasta de pan.",false,"no está: si sacudía la palmera, caerían dátiles maduros para ella."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un arroyito fresco. suelta el aire despacio, como dátiles maduros que caen suaves de la palmera. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"le llevaré algo de beber a alguien que se vea cansado","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura Maryam (19:22-26), contado con sencillez. Los sabios no coinciden en quién la llamó desde abajo: unos dicen el ángel Jibril, otros el recién nacido Isa. La sura 19 lleva el nombre de Maryam.","ask":"cuando te sientes cansado o triste, ¿qué cosa pequeña te hace sentir cuidado?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 19:22-26","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":9,"key":"sulayman-and-the-ant","game":"match","en":{"title":"Sulayman and the little ant","word":"shukr","means":"thankfulness: noticing a gift and saying thanks (شكر)","story":[{"head":"a great march in rows","text":"The Qur'an tells about Sulayman (peace be upon him), a king and a prophet. God taught him the speech of birds. One day his great gathering of people, jinn and birds set out together, marching in rows."},{"head":"a valley full of ants","text":"They came to a valley full of ants. One little ant saw them coming. She called to the other ants to hurry into their homes, so that Sulayman and his armies would not step on them without noticing."},{"head":"a king smiles","text":"Sulayman heard what the little ant said. He smiled, and the Qur'an says he even laughed at her words. A tiny ant had made a great king smile."},{"head":"a thank-you to God","text":"Then Sulayman asked God to help him be thankful for all the gifts given to him and to his parents, and to do good. He was a mighty king, and still he listened to a tiny ant."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each one to the story.","pairs":[["the little ant","told the others to hurry home"],["Sulayman","smiled at the ant's words"],["the valley","was full of ants"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a tiny ant walking home. breathe out slowly, like a king's warm smile. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll watch where I step and look out for little creatures","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah an-Naml, The Ant (27:15-19), retold simply. The surah is named after this ant. Sulayman's prayer of thanks is paraphrased here, not quoted.","ask":"what small creature have you noticed lately, and what was it doing?"}},"es":{"title":"Sulayman y la pequeña hormiga","word":"shukr","means":"gratitud: notar un regalo y dar las gracias (شكر)","story":[{"head":"una gran marcha en filas","text":"El Corán habla de Sulayman (la paz sea con él), un rey y un profeta. Dios le enseñó el lenguaje de los pájaros. Un día su gran ejército de personas, genios y pájaros salió junto, marchando en filas."},{"head":"un valle lleno de hormigas","text":"Llegaron a un valle lleno de hormigas. Una pequeña hormiga los vio venir. Llamó a las demás hormigas para que corrieran a sus casas, para que Sulayman y sus ejércitos no las pisaran sin darse cuenta."},{"head":"un rey sonríe","text":"Sulayman oyó lo que dijo la pequeña hormiga. Sonrió, y el Corán dice que hasta se rió de sus palabras. Una hormiguita había hecho sonreír a un gran rey."},{"head":"un gracias a Dios","text":"Entonces Sulayman pidió a Dios que lo ayudara a ser agradecido por todos los regalos que le había dado a él y a sus padres, y a hacer el bien. Era un rey poderoso, y aun así escuchó a una hormiguita."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada uno con la historia.","pairs":[["la pequeña hormiga","avisó a las demás que corrieran"],["Sulayman","sonrió por lo que dijo"],["el valle","estaba lleno de hormigas"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una hormiguita que camina a casa. suelta el aire despacio, como la sonrisa cálida de un rey. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"miraré dónde piso y cuidaré a los animalitos","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura an-Naml, La Hormiga (27:15-19), contado con sencillez. La sura lleva el nombre de esta hormiga. La oración de gracias de Sulayman se cuenta aquí con otras palabras, sin citarla.","ask":"¿qué animalito has visto últimamente, y qué estaba haciendo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 27:15-19","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"the-hoopoe","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the hoopoe brings news","word":"hudhud","means":"the hoopoe, a bird with a crown of feathers (هدهد)","story":[{"head":"looking over the birds","text":"The Qur'an tells that Sulayman (peace be upon him) knew the speech of birds, and birds were part of his gathering. One day he looked over all the birds and asked why he did not see the hoopoe."},{"head":"where is the hoopoe?","text":"The hoopoe is a small bird with a fan of feathers on its head, like a crown. But it was nowhere to be seen. It had flown off, and Sulayman wanted to know why."},{"head":"back with big news","text":"Before long, the hoopoe came flying back. It said it had found out something that Sulayman did not know. It brought true news from a faraway land called Sheba."},{"head":"a queen on a great throne","text":"In Sheba, the hoopoe said, a queen ruled the people, and she had a magnificent throne. Sulayman listened carefully and said he would see whether the news was true. Even a little bird can bring news worth hearing."}],"game":{"items":[["Sulayman noticed the hoopoe was missing.",true,"yes: he looked over the birds and asked why he did not see the hoopoe."],["the hoopoe came back with news from Sheba.",true,"yes: it had found out something Sulayman did not know."],["the hoopoe brought back a golden crown in its beak.",false,"not in it: it brought news, about a queen in Sheba and her throne."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a hoopoe lifting its crown of feathers. breathe out slowly, like the bird gliding home. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll listen carefully when someone younger tells me something","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah an-Naml (27:20-27), retold simply. In the Qur'an, Sulayman is stern about the hoopoe's absence at first; this gentle telling leaves that out and stops before the rest of the Queen of Sheba's story.","ask":"when did someone smaller or younger than you teach you something new?"}},"es":{"title":"la abubilla trae noticias","word":"hudhud","means":"la abubilla, un pájaro con una corona de plumas (هدهد)","story":[{"head":"revisando a los pájaros","text":"El Corán cuenta que Sulayman (la paz sea con él) conocía el lenguaje de los pájaros, y los pájaros eran parte de su ejército. Un día revisó a todos los pájaros y preguntó por qué no veía a la abubilla."},{"head":"¿dónde está la abubilla?","text":"La abubilla es un pájaro pequeño con un abanico de plumas en la cabeza, como una corona. Pero no se veía por ningún lado. Se había ido volando, y Sulayman quería saber por qué."},{"head":"de vuelta con grandes noticias","text":"Al poco tiempo, la abubilla volvió volando. Dijo que había descubierto algo que Sulayman no sabía. Traía noticias ciertas de una tierra lejana llamada Saba."},{"head":"una reina en un gran trono","text":"En Saba, dijo la abubilla, una reina gobernaba al pueblo, y tenía un trono magnífico. Sulayman escuchó con atención y dijo que vería si la noticia era cierta. Hasta un pajarito puede traer noticias que vale la pena oír."}],"game":{"items":[["Sulayman notó que faltaba la abubilla.",true,"sí: revisó a los pájaros y preguntó por qué no veía a la abubilla."],["la abubilla volvió con noticias de Saba.",true,"sí: había descubierto algo que Sulayman no sabía."],["la abubilla trajo una corona de oro en el pico.",false,"no está: trajo noticias, de una reina en Saba y su trono."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una abubilla que levanta su corona de plumas. suelta el aire despacio, como el pájaro que planea de vuelta a casa. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"escucharé con atención cuando alguien menor me cuente algo","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura an-Naml (27:20-27), contado con sencillez. En el Corán, Sulayman se muestra severo al principio por la ausencia de la abubilla; este relato lo deja fuera y se detiene antes del resto de la historia de la reina de Saba.","ask":"¿cuándo te enseñó algo nuevo alguien más pequeño o más joven que tú?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 27:20-27","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"a-day-in-ramadan","game":"match","en":{"title":"a day in Ramadan","word":"Ramadan","means":"the month of fasting from dawn to sunset (رمضان)","story":[{"head":"a thin new moon","text":"Ramadan begins when a thin new moon is seen. Amira's family looked up at the sky together. The Qur'an says Ramadan is the month in which the Qur'an was sent down, and a month for fasting."},{"head":"breakfast in the dark","text":"Before dawn, while it was still dark, Amira's family ate a meal called suhoor. The Qur'an says people may eat until the white thread of dawn can be told apart from the black thread of night."},{"head":"a long, quiet day","text":"All day, the grown-ups did not eat or drink. Children are not asked to fast, but Amira tried until lunchtime. She read, helped at home, and put some of her coins in the box for people in need."},{"head":"a date at sunset","text":"When the sun went down, everyone broke the fast together, a meal called iftar. Amira started with a date and some water. A companion, Anas, reported that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ broke his fast with dates, and if there were none, with sips of water."}],"game":{"prompt":"match the words to the day.","pairs":[["suhoor","the meal before dawn"],["iftar","the meal at sunset"],["a thin new moon","the sign Ramadan begins"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the first light of dawn. breathe out slowly, like the sun setting at iftar. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll share a snack with someone today","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an on fasting in Ramadan (2:183-187), and a hadith in Sunan Abi Dawud (2356) about breaking the fast with dates or water. Amira and her family are made up for this story. Children are not required to fast; many families let them try a little.","ask":"what would you like to share with someone at a sunset meal?"}},"es":{"title":"un día de Ramadán","word":"Ramadán","means":"el mes de ayuno desde el amanecer hasta la puesta del sol (رمضان)","story":[{"head":"una luna nueva delgadita","text":"Ramadán empieza cuando se ve una luna nueva delgadita. La familia de Amira miró el cielo junta. El Corán dice que Ramadán es el mes en que fue revelado el Corán, y un mes para ayunar."},{"head":"desayuno en la oscuridad","text":"Antes del amanecer, cuando todavía estaba oscuro, la familia de Amira comió una comida llamada suhur. El Corán dice que se puede comer hasta que el hilo blanco del alba se distinga del hilo negro de la noche."},{"head":"un día largo y tranquilo","text":"Todo el día, los adultos no comieron ni bebieron. A los niños no se les pide ayunar, pero Amira lo intentó hasta el almuerzo. Leyó, ayudó en casa y puso algunas monedas en la caja para quienes lo necesitan."},{"head":"un dátil al atardecer","text":"Cuando se puso el sol, todos rompieron el ayuno juntos, en una comida llamada iftar. Amira empezó con un dátil y un poco de agua. Un compañero, Anas, contó que el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ rompía su ayuno con dátiles, y si no había, con unos sorbos de agua."}],"game":{"prompt":"une las palabras con el día.","pairs":[["suhur","la comida antes del amanecer"],["iftar","la comida al atardecer"],["una luna nueva delgadita","la señal de que empieza Ramadán"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la primera luz del amanecer. suelta el aire despacio, como el sol que se pone a la hora del iftar. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy compartiré un bocadillo con alguien","grownups":{"source":"el Corán sobre el ayuno en Ramadán (2:183-187), y un hadiz de Sunan Abi Dawud (2356) sobre romper el ayuno con dátiles o agua. Amira y su familia son inventadas para esta historia. Los niños no están obligados a ayunar; muchas familias los dejan intentarlo un poco.","ask":"¿qué te gustaría compartir con alguien en una comida al atardecer?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 2:183-187","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sunan Abi Dawud 2356","work":"Sunan Abi Dawud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"eid-morning","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Eid morning","word":"Eid","means":"a festival day of joy, prayer and giving (عيد)","story":[{"head":"best clothes on Eid morning","text":"When the new moon of the next month is seen, Ramadan is over and Eid al-Fitr has come. In Amira's house, everyone put on their best clothes, and the kitchen smelled of sweets."},{"head":"a gift before the prayer","text":"Before the Eid prayer, Amira's family gave food for people in need. A companion, Abdullah ibn Umar, reported that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ asked Muslims to give this gift, called zakat al-fitr, before going out to the prayer."},{"head":"a few sweet dates","text":"Before leaving home, Amira ate three dates. Anas, who helped in the Prophet ﷺ's house, reported that on the morning of Eid al-Fitr he would eat some dates before going out, an odd number of them."},{"head":"a different road home","text":"After the prayer, everyone hugged and said Eid Mubarak, a blessed Eid. Then Amira's family walked home a different way. A companion named Jabir reported that on Eid days the Prophet ﷺ went by one road and came back by another."}],"game":{"items":[["Amira's family gave food for people in need before the prayer.",true,"yes: a gift called zakat al-fitr, given before the Eid prayer."],["Amira ate a few dates before going out.",true,"yes: three dates, an odd number, as Anas reported."],["after the prayer, everyone walked home the same way they came.",false,"not in it: Amira's family took a different road home, as Jabir reported."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the smell of Eid sweets in the kitchen. breathe out slowly, like a big Eid hug. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll wish someone a happy day and mean it","grownups":{"source":"hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari: zakat al-fitr before the prayer (1503), dates before going out (953), and a different road home (986), retold simply. Amira's family is made up. Muslims celebrate two Eids: al-Fitr after Ramadan, and al-Adha at the time of Hajj.","ask":"what makes a day feel like a celebration in our family?"}},"es":{"title":"la mañana del Eid","word":"Eid","means":"un día de fiesta, de alegría, oración y generosidad (عيد)","story":[{"head":"la mejor ropa en la mañana del Eid","text":"Cuando se ve la luna nueva del mes siguiente, Ramadán termina y llega el Eid al-Fitr. En la casa de Amira, todos se pusieron su mejor ropa, y la cocina olía a dulces."},{"head":"un regalo antes de la oración","text":"Antes de la oración del Eid, la familia de Amira dio comida para quienes la necesitan. Un compañero, Abdullah ibn Umar, contó que el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ pidió a los musulmanes dar este regalo, llamado zakat al-fitr, antes de salir a la oración."},{"head":"unos dátiles dulces","text":"Antes de salir de casa, Amira comió tres dátiles. Anas, que ayudaba en la casa del Profeta ﷺ, contó que la mañana del Eid al-Fitr él comía algunos dátiles antes de salir, un número impar."},{"head":"otro camino de regreso","text":"Después de la oración, todos se abrazaron y dijeron Eid Mubarak, un Eid bendito. Luego la familia de Amira volvió a casa por otro camino. Un compañero llamado Jabir contó que en los días de Eid el Profeta ﷺ iba por un camino y volvía por otro."}],"game":{"items":[["la familia de Amira dio comida para quienes la necesitan antes de la oración.",true,"sí: un regalo llamado zakat al-fitr, dado antes de la oración del Eid."],["Amira comió unos dátiles antes de salir.",true,"sí: tres dátiles, un número impar, como contó Anas."],["después de la oración, todos volvieron a casa por el mismo camino.",false,"no está: la familia de Amira volvió por otro camino, como contó Jabir."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el olor de los dulces del Eid en la cocina. suelta el aire despacio, como un gran abrazo de Eid. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"le desearé a alguien un feliz día, de corazón","grownups":{"source":"hadices de Sahih al-Bujari: el zakat al-fitr antes de la oración (1503), los dátiles antes de salir (953) y otro camino de regreso (986), contados con sencillez. La familia de Amira es inventada. Los musulmanes celebran dos Eid: al-Fitr después de Ramadán, y al-Adha en el tiempo del Hajj.","ask":"¿qué hace que un día se sienta como una fiesta en nuestra familia?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 1503","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 953","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 986","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"the-thirsty-dog","game":"match","en":{"title":"the man and the thirsty dog","word":"ajr","means":"a reward from God for doing good (أجر)","story":[{"head":"a long, hot road","text":"The companion Abu Hurayra reported that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ told this story. A man was walking along a hot, dusty road, and he grew very, very thirsty."},{"head":"down into the well","text":"He found a well, climbed down inside, and drank the cool water. When he climbed back up, he saw a dog panting, so thirsty that it was licking the damp ground."},{"head":"a shoe full of water","text":"The man thought that this dog was as thirsty as he had been. So he climbed down the well again and filled his leather shoe with water. He held the shoe in his teeth and climbed back up."},{"head":"a drink for the dog","text":"He gave the dog water to drink. The Prophet ﷺ said that God thanked the man and forgave him. The companions asked whether being kind to animals brings a reward too. He told them there is a reward for kindness to every living thing."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the man","climbed down the well twice"],["the dog","licked the damp ground"],["the leather shoe","carried water up the well"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like cool water filling a shoe. breathe out slowly, like a thirsty dog finally drinking. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll fill a water bowl for a bird or animal","grownups":{"source":"a hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (2363) and Sahih Muslim (2244), reported by Abu Hurayra, retold simply. In it the Prophet ﷺ tells a story about a man who is not named.","ask":"where could we leave water for birds or animals near our home?"}},"es":{"title":"el hombre y el perro sediento","word":"ajr","means":"una recompensa de Dios por hacer el bien (أجر)","story":[{"head":"un camino largo y caluroso","text":"El compañero Abu Hurayra contó que el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ narró esta historia. Un hombre caminaba por un camino caluroso y polvoriento, y le dio muchísima sed."},{"head":"dentro del pozo","text":"Encontró un pozo, bajó por dentro y bebió el agua fresca. Cuando volvió a subir, vio a un perro jadeando, con tanta sed que lamía la tierra húmeda."},{"head":"un zapato lleno de agua","text":"El hombre pensó que este perro tenía tanta sed como la que él había tenido. Así que bajó otra vez al pozo y llenó de agua su zapato de cuero. Sostuvo el zapato con los dientes y volvió a subir."},{"head":"agua para el perro","text":"Le dio de beber al perro. El Profeta ﷺ dijo que Dios le agradeció al hombre y lo perdonó. Los compañeros preguntaron si ser bondadoso con los animales también trae recompensa. Él les dijo que hay recompensa por la bondad con todo ser vivo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el hombre","bajó al pozo dos veces"],["el perro","lamía la tierra húmeda"],["el zapato de cuero","subió agua del pozo"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como agua fresca que llena un zapato. suelta el aire despacio, como un perro sediento que por fin bebe. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"llenaré un plato de agua para un pájaro o animal","grownups":{"source":"un hadiz de Sahih al-Bujari (2363) y Sahih Muslim (2244), narrado por Abu Hurayra, contado con sencillez. En él, el Profeta ﷺ cuenta la historia de un hombre cuyo nombre no se da.","ask":"¿dónde podríamos dejar agua para pájaros o animales cerca de casa?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 2363","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih Muslim 2244","work":"Sahih Muslim","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"the-mother-bird","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the bird who wanted her chicks","word":"ihsan","means":"doing good beautifully, with care (إحسان)","story":[{"head":"a stop on a journey","text":"The companion Abdullah ibn Mas'ud reported a day when he was traveling with the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ. The Prophet ﷺ stepped away for a while, and the travelers waited for him."},{"head":"two little chicks","text":"While they waited, the travelers spotted a small bird with two tiny chicks. Some of the travelers picked up the two chicks and took them away from their mother."},{"head":"a worried mother bird","text":"The mother bird came back and found her chicks gone. She flew around and around above the travelers, flapping and spreading her wings, worried and upset."},{"head":"give her chicks back","text":"When the Prophet ﷺ came back, he saw the mother bird. He asked who had upset her by taking her little ones, and he told them to give her chicks back to her. In the story, even a little bird's worry mattered."}],"game":{"items":[["the mother bird flapped her wings above the travelers.",true,"yes: she flew around, flapping and spreading her wings, worried for her chicks."],["the travelers kept the chicks as pets.",false,"not in it: the Prophet ﷺ told them to give the chicks back to their mother."],["the Prophet ﷺ asked who had upset the mother bird.",true,"yes: and he told them to return her little ones."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a bird spreading its wings. breathe out slowly, like the bird folding them, her chicks safe beside her. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll leave nests and little creatures where they belong","grownups":{"source":"a hadith in Sunan Abi Dawud (2675), reported by Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, retold simply. The bird is called a hummara, a small bird; nobody is quite sure which kind. The same report goes on to another lesson about animals, left out here.","ask":"how do animals show us when they're worried or upset?"}},"es":{"title":"el pájaro que quería a sus pollitos","word":"ihsan","means":"hacer el bien de forma hermosa, con cuidado (إحسان)","story":[{"head":"una parada en el camino","text":"El compañero Abdullah ibn Mas'ud contó un día en que viajaba con el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ. El Profeta ﷺ se apartó un rato, y los viajeros lo esperaron."},{"head":"dos pollitos","text":"Mientras esperaban, los viajeros vieron un pajarito con dos pollitos chiquitos. Algunos de los viajeros tomaron los dos pollitos y se los llevaron lejos de su madre."},{"head":"una mamá pájaro preocupada","text":"La mamá pájaro volvió y vio que sus pollitos no estaban. Voló y voló alrededor de los viajeros, aleteando y abriendo las alas, preocupada y angustiada."},{"head":"devuélvanle sus pollitos","text":"Cuando el Profeta ﷺ volvió, vio a la mamá pájaro. Preguntó quién la había angustiado quitándole a sus pequeños, y les dijo que le devolvieran sus pollitos. En la historia, hasta la preocupación de un pajarito importaba."}],"game":{"items":[["la mamá pájaro aleteó sobre los viajeros.",true,"sí: voló alrededor, aleteando y abriendo las alas, preocupada por sus pollitos."],["los viajeros se quedaron con los pollitos como mascotas.",false,"no está: el Profeta ﷺ les dijo que devolvieran los pollitos a su madre."],["el Profeta ﷺ preguntó quién había angustiado a la mamá pájaro.",true,"sí: y les dijo que le devolvieran a sus pequeños."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un pájaro que abre las alas. suelta el aire despacio, como el pájaro que las cierra, con sus pollitos a salvo a su lado. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"dejaré los nidos y los animalitos donde deben estar","grownups":{"source":"un hadiz de Sunan Abi Dawud (2675), narrado por Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, contado con sencillez. El pájaro se llama hummara, un ave pequeña; nadie sabe con certeza de qué tipo. El mismo relato sigue con otra enseñanza sobre animales, que aquí se deja fuera.","ask":"¿cómo nos muestran los animales que están preocupados o angustiados?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sunan Abi Dawud 2675","work":"Sunan Abi Dawud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":15,"key":"soup-for-the-neighbors","game":"match","en":{"title":"a little more water in the soup","word":"jar","means":"neighbor, the person who lives near you (جار)","story":[{"head":"advice for Abu Dharr","text":"A companion named Abu Dharr reported some advice he was given by the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, whom he called his dear friend. The advice was about soup, and it was about neighbors."},{"head":"more water in the pot","text":"The advice was that when he cooked a pot of soup, he should add a little more water. That way the pot stretches a little further, and there is more to go around than before."},{"head":"a bowl for next door","text":"Then he should look to a family living nearby and give them some of it, kindly. A warm bowl of soup carried next door can make a neighbor feel remembered."},{"head":"reminded again and again","text":"In another hadith, Aisha reported that the Prophet ﷺ said the angel Jibril kept reminding him to be good to neighbors, again and again, until he thought neighbors would be given a share of a family's things, like relatives."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each one to the story.","pairs":[["Abu Dharr","was given the soup advice"],["more water","makes the soup go further"],["a neighbor","gets a bowl, given kindly"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like smelling warm soup on the stove. breathe out slowly, like carrying a full bowl next door without spilling. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll do something kind for a neighbor this week","grownups":{"source":"a hadith in Sahih Muslim (2625) reported by Abu Dharr, about adding water to the soup, and one in Sahih al-Bukhari (6014) reported by Aisha, about Jibril's reminders, both retold simply.","ask":"who are our neighbors, and what could we share with them?"}},"es":{"title":"un poco más de agua en la sopa","word":"yar","means":"vecino, la persona que vive cerca de ti (جار)","story":[{"head":"un consejo para Abu Dharr","text":"Un compañero llamado Abu Dharr contó un consejo que le dio el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ, a quien llamaba su querido amigo. El consejo era sobre la sopa, y era sobre los vecinos."},{"head":"más agua en la olla","text":"El consejo era que, cuando cocinara una olla de sopa, le agregara un poco más de agua. Así la olla rinde un poco más, y hay más para repartir que antes."},{"head":"un tazón para los de al lado","text":"Después, que pensara en una familia que vivía cerca y les diera un poco, con cariño. Un tazón de sopa tibia llevado a la casa de al lado puede hacer que un vecino se sienta recordado."},{"head":"recordado una y otra vez","text":"En otro hadiz, Aisha contó que el Profeta ﷺ dijo que el ángel Jibril le recordaba una y otra vez que fuera bueno con los vecinos, hasta que pensó que los vecinos recibirían una parte de las cosas de la familia, como parientes."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada uno con la historia.","pairs":[["Abu Dharr","recibió el consejo de la sopa"],["más agua","hace que la sopa rinda más"],["un vecino","recibe un tazón, con cariño"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como quien huele sopa tibia en la estufa. suelta el aire despacio, como quien lleva un tazón lleno a la casa de al lado sin derramarlo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"esta semana haré algo amable por un vecino","grownups":{"source":"un hadiz de Sahih Muslim (2625) narrado por Abu Dharr, sobre agregar agua a la sopa, y otro de Sahih al-Bujari (6014) narrado por Aisha, sobre los recordatorios de Jibril, ambos contados con sencillez.","ask":"¿quiénes son nuestros vecinos, y qué podríamos compartir con ellos?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sahih Muslim 2625","work":"Sahih Muslim","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 6014","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"peace-be-upon-you","game":"truth","en":{"title":"peace be upon you","word":"salam","means":"peace; as-salamu alaykum means peace be upon you (سلام)","story":[{"head":"a greeting of peace","text":"Many Muslims greet each other with as-salamu alaykum, which means peace be upon you. It is a hello and a kind wish at the same time: may you be safe and well."},{"head":"an answer that's even better","text":"The Qur'an says that when someone greets you, greet them back with something better, or at least the same. So the reply is wa alaykum as-salam, and upon you be peace, and some add even more blessings."},{"head":"a question about good deeds","text":"A companion named Abdullah ibn Amr reported that a man once asked the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ which good deeds were best. The Prophet ﷺ told him to feed people, and to greet with peace the people he knew and the people he didn't know."},{"head":"peace all down the street","text":"So on her walk to school, Hana greeted the baker she knew, and the new neighbor she didn't. One small greeting, again and again, and peace went all the way down the street."}],"game":{"items":[["the Qur'an says to answer a greeting with a better one, or the same.",true,"yes: so the reply often adds a little more peace and blessing."],["the man was told to greet only people he already knew.",false,"not in it: greet the people you know and the people you don't know."],["Hana greeted a neighbor she didn't know yet.",true,"yes: the baker she knew, and the new neighbor she didn't."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a quiet hello. breathe out slowly, like peace passing down the street from door to door. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll greet someone new today with a kind hello","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an, Surah an-Nisa (4:86), and a hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (12) reported by Abdullah ibn Amr, retold simply. Hana is made up for this story.","ask":"how does it feel when someone greets you warmly, and who could we greet?"}},"es":{"title":"la paz sea contigo","word":"salam","means":"paz; as-salamu alaykum quiere decir la paz sea contigo (سلام)","story":[{"head":"un saludo de paz","text":"Muchos musulmanes se saludan con as-salamu alaykum, que quiere decir la paz sea contigo. Es un hola y un buen deseo al mismo tiempo: que estés a salvo y bien."},{"head":"una respuesta todavía mejor","text":"El Corán dice que cuando alguien te saluda, respondas con algo mejor, o al menos igual. Por eso la respuesta es wa alaykum as-salam, y contigo sea la paz, y algunos agregan más bendiciones."},{"head":"una pregunta sobre las buenas obras","text":"Un compañero llamado Abdullah ibn Amr contó que una vez un hombre le preguntó al Profeta Muhammad ﷺ cuáles eran las mejores buenas obras. El Profeta ﷺ le dijo que diera de comer a la gente, y que saludara con paz a quienes conocía y a quienes no conocía."},{"head":"paz por toda la calle","text":"Así que, camino a la escuela, Hana saludó al panadero que conocía, y a la vecina nueva que no conocía. Un saludo pequeño, una y otra vez, y la paz recorrió toda la calle."}],"game":{"items":[["el Corán dice responder un saludo con uno mejor, o igual.",true,"sí: por eso la respuesta muchas veces agrega un poco más de paz y bendición."],["al hombre le dijeron que saludara solo a quienes ya conocía.",false,"no está: saluda a quienes conoces y a quienes no conoces."],["Hana saludó a una vecina que todavía no conocía.",true,"sí: al panadero que conocía, y a la vecina nueva que no conocía."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un hola tranquilo. suelta el aire despacio, como la paz que pasa por la calle de puerta en puerta. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy saludaré a alguien nuevo con un hola amable","grownups":{"source":"el Corán, sura an-Nisa (4:86), y un hadiz de Sahih al-Bujari (12) narrado por Abdullah ibn Amr, contados con sencillez. Hana es inventada para esta historia.","ask":"¿cómo se siente cuando alguien te saluda con cariño, y a quién podríamos saludar?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 4:86","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 12","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"a-smile-is-sadaqa","game":"match","en":{"title":"a smile is a gift","word":"sadaqa","means":"a gift of kindness, given freely (صدقة)","story":[{"head":"a gift that costs no money","text":"Sadaqa means giving to help others. A companion named Abu Dharr reported that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ taught that sadaqa does not have to be money. Even your smile at someone is sadaqa."},{"head":"a smile and a helping hand","text":"Smiling at a friend, a grandparent or a classmate counts. So does showing the way to someone who is lost, or helping someone who cannot see well."},{"head":"a stone moved off the road","text":"If you see a stone, a thorn or a bone lying in the road, moving it out of the way so nobody trips is sadaqa too. It is small and quiet, and it helps everyone who walks by."},{"head":"water poured from your bucket","text":"Pouring some water from your own bucket into a friend's bucket is sadaqa as well. Every day is full of little chances to give, even when your pockets are empty."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each kind act.","pairs":[["a smile","sadaqa you give with your face"],["a stone in the road","move it so nobody trips"],["your bucket","share water with a friend"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a smile spreading across your face. breathe out slowly, like water pouring from one bucket into another. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll give away three smiles today","grownups":{"source":"a hadith in Jami' at-Tirmidhi (1956), reported by Abu Dharr, retold simply. At-Tirmidhi grades it hasan (good). A similar teaching about clearing harm from the road is in Sahih Muslim.","ask":"what is a kind thing you can give that costs no money?"}},"es":{"title":"una sonrisa es un regalo","word":"sadaqa","means":"un regalo de bondad, dado libremente (صدقة)","story":[{"head":"un regalo que no cuesta dinero","text":"Sadaqa quiere decir dar para ayudar a los demás. Un compañero llamado Abu Dharr contó que el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ enseñó que la sadaqa no tiene que ser dinero. Hasta tu sonrisa a alguien es sadaqa."},{"head":"una sonrisa y una mano amiga","text":"Sonreírle a un amigo, a un abuelo o a un compañero de clase cuenta. También cuenta mostrarle el camino a alguien que está perdido, o ayudar a alguien que no ve bien."},{"head":"una piedra quitada del camino","text":"Si ves una piedra, una espina o un hueso tirado en el camino, quitarlo para que nadie se tropiece también es sadaqa. Es algo pequeño y callado, y ayuda a todos los que pasan."},{"head":"agua de tu cubeta","text":"Echar un poco de agua de tu propia cubeta en la cubeta de un amigo también es sadaqa. Cada día está lleno de pequeñas oportunidades de dar, aunque tengas los bolsillos vacíos."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada acto amable.","pairs":[["una sonrisa","sadaqa que das con tu cara"],["una piedra en el camino","quítala para que nadie tropiece"],["tu cubeta","comparte agua con un amigo"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una sonrisa que se extiende por tu cara. suelta el aire despacio, como agua que pasa de una cubeta a otra. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy regalaré tres sonrisas","grownups":{"source":"un hadiz de Jami' at-Tirmidhi (1956), narrado por Abu Dharr, contado con sencillez. At-Tirmidhi lo califica como hasan (bueno). En Sahih Muslim hay una enseñanza parecida sobre quitar del camino lo que hace daño.","ask":"¿qué cosa amable puedes dar que no cueste dinero?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jami' at-Tirmidhi 1956","work":"Jami' at-Tirmidhi","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih Muslim (removing harm from the road)","work":"Sahih Muslim","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"planting-a-tree","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the tree that keeps on giving","word":"shajara","means":"a tree, in Arabic (شجرة)","story":[{"head":"a date seed by the door","text":"Grandma Sumayya pressed a date seed into the soil by her door. Her grandson Bilal watched. He asked why she was planting a tree that would take years and years to grow."},{"head":"a promise in a hadith","text":"She told him about a hadith. A companion named Anas reported that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ said that when a Muslim plants a tree or sows a seed, and a bird, a person or an animal eats from it, it counts as sadaqa, a gift, for the one who planted it."},{"head":"seasons of sun and rain","text":"Bilal watered the little shoot. It grew slowly, through many seasons of sun and rain, until it stood taller than the house, with dates hanging in heavy bunches."},{"head":"everyone comes to eat","text":"Birds came to peck at the dates. A goat nibbled the ones that fell. A tired traveler rested in the shade and ate a handful. Each one, Grandma said, was a gift from the tree, and from the hands that planted it."}],"game":{"items":[["Grandma Sumayya planted a date seed by her door.",true,"yes: and her grandson Bilal watched, and later watered the little shoot."],["the hadith says only people eating from your tree counts.",false,"not in it: a bird, a person or an animal eating from it all count as a gift."],["a goat nibbled the dates that fell.",true,"yes: birds, a goat and a tired traveler all ate from the tree."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a little shoot reaching up toward the sun. breathe out slowly, like a breeze moving through palm leaves. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll water a plant or plant a seed this week","grownups":{"source":"a hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (2320) and Sahih Muslim, reported by Anas ibn Malik, retold simply. Grandma Sumayya and Bilal are made up for this story.","ask":"what could we plant together, and who might enjoy it someday?"}},"es":{"title":"el árbol que no deja de dar","word":"shajara","means":"un árbol, en árabe (شجرة)","story":[{"head":"una semilla de dátil junto a la puerta","text":"La abuela Sumayya enterró una semilla de dátil junto a su puerta. Su nieto Bilal la miraba. Le preguntó por qué plantaba un árbol que tardaría años y años en crecer."},{"head":"una promesa en un hadiz","text":"Ella le habló de un hadiz. Un compañero llamado Anas contó que el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ dijo que cuando un musulmán planta un árbol o siembra una semilla, y un pájaro, una persona o un animal come de él, cuenta como sadaqa, un regalo, para quien lo plantó."},{"head":"temporadas de sol y lluvia","text":"Bilal regaba el pequeño brote. Creció despacio, durante muchas temporadas de sol y lluvia, hasta quedar más alto que la casa, con dátiles colgando en racimos pesados."},{"head":"todos vienen a comer","text":"Los pájaros venían a picotear los dátiles. Una cabra mordisqueaba los que caían. Un viajero cansado descansó a la sombra y comió un puñado. Cada uno, dijo la abuela, era un regalo del árbol, y de las manos que lo plantaron."}],"game":{"items":[["la abuela Sumayya plantó una semilla de dátil junto a su puerta.",true,"sí: y su nieto Bilal miraba, y después regaba el pequeño brote."],["el hadiz dice que solo cuenta si comen personas de tu árbol.",false,"no está: si come un pájaro, una persona o un animal, todo cuenta como regalo."],["una cabra mordisqueaba los dátiles que caían.",true,"sí: pájaros, una cabra y un viajero cansado comieron del árbol."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un brotecito que se estira hacia el sol. suelta el aire despacio, como la brisa que pasa entre las hojas de la palmera. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"esta semana regaré una planta o sembraré una semilla","grownups":{"source":"un hadiz de Sahih al-Bujari (2320) y Sahih Muslim, narrado por Anas ibn Malik, contado con sencillez. La abuela Sumayya y Bilal son inventados para esta historia.","ask":"¿qué podríamos plantar juntos, y quién podría disfrutarlo algún día?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 2320","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih Muslim (Anas, on planting)","work":"Sahih Muslim","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"at-home","game":"match","en":{"title":"what he did at home","word":"khidma","means":"service: helping the people around you (خدمة)","story":[{"head":"a question for Aisha","text":"A man named al-Aswad once asked Aisha, the wife of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, a question. What did the Prophet ﷺ do at home, he wanted to know, when he wasn't out among the people?"},{"head":"busy helping the family","text":"Aisha answered that at home he kept busy helping his family. He served the people he lived with, doing his part in the house like everyone else."},{"head":"time to go and pray","text":"And when the call to prayer came, Aisha said, he would get up and go out to pray. Helping at home and going to prayer, side by side, day after day."},{"head":"never a cross word","text":"Anas, who helped in the house of the Prophet ﷺ when he was a boy, reported something too. In all his years there, he said, the Prophet ﷺ never once grumbled at him, not even a little uff, and never asked crossly why he had done something."}],"game":{"prompt":"who told what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Aisha","told what he did at home"],["Anas","helped in the house as a boy"],["the call to prayer","time to go out and pray"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like folding a warm towel at home. breathe out slowly, like the call to prayer floating over the rooftops. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll help at home today without being asked","grownups":{"source":"hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari: Aisha's answer to al-Aswad (676), and Anas's memory of his years serving in the house (6038), retold simply.","ask":"what is one job at home you could do as a surprise this week?"}},"es":{"title":"lo que hacía en casa","word":"khidma","means":"servicio: ayudar a las personas que te rodean (خدمة)","story":[{"head":"una pregunta para Aisha","text":"Un hombre llamado al-Aswad le hizo una vez una pregunta a Aisha, la esposa del Profeta Muhammad ﷺ. Quería saber qué hacía el Profeta ﷺ en casa, cuando no estaba afuera con la gente."},{"head":"ocupado ayudando a la familia","text":"Aisha respondió que en casa se mantenía ocupado ayudando a su familia. Servía a las personas con quienes vivía, haciendo su parte en la casa como todos los demás."},{"head":"hora de ir a rezar","text":"Y cuando llegaba el llamado a la oración, dijo Aisha, se levantaba y salía a rezar. Ayudar en casa e ir a la oración, lado a lado, día tras día."},{"head":"nunca una palabra brusca","text":"Anas, que ayudaba en la casa del Profeta ﷺ cuando era niño, también contó algo. En todos sus años allí, dijo, el Profeta ﷺ nunca le refunfuñó, ni siquiera con un pequeño uf, y nunca le preguntó de mal modo por qué había hecho algo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién contó qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Aisha","contó lo que hacía en casa"],["Anas","ayudaba en la casa de niño"],["el llamado a la oración","hora de salir a rezar"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como quien dobla una toalla tibia en casa. suelta el aire despacio, como el llamado a la oración que flota sobre los techos. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy ayudaré en casa sin que me lo pidan","grownups":{"source":"hadices de Sahih al-Bujari: la respuesta de Aisha a al-Aswad (676), y el recuerdo de Anas de sus años sirviendo en la casa (6038), contados con sencillez.","ask":"¿qué tarea de la casa podrías hacer como sorpresa esta semana?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 676","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 6038","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"abu-umayr-bird","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Abu Umayr and his little bird","word":"nughayr","means":"a tiny bird, like a little sparrow (نغير)","story":[{"head":"a little brother and his pet","text":"Anas, who helped in the house of the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ, had a little brother named Abu Umayr. Abu Umayr was very small, and he had a pet: a tiny bird called a nughayr, which he loved to play with."},{"head":"a question at every visit","text":"Whenever the Prophet ﷺ came by, Anas reported, he would speak to the little boy, calling him by name and asking him, playfully, what his little bird had been up to."},{"head":"a game between friends","text":"It was a small joke between the two of them. A grown man with many things to do took time to ask a little boy about his pet. The bird mattered, because Abu Umayr mattered."},{"head":"greeting the children","text":"Anas also reported that when he walked past a group of children, he greeted them with salam, peace be upon you, because he had seen the Prophet ﷺ do the same."}],"game":{"items":[["Abu Umayr had a tiny pet bird.",true,"yes: a little bird called a nughayr that he loved to play with."],["the Prophet ﷺ asked Abu Umayr about his bird.",true,"yes: playfully, whenever he came by, he asked what the little bird had been up to."],["Abu Umayr's bird sang a song for every visitor.",false,"not in it: the story only says Abu Umayr loved to play with his little bird."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a little bird hopping closer. breathe out slowly, like a friendly hello to a small child. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll ask someone younger about something they love","grownups":{"source":"hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (6129) and Sahih Muslim (2150) about Abu Umayr and his bird, and in Sahih al-Bukhari (6247) about greeting children, all reported by Anas ibn Malik, retold simply.","ask":"what would you like a grown-up to ask you about?"}},"es":{"title":"Abu Umayr y su pajarito","word":"nughayr","means":"un pajarito pequeño, como un gorrioncito (نغير)","story":[{"head":"un hermanito y su mascota","text":"Anas, que ayudaba en la casa del Profeta Muhammad ﷺ, tenía un hermanito llamado Abu Umayr. Abu Umayr era muy pequeño, y tenía una mascota: un pajarito llamado nughayr, con el que le encantaba jugar."},{"head":"una pregunta en cada visita","text":"Cada vez que el Profeta ﷺ pasaba, contó Anas, le hablaba al niño, llamándolo por su nombre y preguntándole, en broma, qué había hecho su pajarito."},{"head":"un juego entre amigos","text":"Era una pequeña broma entre los dos. Un hombre con muchas cosas que hacer se tomaba el tiempo de preguntarle a un niño por su mascota. El pájaro importaba, porque Abu Umayr importaba."},{"head":"saludando a los niños","text":"Anas también contó que, cuando pasaba junto a un grupo de niños, los saludaba con salam, la paz sea contigo, porque había visto al Profeta ﷺ hacer lo mismo."}],"game":{"items":[["Abu Umayr tenía un pajarito de mascota.",true,"sí: un pajarito llamado nughayr con el que le encantaba jugar."],["el Profeta ﷺ le preguntaba a Abu Umayr por su pájaro.",true,"sí: en broma, cada vez que pasaba, le preguntaba qué había hecho el pajarito."],["el pájaro de Abu Umayr le cantaba una canción a cada visita.",false,"no está: la historia solo dice que a Abu Umayr le encantaba jugar con su pajarito."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un pajarito que se acerca saltando. suelta el aire despacio, como un hola amistoso a un niño pequeño. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"le preguntaré a alguien menor por algo que le encanta","grownups":{"source":"hadices de Sahih al-Bujari (6129) y Sahih Muslim (2150) sobre Abu Umayr y su pájaro, y de Sahih al-Bujari (6247) sobre saludar a los niños, todos narrados por Anas ibn Malik, contados con sencillez.","ask":"¿qué te gustaría que un adulto te preguntara?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 6129","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih Muslim 2150","work":"Sahih Muslim","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 6247","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"river-at-the-door","game":"match","en":{"title":"a river at the door","word":"wudu","means":"washing with clean water to get ready for prayer (وضوء)","story":[{"head":"the call to prayer","text":"Five times a day, many Muslims hear the call to prayer, the adhan. It might ring out from a mosque, or play softly from a phone. It means it is time to get ready."},{"head":"water for hands and face","text":"First comes wudu, washing with clean water. The Qur'an describes washing the face and the arms up to the elbows, wiping the head, and the feet. Many Muslims also rinse the mouth and nose."},{"head":"facing the Kaaba","text":"Then they face toward the Kaaba in Makkah, wherever they are in the world. They stand, bow, kneel with their foreheads to the ground, and sit, praising God."},{"head":"a river at your door","text":"Abu Hurayra reported that the Prophet Muhammad ﷺ once asked his companions whether any dirt would be left on someone who had a river by the door and washed in it five times every day. They said none would be left. He said the five prayers are like that, washing away wrongs."}],"game":{"prompt":"match the words to the prayer.","pairs":[["wudu","washing before prayer"],["the Kaaba","the way everyone faces"],["the river","a picture of the five prayers"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like cool water poured over your hands. breathe out slowly, like a river flowing past the door. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll wash my hands slowly and notice the water","grownups":{"source":"the Qur'an on wudu (5:6) and facing the Kaaba (2:144), and a hadith in Sahih al-Bukhari (528) and Sahih Muslim (667) reported by Abu Hurayra, retold simply. Sunni and Shia Muslims, and the schools of law, differ on some details of wudu and prayer.","ask":"what helps you feel fresh and ready to start again?"}},"es":{"title":"un río en la puerta","word":"wudu","means":"lavarse con agua limpia para prepararse para la oración (وضوء)","story":[{"head":"el llamado a la oración","text":"Cinco veces al día, muchos musulmanes escuchan el llamado a la oración, el adhan. Puede sonar desde una mezquita, o salir suave de un teléfono. Quiere decir que es hora de prepararse."},{"head":"agua para las manos y la cara","text":"Primero viene el wudu, lavarse con agua limpia. El Corán describe lavarse la cara y los brazos hasta los codos, pasarse la mano por la cabeza, y los pies. Muchos musulmanes también se enjuagan la boca y la nariz."},{"head":"mirando hacia la Kaaba","text":"Después se vuelven hacia la Kaaba en La Meca, estén donde estén en el mundo. Se ponen de pie, se inclinan, se arrodillan con la frente en el suelo y se sientan, alabando a Dios."},{"head":"un río en tu puerta","text":"Abu Hurayra contó que el Profeta Muhammad ﷺ preguntó una vez a sus compañeros si le quedaría algo de suciedad a alguien que tuviera un río junto a su puerta y se bañara en él cinco veces cada día. Dijeron que no quedaría nada. Él dijo que las cinco oraciones son así, lavan los errores."}],"game":{"prompt":"une las palabras con la oración.","pairs":[["wudu","lavarse antes de rezar"],["la Kaaba","hacia donde todos miran"],["el río","una imagen de las cinco oraciones"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como agua fresca que cae sobre tus manos. suelta el aire despacio, como un río que pasa frente a la puerta. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"me lavaré las manos despacio y notaré el agua","grownups":{"source":"el Corán sobre el wudu (5:6) y la Kaaba (2:144), y un hadiz de Sahih al-Bujari (528) y Sahih Muslim (667) narrado por Abu Hurayra, contado con sencillez. Los musulmanes sunitas y chiitas, y las escuelas de derecho, difieren en algunos detalles del wudu y la oración.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a sentirte fresco y listo para empezar de nuevo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Qur'an 5:6","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Qur'an 2:144","work":"The Qur'an","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih al-Bukhari 528","work":"Sahih al-Bukhari","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Sahih Muslim 667","work":"Sahih Muslim","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]},"BUDDHISM":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"BUDDHISM","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"baby-in-lumbini","game":"match","en":{"title":"the baby born in the Lumbini garden","word":"Vesak","means":"the full-moon festival when many Buddhists remember the Buddha","story":[{"head":"a queen on her way home","text":"Long ago, Queen Maya was traveling to her parents' home to have her baby. On the way, she stopped to rest in a garden called Lumbini. The sal trees were covered in flowers, and the bees were humming."},{"head":"a baby under the flowering tree","text":"The queen reached up and held a branch of a sal tree. Standing there in the garden, she gave birth to a baby boy. The old story says two gentle streams of water, one cool and one warm, poured from the sky to wash them."},{"head":"seven little steps","text":"Then something wonderful happened, the story says. The baby stood up, took seven steps, and looked all around him, in every direction. Later tellings add that a lotus flower opened under each little foot."},{"head":"lanterns on the full moon","text":"The baby was named Siddhartha. He grew up to become the Buddha, the awakened one. At a festival called Vesak, many Buddhists light lanterns, bring flowers to the temple and remember him. In some places, on his birthday, children pour sweet tea or water over a small statue of the baby Buddha."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Queen Maya","held a branch of a sal tree"],["the baby","took seven steps"],["Vesak","a festival with lanterns"]]},"breath":"let's take four slow breaths. breathe in slowly, like a lotus opening under a tiny foot. breathe out slowly. one breath for each careful step.","carry":"today I'll notice one lovely thing, like flowers in a garden.","grownups":{"source":"The birth in the Lumbini garden is told in the Nidanakatha, the life-story at the start of the Jataka commentary. The seven steps and the streams of water appear in the Majjhima Nikaya (MN 123). The lotuses under his feet come from later tellings.","ask":"how does our family celebrate when a new baby is born?"}},"es":{"title":"el bebé nacido en el jardín de Lumbini","word":"Vesak","means":"la fiesta de luna llena en que muchos budistas recuerdan al Buda","story":[{"head":"una reina de camino a casa","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, la reina Maya viajaba a la casa de sus padres para tener a su bebé. En el camino se detuvo a descansar en un jardín llamado Lumbini. Los árboles de sal estaban cubiertos de flores y las abejas zumbaban."},{"head":"un bebé bajo el árbol en flor","text":"La reina levantó el brazo y se sostuvo de una rama de un árbol de sal. Allí, de pie en el jardín, dio a luz a un niño. La antigua historia dice que dos chorros suaves de agua, uno fresco y otro tibio, cayeron del cielo para lavarlos."},{"head":"siete pasitos","text":"Entonces pasó algo maravilloso, dice la historia. El bebé se puso de pie, dio siete pasos y miró a su alrededor, en todas las direcciones. Relatos posteriores cuentan que una flor de loto se abrió bajo cada piecito."},{"head":"faroles en la luna llena","text":"Al bebé lo llamaron Siddhartha. Creció y llegó a ser el Buda, el que despertó. En una fiesta llamada Vesak, muchos budistas encienden faroles, llevan flores al templo y lo recuerdan. En algunos lugares, en su cumpleaños, los niños vierten té dulce o agua sobre una estatuita del Buda bebé."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la reina Maya","se sostuvo de una rama de sal"],["el bebé","dio siete pasos"],["Vesak","una fiesta con faroles"]]},"breath":"hagamos cuatro respiraciones lentas. inhala despacio, como un loto que se abre bajo un piecito. exhala despacio. una respiración por cada paso con cuidado.","carry":"hoy me fijaré en algo bonito, como las flores de un jardín.","grownups":{"source":"El nacimiento en el jardín de Lumbini se cuenta en el Nidanakatha, la historia de vida al comienzo del comentario de los Jataka. Los siete pasos y los chorros de agua aparecen en el Majjhima Nikaya (MN 123). Los lotos bajo sus pies vienen de relatos posteriores.","ask":"¿cómo celebra nuestra familia cuando nace un bebé?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Majjhima Nikaya 123 (Acchariya-abbhuta Sutta)","work":"Majjhima Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Nidanakatha (the Avidure-nidana: birth at Lumbini)","work":"Jataka commentary, introduction","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"siddhartha-swan","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Prince Siddhartha and the wounded swan","word":"compassion","means":"feeling for someone who is hurting, and wanting to help","story":[{"head":"swans over the palace garden","text":"Young Prince Siddhartha was in the palace garden with his cousin Devadatta. High above them, a line of white swans flew across the blue sky. Devadatta had a bow and arrows, and he aimed at the swans."},{"head":"a hurt swan falls","text":"One arrow struck a swan, and it tumbled down into the garden, hurt and frightened. Siddhartha reached it first. He held it gently in his lap, drew out the arrow, and laid cool leaves and honey on the wound."},{"head":"two cousins want one swan","text":"Devadatta came looking for the bird. “That swan is mine,” he said. “My arrow brought it down.” Siddhartha held it close. “It is mine,” he said, “because I saved its life.” Neither cousin would give in."},{"head":"the wise ones decide","text":"So they asked the wise people of the court to decide. They said that the one who saves a life has more right to it than the one who tried to take it. The swan stayed with Siddhartha. In many tellings, he cared for it until it could fly free."}],"game":{"items":[["Devadatta's arrow struck a swan.",true,"yes: the swan tumbled down into the palace garden, hurt."],["Siddhartha wrapped the swan in a soft blanket.",false,"not in it: he drew out the arrow and laid cool leaves and honey on the wound."],["wise people decided the swan should stay with the one who saved it.",true,"yes: saving a life gives more right than trying to take it."]]},"breath":"take four slow breaths, soft and gentle, like the prince holding the swan in his lap until it felt safe again.","carry":"I can be gentle with every animal I meet today.","grownups":{"source":"This is a later telling of the Buddha's childhood, not found in the oldest Pali texts. Edwin Arnold's poem The Light of Asia (1879) tells it, and it is often told to children. Tellings differ in small details.","ask":"if we found a hurt animal, what could we do together to help it?"}},"es":{"title":"el príncipe Siddhartha y el cisne herido","word":"compasión","means":"sentir con alguien que sufre y querer ayudarlo","story":[{"head":"cisnes sobre el jardín del palacio","text":"El joven príncipe Siddhartha estaba en el jardín del palacio con su primo Devadatta. Muy alto, una fila de cisnes blancos cruzaba el cielo azul. Devadatta tenía un arco y flechas, y apuntó a los cisnes."},{"head":"cae un cisne herido","text":"Una flecha alcanzó a un cisne, y el ave cayó al jardín, herida y asustada. Siddhartha llegó primero. Lo sostuvo con cuidado en su regazo, le sacó la flecha y le puso hojas frescas y miel sobre la herida."},{"head":"dos primos quieren un cisne","text":"Devadatta vino a buscar el ave. “Ese cisne es mío”, dijo. “Mi flecha lo bajó.” Siddhartha lo abrazó. “Es mío”, dijo, “porque le salvé la vida.” Ninguno de los dos primos quería ceder."},{"head":"los sabios deciden","text":"Así que pidieron a los sabios de la corte que decidieran. Ellos dijeron que quien salva una vida tiene más derecho a ella que quien intentó quitarla. El cisne se quedó con Siddhartha. En muchos relatos, lo cuidó hasta que pudo volar libre."}],"game":{"items":[["la flecha de Devadatta alcanzó a un cisne.",true,"sí: el cisne cayó al jardín del palacio, herido."],["Siddhartha envolvió al cisne en una manta suave.",false,"no está: le sacó la flecha y le puso hojas frescas y miel en la herida."],["los sabios decidieron que el cisne se quedara con quien lo salvó.",true,"sí: salvar una vida da más derecho que intentar quitarla."]]},"breath":"haz cuatro respiraciones lentas, suaves y tranquilas, como el príncipe que sostenía al cisne en su regazo hasta que se sintió seguro.","carry":"hoy puedo ser amable con cada animal que encuentre.","grownups":{"source":"Este es un relato posterior de la infancia del Buda, que no está en los textos pali más antiguos. El poema de Edwin Arnold La luz de Asia (1879) lo cuenta, y se cuenta mucho a los niños. Los relatos varían en detalles.","ask":"si encontráramos un animal herido, ¿qué podríamos hacer juntos para ayudarlo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Book the First (the swan)","work":"The Light of Asia, Edwin Arnold (1879)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"sujata-rice-milk","game":"match","en":{"title":"Sujata's bowl of rice-milk","word":"Middle Way","means":"the path between too much and too little","story":[{"head":"a very thin traveler","text":"Siddhartha left the palace to understand why people suffer and how they can be free. For a long time he ate almost nothing, hoping it would help him understand. He grew so thin and weak that he could hardly stand."},{"head":"a gift under the banyan tree","text":"He saw that going hungry was not the way. He sat down to rest beneath a big banyan tree. A young woman named Sujata came with a golden bowl full of rice cooked in sweet milk, and she offered it to him with joy."},{"head":"strength comes back","text":"Siddhartha took the bowl to the riverbank and ate the rice-milk. Slowly his strength came back. Not too much and not too little: many Buddhists call this in-between path the Middle Way."},{"head":"a bowl floats upstream","text":"When he had eaten, he set the golden bowl on the river. “If I am going to wake up,” he said, “let this bowl float upstream.” And the story says the little bowl floated up the river, against the current!"}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Sujata","brought a golden bowl"],["Siddhartha","rested under a banyan tree"],["the bowl","floated up the river"]]},"breath":"breathe in and out slowly, four times. not too fast, not too slow, like the golden bowl drifting gently on the river.","carry":"today I'll thank someone who makes food for me.","grownups":{"source":"Sujata's gift and the floating bowl come from the Nidanakatha, the life-story at the start of the Jataka commentary. The early suttas say he gave up harsh fasting and ate again (Majjhima Nikaya 36). The Middle Way is named in the first teaching (Samyutta Nikaya 56.11).","ask":"who makes food for you, and how could we thank them this week?"}},"es":{"title":"el tazón de arroz con leche de Sujata","word":"Camino Medio","means":"el camino entre demasiado y muy poco","story":[{"head":"un viajero muy delgado","text":"Siddhartha dejó el palacio para entender por qué la gente sufre y cómo puede ser libre. Durante mucho tiempo casi no comió, pensando que eso lo ayudaría a entender. Se puso tan delgado y débil que casi no podía mantenerse en pie."},{"head":"un regalo bajo el baniano","text":"Vio que pasar hambre no era el camino. Se sentó a descansar bajo un gran árbol baniano. Una joven llamada Sujata llegó con un tazón de oro lleno de arroz cocido en leche dulce, y se lo ofreció con alegría."},{"head":"vuelven las fuerzas","text":"Siddhartha llevó el tazón a la orilla del río y comió el arroz con leche. Poco a poco recuperó las fuerzas. Ni demasiado ni muy poco: muchos budistas llaman a este camino de en medio el Camino Medio."},{"head":"un tazón río arriba","text":"Cuando terminó, puso el tazón de oro sobre el río. “Si voy a despertar”, dijo, “que este tazón flote río arriba.” ¡Y la historia dice que el tazoncito flotó río arriba, contra la corriente!"}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Sujata","trajo un tazón de oro"],["Siddhartha","descansó bajo un baniano"],["el tazón","flotó río arriba"]]},"breath":"inhala y exhala despacio, cuatro veces. ni muy rápido ni muy lento, como el tazón de oro que flota suavemente en el río.","carry":"hoy le daré las gracias a quien me prepara la comida.","grownups":{"source":"El regalo de Sujata y el tazón que flota vienen del Nidanakatha, la historia de vida al comienzo del comentario de los Jataka. Los suttas antiguos dicen que dejó el ayuno duro y volvió a comer (Majjhima Nikaya 36). El Camino Medio se nombra en la primera enseñanza (Samyutta Nikaya 56.11).","ask":"¿quién te prepara la comida, y cómo podríamos darle las gracias esta semana?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Nidanakatha (the Avidure-nidana: Sujata's offering)","work":"Jataka commentary, introduction","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Majjhima Nikaya 36 (Mahasaccaka Sutta)","work":"Majjhima Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Samyutta Nikaya 56.11 (Dhammacakkappavattana Sutta)","work":"Samyutta Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":4,"key":"bodhi-tree","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the night under the Bodhi tree","word":"Bodhi","means":"awakening; the Bodhi tree is the tree where the Buddha woke up","story":[{"head":"a seat of soft grass","text":"That evening, Siddhartha came to a great fig tree. A grass-cutter gave him bundles of soft grass, and he made a seat beneath the tree. He sat down and decided not to get up until he understood."},{"head":"worries and wishes from Mara","text":"In the story, a being named Mara came to make him give up. Mara sent wind and rain, worries and wishes, and said, “That seat is not yours!” Siddhartha stayed calm and still."},{"head":"a hand touches the earth","text":"“Who will say the seat is yours?” Mara asked. Siddhartha quietly reached down and touched the ground with his hand. The story says the earth itself answered for him, and Mara went away."},{"head":"awake at dawn","text":"All night he sat in peace, looking deeply at how things really are. As the dawn came, he woke up to the truth. From then on, people called him the Buddha, the awakened one."}],"game":{"items":[["Siddhartha sat on a seat of soft grass.",true,"yes: a grass-cutter gave him the grass for his seat."],["a lion lay down beside him to keep him company.",false,"not in it: he sat beneath the tree, calm and still, all night."],["Siddhartha touched the ground with his hand.",true,"yes: the story says the earth itself answered for him."]]},"breath":"sit tall and still, like Siddhartha under the tree. take four slow breaths, and feel the ground holding you up.","carry":"when I feel wobbly, I can touch the ground and breathe.","grownups":{"source":"The awakening is told in the early Pali suttas (for example Majjhima Nikaya 26 and 36). The grass seat, Mara's challenge and the hand touching the earth come from the Nidanakatha, the life-story at the start of the Jataka commentary.","ask":"what helps you feel calm and steady when you're worried?"}},"es":{"title":"la noche bajo el árbol Bodhi","word":"Bodhi","means":"despertar; el árbol Bodhi es donde el Buda despertó","story":[{"head":"un asiento de pasto suave","text":"Esa tarde, Siddhartha llegó a una gran higuera. Un cortador de pasto le dio unos manojos de pasto suave, y él se hizo un asiento bajo el árbol. Se sentó y decidió no levantarse hasta entender."},{"head":"las preocupaciones de Mara","text":"En la historia, un ser llamado Mara vino para que se rindiera. Mara mandó viento y lluvia, preocupaciones y deseos, y le dijo: “¡Ese asiento no es tuyo!” Siddhartha se quedó tranquilo y quieto."},{"head":"una mano toca la tierra","text":"“¿Quién dirá que el asiento es tuyo?”, preguntó Mara. Siddhartha bajó la mano en silencio y tocó el suelo. La historia dice que la tierra misma respondió por él, y Mara se fue."},{"head":"despierto al amanecer","text":"Toda la noche se quedó sentado en paz, mirando a fondo cómo son las cosas de verdad. Al llegar el amanecer, despertó a la verdad. Desde entonces lo llamaron el Buda, el que despertó."}],"game":{"items":[["Siddhartha se sentó en un asiento de pasto suave.",true,"sí: un cortador de pasto le dio el pasto para su asiento."],["un león se acostó a su lado para acompañarlo.",false,"no está: se quedó bajo el árbol, tranquilo y quieto, toda la noche."],["Siddhartha tocó el suelo con la mano.",true,"sí: la historia dice que la tierra misma respondió por él."]]},"breath":"siéntate derecho y quieto, como Siddhartha bajo el árbol. haz cuatro respiraciones lentas y siente cómo el suelo te sostiene.","carry":"cuando me sienta inquieto, puedo tocar el suelo y respirar.","grownups":{"source":"El despertar se cuenta en los suttas pali antiguos (por ejemplo, Majjhima Nikaya 26 y 36). El asiento de pasto, el desafío de Mara y la mano que toca la tierra vienen del Nidanakatha, la historia de vida al comienzo del comentario de los Jataka.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a sentirte tranquilo y firme cuando estás preocupado?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Majjhima Nikaya 26 (Ariyapariyesana Sutta) and 36 (Mahasaccaka Sutta)","work":"Majjhima Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Nidanakatha (the Avidure-nidana: the seat, Mara, the earth as witness)","work":"Jataka commentary, introduction","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"hare-in-the-moon","game":"match","en":{"title":"the hare in the moon","word":"Jataka","means":"the old tales of the Buddha's past lives","story":[{"head":"four friends in the forest","text":"This is a Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives. Long ago, he was born as a hare. He lived in a forest with three friends: a monkey, a jackal and an otter. The hare taught them to be kind and to share."},{"head":"a hungry traveler comes by","text":"On a holy day, a tired, hungry traveler came by. The otter brought him fish. The jackal brought food he had found. The monkey brought sweet mangoes. But the hare ate only grass, and grass is no meal for a traveler."},{"head":"the hare's great gift","text":"“I have nothing to give but myself,” said the hare. “Please light a fire.” The hare shook his fur three times, so no tiny creature living there would be hurt, and leaped into the flames. But the fire felt as cool as snow!"},{"head":"a picture on the moon","text":"The traveler was really Sakka, king of the gods, testing the hare's kindness. He lifted the hare out safely and drew his picture on the moon, so everyone would remember. On a full moon, many people say you can still see the hare."}],"game":{"prompt":"who gave what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the otter","brought fish"],["the monkey","brought sweet mangoes"],["the hare","offered himself"]]},"breath":"look up at the sky, or picture the round full moon. breathe in slowly, breathe out slowly, four times, and imagine the gentle hare resting there.","carry":"tonight I'll look for the hare in the moon.","grownups":{"source":"A Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives: the Sasa Jataka (no. 316), in Cowell's The Jataka (1895). The traveler is Sakka, king of the gods.","ask":"what is something small you could share with someone today?"}},"es":{"title":"la liebre en la luna","word":"Jataka","means":"los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda","story":[{"head":"cuatro amigos en el bosque","text":"Este es un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda. Hace mucho tiempo, nació como liebre. Vivía en un bosque con tres amigos: un mono, un chacal y una nutria. La liebre les enseñaba a ser amables y a compartir."},{"head":"llega un viajero con hambre","text":"Un día sagrado, llegó un viajero cansado y con hambre. La nutria le trajo pescado. El chacal le trajo comida que había encontrado. El mono le trajo mangos dulces. Pero la liebre solo comía pasto, y el pasto no es comida para un viajero."},{"head":"el gran regalo de la liebre","text":"“No tengo nada que dar más que a mí misma”, dijo la liebre. “Por favor, enciende un fuego.” La liebre se sacudió el pelo tres veces, para que ningún bichito que viviera allí saliera herido, y saltó a las llamas. ¡Pero el fuego se sentía fresco como la nieve!"},{"head":"un dibujo en la luna","text":"El viajero era en realidad Sakka, rey de los dioses, que ponía a prueba la bondad de la liebre. La sacó sana y salva y dibujó su figura en la luna, para que todos la recordaran. En luna llena, mucha gente dice que aún se ve la liebre."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién dio qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la nutria","trajo pescado"],["el mono","trajo mangos dulces"],["la liebre","se ofreció a sí misma"]]},"breath":"mira el cielo, o imagina la luna llena y redonda. inhala despacio, exhala despacio, cuatro veces, e imagina a la liebre tranquila descansando allí.","carry":"esta noche buscaré la liebre en la luna.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda: el Sasa Jataka (n.º 316), en The Jataka de Cowell (1895). El viajero es Sakka, rey de los dioses.","ask":"¿qué cosa pequeña podrías compartir hoy con alguien?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jataka no. 316 (Sasa Jataka)","work":"The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell (1895)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"monkey-king-bridge","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the monkey king who made himself a bridge","word":"Bodhisatta","means":"one who is on the way to becoming a Buddha","story":[{"head":"a mango tree by the river","text":"This is a Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives. Long ago, he was born as a monkey king. He led a big troop of monkeys who loved the sweet mangoes of a great tree beside the river Ganges."},{"head":"the king's men surround the tree","text":"One day a human king tasted one of those mangoes and loved it. He came with his men to find the tree. They saw the monkeys and surrounded it, planning to catch them in the morning. The monkeys were afraid."},{"head":"a long bamboo cane","text":"The monkey king leaped across the river. He tied one end of a long bamboo cane to a tree and the other end around his waist, and leaped back. But the cane was a little too short. So he held on to a branch and stretched his own body to make the bridge."},{"head":"everyone crosses safely","text":"One by one, the monkeys ran across his back to safety on the far side. The human king watched in wonder: this monkey cared more for his troop than for himself. He had the monkey king gently lifted down and looked after."}],"game":{"items":[["the monkeys loved the mangoes of a tree by the river.",true,"yes: a great mango tree beside the river Ganges."],["the monkey king built a boat for his troop.",false,"not in it: he stretched his own body to make a bridge."],["the human king watched the monkeys cross.",true,"yes: he watched in wonder and had the monkey king gently lifted down."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly and stretch tall, like the monkey bridge. breathe out slowly as each little monkey reaches the other side. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I can help someone get where they need to go.","grownups":{"source":"A Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives: the Mahakapi Jataka (no. 407), in Cowell's The Jataka (1895). In the full telling the monkey king is badly hurt and dies after teaching the human king about caring for his people; this telling ends before that.","ask":"what does a good leader do for the people in their care?"}},"es":{"title":"el rey mono que se hizo puente","word":"Bodhisatta","means":"alguien que va en camino de llegar a ser un Buda","story":[{"head":"un mango junto al río","text":"Este es un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda. Hace mucho tiempo, nació como rey de los monos. Guiaba a una gran tropa de monos que amaban los mangos dulces de un gran árbol junto al río Ganges."},{"head":"los hombres del rey rodean el árbol","text":"Un día, un rey humano probó uno de esos mangos y le encantó. Vino con sus hombres a buscar el árbol. Vieron a los monos y rodearon el árbol, pensando atraparlos en la mañana. Los monos tenían miedo."},{"head":"una larga caña de bambú","text":"El rey mono saltó al otro lado del río. Ató una punta de una larga caña de bambú a un árbol y la otra a su cintura, y saltó de regreso. Pero la caña era un poco corta. Así que se agarró de una rama y estiró su propio cuerpo para hacer el puente."},{"head":"todos cruzan a salvo","text":"Uno por uno, los monos corrieron sobre su espalda hasta la otra orilla, a salvo. El rey humano miraba asombrado: este mono se preocupaba más por su tropa que por sí mismo. Mandó que bajaran al rey mono con cuidado y que lo atendieran."}],"game":{"items":[["los monos amaban los mangos de un árbol junto al río.",true,"sí: un gran mango junto al río Ganges."],["el rey mono construyó un bote para su tropa.",false,"no está: estiró su propio cuerpo para hacer un puente."],["el rey humano vio cruzar a los monos.",true,"sí: miraba asombrado y mandó bajar al rey mono con cuidado."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio y estírate, como el puente del mono. exhala despacio mientras cada monito llega al otro lado. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo ayudar a alguien a llegar a donde necesita.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda: el Mahakapi Jataka (n.º 407), en The Jataka de Cowell (1895). En el relato completo el rey mono queda muy herido y muere después de enseñar al rey humano a cuidar a su pueblo; este relato termina antes.","ask":"¿qué hace un buen líder por las personas a su cargo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jataka no. 407 (Mahakapi Jataka)","work":"The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell (1895)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":7,"key":"kindness-to-all-beings","game":"match","en":{"title":"kindness for all beings","word":"metta","means":"loving-kindness: wishing every living being well","story":[{"head":"monks go to a quiet forest","text":"Once, a group of monks went to a forest to sit and practice during the rainy season. Each monk chose a tree to sit beneath. But the spirits who lived in those trees felt crowded out of their homes."},{"head":"strange sounds at night","text":"So the tree spirits made strange sounds and smells to send the monks away. The monks could not settle down. They walked all the way back to the Buddha and asked him what to do."},{"head":"like a mother with her child","text":"The Buddha taught them a teaching about kindness. Just as a mother would protect her only child, he said, let your kind heart reach out to every living being: big or small, near or far, seen or unseen."},{"head":"a friendly forest","text":"The monks went back, wishing well to every creature as they walked. The tree spirits felt that kindness, and the forest grew peaceful and friendly. Many Buddhists still chant these words, called the Metta Sutta."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the monks","went to sit in the forest"],["the tree spirits","made strange sounds"],["the Buddha","taught kindness for all"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly. as you breathe out, send a kind wish: to someone near, someone far, a tiny bug, a big whale. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll wish three creatures well today: may you be happy.","grownups":{"source":"The teaching is the Karaniya Metta Sutta (Sutta Nipata 1.8), paraphrased here; many Buddhists chant it. The story of the monks and the tree spirits comes from the old commentary on that sutta.","ask":"who is someone far away we could send a kind wish to tonight?"}},"es":{"title":"bondad para todos los seres","word":"metta","means":"bondad amorosa: desear el bien a todo ser vivo","story":[{"head":"unos monjes van a un bosque tranquilo","text":"Una vez, un grupo de monjes fue a un bosque a sentarse y practicar durante la temporada de lluvias. Cada monje eligió un árbol para sentarse debajo. Pero los espíritus que vivían en esos árboles sintieron que les quitaban su casa."},{"head":"ruidos extraños de noche","text":"Entonces los espíritus de los árboles hicieron ruidos y olores extraños para que los monjes se fueran. Los monjes no podían calmarse. Caminaron de regreso hasta el Buda y le preguntaron qué hacer."},{"head":"como una madre con su hijo","text":"El Buda les dio una enseñanza sobre la bondad. Así como una madre protegería a su único hijo, dijo, deja que tu corazón bondadoso llegue a todo ser vivo: grande o pequeño, cerca o lejos, visto o no visto."},{"head":"un bosque amigable","text":"Los monjes regresaron, deseando el bien a cada criatura mientras caminaban. Los espíritus de los árboles sintieron esa bondad, y el bosque se volvió tranquilo y amigable. Muchos budistas todavía cantan estas palabras, llamadas el Metta Sutta."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["los monjes","fueron a sentarse al bosque"],["los espíritus","hicieron ruidos extraños"],["el Buda","enseñó bondad para todos"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio. al exhalar, envía un deseo bondadoso: a alguien cerca, a alguien lejos, a un bichito, a una gran ballena. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy le desearé el bien a tres criaturas: que seas feliz.","grownups":{"source":"La enseñanza es el Karaniya Metta Sutta (Sutta Nipata 1.8), en paráfrasis; muchos budistas lo cantan. La historia de los monjes y los espíritus de los árboles viene del antiguo comentario de ese sutta.","ask":"¿a quién que esté lejos podríamos enviarle un deseo bondadoso esta noche?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Sutta Nipata 1.8 (Karaniya Metta Sutta)","work":"Sutta Nipata","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"commentary on Sutta Nipata 1.8 (Paramatthajotika)","work":"Sutta Nipata commentary","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"three-friends-banyan","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the partridge, the monkey and the elephant","word":"respect","means":"treating someone as important and listening to them","story":[{"head":"three friends under a banyan","text":"This is a Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives. Under a great banyan tree lived three friends: a partridge, a monkey and an elephant. But they didn't listen to each other very much."},{"head":"the elephant remembers","text":"“Let's find out which of us is the oldest,” they said, “and we will listen to that friend.” The elephant said, “When I was a baby, this tree was a little bush. Its top leaves tickled my tummy as I walked over it.”"},{"head":"the monkey remembers","text":"The monkey said, “When I was a baby, I sat on the ground and nibbled the very top shoots of this tree. That's how small it was.”"},{"head":"a seed the partridge dropped","text":"The partridge said, “Long ago, I ate a fruit from another tree and dropped its seed right here. This whole banyan grew from that seed!” So the partridge was the oldest. The monkey and the elephant honored him and listened to his good advice, and the three friends lived happily together."}],"game":{"items":[["the elephant said the tree once tickled his tummy.",true,"yes: when he was a baby, the tree was a little bush."],["the monkey said he planted the banyan seed.",false,"not in it: the partridge dropped the seed; the monkey nibbled the top shoots."],["the friends agreed to listen to the oldest.",true,"yes: they honored the partridge and listened to his advice."]]},"breath":"picture a tiny seed growing into a giant banyan tree. breathe in slowly as it grows, breathe out slowly as it spreads its branches. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll listen carefully to someone older than me.","grownups":{"source":"A Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives: the Tittira Jataka (no. 37), in Cowell's The Jataka (1895). In the story the wise partridge is the Buddha in a past life.","ask":"who is the oldest person you know, and what would you like to ask them?"}},"es":{"title":"la perdiz, el mono y el elefante","word":"respeto","means":"tratar a alguien como importante y escucharlo","story":[{"head":"tres amigos bajo un baniano","text":"Este es un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda. Bajo un gran baniano vivían tres amigos: una perdiz, un mono y un elefante. Pero no se escuchaban mucho entre ellos."},{"head":"el elefante recuerda","text":"“Averigüemos quién de nosotros es el mayor”, dijeron, “y escucharemos a ese amigo.” El elefante dijo: “Cuando yo era bebé, este árbol era un arbusto. Sus hojitas de arriba me hacían cosquillas en la panza cuando pasaba por encima.”"},{"head":"el mono recuerda","text":"El mono dijo: “Cuando yo era bebé, me sentaba en el suelo y mordisqueaba los brotes de arriba de este árbol. Así de pequeño era.”"},{"head":"una semilla que dejó la perdiz","text":"La perdiz dijo: “Hace mucho, comí una fruta de otro árbol y dejé caer su semilla justo aquí. ¡Todo este baniano creció de esa semilla!” Así que la perdiz era la mayor. El mono y el elefante la honraron y escucharon sus buenos consejos, y los tres amigos vivieron felices."}],"game":{"items":[["el elefante dijo que el árbol le hacía cosquillas en la panza.",true,"sí: cuando era bebé, el árbol era un arbusto."],["el mono dijo que él plantó la semilla del baniano.",false,"no está: la perdiz dejó caer la semilla; el mono mordisqueaba los brotes."],["los amigos acordaron escuchar al mayor.",true,"sí: honraron a la perdiz y escucharon sus consejos."]]},"breath":"imagina una semillita que crece hasta ser un baniano gigante. inhala despacio mientras crece, exhala despacio mientras abre sus ramas. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy escucharé con atención a alguien mayor que yo.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda: el Tittira Jataka (n.º 37), en The Jataka de Cowell (1895). En la historia, la perdiz sabia es el Buda en una vida pasada.","ask":"¿quién es la persona más mayor que conoces, y qué te gustaría preguntarle?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jataka no. 37 (Tittira Jataka)","work":"The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell (1895)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":9,"key":"quails-lift-the-net","game":"match","en":{"title":"the quails who lifted the net together","word":"harmony","means":"getting along, so everyone can work together","story":[{"head":"a bird-catcher's net","text":"This is a Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives. Long ago, he was a wise quail who led a great flock. A bird-catcher kept throwing his net over the quails to catch them."},{"head":"heads through the holes","text":"The wise quail had a plan. “When the net falls,” he said, “each of us puts our head through a hole in it. Then we all fly up together at the same moment, carrying the net with us.”"},{"head":"up and away with the net","text":"The next time the net came down, they did just that. Whoosh! The whole flock lifted the net high, flew to a thorny bush and dropped it there. Then they slipped out underneath, free and safe."},{"head":"a quarrel on the ground","text":"One day, one quail stepped on another quail's head by mistake. They began to argue: “You lift the net, then!” The wise quail saw that quarreling would make them weak. He flew to a new home with all the quails who wanted to stay friends."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the bird-catcher","threw his net"],["the flock","lifted the net together"],["the wise quail","flew to a new home"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, all together, like the quails rising with the net. breathe out slowly, like the net dropping onto the bush. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll work with someone instead of arguing.","grownups":{"source":"A Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives: the Sammodamana Jataka (no. 33), in Cowell's The Jataka (1895). In the full telling, the quails who keep quarreling are caught; this telling leaves that out.","ask":"what is something our family can only do when we all work together?"}},"es":{"title":"las codornices que levantaron la red juntas","word":"armonía","means":"llevarse bien, para que todos puedan trabajar juntos","story":[{"head":"la red de un cazador de pájaros","text":"Este es un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda. Hace mucho tiempo, era una codorniz sabia que guiaba a una gran bandada. Un cazador de pájaros lanzaba su red sobre las codornices para atraparlas."},{"head":"cabezas por los agujeros","text":"La codorniz sabia tenía un plan. “Cuando caiga la red”, dijo, “cada una mete la cabeza por un agujero. Luego volamos todas juntas al mismo tiempo, llevándonos la red.”"},{"head":"arriba y lejos con la red","text":"La siguiente vez que cayó la red, hicieron justo eso. ¡Fiuuu! Toda la bandada levantó la red muy alto, voló hasta un arbusto de espinas y la dejó caer allí. Luego salieron por debajo, libres y a salvo."},{"head":"una pelea en el suelo","text":"Un día, una codorniz pisó sin querer la cabeza de otra. Empezaron a discutir: “¡Pues levanta tú la red!” La codorniz sabia vio que pelear las haría débiles. Voló a un nuevo hogar con todas las codornices que querían seguir siendo amigas."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el cazador de pájaros","lanzaba su red"],["la bandada","levantó la red junta"],["la codorniz sabia","voló a un nuevo hogar"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, todos juntos, como las codornices que suben con la red. exhala despacio, como la red que cae sobre el arbusto. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy trabajaré con alguien en vez de discutir.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda: el Sammodamana Jataka (n.º 33), en The Jataka de Cowell (1895). En el relato completo, las codornices que siguen peleando son atrapadas; este relato lo deja fuera.","ask":"¿qué puede hacer nuestra familia solo cuando todos trabajamos juntos?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jataka no. 33 (Sammodamana Jataka)","work":"The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell (1895)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"blind-men-elephant","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the elephant and the people who couldn't see","word":"point of view","means":"how something looks from where you are","story":[{"head":"a king's curious idea","text":"The Buddha once told this story. Long ago, a king asked for an elephant to be brought to his palace. Then he gathered some people who had been blind from birth, and he asked each of them to touch it."},{"head":"each one touches one part","text":"One touched the elephant's head. One touched an ear. Others felt a tusk, the trunk, a leg, the tail. “Now tell me,” said the king, “what is an elephant like?”"},{"head":"a pot, a basket, a post","text":"The one who touched the head said, “An elephant is like a big pot!” The one who touched the ear said, “No, it's like a basket for sorting grain!” The one who felt a leg said, “It's like a post!”"},{"head":"everyone knows a little","text":"They argued and argued, each one sure he was right. The Buddha said people can be like this: each knows one part and thinks it is the whole. Each of them was right about a little, and none of them had the whole elephant."}],"game":{"items":[["one person touched an ear and said it was like a basket.",true,"yes: a basket for sorting grain."],["one person climbed up and rode on the elephant's back.",false,"not in it: each one only touched one part with their hands."],["the Buddha was the one who told this story.",true,"yes: he told it about people who each know one part and argue about the whole."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly and notice one sound you can hear. breathe out. notice another. four slow breaths, four new things noticed.","carry":"before I argue, I'll ask: what do you see?","grownups":{"source":"The Buddha tells this parable in the Udana (6.4), an early collection of his sayings, about people who argue when each holds only one part of the truth. Paraphrased here.","ask":"when did you and someone else see the same thing in different ways?"}},"es":{"title":"el elefante y las personas que no podían ver","word":"punto de vista","means":"cómo se ve algo desde donde tú estás","story":[{"head":"una idea curiosa de un rey","text":"El Buda contó una vez esta historia. Hace mucho tiempo, un rey pidió que trajeran un elefante a su palacio. Luego reunió a unas personas que eran ciegas de nacimiento, y les pidió a cada una que lo tocara."},{"head":"cada uno toca una parte","text":"Uno tocó la cabeza del elefante. Otro tocó una oreja. Otros tocaron un colmillo, la trompa, una pata, la cola. “Ahora díganme”, dijo el rey, “¿cómo es un elefante?”"},{"head":"una olla, una canasta, un poste","text":"El que tocó la cabeza dijo: “¡Un elefante es como una olla grande!” El que tocó la oreja dijo: “¡No, es como una canasta para limpiar el grano!” El que tocó una pata dijo: “¡Es como un poste!”"},{"head":"cada uno sabe un poco","text":"Discutieron y discutieron, cada uno seguro de tener razón. El Buda dijo que la gente a veces es así: cada uno conoce una parte y cree que es el todo. Cada uno tenía un poco de razón, y ninguno tenía el elefante entero."}],"game":{"items":[["una persona tocó una oreja y dijo que era como una canasta.",true,"sí: una canasta para limpiar el grano."],["una persona se subió y montó en el lomo del elefante.",false,"no está: cada uno solo tocó una parte con las manos."],["el Buda fue quien contó esta historia.",true,"sí: la contó sobre personas que conocen una parte y discuten sobre el todo."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio y fíjate en un sonido que puedas oír. exhala. fíjate en otro. cuatro respiraciones lentas, cuatro cosas nuevas.","carry":"antes de discutir, preguntaré: ¿tú qué ves?","grownups":{"source":"El Buda cuenta esta parábola en el Udana (6.4), una antigua colección de sus dichos, sobre personas que discuten cuando cada una tiene solo una parte de la verdad. En paráfrasis.","ask":"¿cuándo tú y otra persona vieron la misma cosa de maneras distintas?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Udana 6.4 (Tittha Sutta)","work":"Udana","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"banyan-deer","game":"match","en":{"title":"the Banyan Deer king","word":"dana","means":"giving freely, without wanting anything back","story":[{"head":"two golden deer kings","text":"This is a Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives. Long ago, he was born as a golden deer called the Banyan Deer. He led one herd, and another golden deer, the Branch Deer, led a second herd. Both herds lived in a king's park."},{"head":"one deer each day","text":"The king of that land loved to hunt, and his hunts frightened and hurt many deer. So the deer agreed that, instead, one deer each day would go to the king, and the rest could live in peace."},{"head":"a mother deer's turn","text":"One day it was the turn of a mother deer who was soon to have a baby. “Please,” she asked, “let me wait until my baby is born.” The Banyan Deer said gently, “I will go in your place.”"},{"head":"a king changes his heart","text":"When the king saw the golden deer king come himself, he was amazed. He let the Banyan Deer and the mother deer go free. Moved by such kindness, he promised that all the deer, and the birds and fish and other animals too, would be safe."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the Banyan Deer","went in the mother's place"],["the mother deer","asked to wait for her baby"],["the king","set the deer free"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a deer standing very still in a quiet park. breathe out slowly. four slow breaths, calm and brave.","carry":"today I'll take a turn for someone who needs help.","grownups":{"source":"A Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives: the Nigrodhamiga Jataka (no. 12), in Cowell's The Jataka (1895). In the full telling the deer go to the king's cook by lot; this telling stays gentle.","ask":"how did it feel when someone did something hard to help you?"}},"es":{"title":"el rey Ciervo Baniano","word":"dana","means":"dar con generosidad, sin esperar nada a cambio","story":[{"head":"dos reyes ciervos dorados","text":"Este es un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda. Hace mucho tiempo, nació como un ciervo dorado llamado Ciervo Baniano. Guiaba una manada, y otro ciervo dorado, el Ciervo Rama, guiaba otra. Las dos manadas vivían en el parque de un rey."},{"head":"un ciervo cada día","text":"Al rey de esa tierra le encantaba cazar, y sus cacerías asustaban y lastimaban a muchos ciervos. Así que los ciervos acordaron que, en cambio, cada día un ciervo iría con el rey, y los demás podrían vivir en paz."},{"head":"le toca a una mamá cierva","text":"Un día le tocó a una cierva que pronto iba a tener un bebé. “Por favor”, pidió, “déjenme esperar hasta que nazca mi bebé.” El Ciervo Baniano le dijo con dulzura: “Yo iré en tu lugar.”"},{"head":"un rey cambia su corazón","text":"Cuando el rey vio que el rey de los ciervos venía él mismo, se asombró. Dejó libres al Ciervo Baniano y a la mamá cierva. Conmovido por tanta bondad, prometió que todos los ciervos, y también las aves, los peces y los demás animales, estarían a salvo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el Ciervo Baniano","fue en lugar de la mamá"],["la mamá cierva","pidió esperar a su bebé"],["el rey","dejó libres a los ciervos"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un ciervo muy quieto en un parque tranquilo. exhala despacio. cuatro respiraciones lentas, tranquilas y valientes.","carry":"hoy tomaré el turno de alguien que necesite ayuda.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda: el Nigrodhamiga Jataka (n.º 12), en The Jataka de Cowell (1895). En el relato completo los ciervos van al cocinero del rey por sorteo; este relato es más suave.","ask":"¿cómo te sentiste cuando alguien hizo algo difícil para ayudarte?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jataka no. 12 (Nigrodhamiga Jataka)","work":"The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell (1895)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"gift-not-accepted","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the gift the Buddha didn't take","word":"patience","means":"staying calm and kind when things are hard","story":[{"head":"an angry visitor","text":"One day a man came to the Buddha, very angry. Someone in his family had gone to live as a monk, and the man did not like it at all. He shouted many rude, unkind words at the Buddha."},{"head":"a question about guests","text":"The Buddha listened quietly. Then he asked, “When friends visit your house, do you offer them food?” “Yes,” said the man. “And if they don't take the food, whose is it then?”"},{"head":"it stays with the giver","text":"“It's still mine,” said the man. “It is the same with your unkind words,” said the Buddha gently. “I don't accept them. So they stay with you.”"},{"head":"a quiet change of heart","text":"The man was surprised. The Buddha had not shouted back or grown angry. The man grew quiet and thought about it. The old text says he later asked to become a monk and learn from the Buddha himself."}],"game":{"items":[["the man shouted unkind words at the Buddha.",true,"yes: he was angry that someone in his family had become a monk."],["the Buddha gave the man a bowl of food.",false,"not in it: he only asked about food that guests don't take."],["the Buddha said he didn't accept the unkind words.",true,"yes: like food a guest won't take, they stayed with the giver."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly. breathe out slowly, and picture an unkind word drifting away like a dish nobody takes. four slow breaths.","carry":"if someone is unkind, I can stay calm and kind.","grownups":{"source":"From the Akkosa Sutta (Samyutta Nikaya 7.2), in the early Pali collection of the Buddha's talks, paraphrased here. The angry man is a brahmin of the Bharadvaja clan.","ask":"what helps you stay calm when someone says something unkind?"}},"es":{"title":"el regalo que el Buda no aceptó","word":"paciencia","means":"mantenerse tranquilo y amable cuando las cosas son difíciles","story":[{"head":"un visitante enojado","text":"Un día un hombre vino a ver al Buda, muy enojado. Alguien de su familia se había ido a vivir como monje, y al hombre eso no le gustaba nada. Le gritó al Buda muchas palabras groseras y crueles."},{"head":"una pregunta sobre los invitados","text":"El Buda escuchó en silencio. Luego preguntó: “Cuando tus amigos te visitan, ¿les ofreces comida?” “Sí”, dijo el hombre. “¿Y si no aceptan la comida, de quién es entonces?”"},{"head":"se queda con quien la da","text":"“Sigue siendo mía”, dijo el hombre. “Lo mismo pasa con tus palabras crueles”, dijo el Buda con suavidad. “Yo no las acepto. Así que se quedan contigo.”"},{"head":"un cambio tranquilo","text":"El hombre se sorprendió. El Buda no le había gritado ni se había enojado. El hombre se quedó callado y lo pensó. El texto antiguo dice que después pidió hacerse monje y aprender del Buda mismo."}],"game":{"items":[["el hombre le gritó palabras crueles al Buda.",true,"sí: estaba enojado porque alguien de su familia se hizo monje."],["el Buda le dio al hombre un plato de comida.",false,"no está: solo le preguntó por la comida que los invitados no aceptan."],["el Buda dijo que no aceptaba las palabras crueles.",true,"sí: como la comida que un invitado no acepta, se quedaron con quien las dio."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio. exhala despacio, e imagina una palabra cruel que se aleja como un plato que nadie toma. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"si alguien es cruel conmigo, puedo seguir tranquilo y amable.","grownups":{"source":"Del Akkosa Sutta (Samyutta Nikaya 7.2), en la antigua colección pali de las charlas del Buda, en paráfrasis. El hombre enojado es un brahmán del clan Bharadvaja.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a mantener la calma cuando alguien te dice algo cruel?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Samyutta Nikaya 7.2 (Akkosa Sutta)","work":"Samyutta Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"monkey-and-crocodile","game":"match","en":{"title":"the clever monkey and the crocodile","word":"mindfulness","means":"noticing what is happening, right now","story":[{"head":"a rock in the middle of the river","text":"This is a Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives. He was born as a strong monkey who lived by a river. Each day he jumped to a rock in the middle, then to an island full of fruit, and back home in the evening."},{"head":"a crocodile waits on the rock","text":"A crocodile wanted to catch the monkey. One evening he lay flat on top of the rock, waiting. But the monkey stopped and looked. The rock seemed higher out of the water than usual."},{"head":"hello, rock!","text":"“Hello, rock!” called the monkey. No answer. “Rock, why don't you answer me today?” The crocodile thought rocks must talk, so he called back, “What do you want, monkey?” Now the monkey knew who was there."},{"head":"a hop and a bounce home","text":"The monkey said, “Open your mouth, and I'll come to you.” The story says that when a crocodile opens its mouth wide, its eyes close. So the monkey hopped onto his head and bounced to the bank. The crocodile said, “Monkey, you are wise and brave!”"}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the monkey","noticed the rock looked higher"],["the crocodile","lay flat on the rock"],["the island","was full of fruit"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, and notice what is around you, like the monkey noticing the rock. breathe out slowly. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll notice one thing that's different from yesterday.","grownups":{"source":"A Jataka story, one of the old tales of the Buddha's past lives: the Vanarinda Jataka (no. 57), in Cowell's The Jataka (1895). Other Jatakas tell a different monkey-and-crocodile tale, where the monkey says his heart is hanging in a fig tree.","ask":"what did the monkey notice that kept him safe?"}},"es":{"title":"el mono listo y el cocodrilo","word":"atención plena","means":"darte cuenta de lo que pasa, ahora mismo","story":[{"head":"una roca en medio del río","text":"Este es un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda. Nació como un mono fuerte que vivía junto a un río. Cada día saltaba a una roca en medio del río, luego a una isla llena de fruta, y volvía a casa por la tarde."},{"head":"un cocodrilo espera en la roca","text":"Un cocodrilo quería atrapar al mono. Una tarde se acostó plano sobre la roca, esperando. Pero el mono se detuvo y miró. La roca parecía más alta fuera del agua que de costumbre."},{"head":"¡hola, roca!","text":"“¡Hola, roca!”, gritó el mono. Nada. “Roca, ¿por qué no me contestas hoy?” El cocodrilo pensó que las rocas hablaban, así que respondió: “¿Qué quieres, mono?” Ahora el mono sabía quién estaba allí."},{"head":"un salto y un brinco a casa","text":"El mono dijo: “Abre la boca y voy hacia ti.” La historia dice que cuando un cocodrilo abre bien la boca, cierra los ojos. Así que el mono saltó sobre su cabeza y brincó a la orilla. El cocodrilo dijo: “¡Mono, eres sabio y valiente!”"}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el mono","vio que la roca estaba más alta"],["el cocodrilo","se acostó sobre la roca"],["la isla","estaba llena de fruta"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio y fíjate en lo que hay a tu alrededor, como el mono que miró la roca. exhala despacio. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy me fijaré en algo distinto de ayer.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento Jataka, uno de los antiguos relatos de las vidas pasadas del Buda: el Vanarinda Jataka (n.º 57), en The Jataka de Cowell (1895). Otros Jataka cuentan otra historia de un mono y un cocodrilo, en la que el mono dice que su corazón cuelga de una higuera.","ask":"¿qué notó el mono que lo mantuvo a salvo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Jataka no. 57 (Vanarinda Jataka)","work":"The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell (1895)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"rahula-mirror","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Rahula and the mirror","word":"reflect","means":"to look back and think about what you did","story":[{"head":"an evening visit","text":"Rahula was the Buddha's son. He had become a young novice, learning to live like a monk. One evening the Buddha came to visit him. Rahula got a seat ready and brought water to wash his father's feet."},{"head":"a question about a mirror","text":"After his feet were washed, the Buddha asked, “Rahula, what is a mirror for?” Rahula answered, “A mirror is for looking at yourself, to see how you are.”"},{"head":"look before, during and after","text":"“That is how to look at what you do,” said the Buddha. “Before you do something, look: will it hurt me or anyone else? While you are doing it, look again. After you have done it, look once more.”"},{"head":"making it right","text":"If something would hurt, the Buddha said, don't do it. If you see you did something hurtful, talk about it with a friend you trust, and try again more carefully. If it was good and kind, be glad, and keep on learning."}],"game":{"items":[["Rahula brought water to wash the Buddha's feet.",true,"yes: he got a seat ready and brought water."],["the Buddha gave Rahula a mirror to keep.",false,"not in it: he asked Rahula what a mirror is for."],["the Buddha said to look before, during and after.",true,"yes: before you act, while you act, and after you act."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, and look inside, like looking into a calm, clear mirror. breathe out slowly. four slow breaths, still and clear.","carry":"before I do something, I'll stop and look first.","grownups":{"source":"From the Ambalatthika-Rahulovada Sutta (Majjhima Nikaya 61), the Buddha's advice to his son Rahula, paraphrased here. The full talk also uses a little water left in a dipper to teach about honesty.","ask":"what is one thing you did today that you're glad about?"}},"es":{"title":"Rahula y el espejo","word":"reflexionar","means":"mirar hacia atrás y pensar en lo que hiciste","story":[{"head":"una visita por la tarde","text":"Rahula era el hijo del Buda. Se había hecho novicio, y aprendía a vivir como monje. Una tarde el Buda vino a visitarlo. Rahula preparó un asiento y trajo agua para lavar los pies de su padre."},{"head":"una pregunta sobre un espejo","text":"Después de lavarse los pies, el Buda preguntó: “Rahula, ¿para qué sirve un espejo?” Rahula respondió: “Un espejo sirve para mirarse a uno mismo, para ver cómo está.”"},{"head":"mirar antes, durante y después","text":"“Así hay que mirar lo que haces”, dijo el Buda. “Antes de hacer algo, mira: ¿me hará daño a mí o a alguien más? Mientras lo haces, vuelve a mirar. Después de hacerlo, mira una vez más.”"},{"head":"arreglar las cosas","text":"Si algo haría daño, dijo el Buda, no lo hagas. Si ves que hiciste algo que lastimó, háblalo con un amigo de confianza, e inténtalo otra vez con más cuidado. Si fue bueno y amable, alégrate, y sigue aprendiendo."}],"game":{"items":[["Rahula trajo agua para lavar los pies del Buda.",true,"sí: preparó un asiento y trajo agua."],["el Buda le regaló a Rahula un espejo.",false,"no está: le preguntó a Rahula para qué sirve un espejo."],["el Buda dijo que hay que mirar antes, durante y después.",true,"sí: antes de actuar, mientras actúas y después de actuar."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio y mira hacia dentro, como en un espejo tranquilo y claro. exhala despacio. cuatro respiraciones lentas, quietas y claras.","carry":"antes de hacer algo, me detendré a mirar primero.","grownups":{"source":"Del Ambalatthika-Rahulovada Sutta (Majjhima Nikaya 61), el consejo del Buda a su hijo Rahula, en paráfrasis. La charla completa también usa un poco de agua en un cucharón para enseñar sobre la honestidad.","ask":"¿qué hiciste hoy que te alegra?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Majjhima Nikaya 61 (Ambalatthika-Rahulovada Sutta)","work":"Majjhima Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":15,"key":"nalagiri-elephant","game":"match","en":{"title":"the wild elephant who grew calm","word":"calm","means":"quiet and steady inside, even when things are wild","story":[{"head":"a wild elephant in the city","text":"One morning, the Buddha was walking through the city of Rajagaha with his monks. Suddenly a huge elephant named Nalagiri came charging down the street. He had been let loose, and he was wild and angry."},{"head":"people hurry inside","text":"People shouted and hurried indoors. “Turn back!” they called to the Buddha. But the Buddha did not run. He stood still on the road, and he filled his heart with kindness toward the elephant."},{"head":"a great trunk comes down","text":"Nalagiri felt that kindness. He slowed down. He stopped. He lowered his great trunk and stood quietly in front of the Buddha. The Buddha reached out and gently stroked the elephant's forehead."},{"head":"the gentle walk home","text":"The Buddha spoke softly to him. The story says Nalagiri lifted dust from the Buddha's feet with his trunk and put it on his own head, to show respect. Then he walked calmly back to his stable."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Nalagiri","came charging down the street"],["the Buddha","stood still and kind"],["the people","hurried indoors"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, standing still like the Buddha on the road. breathe out slowly, and send kindness to something wild. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll send a kind wish to someone who is upset.","grownups":{"source":"Told in the Vinaya, the monks' rule books (Cullavagga 7), paraphrased. In that telling the Buddha's cousin Devadatta arranged for the elephant to be let loose; this telling leaves that out to keep it gentle.","ask":"what helps you calm down when you feel wild inside?"}},"es":{"title":"el elefante salvaje que se calmó","word":"calma","means":"estar tranquilo y firme por dentro, aunque todo esté agitado","story":[{"head":"un elefante salvaje en la ciudad","text":"Una mañana, el Buda caminaba por la ciudad de Rajagaha con sus monjes. De pronto, un elefante enorme llamado Nalagiri vino corriendo por la calle. Lo habían soltado, y estaba salvaje y furioso."},{"head":"la gente corre a sus casas","text":"La gente gritaba y corría a meterse en sus casas. “¡Regresa!”, le gritaban al Buda. Pero el Buda no corrió. Se quedó quieto en el camino y llenó su corazón de bondad hacia el elefante."},{"head":"baja una gran trompa","text":"Nalagiri sintió esa bondad. Fue más despacio. Se detuvo. Bajó su gran trompa y se quedó quieto frente al Buda. El Buda extendió la mano y le acarició la frente al elefante con suavidad."},{"head":"el regreso tranquilo","text":"El Buda le habló con dulzura. La historia dice que Nalagiri tomó con la trompa polvo de los pies del Buda y se lo puso en la cabeza, en señal de respeto. Luego volvió tranquilo a su establo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Nalagiri","vino corriendo por la calle"],["el Buda","se quedó quieto y bondadoso"],["la gente","corrió a sus casas"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, quieto como el Buda en el camino. exhala despacio y envía bondad a algo salvaje. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy enviaré un deseo bondadoso a alguien que esté molesto.","grownups":{"source":"Se cuenta en el Vinaya, los libros de reglas de los monjes (Cullavagga 7), en paráfrasis. Allí, Devadatta, primo del Buda, hizo que soltaran al elefante; este relato lo deja fuera para que sea suave.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a calmarte cuando te sientes salvaje por dentro?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Vinaya Pitaka, Cullavagga 7 (the Nalagiri episode)","work":"Vinaya Pitaka","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"sona-lute","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Sona and the strings of the lute","word":"balance","means":"not too much, not too little: just right","story":[{"head":"a monk who tried too hard","text":"There once was a monk named Sona. He wanted to wake up so much that he practiced walking meditation day and night, pushing himself very hard. He grew tired and discouraged, and he thought about giving up."},{"head":"a question about music","text":"The Buddha came to see him. “Sona,” he asked, “before you became a monk, you played the lute, didn't you?” “Yes,” said Sona. He had been skilled at it."},{"head":"too tight or too loose?","text":"“When the strings are too tight, does the lute sound sweet?” “No,” said Sona. “And when they're too loose?” “No.” “And when they're not too tight and not too loose?” “Then it sounds just right.”"},{"head":"tuned just right","text":"“Trying is like that,” the Buddha said. “Too hard, and you get restless. Too soft, and you get lazy. Find the balance in between.” Sona listened, practiced this way, and found the peace he was looking for."}],"game":{"items":[["Sona used to play the lute.",true,"yes: he was skilled at it before he became a monk."],["the Buddha gave Sona a new lute.",false,"not in it: they only talked about how lute strings sound."],["strings that are too tight don't sound sweet.",true,"yes: not too tight and not too loose is just right."]]},"breath":"breathe in, not too fast, not too slow. breathe out the same way, like tuning a string just right. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll try my best, and rest when I need to.","grownups":{"source":"From the Sona Sutta (Anguttara Nikaya 6.55), paraphrased. The Vinaya (Mahavagga 5) tells the same meeting between the Buddha and Sona Kolivisa.","ask":"when do you try too hard, and when could you try a little harder?"}},"es":{"title":"Sona y las cuerdas del laúd","word":"equilibrio","means":"ni demasiado ni muy poco: justo lo necesario","story":[{"head":"un monje que se esforzaba demasiado","text":"Había una vez un monje llamado Sona. Quería despertar con tantas ganas que practicaba la meditación caminando día y noche, exigiéndose muchísimo. Se cansó y se desanimó, y pensó en rendirse."},{"head":"una pregunta sobre la música","text":"El Buda fue a verlo. “Sona”, le preguntó, “antes de ser monje tocabas el laúd, ¿verdad?” “Sí”, dijo Sona. Era muy hábil tocándolo."},{"head":"¿muy tensas o muy flojas?","text":"“Cuando las cuerdas están muy tensas, ¿suena dulce el laúd?” “No”, dijo Sona. “¿Y cuando están muy flojas?” “No.” “¿Y cuando no están ni muy tensas ni muy flojas?” “Entonces suena justo bien.”"},{"head":"afinado justo bien","text":"“Esforzarse es así”, dijo el Buda. “Demasiado, y te pones inquieto. Muy poco, y te da pereza. Encuentra el equilibrio en medio.” Sona escuchó, practicó así y encontró la paz que buscaba."}],"game":{"items":[["Sona tocaba el laúd.",true,"sí: era hábil tocándolo antes de hacerse monje."],["el Buda le regaló a Sona un laúd nuevo.",false,"no está: solo hablaron de cómo suenan las cuerdas del laúd."],["las cuerdas muy tensas no suenan dulce.",true,"sí: ni muy tensas ni muy flojas es justo bien."]]},"breath":"inhala, ni muy rápido ni muy lento. exhala igual, como cuando afinas una cuerda justo bien. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy daré lo mejor de mí, y descansaré cuando lo necesite.","grownups":{"source":"Del Sona Sutta (Anguttara Nikaya 6.55), en paráfrasis. El Vinaya (Mahavagga 5) cuenta el mismo encuentro entre el Buda y Sona Kolivisa.","ask":"¿cuándo te esfuerzas demasiado, y cuándo podrías esforzarte un poco más?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Anguttara Nikaya 6.55 (Sona Sutta)","work":"Anguttara Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Vinaya Pitaka, Mahavagga 5","work":"Vinaya Pitaka","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"cula-panthaka-cloth","game":"match","en":{"title":"the monk and the white cloth","word":"anicca","means":"change: nothing stays exactly the same","story":[{"head":"a monk who couldn't remember","text":"Cula-Panthaka was a monk who tried and tried, but he could not remember even one short verse. His big brother, also a monk, thought he might never learn. Cula-Panthaka felt sad and was ready to give up."},{"head":"a clean white cloth","text":"The Buddha saw him and gave him a clean white cloth. “Sit in the sun,” the Buddha said, “and rub this cloth gently. As you rub, say: taking away the dirt, taking away the dirt.”"},{"head":"the cloth turns gray","text":"Cula-Panthaka rubbed and rubbed. Slowly, the white cloth turned gray and grubby from his hands. He looked at it and thought: it was clean, and now it has changed. Everything changes."},{"head":"a mind made clear","text":"In that moment his mind became clear and bright, and he understood deeply. The monk who couldn't remember a verse became wise, and he went on to teach others. The story shows that everyone can learn, in their own way."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Cula-Panthaka","couldn't remember a verse"],["the Buddha","gave him a clean cloth"],["the white cloth","turned gray"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the warm sun on the cloth. breathe out slowly, and let one worry go. four slow breaths, nice and easy.","carry":"if learning feels hard, I'll keep going in my own way.","grownups":{"source":"From the commentary on the Dhammapada (verse 25) and the opening of the Cullakasetthi Jataka (no. 4), old Buddhist story collections. Paraphrased here.","ask":"what was hard for you to learn that you can do now?"}},"es":{"title":"el monje y el paño blanco","word":"anicca","means":"cambio: nada se queda exactamente igual","story":[{"head":"un monje que no podía recordar","text":"Cula-Panthaka era un monje que lo intentaba una y otra vez, pero no lograba recordar ni un verso corto. Su hermano mayor, también monje, pensaba que quizá nunca aprendería. Cula-Panthaka estaba triste y quería rendirse."},{"head":"un paño blanco y limpio","text":"El Buda lo vio y le dio un paño blanco y limpio. “Siéntate al sol”, le dijo el Buda, “y frota este paño con suavidad. Mientras frotas, di: quitando el polvo, quitando el polvo.”"},{"head":"el paño se pone gris","text":"Cula-Panthaka frotó y frotó. Poco a poco, el paño blanco se puso gris y sucio por sus manos. Lo miró y pensó: estaba limpio, y ahora cambió. Todo cambia."},{"head":"una mente clara","text":"En ese momento su mente se volvió clara y brillante, y entendió a fondo. El monje que no podía recordar un verso se volvió sabio, y llegó a enseñar a otros. La historia muestra que todos pueden aprender, a su manera."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Cula-Panthaka","no podía recordar un verso"],["el Buda","le dio un paño limpio"],["el paño blanco","se puso gris"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el sol tibio sobre el paño. exhala despacio y deja ir una preocupación. cuatro respiraciones lentas, suaves y tranquilas.","carry":"si aprender es difícil, seguiré adelante a mi manera.","grownups":{"source":"Del comentario del Dhammapada (verso 25) y del comienzo del Cullakasetthi Jataka (n.º 4), antiguas colecciones de historias budistas. En paráfrasis.","ask":"¿qué te costó aprender y ahora ya puedes hacer?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Dhammapada commentary on verse 25 (the story of Cula-Panthaka)","work":"Dhammapada-atthakatha","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Jataka no. 4 (Cullakasetthi Jataka), introduction","work":"The Jataka, ed. E. B. Cowell (1895)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"lotus-footprints","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the footprints and the lotus","word":"lotus","means":"a water flower that rises and blooms clean above the pond","story":[{"head":"footprints with wheels","text":"A man named Dona was walking along a road when he saw footprints in the dust. They were special: each one had the shape of a wheel in it. “Who made these?” he wondered, and he followed them."},{"head":"a quiet man under a tree","text":"The footprints led to the Buddha, sitting peacefully under a tree. Dona asked, “Are you a god?” “No,” said the Buddha. “A spirit of the sky? A spirit of the forest?” “No.” “Then are you a human being?” “No.”"},{"head":"a flower above the water","text":"“Think of a lotus,” the Buddha said. “It begins down in the pond and grows up through the water. Then it rises above the water and blooms, and the water doesn't stick to it.”"},{"head":"remember me as awake","text":"“In the same way, I grew up in the world, but the world doesn't stick to me,” he said. “Remember me as awake: as a Buddha.” Many Buddhists still use the lotus as a picture of a clear, kind heart."}],"game":{"items":[["Dona saw footprints with wheel shapes in them.",true,"yes: so he followed them to the Buddha."],["Dona brought the Buddha a lotus flower.",false,"not in it: the Buddha only talked about a lotus, as a picture."],["the Buddha said the water doesn't stick to the lotus.",true,"yes: it rises above the water and blooms clean."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a lotus rising up through the water. breathe out slowly as its petals open wide. four slow breaths.","carry":"even on a muddy day, I can bloom a little.","grownups":{"source":"From the Dona Sutta (Anguttara Nikaya 4.36), paraphrased. The lotus that rises clean above the water appears often in Buddhist art and teaching.","ask":"what helps you stay kind even when things around you are messy?"}},"es":{"title":"las huellas y el loto","word":"loto","means":"una flor de agua que sube y florece limpia sobre el estanque","story":[{"head":"huellas con ruedas","text":"Un hombre llamado Dona iba por un camino cuando vio unas huellas en el polvo. Eran especiales: cada una tenía la forma de una rueda. “¿Quién hizo estas huellas?”, se preguntó, y las siguió."},{"head":"un hombre tranquilo bajo un árbol","text":"Las huellas llevaban al Buda, sentado en paz bajo un árbol. Dona preguntó: “¿Eres un dios?” “No”, dijo el Buda. “¿Un espíritu del cielo? ¿Un espíritu del bosque?” “No.” “¿Entonces eres un ser humano?” “No.”"},{"head":"una flor sobre el agua","text":"“Piensa en un loto”, dijo el Buda. “Empieza en el fondo del estanque y crece a través del agua. Luego sube por encima del agua y florece, y el agua no se le pega.”"},{"head":"recuérdame como despierto","text":"“De la misma manera, crecí en el mundo, pero el mundo no se me pega”, dijo. “Recuérdame como despierto: como un Buda.” Muchos budistas todavía usan el loto como imagen de un corazón claro y bondadoso."}],"game":{"items":[["Dona vio huellas con forma de rueda.",true,"sí: por eso las siguió hasta el Buda."],["Dona le llevó al Buda una flor de loto.",false,"no está: el Buda solo habló de un loto, como imagen."],["el Buda dijo que el agua no se le pega al loto.",true,"sí: sube por encima del agua y florece limpio."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un loto que sube a través del agua. exhala despacio mientras abre sus pétalos. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"aun en un día lodoso, puedo florecer un poquito.","grownups":{"source":"Del Dona Sutta (Anguttara Nikaya 4.36), en paráfrasis. El loto que sube limpio sobre el agua aparece a menudo en el arte y la enseñanza budistas.","ask":"¿qué te ayuda a seguir siendo amable cuando todo a tu alrededor está desordenado?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Anguttara Nikaya 4.36 (Dona Sutta)","work":"Anguttara Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"caring-for-the-sick-monk","game":"match","en":{"title":"the Buddha cares for a sick monk","word":"care","means":"looking after someone who needs help","story":[{"head":"a monk alone and unwell","text":"One day the Buddha and his helper Ananda were walking past the huts where the monks lived. In one hut they found a monk who was very sick with a bad tummy. He was lying all alone, and his bed was dirty."},{"head":"nobody was helping","text":"“Why is nobody looking after you?” the Buddha asked. The monk said, “I can't do anything useful for the other monks, so they don't look after me.”"},{"head":"warm water and gentle hands","text":"The Buddha asked Ananda to fetch water. Together they washed the monk gently. Then the Buddha held his head, Ananda held his feet, and they lifted him onto a clean bed."},{"head":"look after each other","text":"Then the Buddha gathered the monks. “You have no mother or father here to care for you,” he said, “so look after each other. Whoever would look after me should look after the sick.”"}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the sick monk","was lying all alone"],["Ananda","fetched the water"],["the Buddha","held the monk's head"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like warm water poured with care. breathe out slowly. four slow breaths, and think of someone who might need a little help.","carry":"today I'll check on someone who seems left out.","grownups":{"source":"From the Vinaya, the monks' rule books (Mahavagga 8.26), paraphrased. The Buddha's words, that whoever would look after him should look after the sick, are paraphrased too.","ask":"who do we know who might be lonely or unwell, and how could we help?"}},"es":{"title":"el Buda cuida a un monje enfermo","word":"cuidado","means":"atender a alguien que necesita ayuda","story":[{"head":"un monje solo y enfermo","text":"Un día el Buda y su ayudante Ananda pasaban junto a las chozas donde vivían los monjes. En una choza encontraron a un monje muy enfermo del estómago. Estaba acostado solito, y su cama estaba sucia."},{"head":"nadie lo ayudaba","text":"“¿Por qué nadie te cuida?”, preguntó el Buda. El monje dijo: “No puedo hacer nada útil por los otros monjes, así que ellos no me cuidan.”"},{"head":"agua tibia y manos suaves","text":"El Buda le pidió a Ananda que trajera agua. Juntos lavaron al monje con suavidad. Luego el Buda le sostuvo la cabeza, Ananda los pies, y lo pasaron a una cama limpia."},{"head":"cuídense unos a otros","text":"Después el Buda reunió a los monjes. “Aquí no tienen madre ni padre que los cuide”, dijo, “así que cuídense unos a otros. Quien quiera cuidarme a mí, que cuide a los enfermos.”"}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el monje enfermo","estaba acostado solito"],["Ananda","trajo el agua"],["el Buda","sostuvo la cabeza del monje"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como agua tibia que se vierte con cuidado. exhala despacio. cuatro respiraciones lentas, y piensa en alguien que necesite un poco de ayuda.","carry":"hoy buscaré a alguien que parezca quedarse solo.","grownups":{"source":"Del Vinaya, los libros de reglas de los monjes (Mahavagga 8.26), en paráfrasis. Las palabras del Buda, que quien quiera cuidarlo a él cuide a los enfermos, también están en paráfrasis.","ask":"¿a quién conocemos que esté solo o enfermo, y cómo podríamos ayudar?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Vinaya Pitaka, Mahavagga 8.26","work":"Vinaya Pitaka","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"elephant-in-the-forest","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the elephant who looked after the Buddha","word":"friendship","means":"being kind and loyal to each other","story":[{"head":"monks who couldn't agree","text":"Once, in the city of Kosambi, a group of monks began to quarrel. They argued and argued and would not make peace, even when the Buddha asked them to. So the Buddha went off quietly to stay alone in a forest."},{"head":"a big elephant moves in","text":"In that forest lived a great elephant who had also left his crowded herd. He found the Buddha and stayed near him. With his trunk he brought him water, and he kept the ground around him clear."},{"head":"quiet days together","text":"The two of them lived peacefully in the forest, the Buddha sitting in calm and the elephant watching over him. Back in the city, the monks thought again about their quarrel, and in the end they made peace."},{"head":"an old rule about hatred","text":"Many Buddhists remember these words of the Buddha from the Dhammapada: “For hatred does not cease by hatred at any time: hatred ceases by love—this is an old rule.”"}],"game":{"items":[["the elephant brought the Buddha water with his trunk.",true,"yes: and he kept the ground around the Buddha clear."],["the Buddha rode the elephant back to the city.",false,"not in it: the elephant looked after him in the forest."],["the monks in Kosambi made peace in the end.",true,"yes: they thought again about their quarrel and made peace."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the big elephant drinking cool water. breathe out slowly, peaceful and gentle. four slow breaths.","carry":"when we argue, I'll be the first to make peace.","grownups":{"source":"The quarrel at Kosambi is told in the Vinaya (Mahavagga 10), and the elephant in the Udana (4.5). The closing line is Dhammapada verse 5, quoted from Max Müller's translation (1881).","ask":"after an argument, what helps us make peace again?"}},"es":{"title":"el elefante que cuidó al Buda","word":"amistad","means":"ser amables y leales unos con otros","story":[{"head":"monjes que no se ponían de acuerdo","text":"Una vez, en la ciudad de Kosambi, un grupo de monjes empezó a pelear. Discutían y discutían y no querían hacer las paces, aunque el Buda se lo pidió. Así que el Buda se fue en silencio a quedarse solo en un bosque."},{"head":"llega un gran elefante","text":"En ese bosque vivía un gran elefante que también había dejado su manada, donde había demasiados elefantes. Encontró al Buda y se quedó cerca de él. Con la trompa le traía agua, y mantenía limpio el suelo a su alrededor."},{"head":"días tranquilos juntos","text":"Los dos vivían en paz en el bosque, el Buda sentado en calma y el elefante cuidándolo. En la ciudad, los monjes volvieron a pensar en su pelea, y al final hicieron las paces."},{"head":"una regla antigua sobre el odio","text":"Muchos budistas recuerdan estas palabras del Buda en el Dhammapada, que dicen más o menos así: el odio nunca termina con odio; el odio termina con amor. Esta es una regla antigua."}],"game":{"items":[["el elefante le traía agua al Buda con la trompa.",true,"sí: y mantenía limpio el suelo alrededor del Buda."],["el Buda volvió a la ciudad montado en el elefante.",false,"no está: el elefante lo cuidó en el bosque."],["los monjes de Kosambi hicieron las paces al final.",true,"sí: volvieron a pensar en su pelea e hicieron las paces."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el gran elefante bebiendo agua fresca. exhala despacio, en paz y con suavidad. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"cuando discutamos, seré el primero en hacer las paces.","grownups":{"source":"La pelea de Kosambi se cuenta en el Vinaya (Mahavagga 10), y el elefante en el Udana (4.5). La frase final es el verso 5 del Dhammapada, en paráfrasis de la traducción de Max Müller (1881).","ask":"después de una discusión, ¿qué nos ayuda a hacer las paces otra vez?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Dhammapada 5","work":"The Dhammapada","translation":"Max Müller 1881","quoted":"For hatred does not cease by hatred at any time: hatred ceases by love—this is an old rule."},{"ref":"Udana 4.5 (Naga Sutta)","work":"Udana","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Vinaya Pitaka, Mahavagga 10 (the Kosambi quarrel)","work":"Vinaya Pitaka","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"the-raft","game":"match","en":{"title":"the raft","word":"Dharma","means":"the Buddha's teaching (in Pali, Dhamma)","story":[{"head":"a traveler at a wide river","text":"The Buddha once told this story. A traveler came to a great, wide river. On his side, the land was rough and unsafe. On the far side, the land was safe and peaceful. But there was no bridge and no boat."},{"head":"sticks, grass and leaves","text":"So the traveler gathered grass, sticks, branches and leaves. He tied them together to make a raft. He climbed on and paddled with his hands and feet, all the way across to the far shore."},{"head":"should he carry the raft?","text":"On the far side, he thought, “This raft helped me so much. Should I lift it onto my head and carry it everywhere I go?” “What do you think?” the Buddha asked his monks. “No,” they said."},{"head":"leaving the raft on the shore","text":"The wise traveler pulls the raft up onto the shore, leaves it there, and walks on lightly. “My teaching is like that raft,” the Buddha said. “It is for crossing over, not for holding on to.”"}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the traveler","built a raft"],["the far shore","was safe and peaceful"],["the raft","was left on the shore"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like paddling gently across the wide river. breathe out slowly as you reach the far shore. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll let go of something I don't need to hold.","grownups":{"source":"The parable of the raft is in the Alagaddupama Sutta (Majjhima Nikaya 22), paraphrased here. Buddhists understand it in different ways; many say the teachings are a help to use, not something to cling to.","ask":"what is something that helped you once that you don't need anymore?"}},"es":{"title":"la balsa","word":"Dharma","means":"la enseñanza del Buda (en pali, Dhamma)","story":[{"head":"un viajero ante un río ancho","text":"El Buda contó una vez esta historia. Un viajero llegó a un río grande y ancho. De su lado, la tierra era dura y peligrosa. Del otro lado, la tierra era segura y tranquila. Pero no había puente ni bote."},{"head":"palitos, pasto y hojas","text":"Así que el viajero juntó pasto, palitos, ramas y hojas. Los ató para hacer una balsa. Se subió y remó con las manos y los pies, hasta llegar a la otra orilla."},{"head":"¿debe cargar la balsa?","text":"Del otro lado, pensó: “Esta balsa me ayudó mucho. ¿Debo ponérmela en la cabeza y cargarla a dondequiera que vaya?” “¿Qué piensan?”, preguntó el Buda a sus monjes. “No”, dijeron."},{"head":"dejar la balsa en la orilla","text":"El viajero sabio sube la balsa a la orilla, la deja allí y sigue caminando ligero. “Mi enseñanza es como esa balsa”, dijo el Buda. “Es para cruzar, no para aferrarse a ella.”"}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el viajero","hizo una balsa"],["la otra orilla","era segura y tranquila"],["la balsa","quedó en la orilla"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como si remaras suave por el río ancho. exhala despacio al llegar a la otra orilla. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy soltaré algo que no necesito cargar.","grownups":{"source":"La parábola de la balsa está en el Alagaddupama Sutta (Majjhima Nikaya 22), en paráfrasis. Los budistas la entienden de distintas maneras; muchos dicen que las enseñanzas son una ayuda para usar, no algo a qué aferrarse.","ask":"¿qué cosa te ayudó una vez y ya no necesitas?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Majjhima Nikaya 22 (Alagaddupama Sutta)","work":"Majjhima Nikaya","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]},"SIKHISM":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"SIKHISM","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"nanak-river","game":"match","en":{"title":"Guru Nanak and the river","word":"Ik Onkar","means":"ੴ: there is one Creator, in everything and everyone","story":[{"head":"a morning swim in the river","text":"Long ago, in a town called Sultanpur, a young man named Nanak went down to the river Bein, just as he did every morning. He waded into the cool water to bathe, while a friend waited on the bank. But Nanak did not come back up."},{"head":"three days of waiting","text":"His friends searched the riverbank and called his name, but nobody could find him. The janamsakhis, the old life-stories of Guru Nanak, tell that he was gone for three days, and that in those days he was very close to God."},{"head":"back from the river, calm and bright","text":"At last, the story says, Nanak walked out of the river. His face was calm and bright. For a long while he said nothing at all, and people gathered around him, wondering what had happened."},{"head":"no Hindu, no Muslim","text":"When he spoke, tradition says his first words were: there is no Hindu and no Muslim. Many Sikhs understand this to mean that God sees past every label, and that we are all one family. Sikh scripture begins with Ik Onkar: one Creator."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Nanak","went into the river to bathe"],["Sultanpur","the town by the river"],["Ik Onkar","one Creator"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like cool river water rising around you. breathe out slowly, like Nanak walking calmly back to the bank. four slow breaths.","carry":"everyone I meet today is part of one family","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, the traditional life-stories of Guru Nanak, which tell of his time in the Bein river at Sultanpur. Tellings differ in detail. Ik Onkar opens the Mool Mantar on the first page (Ang 1) of the Guru Granth Sahib.","ask":"what would it feel like to see everyone you meet as part of one family?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Nanak y el río","word":"Ik Onkar","means":"ੴ: hay un solo Creador, en todo y en todos","story":[{"head":"un baño en el río por la mañana","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en un pueblo llamado Sultanpur, un joven llamado Nanak bajó al río Bein, como cada mañana. Entró en el agua fresca para bañarse, mientras un amigo lo esperaba en la orilla. Pero Nanak no volvió a salir."},{"head":"tres días de espera","text":"Sus amigos buscaron por toda la orilla y lo llamaron por su nombre, pero nadie lo encontró. Los janamsakhis, las antiguas historias de la vida del Gurú Nanak, cuentan que estuvo fuera tres días, y que en esos días estuvo muy cerca de Dios."},{"head":"de vuelta del río, sereno y luminoso","text":"Por fin, dice la historia, Nanak salió del río. Su rostro estaba sereno y luminoso. Durante un buen rato no dijo nada, y la gente se reunió a su alrededor, preguntándose qué había pasado."},{"head":"ni hindú ni musulmán","text":"Cuando habló, la tradición dice que sus primeras palabras fueron: no hay hindú ni musulmán. Muchos sijs entienden que Dios mira más allá de cada etiqueta, y que todos somos una sola familia. La escritura sij empieza con Ik Onkar: un solo Creador."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Nanak","entró al río a bañarse"],["Sultanpur","el pueblo junto al río"],["Ik Onkar","un solo Creador"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el agua fresca del río que sube a tu alrededor. exhala despacio, como Nanak caminando tranquilo hacia la orilla. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"todas las personas que veo hoy son parte de una familia","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, las historias tradicionales de la vida del Gurú Nanak, que cuentan su tiempo en el río Bein, en Sultanpur. Los relatos varían en los detalles. Ik Onkar abre el Mool Mantar en la primera página (Ang 1) del Gurú Granth Sahib.","ask":"¿cómo se sentiría ver a cada persona que conoces como parte de una sola familia?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Bein river episode at Sultanpur","work":"Janamsakhi tradition (Puratan and Bhai Bala janamsakhis)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Ang 1 (Mool Mantar)","work":"Guru Granth Sahib","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"sacha-sauda","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the true bargain","word":"sacha sauda","means":"ਸੱਚਾ ਸੌਦਾ: a true bargain, a trade that is truly good","story":[{"head":"twenty rupees for a good deal","text":"When Nanak was a young man, his father, Mehta Kalu, wanted him to learn to trade. He gave Nanak twenty rupees and sent him off to the market, telling him to make a good bargain, a deal that would bring something back."},{"head":"hungry people under the trees","text":"On the way, Nanak met a group of holy men resting under the trees. The janamsakhis tell that they had not eaten for days. They were tired and very hungry, and they had nothing at all."},{"head":"the money becomes a meal","text":"Nanak went to the market and spent all twenty rupees on flour, lentils, ghee and other food. He brought it back to the holy men and made sure every one of them ate until they were full."},{"head":"the truest deal of all","text":"Nanak came home with no money and no goods, and his father was puzzled and upset. But the story says Nanak felt that feeding hungry people was the truest bargain he could make. A gurdwara called Sacha Sauda stands at that place today."}],"game":{"items":[["Nanak's father gave him twenty rupees.",true,"yes: twenty rupees, to make a good bargain at the market."],["Nanak bought a cow at the market.",false,"not in it: he spent all the money on food for the hungry holy men."],["the holy men ate until they were full.",true,"yes: Nanak made sure every one of them ate."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the smell of warm bread. breathe out slowly, like sharing it with someone who is hungry. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I can share something good with someone","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, the traditional life-stories of Guru Nanak. Sikhs call this story Sacha Sauda, the true bargain; Gurdwara Sacha Sauda in present-day Pakistan marks the place. Details such as who the holy men were vary between tellings.","ask":"if you had some money to spend on a true bargain, what would you do with it?"}},"es":{"title":"el verdadero trato","word":"sacha sauda","means":"ਸੱਚਾ ਸੌਦਾ: un trato verdadero, un negocio de veras bueno","story":[{"head":"veinte rupias para un buen trato","text":"Cuando Nanak era joven, su padre, Mehta Kalu, quería que aprendiera a comerciar. Le dio veinte rupias y lo mandó al mercado, pidiéndole que hiciera un buen trato, un negocio que le trajera algo de vuelta."},{"head":"gente con hambre bajo los árboles","text":"En el camino, Nanak encontró a un grupo de hombres santos descansando bajo los árboles. Los janamsakhis cuentan que llevaban días sin comer. Estaban cansados y con mucha hambre, y no tenían nada."},{"head":"el dinero se vuelve comida","text":"Nanak fue al mercado y gastó las veinte rupias en harina, lentejas, ghee y otros alimentos. Se los llevó a los hombres santos y se aseguró de que cada uno comiera hasta quedar satisfecho."},{"head":"el trato más verdadero","text":"Nanak volvió a casa sin dinero y sin mercancía, y su padre quedó confundido y molesto. Pero la historia dice que Nanak sentía que dar de comer a quien tiene hambre era el trato más verdadero. Hoy hay un gurdwara llamado Sacha Sauda en ese lugar."}],"game":{"items":[["el padre de Nanak le dio veinte rupias.",true,"sí: veinte rupias, para hacer un buen trato en el mercado."],["Nanak compró una vaca en el mercado.",false,"no está: gastó todo el dinero en comida para los hombres santos con hambre."],["los hombres santos comieron hasta quedar satisfechos.",true,"sí: Nanak se aseguró de que cada uno comiera."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el olor del pan caliente. exhala despacio, como si lo compartieras con alguien que tiene hambre. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo compartir algo bueno con alguien","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, las historias tradicionales de la vida del Gurú Nanak. Los sijs llaman a esta historia Sacha Sauda, el verdadero trato; el Gurdwara Sacha Sauda, en el actual Pakistán, marca el lugar. Detalles como quiénes eran los hombres santos varían.","ask":"si tuvieras algo de dinero para un trato verdadero, ¿qué harías con él?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Sacha Sauda episode (Chuharkana)","work":"Janamsakhi tradition (Puratan and Bhai Bala janamsakhis)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"lalo-and-bhago","game":"match","en":{"title":"Guru Nanak, Bhai Lalo and Malik Bhago","word":"kirat","means":"ਕਿਰਤ: honest work, earning by your own hands","story":[{"head":"a carpenter's simple home","text":"On his travels, Guru Nanak stayed in a town with Bhai Lalo, a carpenter. Lalo worked hard with his hands all day; Sikhs call this kirat, honest work. In the evening he shared his plain bread with his guest."},{"head":"an invitation to a grand feast","text":"A rich official in the town, Malik Bhago, held a huge feast. He invited all the holy men nearby, and he invited Guru Nanak too. But Guru Nanak stayed with Lalo and did not go."},{"head":"a piece of bread in each hand","text":"Malik Bhago asked why. The janamsakhis, the old life-stories of Guru Nanak, tell that Guru Nanak took a piece of Lalo's plain bread in one hand and a piece of Bhago's rich bread in the other, and gently squeezed them both."},{"head":"what came out of the bread","text":"From Lalo's bread came drops of milk. From Bhago's bread, the story says, came drops of blood, because it was made from other people's hard work, taken unfairly. In many tellings, Malik Bhago saw it and chose to change and share."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Bhai Lalo","a carpenter who worked hard"],["Malik Bhago","held a huge feast"],["Lalo's bread","gave drops of milk"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the smell of Lalo's fresh bread. breathe out slowly, like a carpenter resting after a day of honest work. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can do my work honestly today","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, the traditional life-stories of Guru Nanak, set in the town of Saidpur (Eminabad, in present-day Pakistan). Sikhs tell it to teach kirat karni, earning by honest work. Tellings differ on how Malik Bhago changed.","ask":"what is some honest work you did this week that you feel proud of?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Nanak, Bhai Lalo y Malik Bhago","word":"kirat","means":"ਕਿਰਤ: trabajo honesto, ganarse la vida con tus manos","story":[{"head":"la casa sencilla de un carpintero","text":"En sus viajes, el Gurú Nanak se quedó en un pueblo con Bhai Lalo, un carpintero. Lalo trabajaba duro con sus manos todo el día; los sijs llaman a esto kirat, trabajo honesto. Por la tarde compartía su pan sencillo con su invitado."},{"head":"una invitación a un gran banquete","text":"Un funcionario rico del pueblo, Malik Bhago, organizó un enorme banquete. Invitó a todos los hombres santos de la zona, y también invitó al Gurú Nanak. Pero el Gurú Nanak se quedó con Lalo y no fue."},{"head":"un trozo de pan en cada mano","text":"Malik Bhago preguntó por qué. Los janamsakhis, las antiguas historias de la vida del Gurú Nanak, cuentan que el Gurú tomó un trozo del pan sencillo de Lalo en una mano y un trozo del pan rico de Bhago en la otra, y apretó los dos con suavidad."},{"head":"lo que salió del pan","text":"Del pan de Lalo salieron gotas de leche. Del pan de Bhago, dice la historia, salieron gotas de sangre, porque estaba hecho con el trabajo de otras personas, tomado sin justicia. En muchos relatos, Malik Bhago lo vio y decidió cambiar y compartir."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Bhai Lalo","un carpintero muy trabajador"],["Malik Bhago","hizo un enorme banquete"],["el pan de Lalo","dio gotas de leche"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el olor del pan recién hecho de Lalo. exhala despacio, como un carpintero que descansa después de un día de trabajo honesto. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo hacer mi trabajo con honestidad","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, las historias tradicionales de la vida del Gurú Nanak, en el pueblo de Saidpur (Eminabad, en el actual Pakistán). Los sijs la cuentan para enseñar kirat karni, ganarse la vida con trabajo honesto. Los relatos varían sobre cómo cambió Malik Bhago.","ask":"¿qué trabajo honesto hiciste esta semana que te hace sentir orgullo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Bhai Lalo and Malik Bhago episode at Saidpur (Eminabad)","work":"Janamsakhi tradition (Bhai Bala janamsakhi and later tellings)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":4,"key":"langar","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the kitchen where everyone sits together","word":"langar","means":"ਲੰਗਰ: the free kitchen where everyone eats together","story":[{"head":"a kitchen open to all","text":"When Guru Nanak settled in the village of Kartarpur, he and the people around him farmed the land and cooked together. Sikhs remember the langar beginning there: a free kitchen where anyone could come and eat."},{"head":"everyone on the same floor","text":"In the langar, nobody gets a special seat. Rich and poor, rulers and farmers, people of every faith sit side by side on the floor, in rows. Everyone eats the same food."},{"head":"Mata Khivi's sweet kheer","text":"Later, Mata Khivi, the wife of Guru Angad, ran the langar in the village of Khadur. The Sikh scripture praises her kindness, and says she gave out kheer, a rich rice pudding made with ghee, to everyone who came."},{"head":"the langar today","text":"Today, gurdwaras around the world still serve langar to anyone who comes. Volunteers chop vegetables, stir big pots of dal, roll rotis and wash dishes. Many Sikhs see cooking and serving as a way of serving God."}],"game":{"items":[["in the langar, everyone sits side by side.",true,"yes: rich and poor, of every faith, sit together and eat the same food."],["only Sikhs may eat in the langar.",false,"not in it: the story says anyone can come and eat, of any faith."],["Mata Khivi gave out sweet kheer.",true,"yes: rich rice pudding made with ghee, for everyone who came."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like steam rising from a pot of dal. breathe out slowly, like passing a plate down the row. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can help set the table for everyone","grownups":{"source":"Langar is remembered as beginning with Guru Nanak at Kartarpur. Mata Khivi is praised in the Var of Satta and Balvand in Raag Ramkali (Ang 967 of the Guru Granth Sahib), which speaks of her langar and kheer with ghee.","ask":"who would you love to share a meal with, sitting side by side on the floor?"}},"es":{"title":"la cocina donde todos se sientan juntos","word":"langar","means":"ਲੰਗਰ: la cocina gratuita donde todos comen juntos","story":[{"head":"una cocina abierta a todos","text":"Cuando el Gurú Nanak se estableció en la aldea de Kartarpur, él y la gente a su alrededor cultivaban la tierra y cocinaban juntos. Los sijs recuerdan que allí empezó el langar: una cocina gratuita donde cualquiera podía venir a comer."},{"head":"todos en el mismo suelo","text":"En el langar, nadie tiene un asiento especial. Ricos y pobres, gobernantes y campesinos, personas de todas las creencias se sientan lado a lado en el suelo, en filas. Todos comen la misma comida."},{"head":"el dulce kheer de Mata Khivi","text":"Más tarde, Mata Khivi, la esposa del Gurú Angad, se encargó del langar en la aldea de Khadur. La escritura sij alaba su bondad, y dice que repartía kheer, un arroz con leche espeso hecho con ghee, a todos los que llegaban."},{"head":"el langar hoy","text":"Hoy, los gurdwaras de todo el mundo siguen sirviendo langar a quien llegue. Voluntarios pican verduras, revuelven grandes ollas de dal, extienden rotis y lavan platos. Muchos sijs ven el cocinar y servir como una manera de servir a Dios."}],"game":{"items":[["en el langar, todos se sientan lado a lado.",true,"sí: ricos y pobres, de todas las creencias, se sientan juntos y comen lo mismo."],["solo los sijs pueden comer en el langar.",false,"no está: la historia dice que cualquiera puede venir a comer, sea de la fe que sea."],["Mata Khivi repartía dulce kheer.",true,"sí: arroz con leche espeso hecho con ghee, para todos los que llegaban."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el vapor que sube de una olla de dal. exhala despacio, como si pasaras un plato a lo largo de la fila. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ayudar a poner la mesa para todos","grownups":{"source":"Se recuerda que el langar empezó con el Gurú Nanak en Kartarpur. Mata Khivi es alabada en la Var de Satta y Balvand, en el Raag Ramkali (Ang 967 del Gurú Granth Sahib), que habla de su langar y del kheer con ghee.","ask":"¿con quién te encantaría compartir una comida, sentados lado a lado en el suelo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Ang 967 (Ramkali ki Var of Satta and Balvand)","work":"Guru Granth Sahib","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Guru Nanak at Kartarpur","work":"Sikh tradition and the janamsakhis","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"duni-chand-needle","game":"match","en":{"title":"Guru Nanak and the needle","word":"nam","means":"ਨਾਮ: God's Name, remembered with love","story":[{"head":"a rich man's flags","text":"In the city of Lahore lived a rich man named Duni Chand. The janamsakhis, the old life-stories of Guru Nanak, tell that flags flew over his house, one for each great pile of money he had. He invited Guru Nanak to a feast."},{"head":"a small sewing needle","text":"After the meal, Guru Nanak gave Duni Chand a small sewing needle. As the story tells it, he asked Duni Chand to keep it safe and give it back to him in the next world, after this life."},{"head":"a wife's simple question","text":"Duni Chand happily agreed and showed the needle to his wife. She thought for a moment and asked him a simple question: how will you carry a needle with you when you leave this world?"},{"head":"what we can take with us","text":"Duni Chand went back to Guru Nanak, puzzled. Sikhs tell that the Guru gently explained that money cannot travel with us either, but kindness and nam, remembering God's Name, can. In many tellings, Duni Chand began to share what he had."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Duni Chand","had flags over his house"],["the needle","to give back in the next world"],["Duni Chand's wife","asked a simple question"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a thread sliding through the tiny eye of a needle. breathe out slowly, and let something go. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can share something that's mine today","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, the traditional life-stories of Guru Nanak, set in Lahore. Tellings vary on details such as the flags and the wife's part. Sikhs tell it to show that goodness and remembrance of God go with us; wealth does not.","ask":"what is something you have that could never fit in a pocket, like kindness?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Nanak y la aguja","word":"nam","means":"ਨਾਮ: el Nombre de Dios, recordado con amor","story":[{"head":"las banderas de un hombre rico","text":"En la ciudad de Lahore vivía un hombre rico llamado Duni Chand. Los janamsakhis, las antiguas historias de la vida del Gurú Nanak, cuentan que sobre su casa ondeaban banderas, una por cada gran montón de dinero que tenía. Invitó al Gurú Nanak a un banquete."},{"head":"una pequeña aguja de coser","text":"Después de la comida, el Gurú Nanak le dio a Duni Chand una pequeña aguja de coser. Según la historia, le pidió que la guardara bien y se la devolviera en el otro mundo, después de esta vida."},{"head":"la pregunta sencilla de una esposa","text":"Duni Chand aceptó feliz y le mostró la aguja a su esposa. Ella pensó un momento y le hizo una pregunta sencilla: ¿cómo vas a llevar una aguja contigo cuando dejes este mundo?"},{"head":"lo que podemos llevar con nosotros","text":"Duni Chand volvió con el Gurú Nanak, confundido. Los sijs cuentan que el Gurú le explicó con suavidad que el dinero tampoco viaja con nosotros, pero la bondad y el nam, recordar el Nombre de Dios, sí. En muchos relatos, Duni Chand empezó a compartir lo que tenía."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Duni Chand","tenía banderas sobre su casa"],["la aguja","para devolver en el otro mundo"],["la esposa de Duni Chand","hizo una pregunta sencilla"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un hilo que pasa por el ojito de una aguja. exhala despacio, y suelta algo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo compartir algo que es mío","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, las historias tradicionales de la vida del Gurú Nanak, en Lahore. Los relatos varían en detalles como las banderas y el papel de la esposa. Los sijs la cuentan para mostrar que la bondad y el recuerdo de Dios van con nosotros; la riqueza no.","ask":"¿qué tienes que nunca cabría en un bolsillo, como la bondad?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Duni Chand episode at Lahore","work":"Janamsakhi tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"sajjan","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Guru Nanak and Sajjan","word":"sajjan","means":"ਸੱਜਣ: a true friend; Sajjan's own name means friend","story":[{"head":"a rest house for travelers","text":"On a long journey, Guru Nanak and his friend Mardana came to a rest house owned by a man called Sajjan. Sajjan welcomed every traveler warmly. He had built a temple and a mosque there, so all his guests could pray."},{"head":"a secret kept at night","text":"But Sajjan had a secret. The janamsakhis, the old life-stories of Guru Nanak, tell that at night he took his guests' money and belongings. He thought Guru Nanak looked like a guest with many treasures."},{"head":"a song about shiny bronze","text":"That night, Guru Nanak asked Mardana to play his rabab, and the Guru sang. The song was about shiny bronze that looks bright outside but leaves black marks when you rub it. Sajjan listened, and understood the song was about him."},{"head":"a true friend at last","text":"Sajjan cried and asked for help to change. He gave back what he had taken and began to welcome travelers honestly. Sikhs remember that his rest house became a place of prayer and welcome, and that Sajjan finally lived up to his name."}],"game":{"items":[["Sajjan built a temple and a mosque for his guests.",true,"yes: so that every guest could pray."],["Guru Nanak sang about shiny bronze.",true,"yes: bronze that looks bright but leaves black marks when you rub it."],["Sajjan ran away into the forest.",false,"not in it: he stayed, gave back what he had taken, and changed."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Mardana tuning his rabab. breathe out slowly, like a song filling a quiet room. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can say sorry and make something right","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, set near Tulamba in present-day Pakistan. Sikhs link the story to Guru Nanak's hymn in Raag Suhi (Ang 729 of the Guru Granth Sahib), about bronze that shines but leaves a black mark. Tellings vary.","ask":"why do you think a song could change someone's heart?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Nanak y Sajjan","word":"sajjan","means":"ਸੱਜਣ: un amigo de verdad; el nombre de Sajjan significa amigo","story":[{"head":"una posada para viajeros","text":"En un largo viaje, el Gurú Nanak y su amigo Mardana llegaron a una posada de un hombre llamado Sajjan. Sajjan recibía a cada viajero con cariño. Había construido allí un templo y una mezquita, para que todos sus huéspedes pudieran orar."},{"head":"el secreto de Sajjan por la noche","text":"Pero Sajjan tenía un secreto. Los janamsakhis, las antiguas historias de la vida del Gurú Nanak, cuentan que por la noche se quedaba con el dinero y las cosas de sus huéspedes. Pensó que el Gurú Nanak era un huésped con muchos tesoros."},{"head":"una canción sobre el bronce brillante","text":"Esa noche, el Gurú Nanak le pidió a Mardana que tocara su rabab, y el Gurú cantó. La canción hablaba de un bronce que brilla por fuera pero deja manchas negras cuando lo frotas. Sajjan escuchó, y entendió que la canción hablaba de él."},{"head":"un amigo de verdad, por fin","text":"Sajjan lloró y pidió ayuda para cambiar. Devolvió lo que había tomado y empezó a recibir a los viajeros con honestidad. Los sijs recuerdan que su posada se volvió un lugar de oración y de bienvenida, y que Sajjan por fin hizo honor a su nombre."}],"game":{"items":[["Sajjan construyó un templo y una mezquita para sus huéspedes.",true,"sí: para que cada huésped pudiera orar."],["el Gurú Nanak cantó sobre un bronce brillante.",true,"sí: un bronce que brilla pero deja manchas negras cuando lo frotas."],["Sajjan huyó al bosque.",false,"no está: se quedó, devolvió lo que había tomado y cambió."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Mardana afinando su rabab. exhala despacio, como una canción que llena un cuarto en silencio. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo pedir perdón y arreglar algo","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, cerca de Tulamba, en el actual Pakistán. Los sijs relacionan la historia con un himno del Gurú Nanak en el Raag Suhi (Ang 729 del Gurú Granth Sahib), sobre el bronce que brilla pero deja una mancha negra. Los relatos varían.","ask":"¿por qué crees que una canción puede cambiar el corazón de alguien?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Sajjan episode near Tulamba","work":"Janamsakhi tradition (Puratan janamsakhi)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Ang 729 (Suhi, Mahala 1)","work":"Guru Nanak's hymn in Raag Suhi, Guru Granth Sahib","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":7,"key":"angad-letters","game":"match","en":{"title":"Guru Angad and the letters","word":"Gurmukhi","means":"ਗੁਰਮੁਖੀ: the letters used to write Punjabi and the Sikh hymns","story":[{"head":"a new Guru at Khadur","text":"Before his life on earth ended, Guru Nanak chose his devoted follower Lehna to carry on after him, and gave him a new name, Angad. Guru Angad lived in the village of Khadur, where people came to hear the hymns."},{"head":"children come to learn","text":"Sikhs tell that Guru Angad loved teaching children. The children gathered around him, and he helped them learn to read and write, so they could read the hymns for themselves one day."},{"head":"letters for everyone","text":"Sikh tradition says Guru Angad shaped and set in order the letters called Gurmukhi, so the words of the Gurus could be written down clearly and read by everyone, not only by a few learned people."},{"head":"games and wrestling too","text":"Guru Angad cared for children's bodies as well as their minds. Tradition says he encouraged wrestling and games, so young people would grow strong and healthy. Today, children all over the world still learn Gurmukhi, letter by letter."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Lehna","was given the name Angad"],["Khadur","the village where Angad lived"],["Gurmukhi","letters for writing the hymns"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a pen drawing the first curve of a letter. breathe out slowly, like finishing the whole word. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can help someone learn something new","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Guru Angad, the second Guru, at Khadur. Tradition credits him with shaping Gurmukhi script; historians note the letters grew from older scripts of the region. His care for wrestling and games is also remembered at Khadur Sahib.","ask":"what word would you love to learn to write in a new alphabet?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Angad y las letras","word":"Gurmukhi","means":"ਗੁਰਮੁਖੀ: las letras con que se escriben el panyabí y los himnos sijs","story":[{"head":"un nuevo Gurú en Khadur","text":"Antes de que terminara su vida en la tierra, el Gurú Nanak eligió a su fiel seguidor Lehna para continuar después de él, y le dio un nuevo nombre, Angad. El Gurú Angad vivía en la aldea de Khadur, adonde la gente iba a escuchar los himnos."},{"head":"los niños vienen a aprender","text":"Los sijs cuentan que al Gurú Angad le encantaba enseñar a los niños. Los niños se reunían a su alrededor, y él los ayudaba a leer y escribir, para que algún día pudieran leer los himnos por sí mismos."},{"head":"letras para todos","text":"La tradición sij dice que el Gurú Angad dio forma y orden a las letras llamadas Gurmukhi, para que las palabras de los Gurús se pudieran escribir con claridad y todos pudieran leerlas, no solo unas pocas personas sabias."},{"head":"juegos y lucha también","text":"Al Gurú Angad le importaba el cuerpo de los niños tanto como su mente. La tradición dice que animaba la lucha y los juegos, para que los jóvenes crecieran fuertes y sanos. Hoy, niños de todo el mundo siguen aprendiendo Gurmukhi, letra por letra."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Lehna","recibió el nombre de Angad"],["Khadur","la aldea donde vivía Angad"],["Gurmukhi","letras para escribir los himnos"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una pluma que dibuja la primera curva de una letra. exhala despacio, como si terminaras la palabra entera. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ayudar a alguien a aprender algo nuevo","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre el Gurú Angad, el segundo Gurú, en Khadur. La tradición le atribuye dar forma a la escritura Gurmukhi; los historiadores señalan que las letras vienen de escrituras más antiguas de la región. También se recuerda su gusto por la lucha y los juegos.","ask":"¿qué palabra te encantaría aprender a escribir en un alfabeto nuevo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Angad at Khadur","work":"Sikh tradition (Gur Bilas and later Sikh histories)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"akbar-in-the-pangat","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the emperor who sat on the floor","word":"pangat","means":"ਪੰਗਤ: a row of people sitting on the floor to eat together","story":[{"head":"a Guru who carried water","text":"Sikhs remember that Guru Amar Das was already old when he became the third Guru. For years before, he carried water from the river for Guru Angad every day, with great love."},{"head":"first, eat in the langar","text":"At Goindval, Guru Amar Das asked that every visitor first eat in the langar before coming to see him. Rich or poor, everyone sat down in the pangat, the row on the floor, and shared the same simple food."},{"head":"an emperor comes to visit","text":"Tradition says that one day Akbar, the emperor, came to visit. He had servants, horses and fine clothes. But before he met the Guru, he too sat on the floor in the pangat and ate the same food as everyone else."},{"head":"nobody too important for the floor","text":"After the meal, Akbar met the Guru. Sikhs tell this story to show that in the langar, an emperor and a farmer are equal. Nobody is too important to sit on the floor and eat with everyone."}],"game":{"items":[["Akbar sat on the floor to eat.",true,"yes: he sat in the pangat and ate the same food as everyone."],["Akbar was given a special golden table.",false,"not in it: nobody got a special seat; he sat on the floor in the row."],["Guru Amar Das carried water for Guru Angad.",true,"yes: Sikhs remember he carried it from the river every day."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like sitting down on a cool floor. breathe out slowly, like passing bread to the person next to you. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll treat everyone at my table the same","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Guru Amar Das, the third Guru, at Goindval, and the saying pehle pangat, pichhe sangat (first the row, then the gathering). Akbar's visit is reported in Sikh tradition; tellings differ in detail.","ask":"why do you think the Guru asked everyone to eat in the langar first?"}},"es":{"title":"el emperador que se sentó en el suelo","word":"pangat","means":"ਪੰਗਤ: una fila de personas sentadas en el suelo para comer juntas","story":[{"head":"un Gurú que cargaba agua","text":"Los sijs recuerdan que el Gurú Amar Das ya era mayor cuando se convirtió en el tercer Gurú. Durante años, antes de eso, cargaba agua del río para el Gurú Angad cada día, con mucho amor."},{"head":"primero, comer en el langar","text":"En Goindval, el Gurú Amar Das pidió que cada visitante comiera primero en el langar antes de ir a verlo. Ricos o pobres, todos se sentaban en el pangat, la fila en el suelo, y compartían la misma comida sencilla."},{"head":"un emperador viene de visita","text":"La tradición dice que un día Akbar, el emperador, vino de visita. Tenía sirvientes, caballos y ropa fina. Pero antes de ver al Gurú, él también se sentó en el suelo en el pangat y comió lo mismo que todos."},{"head":"nadie es demasiado importante","text":"Después de la comida, Akbar se encontró con el Gurú. Los sijs cuentan esta historia para mostrar que en el langar un emperador y un campesino son iguales. Nadie es demasiado importante para sentarse en el suelo y comer con todos."}],"game":{"items":[["Akbar se sentó en el suelo para comer.",true,"sí: se sentó en el pangat y comió lo mismo que todos."],["a Akbar le dieron una mesa especial de oro.",false,"no está: nadie tenía asiento especial; se sentó en el suelo, en la fila."],["el Gurú Amar Das cargaba agua para el Gurú Angad.",true,"sí: los sijs recuerdan que la traía del río cada día."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como si te sentaras en un suelo fresco. exhala despacio, como si pasaras pan a la persona de al lado. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy trataré igual a todos en mi mesa","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre el Gurú Amar Das, el tercer Gurú, en Goindval, y el dicho pehle pangat, pichhe sangat (primero la fila, luego la reunión). La visita de Akbar la cuenta la tradición sij; los relatos varían en los detalles.","ask":"¿por qué crees que el Gurú pedía a todos comer primero en el langar?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Amar Das at Goindval; Akbar's visit","work":"Sikh tradition (Mahima Prakash and later Sikh histories)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":9,"key":"jetha-serves","game":"match","en":{"title":"the boy who sold chickpeas","word":"Ram Das","means":"the name of the fourth Guru; it means servant of God","story":[{"head":"a boy with a basket","text":"Long ago in Lahore lived a boy named Jetha. Sikhs remember that he was raised by his grandmother, and that he sold boiled chickpeas from a basket to help earn their food."},{"head":"a journey to Goindval","text":"One day Jetha went with a group of travelers to Goindval to see Guru Amar Das. He loved being there so much that he stayed. He helped in the langar and carried baskets of earth to build a deep well with steps."},{"head":"serving with a happy heart","text":"Jetha served day after day without asking for anything. He fetched, carried, cooked and cleaned. Guru Amar Das saw his kind and humble heart, and in time Jetha married the Guru's daughter, Bibi Bhani."},{"head":"the servant becomes the Guru","text":"Later, Guru Amar Das chose Jetha to be the next Guru, and he became Guru Ram Das, a name that means servant of God. Guru Ram Das began a town around a pool of water; that town later became Amritsar."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Jetha","sold boiled chickpeas"],["Bibi Bhani","Guru Amar Das's daughter"],["Ram Das","means servant of God"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like lifting a heavy basket. breathe out slowly, like setting it down right where it's needed. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can help today without asking for anything back","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Guru Ram Das, the fourth Guru, born Jetha in Lahore. His service at Goindval and his founding of the town later called Amritsar are widely told; details of his childhood vary between tellings.","ask":"what is a job you could do for your family without being asked?"}},"es":{"title":"el niño que vendía garbanzos","word":"Ram Das","means":"el nombre del cuarto Gurú; significa servidor de Dios","story":[{"head":"un niño con una canasta","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en Lahore, vivía un niño llamado Jetha. Los sijs recuerdan que lo crio su abuela, y que vendía garbanzos hervidos de una canasta para ayudar a ganar la comida."},{"head":"un viaje a Goindval","text":"Un día Jetha fue con un grupo de viajeros a Goindval para ver al Gurú Amar Das. Le gustó tanto estar allí que se quedó. Ayudaba en el langar y cargaba canastas de tierra para construir un pozo hondo con escalones."},{"head":"servir con un corazón alegre","text":"Jetha servía día tras día sin pedir nada. Traía, cargaba, cocinaba y limpiaba. El Gurú Amar Das vio su corazón bueno y humilde, y con el tiempo Jetha se casó con la hija del Gurú, Bibi Bhani."},{"head":"el servidor se vuelve Gurú","text":"Más tarde, el Gurú Amar Das eligió a Jetha como el siguiente Gurú, y él se convirtió en el Gurú Ram Das, un nombre que significa servidor de Dios. El Gurú Ram Das fundó un pueblo alrededor de un estanque; ese pueblo más tarde fue Amritsar."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Jetha","vendía garbanzos hervidos"],["Bibi Bhani","la hija del Gurú Amar Das"],["Ram Das","significa servidor de Dios"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como si levantaras una canasta pesada. exhala despacio, como si la dejaras justo donde hace falta. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo ayudar sin pedir nada a cambio","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre el Gurú Ram Das, el cuarto Gurú, que nació como Jetha en Lahore. Su servicio en Goindval y la fundación del pueblo que luego se llamó Amritsar se cuentan mucho; los detalles de su niñez varían.","ask":"¿qué tarea podrías hacer por tu familia sin que te la pidan?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Ram Das (Bhai Jetha) at Lahore and Goindval","work":"Sikh tradition (Mahima Prakash and later Sikh histories)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"harmandir-four-doors","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the temple with four doors","word":"Harmandir Sahib","means":"the house of God in Amritsar; many call it the Golden Temple","story":[{"head":"a pool of shining water","text":"In the town Guru Ram Das had begun, there was a pool of water called the sarovar. His son, Guru Arjan, the fifth Guru, wanted to build a house of God right in the middle of it."},{"head":"built low, not high","text":"Sikhs tell that Guru Arjan built the Harmandir lower than the land around it, instead of high up. So everyone walks down steps to reach it, humbly, whoever they are."},{"head":"a door on every side","text":"The Harmandir has four doors, one facing each direction: north, south, east and west. Sikhs say this means it is open to everyone, coming from every direction, of every faith and every background."},{"head":"the holy book comes home","text":"When it was ready, Guru Arjan placed the book of Sikh hymns inside. Today the temple is covered in gold, kirtan fills it through the day, and visitors walk slowly around the shining pool."}],"game":{"items":[["the temple has four doors.",true,"yes: one on each side, open to everyone."],["visitors climb a tall staircase up to the temple.",false,"not in it: it is built low, so everyone walks down steps to reach it."],["the temple stands in a pool of water.",true,"yes: in the middle of the sarovar, the shining pool."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like walking down the steps toward the water. breathe out slowly, like ripples spreading across the shining pool. four slow breaths.","carry":"everyone is welcome at my door today","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Guru Arjan, the fifth Guru, who built the Harmandir Sahib in Amritsar and installed the Adi Granth there. The gold covering was added much later. Sikhs often explain its four doors as openness to all.","ask":"if you built a place for everyone, what would you put at its door?"}},"es":{"title":"el templo de las cuatro puertas","word":"Harmandir Sahib","means":"la casa de Dios en Amritsar; muchos lo llaman el Templo Dorado","story":[{"head":"un estanque de agua brillante","text":"En el pueblo que había fundado el Gurú Ram Das había un estanque llamado sarovar. Su hijo, el Gurú Arjan, el quinto Gurú, quiso construir una casa de Dios justo en medio del agua."},{"head":"construido abajo, no arriba","text":"Los sijs cuentan que el Gurú Arjan construyó el Harmandir más bajo que la tierra a su alrededor, en vez de en lo alto. Así, todos bajan escalones para llegar, con humildad, sean quienes sean."},{"head":"una puerta en cada lado","text":"El Harmandir tiene cuatro puertas, una hacia cada dirección: norte, sur, este y oeste. Los sijs dicen que significa que está abierto a todos, vengan de donde vengan, de cualquier fe y cualquier origen."},{"head":"el libro sagrado llega a casa","text":"Cuando estuvo listo, el Gurú Arjan colocó dentro el libro de los himnos sijs. Hoy el templo está cubierto de oro, el kirtan lo llena durante el día, y los visitantes caminan despacio alrededor del estanque brillante."}],"game":{"items":[["el templo tiene cuatro puertas.",true,"sí: una en cada lado, abiertas a todos."],["los visitantes suben una escalera alta hasta el templo.",false,"no está: está construido abajo, así que todos bajan escalones para llegar."],["el templo está en medio de un estanque.",true,"sí: en medio del sarovar, el estanque brillante."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como si bajaras los escalones hacia el agua. exhala despacio, como las ondas que se extienden por el estanque brillante. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy todos son bienvenidos a mi puerta","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre el Gurú Arjan, el quinto Gurú, que construyó el Harmandir Sahib en Amritsar y colocó allí el Adi Granth. La cubierta de oro se añadió mucho después. Los sijs suelen explicar sus cuatro puertas como apertura a todos.","ask":"si construyeras un lugar para todos, ¿qué pondrías en su puerta?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Arjan and the building of the Harmandir Sahib","work":"Sikh tradition and Sikh histories","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"har-rai-flower","game":"match","en":{"title":"Guru Har Rai and the flower","word":"daya","means":"ਦਇਆ: kindness and gentle care for every living thing","story":[{"head":"a boy in a wide robe","text":"When Har Rai was a boy, he lived with his grandfather, Guru Hargobind. One day he was walking in the garden, wearing a wide, flowing robe that swept around him as he went."},{"head":"a flower falls","text":"As he passed, the edge of his robe caught a flowering plant. A flower broke off and fell to the ground. Har Rai stopped, and he was sad to see the broken flower lying there."},{"head":"a grandfather's gentle words","text":"Guru Hargobind saw what had happened. Sikhs tell that he gently reminded his grandson that a wide robe must be carried with care, so that it does not harm things along the way."},{"head":"a lifetime of care","text":"From then on, the story says, Har Rai held his robe close when he walked. When he became the seventh Guru, he kept gardens, cared for animals, and kept medicines for sick people. Sikhs call this kind of care daya."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Har Rai","wore a wide, flowing robe"],["Guru Hargobind","Har Rai's grandfather"],["the flower","broke off and fell"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like smelling a flower in the garden. breathe out slowly, like walking carefully past it. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can move gently around living things today","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Guru Har Rai, the seventh Guru, as a boy in the household of his grandfather, Guru Hargobind. Tradition also remembers his gardens, his care for animals and the medicines he kept for the sick.","ask":"what is something small and alive near our home that we could take care of?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Har Rai y la flor","word":"daya","means":"ਦਇਆ: bondad y cuidado suave por todo ser vivo","story":[{"head":"un niño con una túnica ancha","text":"Cuando Har Rai era niño, vivía con su abuelo, el Gurú Hargobind. Un día caminaba por el jardín, con una túnica ancha y suelta que se movía a su alrededor al andar."},{"head":"una flor cae","text":"Al pasar, el borde de su túnica rozó una planta en flor. Una flor se rompió y cayó al suelo. Har Rai se detuvo, y le dio tristeza ver la flor rota en el suelo."},{"head":"las palabras suaves de un abuelo","text":"El Gurú Hargobind vio lo que había pasado. Los sijs cuentan que le recordó con suavidad a su nieto que una túnica ancha hay que llevarla con cuidado, para no dañar nada en el camino."},{"head":"una vida entera de cuidado","text":"Desde entonces, dice la historia, Har Rai sostenía su túnica cerca al caminar. Cuando fue el séptimo Gurú, cuidaba jardines y animales, y guardaba medicinas para los enfermos. Los sijs llaman daya a este cuidado."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Har Rai","llevaba una túnica ancha"],["el Gurú Hargobind","el abuelo de Har Rai"],["la flor","se rompió y cayó"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como si olieras una flor del jardín. exhala despacio, como si pasaras a su lado con cuidado. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo moverme con cuidado cerca de los seres vivos","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre el Gurú Har Rai, el séptimo Gurú, de niño en la casa de su abuelo, el Gurú Hargobind. La tradición también recuerda sus jardines, su cuidado de los animales y las medicinas que guardaba para los enfermos.","ask":"¿qué ser pequeño y vivo cerca de casa podríamos cuidar?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Har Rai as a boy at Kiratpur","work":"Sikh tradition and Sikh histories","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"har-krishan-helps","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Guru Har Krishan, the child Guru","word":"Bangla Sahib","means":"a gurdwara in Delhi where Guru Har Krishan stayed","story":[{"head":"a very young Guru","text":"Har Krishan was still a small child when he became the eighth Guru, after his father, Guru Har Rai. Sikhs remember him as gentle and wise, much wiser than his years."},{"head":"a sickness in the city","text":"The young Guru traveled to the great city of Delhi. While he was there, a sickness spread through the city. Many people were ill, and their families were very worried."},{"head":"a child who came to help","text":"Sikhs tell that Guru Har Krishan did not stay away. He went among the sick people, comforting them and helping to care for them, whoever they were, rich or poor, of any faith."},{"head":"water and food at Bangla Sahib","text":"The house where he stayed is now Gurdwara Bangla Sahib. Visitors there still take water from its pool and share food in its langar, remembering the child Guru who cared for everyone."}],"game":{"items":[["Guru Har Krishan was a small child when he became Guru.",true,"yes: he was still a small child, remembered as gentle and wise."],["Guru Har Krishan stayed far away from the sick.",false,"not in it: he went among the sick people to comfort and care for them."],["the house where he stayed is now a gurdwara.",true,"yes: Gurdwara Bangla Sahib in Delhi."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a cool cup of water. breathe out slowly, like handing it to someone who needs it. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can check on someone who isn't feeling well","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Guru Har Krishan, the eighth Guru, who became Guru as a young child and stayed in Delhi during an outbreak of illness. Gurdwara Bangla Sahib stands on the site. Sources give his age differently, so we leave it out.","ask":"how can a child help someone who is sick or sad?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Har Krishan, el Gurú niño","word":"Bangla Sahib","means":"un gurdwara en Delhi donde se quedó el Gurú Har Krishan","story":[{"head":"un Gurú muy pequeño","text":"Har Krishan todavía era un niño pequeño cuando se convirtió en el octavo Gurú, después de su padre, el Gurú Har Rai. Los sijs lo recuerdan amable y sabio, mucho más sabio de lo que su edad hacía pensar."},{"head":"una enfermedad en la ciudad","text":"El joven Gurú viajó a la gran ciudad de Delhi. Mientras estaba allí, una enfermedad se extendió por la ciudad. Mucha gente estaba enferma, y sus familias estaban muy preocupadas."},{"head":"un niño que vino a ayudar","text":"Los sijs cuentan que el Gurú Har Krishan no se quedó lejos. Fue entre los enfermos, consolándolos y ayudando a cuidarlos, fueran quienes fueran, ricos o pobres, de cualquier fe."},{"head":"agua y comida en Bangla Sahib","text":"La casa donde se quedó es hoy el Gurdwara Bangla Sahib. Los visitantes todavía toman agua de su estanque y comparten comida en su langar, recordando al Gurú niño que cuidó de todos."}],"game":{"items":[["el Gurú Har Krishan era un niño pequeño cuando fue Gurú.",true,"sí: todavía era un niño pequeño, recordado como amable y sabio."],["el Gurú Har Krishan se quedó lejos de los enfermos.",false,"no está: fue entre los enfermos para consolarlos y cuidarlos."],["la casa donde se quedó hoy es un gurdwara.",true,"sí: el Gurdwara Bangla Sahib, en Delhi."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un vaso de agua fresca. exhala despacio, como si se lo dieras a alguien que lo necesita. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo preguntar cómo está alguien que se siente mal","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre el Gurú Har Krishan, el octavo Gurú, que llegó a ser Gurú siendo muy niño y estuvo en Delhi durante una epidemia. El Gurdwara Bangla Sahib está en ese lugar. Las fuentes dan su edad de forma distinta, así que no la decimos.","ask":"¿cómo puede un niño ayudar a alguien que está enfermo o triste?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Har Krishan in Delhi","work":"Sikh tradition and Sikh histories","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"bandi-chhor","game":"match","en":{"title":"Guru Hargobind and the cloak","word":"Bandi Chhor","means":"ਬੰਦੀ ਛੋੜ: the one who sets captives free","story":[{"head":"a Guru kept in a fort","text":"Guru Hargobind, the sixth Guru, was once kept in the fort of Gwalior by the emperor Jahangir. Also held in the fort were many princes and rulers from different lands."},{"head":"free, but not alone","text":"After a time, the emperor agreed to let the Guru go. But Sikhs tell that Guru Hargobind would not leave unless the princes held there with him were set free too."},{"head":"a cloak with many tassels","text":"The emperor said that as many princes as could hold on to the Guru's cloak could leave with him. So the Guru had a special cloak made, with long tassels, fifty-two of them, tradition says, one for each prince."},{"head":"lamps for the homecoming","text":"Holding the tassels, every prince walked out of the fort, free. When Guru Hargobind reached Amritsar, people lit lamps everywhere. Sikhs still light lamps on Bandi Chhor Divas, around the time of Diwali."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Guru Hargobind","the sixth Guru"],["the cloak","had long tassels"],["Bandi Chhor Divas","a day for lighting lamps"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a lamp being lit. breathe out slowly, like its light spreading across the water. four slow breaths.","carry":"when something good happens, I can bring others along","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Guru Hargobind's release from Gwalior Fort. The number of princes, fifty-two, comes from tradition, and historical details are debated. Sikhs mark his return to Amritsar as Bandi Chhor Divas, near Diwali.","ask":"who would you want to bring along with you when something good happens?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Hargobind y el manto","word":"Bandi Chhor","means":"ਬੰਦੀ ਛੋੜ: el que libera a los cautivos","story":[{"head":"un Gurú retenido en un fuerte","text":"El Gurú Hargobind, el sexto Gurú, estuvo una vez retenido en el fuerte de Gwalior por el emperador Jahangir. En el fuerte también estaban retenidos muchos príncipes y gobernantes de distintas tierras."},{"head":"libre, pero no solo","text":"Después de un tiempo, el emperador aceptó dejar ir al Gurú. Pero los sijs cuentan que el Gurú Hargobind no quiso salir a menos que también liberaran a los príncipes que estaban con él."},{"head":"un manto con muchas borlas","text":"El emperador dijo que podrían salir con él todos los príncipes que alcanzaran a agarrarse de su manto. Entonces el Gurú mandó hacer un manto especial, con borlas largas, cincuenta y dos, dice la tradición, una para cada príncipe."},{"head":"lámparas para el regreso","text":"Agarrados de las borlas, todos los príncipes salieron del fuerte, libres. Cuando el Gurú Hargobind llegó a Amritsar, la gente encendió lámparas por todas partes. Los sijs todavía encienden lámparas en Bandi Chhor Divas, cerca de Diwali."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el Gurú Hargobind","el sexto Gurú"],["el manto","tenía borlas largas"],["Bandi Chhor Divas","un día para encender lámparas"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una lámpara que se enciende. exhala despacio, como su luz que se extiende sobre el agua. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"cuando pasa algo bueno, puedo llevar a otros conmigo","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre la liberación del Gurú Hargobind del fuerte de Gwalior. El número de príncipes, cincuenta y dos, viene de la tradición, y los detalles históricos se discuten. Los sijs celebran su regreso a Amritsar como Bandi Chhor Divas, cerca de Diwali.","ask":"¿a quién te gustaría llevar contigo cuando pasa algo bueno?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Hargobind at Gwalior Fort","work":"Sikh tradition (Gurbilas Patshahi 6 and later Sikh histories)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"vaisakhi-khalsa","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Vaisakhi and the five beloved ones","word":"Panj Pyare","means":"ਪੰਜ ਪਿਆਰੇ: the five beloved ones, the first of the Khalsa","story":[{"head":"a great gathering at Anandpur","text":"At the spring festival of Vaisakhi, Guru Gobind Singh, the tenth Guru, called Sikhs together at Anandpur. Tradition says a huge crowd came from near and far to be with their Guru."},{"head":"who will give everything?","text":"The Guru asked a hard question: who loved the Guru enough to give everything, even their own life? The crowd grew very quiet. Then, one by one, five people stepped forward, brave and willing."},{"head":"five from different places","text":"The five came from different places and families: Daya Ram, Dharam Das, Himmat Rai, Mohkam Chand and Sahib Chand. Sikhs call them the Panj Pyare, the five beloved ones."},{"head":"sweet amrit in an iron bowl","text":"The Guru stirred water in an iron bowl with a khanda, a double-edged sword, while prayers were recited. Tradition says Mata Sahib Kaur added sugar sweets. The five drank this amrit and became the Khalsa, and then the Guru asked them to give amrit to him too."}],"game":{"items":[["five people stepped forward.",true,"yes: five, from different places and families."],["the five were brothers from one family.",false,"not in it: they came from different places and families."],["sugar sweets were added to the amrit.",true,"yes: tradition says Mata Sahib Kaur added them."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like fresh spring air at Vaisakhi. breathe out slowly, like sweet water swirling in the iron bowl. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can be brave for something good today","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about the founding of the Khalsa by Guru Gobind Singh at Anandpur on Vaisakhi. Many tellings include more dramatic details, left out here for young children. The names of the five and Mata Sahib Kaur's sweets are traditional.","ask":"what does being brave for something good mean to you?"}},"es":{"title":"Vaisakhi y los cinco amados","word":"Panj Pyare","means":"ਪੰਜ ਪਿਆਰੇ: los cinco amados, los primeros del Khalsa","story":[{"head":"una gran reunión en Anandpur","text":"En la fiesta de primavera de Vaisakhi, el Gurú Gobind Singh, el décimo Gurú, reunió a los sijs en Anandpur. La tradición dice que llegó una multitud enorme, de cerca y de lejos, para estar con su Gurú."},{"head":"¿quién lo dará todo?","text":"El Gurú hizo una pregunta difícil: ¿quién amaba al Gurú tanto como para darlo todo, incluso su propia vida? La multitud quedó en silencio. Entonces, uno por uno, cinco personas dieron un paso al frente, valientes y dispuestas."},{"head":"cinco de lugares distintos","text":"Los cinco venían de lugares y familias distintas: Daya Ram, Dharam Das, Himmat Rai, Mohkam Chand y Sahib Chand. Los sijs los llaman los Panj Pyare, los cinco amados."},{"head":"amrit dulce en un tazón de hierro","text":"El Gurú revolvió agua en un tazón de hierro con un khanda, una espada de doble filo, mientras se recitaban oraciones. La tradición dice que Mata Sahib Kaur añadió dulces de azúcar. Los cinco bebieron este amrit y formaron el Khalsa, y luego el Gurú les pidió que le dieran amrit a él también."}],"game":{"items":[["cinco personas dieron un paso al frente.",true,"sí: cinco, de lugares y familias distintas."],["los cinco eran hermanos de una misma familia.",false,"no está: venían de lugares y familias distintas."],["al amrit le añadieron dulces de azúcar.",true,"sí: la tradición dice que Mata Sahib Kaur los añadió."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el aire fresco de la primavera en Vaisakhi. exhala despacio, como el agua dulce que gira en el tazón de hierro. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo ser valiente por algo bueno","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre la fundación del Khalsa por el Gurú Gobind Singh en Anandpur, en Vaisakhi. Muchos relatos incluyen detalles más dramáticos, que aquí dejamos fuera para los niños. Los nombres de los cinco y los dulces de Mata Sahib Kaur son tradicionales.","ask":"¿qué significa para ti ser valiente por algo bueno?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the founding of the Khalsa at Anandpur on Vaisakhi","work":"Sikh tradition (Sri Gur Sobha, Gurbilas literature and later Sikh histories)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":15,"key":"bhai-kanhaiya","game":"match","en":{"title":"Bhai Kanhaiya and the water bag","word":"sarbat da bhala","means":"ਸਰਬੱਤ ਦਾ ਭਲਾ: the good of all, everyone, everywhere","story":[{"head":"a water carrier at Anandpur","text":"Bhai Kanhaiya was a Sikh of Guru Gobind Singh. He served at Anandpur, carrying water in a leather bag on his shoulder and giving a drink to anyone who was thirsty."},{"head":"thirsty people on both sides","text":"During a time of fighting, many soldiers lay hurt and thirsty. Bhai Kanhaiya walked among all of them with his water bag. He gave water to the Guru's soldiers, and to soldiers from the other side too."},{"head":"a question for the Guru","text":"Some Sikhs were upset and brought him to Guru Gobind Singh. Sikhs tell that Bhai Kanhaiya answered that he did not see friends or enemies there. In every thirsty face, he saw only the Guru."},{"head":"water and a healing balm","text":"Sikhs tell that the Guru was pleased, and gave Bhai Kanhaiya a healing balm too, so he could care for everyone's hurts as well as give them water. Many Sikhs remember him when they pray for sarbat da bhala, the good of all."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Bhai Kanhaiya","carried a water bag"],["the thirsty soldiers","lay hurt on both sides"],["the healing balm","a gift from the Guru"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a cool drink of water. breathe out slowly, like pouring it gently for someone else. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can be kind to someone who isn't my friend","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition about Bhai Kanhaiya at Anandpur in the time of Guru Gobind Singh. Tellings vary in detail. Many Sikhs see him as an example of seva for all, and sarbat da bhala (the good of all) is prayed for in the Ardas.","ask":"what small kindness could you show to someone who isn't your friend yet?"}},"es":{"title":"Bhai Kanhaiya y la bolsa de agua","word":"sarbat da bhala","means":"ਸਰਬੱਤ ਦਾ ਭਲਾ: el bien de todos, en todas partes","story":[{"head":"un aguador en Anandpur","text":"Bhai Kanhaiya era un sij del Gurú Gobind Singh. Servía en Anandpur, cargando agua en una bolsa de cuero sobre el hombro y dando de beber a cualquiera que tuviera sed."},{"head":"gente con sed en los dos lados","text":"En un tiempo de lucha, muchos soldados estaban heridos y con sed. Bhai Kanhaiya caminó entre todos ellos con su bolsa de agua. Dio agua a los soldados del Gurú, y también a los soldados del otro lado."},{"head":"una pregunta ante el Gurú","text":"Algunos sijs se molestaron y lo llevaron ante el Gurú Gobind Singh. Los sijs cuentan que Bhai Kanhaiya respondió que allí no veía amigos ni enemigos. En cada rostro con sed, solo veía al Gurú."},{"head":"agua y un bálsamo que sana","text":"Los sijs cuentan que el Gurú quedó contento, y le dio a Bhai Kanhaiya también un bálsamo para sanar, para que cuidara las heridas de todos además de darles agua. Muchos sijs lo recuerdan cuando piden en oración sarbat da bhala, el bien de todos."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Bhai Kanhaiya","cargaba una bolsa de agua"],["los soldados con sed","heridos en los dos lados"],["el bálsamo que sana","un regalo del Gurú"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como un trago de agua fresca. exhala despacio, como si la sirvieras con cuidado para otra persona. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ser amable con alguien que no es mi amigo","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij sobre Bhai Kanhaiya en Anandpur, en tiempos del Gurú Gobind Singh. Los relatos varían en los detalles. Muchos sijs lo ven como ejemplo de seva para todos, y sarbat da bhala (el bien de todos) se pide en la Ardas.","ask":"¿qué pequeña bondad podrías mostrar a alguien que todavía no es tu amigo?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Bhai Kanhaiya at Anandpur","work":"Sikh tradition and Sikh histories","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the closing of the Ardas (sarbat da bhala)","work":"Ardas, the Sikh congregational prayer","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"seva-shoes","game":"truth","en":{"title":"seva at the gurdwara","word":"seva","means":"ਸੇਵਾ: serving others freely, without wanting anything back","story":[{"head":"shoes off at the door","text":"At many gurdwaras, the first thing visitors do is take off their shoes and cover their heads. They hand their shoes to volunteers, who keep them safe on long shelves until the visitors return."},{"head":"polishing a stranger's shoes","text":"Some of those volunteers are doctors, some are grandparents, some are children. Many Sikhs say that wiping and polishing a stranger's shoes is a way to set pride aside and serve with love."},{"head":"many hands, many jobs","text":"Inside, there is seva everywhere. People wash the floors, sweep the courtyard, chop onions for the langar, scrub big pots and hand out water. Nobody is paid. Everyone helps because they want to."},{"head":"a gift that asks nothing back","text":"Sikhs call this seva: serving freely, without wanting thanks or a reward. The stories say the Gurus themselves served in the langar and cared for visitors. Anyone, of any faith, can join in."}],"game":{"items":[["volunteers keep visitors' shoes safe.",true,"yes: on long shelves until the visitors come back."],["the volunteers at the gurdwara are paid.",false,"not in it: nobody is paid; everyone helps because they want to."],["people chop onions for the langar.",true,"yes: along with washing floors, scrubbing pots and handing out water."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like lifting a pair of shoes from the shelf. breathe out slowly, like setting them down clean and ready. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can tidy something that isn't mine today","grownups":{"source":"This describes seva, selfless service, as many Sikhs practice it at gurdwaras today. Customs vary from gurdwara to gurdwara. Looking after the shoes, often called joda ghar seva, is a well-known form of humble service.","ask":"what kind of seva would you most like to try?"}},"es":{"title":"seva en el gurdwara","word":"seva","means":"ਸੇਵਾ: servir a otros libremente, sin querer nada a cambio","story":[{"head":"zapatos fuera en la puerta","text":"En muchos gurdwaras, lo primero que hacen los visitantes es quitarse los zapatos y cubrirse la cabeza. Entregan sus zapatos a voluntarios, que los guardan en estantes largos hasta que los visitantes regresan."},{"head":"lustrar los zapatos de un extraño","text":"Algunos de esos voluntarios son médicos, otros son abuelos, otros son niños. Muchos sijs dicen que limpiar y lustrar los zapatos de un extraño es una manera de dejar el orgullo a un lado y servir con amor."},{"head":"muchas manos, muchas tareas","text":"Adentro, hay seva por todas partes. La gente lava los pisos, barre el patio, pica cebollas para el langar, friega ollas grandes y reparte agua. A nadie le pagan. Todos ayudan porque quieren."},{"head":"un regalo que no pide nada","text":"Los sijs llaman a esto seva: servir libremente, sin querer gracias ni premio. Las historias dicen que los mismos Gurús servían en el langar y atendían a los visitantes. Cualquiera, de cualquier fe, puede participar."}],"game":{"items":[["los voluntarios guardan los zapatos de los visitantes.",true,"sí: en estantes largos hasta que los visitantes regresan."],["a los voluntarios del gurdwara les pagan.",false,"no está: a nadie le pagan; todos ayudan porque quieren."],["la gente pica cebollas para el langar.",true,"sí: además de lavar pisos, fregar ollas y repartir agua."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como si levantaras un par de zapatos del estante. exhala despacio, como si los dejaras limpios y listos. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo ordenar algo que no es mío","grownups":{"source":"Esto describe la seva, el servicio desinteresado, como muchos sijs la practican hoy en los gurdwaras. Las costumbres cambian de un gurdwara a otro. Cuidar los zapatos, llamado a menudo joda ghar seva, es una forma muy conocida de servicio humilde.","ask":"¿qué tipo de seva te gustaría probar?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the section on seva (voluntary service)","work":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (the Sikh code of conduct)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"five-ks","game":"match","en":{"title":"the five Ks","word":"Khalsa","means":"ਖ਼ਾਲਸਾ: Sikhs who have taken amrit and keep the five Ks","story":[{"head":"five things, five reminders","text":"Sikhs who have joined the Khalsa keep five things with them every day. In Punjabi, each name begins with the sound k, so they are called the five Ks. Each one carries a meaning."},{"head":"hair and a comb","text":"Kesh is uncut hair, kept as God made it, often wrapped in a turban. Kangha is a small wooden comb, tucked into the hair, to keep it clean and tidy."},{"head":"a bracelet and cotton shorts","text":"Kara is a steel bracelet worn on the wrist. It is a circle with no end, a reminder of God, who has no beginning and no end. Kachera are cotton shorts worn underneath, a reminder of self-control."},{"head":"a small sword in its cover","text":"Kirpan is a small, curved sword, kept in its cover. Many Sikhs explain it as a promise to stand up for people who need protection. Many other Sikhs keep some of the five Ks, in their own way."}],"game":{"prompt":"match each K to what it is.","pairs":[["kesh","uncut hair"],["kara","a steel bracelet"],["kangha","a small wooden comb"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like tracing a finger around a kara. breathe out slowly, all the way around the circle. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can stand up for someone who needs help today","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh practice: the five Ks kept by Sikhs initiated into the Khalsa, which tradition traces to Guru Gobind Singh. Explanations of each article vary among Sikhs; these are common ones.","ask":"if you could carry one reminder with you every day, what would it be?"}},"es":{"title":"las cinco K","word":"Khalsa","means":"ਖ਼ਾਲਸਾ: sijs que han tomado amrit y llevan las cinco K","story":[{"head":"cinco cosas, cinco recordatorios","text":"Los sijs que se han unido al Khalsa llevan cinco cosas consigo cada día. En panyabí, cada nombre empieza con el sonido k, por eso se llaman las cinco K. Cada una tiene un significado."},{"head":"el cabello y un peine","text":"Kesh es el cabello sin cortar, como Dios lo hizo, muchas veces envuelto en un turbante. Kangha es un pequeño peine de madera, metido en el cabello, para mantenerlo limpio y ordenado."},{"head":"un brazalete y un short de algodón","text":"Kara es un brazalete de acero en la muñeca. Es un círculo sin final, que recuerda a Dios, que no tiene principio ni fin. Kachera es un short de algodón que se lleva debajo, un recordatorio de autocontrol."},{"head":"una espada pequeña en su funda","text":"Kirpan es una espada pequeña y curva, guardada en su funda. Muchos sijs la explican como una promesa de defender a quien necesita protección. Muchos otros sijs llevan algunas de las cinco K, a su manera."}],"game":{"prompt":"une cada K con lo que es.","pairs":[["kesh","cabello sin cortar"],["kara","un brazalete de acero"],["kangha","un pequeño peine de madera"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como si recorrieras un kara con el dedo. exhala despacio, dando toda la vuelta al círculo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo defender a alguien que necesita ayuda","grownups":{"source":"De la práctica sij: las cinco K que llevan los sijs iniciados en el Khalsa, que la tradición remonta al Gurú Gobind Singh. Las explicaciones de cada una varían entre los sijs; estas son comunes.","ask":"si pudieras llevar un recordatorio contigo cada día, ¿cuál sería?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the section on the five Ks (kakars) for amritdhari Sikhs","work":"Sikh Rehat Maryada (the Sikh code of conduct)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"living-guru","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the book that is the Guru","word":"Guru Granth Sahib","means":"the Sikh scripture, honored by Sikhs as the living Guru","story":[{"head":"ten Gurus, one light","text":"Sikhs remember ten Gurus, from Guru Nanak to Guru Gobind Singh. Sikhs say the same light shone through each of them, passed on like a flame from one lamp to the next."},{"head":"the Guru from now on","text":"Near the end of his life, Guru Gobind Singh named the holy book, the Granth, as the Guru for all time. From then on, Sikhs say, there would be no more human Gurus. The words themselves would be the Guru."},{"head":"many voices in one book","text":"The book holds the hymns of the Gurus. It also holds songs by holy people from other backgrounds, Hindu and Muslim, such as Kabir, Ravidas and Sheikh Farid. All of them are sung together."},{"head":"a day with the living Guru","text":"In the morning, the Guru Granth Sahib is opened under a canopy, and a hymn is read for the day. Someone gently waves a whisk above it, in respect. At night it is wrapped in cloth and carried to rest."}],"game":{"items":[["the book holds songs by Kabir and Sheikh Farid.",true,"yes: alongside the Gurus' hymns, songs by holy people from other backgrounds."],["the book is kept locked in a box all day.",false,"not in it: it is opened in the morning under a canopy and read."],["Guru Gobind Singh named the book as the Guru.",true,"yes: near the end of his life, as the Guru for all time."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a page turning softly. breathe out slowly, like a cloth folded gently over the book at night. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can listen carefully to wise words today","grownups":{"source":"From Sikh tradition: Guru Gobind Singh named the Granth as Guru at Nanded. The Guru Granth Sahib includes hymns of six of the Gurus and of bhagats such as Kabir, Ravidas and Sheikh Farid. Daily customs vary between gurdwaras.","ask":"what wise words would you like to hear again and again?"}},"es":{"title":"el libro que es el Gurú","word":"Gurú Granth Sahib","means":"la escritura sij, honrada por los sijs como el Gurú vivo","story":[{"head":"diez Gurús, una sola luz","text":"Los sijs recuerdan a diez Gurús, desde el Gurú Nanak hasta el Gurú Gobind Singh. Los sijs dicen que la misma luz brilló en cada uno, pasando como una llama de una lámpara a la siguiente."},{"head":"el Gurú de ahora en adelante","text":"Cerca del final de su vida, el Gurú Gobind Singh nombró al libro sagrado, el Granth, como el Gurú para siempre. Desde entonces, dicen los sijs, ya no habría más Gurús humanos. Las palabras mismas serían el Gurú."},{"head":"muchas voces en un libro","text":"El libro guarda los himnos de los Gurús. También guarda canciones de personas santas de otros orígenes, hindúes y musulmanes, como Kabir, Ravidas y el jeque Farid. Todas se cantan juntas."},{"head":"un día con el Gurú vivo","text":"Por la mañana, el Gurú Granth Sahib se abre bajo un dosel, y se lee un himno para el día. Alguien agita con suavidad un abanico sobre él, en señal de respeto. Por la noche se envuelve en tela y se lleva a descansar."}],"game":{"items":[["el libro guarda canciones de Kabir y del jeque Farid.",true,"sí: junto a los himnos de los Gurús, canciones de personas santas de otros orígenes."],["el libro se guarda bajo llave en una caja todo el día.",false,"no está: se abre por la mañana bajo un dosel y se lee."],["el Gurú Gobind Singh nombró al libro como el Gurú.",true,"sí: cerca del final de su vida, como el Gurú para siempre."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una página que pasa suavemente. exhala despacio, como una tela que cubre el libro con cuidado por la noche. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo escuchar con atención palabras sabias","grownups":{"source":"De la tradición sij: el Gurú Gobind Singh nombró al Granth como Gurú en Nanded. El Gurú Granth Sahib incluye himnos de seis de los Gurús y de bhagats como Kabir, Ravidas y el jeque Farid. Las costumbres diarias varían entre gurdwaras.","ask":"¿qué palabras sabias te gustaría escuchar una y otra vez?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the Guru Granth Sahib as Guru (Nanded)","work":"Sikh tradition and the Sikh Rehat Maryada","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the bhagat bani (hymns of Kabir, Ravidas, Sheikh Farid)","work":"Guru Granth Sahib","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"mardana-kirtan","game":"match","en":{"title":"Guru Nanak and Mardana's music","word":"kirtan","means":"ਕੀਰਤਨ: singing hymns of praise to God, with music","story":[{"head":"two friends on the road","text":"Guru Nanak traveled far on foot to share his message. With him went his friend Bhai Mardana, a Muslim musician who played the rabab, a stringed instrument he carried everywhere."},{"head":"play, the words are coming","text":"The janamsakhis, the old life-stories of Guru Nanak, tell that when a hymn came to Guru Nanak, he would ask Mardana to play. Mardana would tune his rabab, and the Guru would sing the new words to his music."},{"head":"tunes for every time of day","text":"The Guru's hymns were sung in raags, tunes meant for different times of day and different feelings. In the Guru Granth Sahib, the hymns are still arranged by their raag."},{"head":"kirtan in the gurdwara today","text":"Today kirtan fills gurdwaras around the world. Musicians play harmonium and tabla, and everyone sings together. Many Sikhs sing Waheguru, a name for God full of wonder, again and again."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Bhai Mardana","played the rabab"],["Guru Nanak","sang the hymns"],["a raag","a tune for a time of day"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like Mardana tuning his strings. breathe out slowly, like the first long note of a song. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can sing or hum something joyful today","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, which tell of Guru Nanak's journeys with Bhai Mardana, a Muslim rabab player. The Guru Granth Sahib arranges its hymns by raag. Waheguru is a name for God often used in Sikh worship.","ask":"what song makes you feel calm or joyful?"}},"es":{"title":"el Gurú Nanak y la música de Mardana","word":"kirtan","means":"ਕੀਰਤਨ: cantar himnos de alabanza a Dios, con música","story":[{"head":"dos amigos en el camino","text":"El Gurú Nanak viajó lejos a pie para compartir su mensaje. Con él iba su amigo Bhai Mardana, un músico musulmán que tocaba el rabab, un instrumento de cuerdas que llevaba a todas partes."},{"head":"toca, ya vienen las palabras","text":"Los janamsakhis, las antiguas historias de la vida del Gurú Nanak, cuentan que cuando un himno llegaba al Gurú Nanak, le pedía a Mardana que tocara. Mardana afinaba su rabab, y el Gurú cantaba las palabras nuevas con su música."},{"head":"melodías para cada hora del día","text":"Los himnos del Gurú se cantaban en raags, melodías pensadas para distintas horas del día y distintos sentimientos. En el Gurú Granth Sahib, los himnos todavía están ordenados por su raag."},{"head":"kirtan en el gurdwara hoy","text":"Hoy el kirtan llena gurdwaras en todo el mundo. Los músicos tocan armonio y tabla, y todos cantan juntos. Muchos sijs cantan Waheguru, un nombre de Dios lleno de asombro, una y otra vez."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Bhai Mardana","tocaba el rabab"],["el Gurú Nanak","cantaba los himnos"],["un raag","una melodía para una hora"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como Mardana afinando sus cuerdas. exhala despacio, como la primera nota larga de una canción. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy puedo cantar o tararear algo alegre","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, que cuentan los viajes del Gurú Nanak con Bhai Mardana, un músico musulmán que tocaba el rabab. El Gurú Granth Sahib ordena sus himnos por raag. Waheguru es un nombre de Dios muy usado en el culto sij.","ask":"¿qué canción te hace sentir en calma o con alegría?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Nanak's journeys (udasis) with Bhai Mardana","work":"Janamsakhi tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"the arrangement of hymns by raag","work":"Guru Granth Sahib","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"tera-tera","game":"truth","en":{"title":"tera, tera: all is yours","word":"tera","means":"ਤੇਰਾ: yours, and also thirteen in Punjabi","story":[{"head":"a job at the storehouse","text":"As a young man, Nanak worked at a big storehouse in Sultanpur, which belonged to the ruler of the town. His job was to weigh out grain for the people who came to buy it."},{"head":"counting the scoops","text":"The janamsakhis, the old life-stories of Guru Nanak, tell that one day he was weighing grain for a customer, counting each scoop out loud: one, two, three, all the way up to twelve."},{"head":"thirteen, yours, yours","text":"When he reached thirteen, in Punjabi tera, he stopped counting, because tera also means yours. Nanak kept filling scoops and saying tera, tera, yours, yours, thinking of God, who gives everything."},{"head":"nothing was missing","text":"Some people complained that he was giving away too much. But the story says that when the storehouse was checked, nothing was missing at all. Sikhs tell this story to show that everything we have is a gift."}],"game":{"items":[["Nanak's job was weighing grain.",true,"yes: at a big storehouse in Sultanpur."],["Nanak stopped at thirteen and went home.",false,"not in it: he kept filling scoops, saying tera, tera, yours, yours."],["the storehouse was checked and nothing was missing.",true,"yes: the story says nothing was missing at all."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a scoop filling with grain. breathe out slowly, like pouring it out and saying yours. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can thank someone for something they gave me","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, the traditional life-stories of Guru Nanak, about his work at the modikhana (storehouse) of Daulat Khan Lodi in Sultanpur. Tellings differ on details such as the complaint and the checking.","ask":"what is one gift in your life today that you feel thankful for?"}},"es":{"title":"tera, tera: todo es tuyo","word":"tera","means":"ਤੇਰਾ: tuyo, y también trece en panyabí","story":[{"head":"un trabajo en el almacén","text":"De joven, Nanak trabajaba en un gran almacén en Sultanpur, que pertenecía al gobernante del pueblo. Su trabajo era pesar el grano para la gente que venía a comprarlo."},{"head":"contando los puñados","text":"Los janamsakhis, las antiguas historias de la vida del Gurú Nanak, cuentan que un día pesaba grano para un cliente, contando cada medida en voz alta: uno, dos, tres, hasta llegar a doce."},{"head":"trece, tuyo, tuyo","text":"Cuando llegó a trece, que en panyabí se dice tera, dejó de contar, porque tera también significa tuyo. Nanak siguió llenando medidas y diciendo tera, tera, tuyo, tuyo, pensando en Dios, que lo da todo."},{"head":"no faltaba nada","text":"Algunas personas se quejaron de que regalaba demasiado. Pero la historia dice que cuando revisaron el almacén, no faltaba absolutamente nada. Los sijs cuentan esta historia para mostrar que todo lo que tenemos es un regalo."}],"game":{"items":[["el trabajo de Nanak era pesar grano.",true,"sí: en un gran almacén en Sultanpur."],["Nanak se detuvo en trece y se fue a casa.",false,"no está: siguió llenando medidas, diciendo tera, tera, tuyo, tuyo."],["revisaron el almacén y no faltaba nada.",true,"sí: la historia dice que no faltaba absolutamente nada."]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como una medida que se llena de grano. exhala despacio, como si la vaciaras diciendo tuyo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo dar las gracias a alguien por algo que me dio","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, las historias tradicionales de la vida del Gurú Nanak, sobre su trabajo en el modikhana (almacén) de Daulat Khan Lodi en Sultanpur. Los relatos varían en detalles como la queja y la revisión.","ask":"¿qué regalo de tu vida de hoy te hace sentir gratitud?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Nanak at the modikhana in Sultanpur","work":"Janamsakhi tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"jasmine-on-milk","game":"match","en":{"title":"a jasmine flower on the milk","word":"nimrata","means":"ਨਿਮਰਤਾ: humility, gently making room for others","story":[{"head":"a city full of holy teachers","text":"The janamsakhis, the old life-stories of Guru Nanak, tell that Guru Nanak and Mardana came to the city of Multan, where many holy teachers already lived. Word of the visitors reached them quickly."},{"head":"a bowl filled to the brim","text":"The holy teachers sent Guru Nanak a bowl of milk filled right to the very top. Its message was polite but clear: our city is already full, and there is no room for another teacher."},{"head":"a flower floats on top","text":"Guru Nanak smiled. He picked a small jasmine flower and laid it gently on the milk. The flower floated there, and not one drop spilled over the edge."},{"head":"room for something sweet","text":"The bowl was sent back, and the holy teachers understood: there is always room for someone who takes up no space and adds a sweet scent. In the story, they welcomed him. Sikhs call this gentle spirit nimrata."}],"game":{"prompt":"who or what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the bowl of milk","filled to the very top"],["the jasmine flower","floated without spilling"],["Multan","a city of holy teachers"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the scent of a jasmine flower. breathe out slowly, as gently as it floats on the milk. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can be gentle and make room for others","grownups":{"source":"From the janamsakhis, the traditional life-stories of Guru Nanak, set at Multan. Similar stories of a full bowl and a floating flower are told of other teachers too, and tellings differ on the place and details.","ask":"how can we make someone new feel welcome without taking up a lot of space?"}},"es":{"title":"una flor de jazmín sobre la leche","word":"nimrata","means":"ਨਿਮਰਤਾ: humildad, hacer lugar a otros con suavidad","story":[{"head":"una ciudad llena de maestros santos","text":"Los janamsakhis, las antiguas historias de la vida del Gurú Nanak, cuentan que el Gurú Nanak y Mardana llegaron a la ciudad de Multán, donde ya vivían muchos maestros santos. La noticia de los visitantes les llegó rápido."},{"head":"un tazón lleno hasta el borde","text":"Los maestros santos le enviaron al Gurú Nanak un tazón de leche lleno hasta el mismo borde. El mensaje era amable pero claro: nuestra ciudad ya está llena, y no hay lugar para otro maestro."},{"head":"una flor flota encima","text":"El Gurú Nanak sonrió. Tomó una pequeña flor de jazmín y la puso con suavidad sobre la leche. La flor flotó allí, y no se derramó ni una gota por el borde."},{"head":"lugar para algo dulce","text":"Devolvieron el tazón, y los maestros santos entendieron: siempre hay lugar para alguien que no ocupa espacio y añade un aroma dulce. En la historia, le dieron la bienvenida. Los sijs llaman nimrata a este espíritu suave."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién o qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el tazón de leche","lleno hasta el mismo borde"],["la flor de jazmín","flotó sin derramar nada"],["Multán","una ciudad de maestros santos"]]},"breath":"inhala despacio, como el aroma de una flor de jazmín. exhala despacio, tan suave como ella flota sobre la leche. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"puedo ser amable y hacer lugar a otros","grownups":{"source":"De los janamsakhis, las historias tradicionales de la vida del Gurú Nanak, en Multán. Historias parecidas de un tazón lleno y una flor que flota se cuentan también de otros maestros, y los relatos varían en el lugar y los detalles.","ask":"¿cómo podemos hacer que alguien nuevo se sienta bienvenido sin ocupar mucho espacio?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Guru Nanak at Multan","work":"Janamsakhi tradition","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]},"SPIRITUAL":{"format":"ih-kids/1","door":"SPIRITUAL","lessons":[{"day":1,"key":"lion-and-mouse","game":"match","en":{"title":"the lion and the mouse","word":"kindness","means":"being gentle and helpful, even to someone small","story":[{"head":"a mouse wakes a sleeping lion","text":"A great lion was asleep in the warm sun. A tiny mouse ran right across his nose. Up jumped the lion, and he caught the mouse under one enormous paw."},{"head":"the lion lets the mouse go","text":"“Please let me go,” squeaked the mouse. “One day I might help you.” The lion laughed. How could something so small help a lion? But he lifted his paw, and the mouse ran home."},{"head":"the lion is caught in a net","text":"Some days later, hunters set a net of strong ropes. The lion walked right into it and was stuck. He pulled and roared, but the ropes held tight."},{"head":"the mouse chews the ropes","text":"The little mouse heard the roar and came running. Nibble, nibble, nibble: he chewed through one rope after another until the lion was free. Small friends can turn out to be great friends."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the lion","let the mouse go"],["the mouse","chewed through the ropes"],["the hunters","set a net of ropes"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the lion stretching in the warm sun. breathe out gently, like a tiny mouse tiptoeing home. four slow breaths.","carry":"I can help someone today, even if I'm small.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the animal stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words.","ask":"When has someone small, or someone you didn't expect, helped you?"}},"es":{"title":"el león y el ratón","word":"bondad","means":"ser amable y ayudar, incluso a alguien pequeño","story":[{"head":"un ratón despierta a un león","text":"Un gran león dormía bajo el sol tibio. Un ratoncito pasó corriendo justo sobre su nariz. El león saltó y atrapó al ratón bajo una pata enorme."},{"head":"el león deja ir al ratón","text":"—Por favor, déjame ir —chilló el ratón—. Algún día podría ayudarte. El león se rio. ¿Cómo podría algo tan pequeño ayudar a un león? Pero levantó la pata, y el ratón corrió a casa."},{"head":"el león cae en una red","text":"Unos días después, unos cazadores pusieron una red de cuerdas fuertes. El león caminó justo hacia ella y quedó atrapado. Jaló y rugió, pero las cuerdas no cedían."},{"head":"el ratón roe las cuerdas","text":"El ratoncito oyó el rugido y llegó corriendo. Ñam, ñam, ñam: royó una cuerda tras otra hasta que el león quedó libre. Los amigos pequeños pueden resultar grandes amigos."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el león","dejó ir al ratón"],["el ratón","royó las cuerdas"],["los cazadores","pusieron una red de cuerdas"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el león que se estira bajo el sol tibio. suelta el aire suave, como un ratoncito que vuelve a casa de puntitas. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy ayudo a alguien, aunque mi ayuda sea pequeña.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias de animales que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Cuándo te ayudó alguien pequeño, o alguien que no esperabas?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Lion and the Mouse”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":2,"key":"hillel-one-foot","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Hillel and the man on one foot","word":"Hillel","means":"a Jewish teacher the Talmud remembers for his patience","story":[{"head":"a man with a big question","text":"Long ago, a man who wanted to become Jewish had a big question. He wanted to learn the whole Torah, every bit of it, while standing on one foot!"},{"head":"the first teacher sends him away","text":"First he asked a teacher named Shammai, who sent him away. So the man went off to find another teacher, still hoping someone would answer him."},{"head":"a patient teacher named Hillel","text":"He found Hillel, a teacher the old stories remember for his patience. Hillel did not laugh at him or send him away. He listened carefully, and he said yes."},{"head":"the whole Torah in one line","text":"The man stood on one foot. Hillel told him: what you would not like done to you, don't do to your neighbor. That is the whole Torah. The rest explains it. Now go and learn it."}],"game":{"items":[["the man wanted to learn the whole Torah on one foot.",true,"yes: he asked to learn all of it while standing on one foot."],["Hillel told the man to come back next year.",false,"not in it: Hillel answered him right away, kindly and patiently."],["Hillel said the rest explains it, so go and learn it.",true,"yes: one line first, then: the rest explains it, now go and learn it."]]},"breath":"sit tall or stand tall, and breathe in slowly. breathe out like Hillel, calm and patient. four slow breaths, steady as a person balancing on one foot.","carry":"what I wouldn't like, I won't do to someone else.","grownups":{"source":"The Babylonian Talmud, tractate Shabbat 31a, a great book of Jewish teaching and discussion. Jews tell this story about the teachers Hillel and Shammai. We retell it in our own words.","ask":"Can you think of one thing you don't like that you'll be careful not to do to someone today?"}},"es":{"title":"Hillel y el hombre en un pie","word":"Hillel","means":"un maestro judío que el Talmud recuerda por su paciencia","story":[{"head":"un hombre con una gran pregunta","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, un hombre que quería hacerse judío tenía una gran pregunta. ¡Quería aprender toda la Torá, de principio a fin, mientras se paraba en un solo pie!"},{"head":"el primer maestro lo despide","text":"Primero le preguntó a un maestro llamado Shamai, que lo despidió. Así que el hombre se fue a buscar a otro maestro, todavía con la esperanza de que alguien le respondiera."},{"head":"un maestro paciente llamado Hillel","text":"Encontró a Hillel, un maestro al que las historias antiguas recuerdan por su paciencia. Hillel no se rio de él ni lo despidió. Lo escuchó con atención y le dijo que sí."},{"head":"toda la Torá en una frase","text":"El hombre se paró en un pie. Hillel le dijo: lo que no te gustaría que te hicieran, no se lo hagas a tu prójimo. Esa es toda la Torá. Lo demás lo explica. Ahora ve y apréndelo."}],"game":{"items":[["el hombre quería aprender toda la Torá en un solo pie.",true,"sí: pidió aprenderla toda mientras se paraba en un pie."],["Hillel le dijo al hombre que volviera el año siguiente.",false,"no está: Hillel le respondió enseguida, con amabilidad y paciencia."],["Hillel dijo que lo demás lo explica, y que fuera a aprenderlo.",true,"sí: primero una frase, y luego: lo demás lo explica, ahora ve y apréndelo."]]},"breath":"siéntate o párate derecho, y respira despacio. suelta el aire como Hillel, con calma y paciencia. cuatro respiraciones lentas, firmes como alguien que se equilibra en un pie.","carry":"lo que no me gustaría, no se lo hago a nadie.","grownups":{"source":"El Talmud de Babilonia, tratado Shabat 31a, un gran libro de enseñanza y discusión judía. Los judíos cuentan esta historia sobre los maestros Hillel y Shamai. La contamos con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Puedes pensar en algo que no te gusta y que hoy vas a cuidar de no hacerle a nadie?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Shabbat 31a","work":"Babylonian Talmud","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":3,"key":"crow-and-pitcher","game":"match","en":{"title":"the crow and the pitcher","word":"patience","means":"waiting and trying again, calmly, one step at a time","story":[{"head":"a very thirsty crow","text":"It was a hot, dry day. A crow had flown a long way, and he was very thirsty. At last he spotted a tall pitcher with a little water inside."},{"head":"the water is too low","text":"He pushed his beak in, but the water was down at the very bottom. His beak could not reach it, and the pitcher was too heavy to tip over."},{"head":"one pebble at a time","text":"The crow looked around and had an idea. He picked up a pebble and dropped it in. Plop. Then another. Plop. Then another, and another, and another."},{"head":"the crow gets his drink","text":"With every pebble, the water rose a little higher. At last it reached the top, and the crow drank and drank. Slow, patient work had solved his problem."}],"game":{"prompt":"what did each one do? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the crow","dropped in pebbles"],["the pitcher","had a little water inside"],["each pebble","made the water rise"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like water rising in the pitcher. breathe out, like a pebble going plop. four slow breaths, one at a time.","carry":"when something is hard, I'll try one small step.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the animal stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words.","ask":"What is something hard you could do one little pebble at a time?"}},"es":{"title":"el cuervo y la jarra","word":"paciencia","means":"esperar y volver a intentar, con calma, paso a paso","story":[{"head":"un cuervo con mucha sed","text":"Era un día caluroso y seco. Un cuervo había volado muy lejos y tenía mucha sed. Por fin vio una jarra alta con un poco de agua adentro."},{"head":"el agua está muy abajo","text":"Metió el pico, pero el agua estaba hasta el fondo. Su pico no alcanzaba, y la jarra era demasiado pesada para voltearla."},{"head":"una piedrita a la vez","text":"El cuervo miró a su alrededor y tuvo una idea. Tomó una piedrita y la dejó caer adentro. Plop. Luego otra. Plop. Y otra, y otra, y otra."},{"head":"el cuervo por fin bebe","text":"Con cada piedrita, el agua subía un poco más. Por fin llegó arriba, y el cuervo bebió y bebió. Un trabajo lento y paciente había resuelto su problema."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué hizo cada uno? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el cuervo","echó piedritas"],["la jarra","tenía un poco de agua"],["cada piedrita","hizo subir el agua"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el agua que sube en la jarra. suelta el aire, como una piedrita que cae: plop. cuatro respiraciones lentas, una a la vez.","carry":"cuando algo es difícil, doy un pasito.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias de animales que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Qué cosa difícil podrías hacer de piedrita en piedrita?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Crow and the Pitcher”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":4,"key":"stone-soup","game":"truth","en":{"title":"stone soup","word":"sharing","means":"letting others have some of what you have","story":[{"head":"hungry travelers and an empty pot","text":"Some tired, hungry travelers came to a village. They knocked on doors, but everyone said, “Sorry, we have nothing to spare.” So the travelers set an empty pot over a fire in the square."},{"head":"a stone in the water","text":"They filled the pot with water and dropped in a smooth, clean stone. “What are you making?” asked a curious child. “Stone soup!” they said. “It's delicious. It just needs a little something more.”"},{"head":"everyone adds a little","text":"One neighbor brought a carrot. Another brought an onion. Someone found some potatoes, and someone else a pinch of salt and some herbs. Bit by bit, the pot filled up, and it smelled wonderful."},{"head":"a feast for the whole village","text":"Soon there was soup for everyone. The whole village sat down and ate together. Nobody had much alone, but together they made a feast. And the stone? It stayed at the bottom of the pot."}],"game":{"items":[["the travelers dropped a stone into the pot.",true,"yes: a smooth, clean stone went into the water first."],["a king sent the travelers a basket of bread.",false,"not in it: there was no king. the neighbors each brought a little."],["one neighbor brought a carrot.",true,"yes: then came an onion, potatoes, salt and herbs."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like smelling warm soup bubbling in the pot. breathe out, and picture the whole village sharing it. four slow breaths.","carry":"a little from everyone makes a feast.","grownups":{"source":"A folk tale told in many countries, with no single author. In some places it is nail soup or axe soup instead of stone soup. We tell a common version in our own words.","ask":"What could our family share with someone this week?"}},"es":{"title":"sopa de piedra","word":"compartir","means":"dejar que otros tengan un poco de lo que tú tienes","story":[{"head":"viajeros con hambre y una olla vacía","text":"Unos viajeros cansados y con hambre llegaron a un pueblo. Tocaron puertas, pero todos decían: «Lo siento, no tenemos nada que dar». Así que los viajeros pusieron una olla vacía sobre el fuego, en la plaza."},{"head":"una piedra en el agua","text":"Llenaron la olla de agua y echaron una piedra lisa y limpia. —¿Qué están cocinando? —preguntó un niño curioso. —¡Sopa de piedra! —dijeron—. Es deliciosa. Solo le falta un poquito más."},{"head":"cada quien pone un poco","text":"Una vecina trajo una zanahoria. Otro trajo una cebolla. Alguien encontró unas papas, y alguien más, una pizca de sal y unas hierbas. Poco a poco, la olla se llenó, y olía de maravilla."},{"head":"un banquete para todo el pueblo","text":"Pronto hubo sopa para todos. Todo el pueblo se sentó a comer, todos juntos. Nadie tenía mucho solo, pero juntos hicieron un banquete. ¿Y la piedra? Se quedó en el fondo de la olla."}],"game":{"items":[["los viajeros echaron una piedra en la olla.",true,"sí: primero entró al agua una piedra lisa y limpia."],["un rey les mandó a los viajeros una canasta de pan.",false,"no está: no había ningún rey. cada vecino trajo un poquito."],["una vecina trajo una zanahoria.",true,"sí: luego llegaron una cebolla, papas, sal y hierbas."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si olieras una sopa tibia que hierve en la olla. suelta el aire, e imagina a todo el pueblo compartiéndola. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"un poquito de cada quien hace un banquete.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento popular que se cuenta en muchos países, sin un solo autor. En algunos lugares es sopa de clavo o sopa de hacha en vez de sopa de piedra. Contamos una versión común con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Qué podríamos compartir en familia con alguien esta semana?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"traditional folk tale, many versions","work":"Stone Soup (European folk tale; also nail soup and axe soup)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":5,"key":"north-wind-and-sun","game":"match","en":{"title":"the north wind and the sun","word":"gentleness","means":"being soft and kind instead of rough or pushy","story":[{"head":"who is stronger?","text":"The North Wind and the Sun were arguing. “I am stronger,” said the Wind. “No, I am,” said the Sun. Just then, they saw a traveler walking down the road in a warm cloak."},{"head":"a contest with a cloak","text":"“Let's have a contest,” said the Sun. “Whoever can get that traveler to take off his cloak is the stronger one.” The Wind agreed, and he went first."},{"head":"the wind blows and blows","text":"The Wind blew a cold, howling blast. But the harder he blew, the tighter the traveler pulled his cloak around him. At last the Wind was out of breath, and he gave up."},{"head":"the sun shines warmly","text":"Then the Sun came out and shone, soft and warm. The traveler smiled, loosened his cloak, and soon took it off and sat in the sunshine. Gentle warmth had done what pushing could not."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the North Wind","blew a cold blast"],["the Sun","shone soft and warm"],["the traveler","took off his cloak"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like warm sunshine on your face. breathe out softly, like a gentle breeze, not a howling wind. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll try a gentle word instead of a push.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words.","ask":"When does a gentle way work better than a pushy way in our family?"}},"es":{"title":"el viento del norte y el sol","word":"dulzura","means":"ser suave y amable en vez de brusco o insistente","story":[{"head":"¿quién es más fuerte?","text":"El Viento del Norte y el Sol discutían. —Yo soy más fuerte —dijo el Viento. —No, yo lo soy —dijo el Sol. En ese momento vieron a un viajero que iba por el camino con una capa abrigadora."},{"head":"un concurso con una capa","text":"—Hagamos un concurso —dijo el Sol—. Quien logre que ese viajero se quite la capa será el más fuerte. El Viento aceptó y empezó primero."},{"head":"el viento sopla y sopla","text":"El Viento sopló una ráfaga fría y ruidosa. Pero cuanto más fuerte soplaba, más se envolvía el viajero en su capa. Al final, el Viento se quedó sin aliento y se rindió."},{"head":"el sol brilla con calidez","text":"Entonces salió el Sol y brilló, suave y tibio. El viajero sonrió, se aflojó la capa, y pronto se la quitó y se sentó al sol. La calidez suave logró lo que empujar no pudo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el Viento del Norte","sopló una ráfaga fría"],["el Sol","brilló suave y tibio"],["el viajero","se quitó la capa"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como el sol tibio en tu cara. suelta el aire con suavidad, como una brisa tranquila, no un viento ruidoso. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy pruebo una palabra suave en vez de empujar.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Cuándo funciona mejor en nuestra familia una manera suave que una manera brusca?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The North Wind and the Sun”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":6,"key":"elephant-and-the-blind-men","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the blind men and the elephant","word":"humble","means":"knowing you may see only part of the whole picture","story":[{"head":"a king brings an elephant","text":"In a story the Buddha told, a king gathered some men who had been blind all their lives. He brought in an elephant and said, “Touch it, and tell me what an elephant is like.”"},{"head":"each man touches one part","text":"One man felt the head and said, “An elephant is like a big pot.” One felt the ear: “Like a basket!” One felt the leg: “Like a post!” One felt the tip of the tail: “Like a broom!”"},{"head":"everyone argues","text":"“A pot!” “No, a basket!” “A post!” “A broom!” Each man was sure he was right, and they began to argue loudly. Each had touched something real, but only one part."},{"head":"the poet Rumi tells it in the dark","text":"The Buddha told this story about people who argue when each sees only a part. Much later, the poet Rumi told his own story: people felt an elephant in a dark room. With a candle, Rumi said, they would have seen it whole."}],"game":{"items":[["one man felt the elephant's ear and said it was like a basket.",true,"yes: to him, the ear felt like a basket."],["the men all agreed right away about the elephant.",false,"not in it: each was sure he was right, and they argued."],["in Rumi's story, people felt the elephant in a dark room.",true,"yes: Rumi said a candle would have let them see it whole."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, as if you're lighting a small candle in a dark room. breathe out, and let the whole room glow. four slow breaths.","carry":"I only see part of it. I'll ask what you see.","grownups":{"source":"The Buddha's story is in the Udana (6.4), an early Buddhist collection. The Persian poet Rumi tells a different story, the elephant in the dark, in his Masnavi, Book III. They are two separate stories, each retold in our own words.","ask":"Can you remember a time we each saw only part of something, and talking helped us see more?"}},"es":{"title":"los ciegos y el elefante","word":"humilde","means":"saber que quizás ves solo una parte de todo","story":[{"head":"un rey trae un elefante","text":"En una historia que contó el Buda, un rey reunió a unos hombres que habían sido ciegos toda su vida. Trajo un elefante y dijo: «Tóquenlo y díganme cómo es un elefante»."},{"head":"cada hombre toca una parte","text":"Uno tocó la cabeza y dijo: «Un elefante es como una olla grande». Otro tocó la oreja: «¡Como una canasta!». Otro tocó la pata: «¡Como un poste!». Otro tocó la punta de la cola: «¡Como una escoba!»."},{"head":"todos discuten","text":"«¡Una olla!». «¡No, una canasta!». «¡Un poste!». «¡Una escoba!». Cada uno estaba seguro de tener razón, y empezaron a discutir en voz alta. Cada uno había tocado algo real, pero solo una parte."},{"head":"el poeta Rumi lo cuenta a oscuras","text":"El Buda contó esta historia sobre la gente que discute cuando cada quien ve solo una parte. Mucho después, el poeta Rumi contó su propia historia: unas personas tocaban un elefante en un cuarto oscuro. Con una vela, dijo Rumi, lo habrían visto entero."}],"game":{"items":[["un hombre tocó la oreja del elefante y dijo que era como una canasta.",true,"sí: para él, la oreja se sentía como una canasta."],["todos los hombres se pusieron de acuerdo enseguida.",false,"no está: cada uno estaba seguro de tener razón, y discutieron."],["en la historia de Rumi, unas personas tocaban el elefante en un cuarto oscuro.",true,"sí: Rumi dijo que con una vela lo habrían visto entero."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si encendieras una velita en un cuarto oscuro. suelta el aire, y deja que todo el cuarto se ilumine. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"yo veo solo una parte. te pregunto qué ves tú.","grownups":{"source":"La historia del Buda está en el Udana (6.4), una antigua colección budista. El poeta persa Rumi cuenta otra historia, el elefante en la oscuridad, en su Masnavi, libro III. Son dos historias distintas, contadas con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Te acuerdas de una vez en que cada uno veía solo una parte de algo, y hablar nos ayudó a ver más?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Udana 6.4 (Tittha Sutta)","work":"Udana (Khuddaka Nikaya, Pali Canon)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Masnavi, Book III (the elephant in the dark house)","work":"Masnavi of Jalal al-Din Rumi","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":7,"key":"tortoise-and-hare","game":"match","en":{"title":"the tortoise and the hare","word":"steady","means":"going on and on at your own pace, without stopping","story":[{"head":"the hare laughs at slow feet","text":"A hare was very proud of his fast legs. He laughed at the tortoise. “You're so slow! I could beat you in a race without even trying.” The tortoise said calmly, “Let's race, then.”"},{"head":"the race begins","text":"All the animals came to watch. Off went the hare like the wind, zip, out of sight. The tortoise set off too: step, step, step, slow and steady."},{"head":"the hare takes a nap","text":"Far ahead, the hare looked back. The tortoise was a tiny dot. “Plenty of time,” he yawned, and he lay down under a shady tree for a nap."},{"head":"the tortoise crosses the line","text":"The tortoise never stopped. Step, step, step, he walked right past the sleeping hare. When the hare woke up and raced to the finish, the tortoise was already there, smiling."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the hare","took a nap under a tree"],["the tortoise","never stopped walking"],["the animals","came to watch the race"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, step by step, like the tortoise. breathe out slowly, no hurry at all. four slow, steady breaths.","carry":"slow and steady: I'll keep going and finish what I start.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the animal stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words.","ask":"What's something you're working on slowly and steadily right now?"}},"es":{"title":"la tortuga y la liebre","word":"constante","means":"seguir y seguir a tu propio paso, sin parar","story":[{"head":"la liebre se ríe de las patas lentas","text":"Una liebre estaba muy orgullosa de sus patas rápidas. Se reía de la tortuga: «¡Eres tan lenta! Te ganaría una carrera sin siquiera intentarlo». La tortuga dijo con calma: «Entonces, hagamos una carrera»."},{"head":"empieza la carrera","text":"Todos los animales vinieron a mirar. La liebre salió como el viento, ¡zum!, y desapareció. La tortuga también salió: paso, paso, paso, lenta pero constante."},{"head":"la liebre toma una siesta","text":"Muy adelante, la liebre miró hacia atrás. La tortuga era un puntito. «Tengo mucho tiempo», bostezó, y se acostó a dormir una siesta bajo un árbol con sombra."},{"head":"la tortuga cruza la meta","text":"La tortuga nunca se detuvo. Paso, paso, paso, pasó justo al lado de la liebre dormida. Cuando la liebre despertó y corrió a la meta, la tortuga ya estaba allí, sonriendo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la liebre","durmió bajo un árbol"],["la tortuga","nunca dejó de caminar"],["los animales","vinieron a mirar la carrera"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, paso a paso, como la tortuga. suelta el aire despacio, sin ninguna prisa. cuatro respiraciones lentas y constantes.","carry":"lento pero constante: sigo hasta terminar lo que empiezo.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias de animales que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿En qué estás trabajando ahora, lento pero constante?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Hare and the Tortoise”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":8,"key":"nasruddin-coat","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Nasruddin and the coat at the feast","word":"respect","means":"treating every person as important, whatever they wear","story":[{"head":"an invitation for Nasruddin","text":"Nasruddin was invited to a big feast. He came straight from working in his garden, in his old, dusty clothes. Nobody greeted him. Nobody offered him a seat or a bowl of soup."},{"head":"a fine coat from home","text":"So Nasruddin went home and put on his finest coat, with shiny buttons and long, soft sleeves. Then he walked back to the feast."},{"head":"the best seat and a bowl of soup","text":"This time everyone hurried to greet him. “Welcome! Sit here, in the best seat!” They set a bowl of hot soup in front of him."},{"head":"soup for Nasruddin's coat","text":"Nasruddin dipped his sleeve into the soup. “Eat, coat, eat!” he said. Everyone stared. “Well,” he said with a smile, “it seems it was my coat you invited, not me.” The hosts laughed, a little red-faced, and understood."}],"game":{"items":[["Nasruddin came to the feast first in his old, dusty clothes.",true,"yes: straight from his garden, and nobody greeted him."],["Nasruddin brought a big cake to the feast.",false,"not in it: he brought only himself, and then his fine coat."],["Nasruddin dipped his sleeve into the soup.",true,"yes: “eat, coat, eat!” he said, with a smile."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like smelling a warm bowl of soup. breathe out, and picture a feast where everyone is welcome. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll say hello to someone, whatever they're wearing.","grownups":{"source":"A tale of Nasruddin (Nasreddin Hodja), a wise and funny character in stories told in Turkey, Iran, Central Asia and beyond. The tales have no single author and tellings differ; we retell a common one in our own words.","ask":"Have you ever judged someone by their clothes, or felt judged by yours?"}},"es":{"title":"Nasrudín y el abrigo en el banquete","word":"respeto","means":"tratar a cada persona como importante, se vista como se vista","story":[{"head":"una invitación para Nasrudín","text":"Invitaron a Nasrudín a un gran banquete. Llegó directo de trabajar en su huerto, con su ropa vieja y llena de polvo. Nadie lo saludó. Nadie le ofreció un asiento ni un plato de sopa."},{"head":"un abrigo elegante de casa","text":"Así que Nasrudín fue a su casa y se puso su abrigo más elegante, con botones brillantes y mangas largas y suaves. Luego volvió caminando al banquete."},{"head":"el mejor asiento y un plato de sopa","text":"Esta vez todos corrieron a saludarlo. «¡Bienvenido! ¡Siéntate aquí, en el mejor asiento!». Le pusieron enfrente un plato de sopa caliente."},{"head":"sopa para el abrigo de Nasrudín","text":"Nasrudín metió la manga en la sopa. «¡Come, abrigo, come!», dijo. Todos se quedaron mirando. «Bueno», dijo sonriendo, «parece que invitaron a mi abrigo, no a mí». Los anfitriones se rieron, un poco colorados, y entendieron."}],"game":{"items":[["Nasrudín llegó primero al banquete con su ropa vieja y llena de polvo.",true,"sí: directo de su huerto, y nadie lo saludó."],["Nasrudín llevó un pastel grande al banquete.",false,"no está: solo se llevó a sí mismo, y luego su abrigo elegante."],["Nasrudín metió la manga en la sopa.",true,"sí: «¡come, abrigo, come!», dijo sonriendo."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si olieras un plato de sopa tibia. suelta el aire, e imagina un banquete donde todos son bienvenidos. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"saludo a alguien hoy, se vista como se vista.","grownups":{"source":"Un cuento de Nasrudín (Nasreddin Hodja), un personaje sabio y gracioso de historias que se cuentan en Turquía, Irán, Asia Central y más allá. Los cuentos no tienen un solo autor y cambian según quien los cuenta; contamos uno común con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Alguna vez juzgaste a alguien por su ropa, o sentiste que te juzgaban por la tuya?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"the coat at the feast (traditional)","work":"Tales of Nasreddin Hodja / Mulla Nasruddin (folk tradition)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":9,"key":"dove-and-ant","game":"match","en":{"title":"the dove and the ant","word":"gratitude","means":"the warm feeling of thanks when someone helps you","story":[{"head":"an ant falls into the stream","text":"An ant went down to a stream for a drink. Whoosh! The rushing water swept her away. She paddled her tiny legs, but she couldn't reach the bank."},{"head":"the dove drops a leaf","text":"A dove sitting in a tree above saw her. Quickly, the dove picked a leaf and let it fall onto the water, right next to the ant."},{"head":"the ant floats to safety","text":"The ant climbed onto the leaf like a little boat. It carried her gently to the bank, and she stepped out onto dry land, safe and thankful."},{"head":"the ant nips the bird catcher","text":"Soon after, a bird catcher crept under the tree to catch the dove. The ant saw him and nipped his foot. “Ow!” he cried, and the noise made the dove fly safely away."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the ant","nipped the bird catcher's foot"],["the dove","dropped a leaf on the water"],["the leaf","carried the ant like a boat"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a leaf floating on the water. breathe out, like a dove flying safely into the sky. four slow breaths.","carry":"someone helped me once. today I'll help back.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the animal stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, where it is called “The Ant and the Dove,” retold in our own words.","ask":"Who has helped you lately, and how could you help them back?"}},"es":{"title":"la paloma y la hormiga","word":"gratitud","means":"el sentimiento cálido de dar gracias cuando alguien te ayuda","story":[{"head":"una hormiga cae al arroyo","text":"Una hormiga bajó a un arroyo a beber agua. ¡Fiuuu! El agua que corría se la llevó. Movía sus patitas, pero no podía llegar a la orilla."},{"head":"la paloma deja caer una hoja","text":"Una paloma que estaba en un árbol la vio. Rápido, la paloma arrancó una hoja y la dejó caer sobre el agua, justo al lado de la hormiga."},{"head":"la hormiga flota a salvo","text":"La hormiga se subió a la hoja como a un barquito. La hoja la llevó suavemente a la orilla, y ella bajó a tierra seca, a salvo y agradecida."},{"head":"la hormiga pica al cazador","text":"Poco después, un cazador de pájaros se acercó en silencio bajo el árbol para atrapar a la paloma. La hormiga lo vio y le picó el pie. «¡Ay!», gritó, y el ruido hizo que la paloma volara lejos, a salvo."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la hormiga","le picó el pie al cazador"],["la paloma","dejó caer una hoja al agua"],["la hoja","llevó a la hormiga como barquito"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como una hoja que flota en el agua. suelta el aire, como una paloma que vuela a salvo por el cielo. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"alguien me ayudó. hoy yo ayudo de vuelta.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias de animales que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), donde se llama «La hormiga y la paloma», contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Quién te ayudó hace poco, y cómo podrías ayudarle tú?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Ant and the Dove”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":10,"key":"jesus-golden-rule","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Jesus on the mountainside: the golden rule","word":"golden rule","means":"treat others the way you'd like to be treated","story":[{"head":"a crowd on a mountainside","text":"In the Gospel of Matthew, Jesus goes up onto a mountainside, and people gather to listen. He sits down and begins to teach them, one picture after another."},{"head":"bread, not a stone","text":"He asks them: if a child asks a parent for bread, will the parent hand over a stone? If the child asks for a fish, will the parent give a snake? Of course not!"},{"head":"good gifts from parents","text":"Jesus says that parents know how to give good things to their children. And he says God gives good things, even more, to those who ask. Christians believe this about God."},{"head":"the golden rule","text":"Then he says, in the King James Bible's words: “all things whatsoever ye would that men should do to you, do ye even so to them.” Treat others the way you'd like them to treat you. People later called it the golden rule."}],"game":{"items":[["Jesus talked about a child asking a parent for bread.",true,"yes: and no loving parent would hand the child a stone."],["Jesus taught the crowd from inside a boat.",false,"not in it: in this story he sits down on a mountainside to teach."],["Jesus said to treat others the way you'd like to be treated.",true,"yes: “do ye even so to them,” in the King James words."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like sitting on a sunny mountainside. breathe out, and think of someone you'd like to be kind to. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll treat someone the way I'd like to be treated.","grownups":{"source":"The Gospel of Matthew, chapters 5 to 7, the Sermon on the Mount in the Christian Bible. The quotation is Matthew 7:12 in the King James Version. Christians read these words as the teaching of Jesus.","ask":"What is one way you like to be treated, and who could you treat that way today?"}},"es":{"title":"Jesús en el monte: la regla de oro","word":"regla de oro","means":"tratar a los demás como te gustaría que te trataran","story":[{"head":"una multitud en el monte","text":"En el Evangelio de Mateo, Jesús sube a un monte, y la gente se reúne para escucharlo. Se sienta y empieza a enseñarles, una imagen tras otra."},{"head":"pan, no una piedra","text":"Les pregunta: si un niño le pide pan a su mamá o a su papá, ¿le darán una piedra? Si le pide un pescado, ¿le darán una serpiente? ¡Claro que no!"},{"head":"buenos regalos de los padres","text":"Jesús dice que los padres saben dar cosas buenas a sus hijos. Y dice que Dios da cosas buenas, aún más, a quienes le piden. Los cristianos creen esto sobre Dios."},{"head":"la regla de oro","text":"Luego dice algo así: todo lo que quieras que los demás hagan contigo, hazlo tú también con ellos. Trata a los demás como te gustaría que te trataran. Más tarde, la gente lo llamó la regla de oro."}],"game":{"items":[["Jesús habló de un niño que le pide pan a su mamá o a su papá.",true,"sí: y ningún padre que ama a su hijo le daría una piedra."],["Jesús le enseñó a la gente desde una barca.",false,"no está: en esta historia se sienta en un monte para enseñar."],["Jesús dijo que tratáramos a los demás como nos gustaría que nos trataran.",true,"sí: hazlo tú también con ellos, dice Jesús en Mateo."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si estuvieras sentado en un monte con sol. suelta el aire, y piensa en alguien con quien te gustaría ser amable. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"trato a alguien como me gustaría que me trataran.","grownups":{"source":"El Evangelio de Mateo, capítulos 5 a 7, el Sermón del Monte en la Biblia cristiana. La regla de oro está en Mateo 7:12; aquí la contamos con nuestras palabras. Los cristianos leen estas palabras como la enseñanza de Jesús.","ask":"¿De qué manera te gusta que te traten, y a quién podrías tratar así hoy?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Matthew 7:12","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":"KJV (1769)","quoted":"all things whatsoever ye would that men should do to you, do ye even so to them"},{"ref":"Matthew 5:1; 7:9-11","work":"The Bible (Gospel of Matthew)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":11,"key":"bundle-of-sticks","game":"match","en":{"title":"the bundle of sticks","word":"together","means":"side by side, helping each other, as one team","story":[{"head":"brothers who quarrel","text":"A father had several sons who were always quarreling. Whatever he said, they kept on arguing. So one day he asked them to bring him a bundle of sticks."},{"head":"nobody can break the bundle","text":"He tied the sticks tightly together and handed the bundle to each son. “Break it,” he said. Each one tried, pulling and pushing with all his strength, but the bundle would not break."},{"head":"one stick snaps easily","text":"Then the father untied the bundle and gave each son just one stick. “Now try.” Snap! Snap! Snap! Every single stick broke easily."},{"head":"stronger together","text":"“You see,” said the father, “if you stick together and help each other, you are strong like the bundle. If you quarrel and pull apart, you are like one stick alone.”"}],"game":{"prompt":"what happened? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the bundle","would not break"],["one stick","snapped easily"],["the father","tied the sticks together"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, and picture all the sticks tied snug together. breathe out, and feel how steady you are. four slow breaths.","carry":"we're stronger when we help each other.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words.","ask":"When does our family feel strongest together?"}},"es":{"title":"el manojo de varitas","word":"unidos","means":"uno al lado del otro, ayudándose, como un solo equipo","story":[{"head":"hermanos que pelean","text":"Un padre tenía varios hijos que siempre estaban peleando. Dijera lo que dijera, ellos seguían discutiendo. Así que un día les pidió que le trajeran un manojo de varitas."},{"head":"nadie puede romper el manojo","text":"Ató bien las varitas juntas y le dio el manojo a cada hijo. —Rómpanlo —dijo. Cada uno lo intentó, jalando y empujando con todas sus fuerzas, pero el manojo no se rompía."},{"head":"una varita se rompe fácil","text":"Luego el padre desató el manojo y le dio a cada hijo una sola varita. —Ahora inténtenlo. ¡Crac! ¡Crac! ¡Crac! Cada varita se rompió fácilmente."},{"head":"más fuertes juntos","text":"—¿Ven? —dijo el padre—. Si se mantienen unidos y se ayudan, son fuertes como el manojo. Si pelean y se separan, son como una varita sola."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué pasó? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el manojo","no se rompió"],["una varita","se rompió fácil"],["el padre","ató las varitas juntas"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, e imagina todas las varitas bien atadas juntas. suelta el aire, y siente lo firme que estás. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"somos más fuertes cuando nos ayudamos.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Cuándo se siente nuestra familia más fuerte, todos juntos?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Father and His Sons”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":12,"key":"butterfly-dream","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Zhuangzi's butterfly dream","word":"wonder","means":"the happy, curious feeling of asking big questions","story":[{"head":"the thinker Zhuangzi falls asleep","text":"Long ago in China lived a thinker named Zhuangzi, who loved big, curious questions. One day he lay down and fell fast asleep."},{"head":"a happy butterfly","text":"He dreamed he was a butterfly, fluttering here and there, light and happy. In the dream he didn't remember being Zhuangzi at all. He was just a butterfly, enjoying the air."},{"head":"waking up as Zhuangzi again","text":"Then he woke up. There he was, plain Zhuangzi again, lying down, with arms and legs instead of wings, and a little surprised."},{"head":"a question to wonder about","text":"And he wondered: was I a man dreaming I was a butterfly? Or am I a butterfly, now dreaming I'm a man? Zhuangzi left the question open, for us to wonder about too."}],"game":{"items":[["Zhuangzi dreamed he was a butterfly.",true,"yes: fluttering here and there, light and happy."],["the butterfly flew to a palace garden.",false,"not in it: the dream just shows a happy butterfly fluttering about."],["when he woke, Zhuangzi wondered who was dreaming whom.",true,"yes: was he a man dreaming of a butterfly, or the other way around?"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, light as a butterfly. breathe out softly, and let your wings rest. four slow, fluttering breaths.","carry":"I can enjoy a big question without rushing to answer.","grownups":{"source":"From the Zhuangzi, chapter 2, a book of Chinese stories and sayings named after the thinker Zhuangzi (also spelled Chuang Tzu). We retell it in our own words; translations word the ending differently.","ask":"If you dreamed you were any animal, which would you be, and what would you wonder about?"}},"es":{"title":"el sueño de la mariposa de Zhuangzi","word":"asombro","means":"la sensación feliz y curiosa de hacerse grandes preguntas","story":[{"head":"el pensador Zhuangzi se duerme","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en China, vivió un pensador llamado Zhuangzi, al que le encantaban las preguntas grandes y curiosas. Un día se recostó y se quedó profundamente dormido."},{"head":"una mariposa feliz","text":"Soñó que era una mariposa que revoloteaba aquí y allá, ligera y feliz. En el sueño no recordaba para nada que era Zhuangzi. Era solo una mariposa, disfrutando el aire."},{"head":"despierta otra vez como Zhuangzi","text":"Entonces despertó. Ahí estaba, Zhuangzi de nuevo, acostado, con brazos y piernas en vez de alas, y un poco sorprendido."},{"head":"una pregunta para pensar","text":"Y se preguntó: ¿era yo un hombre que soñaba que era mariposa? ¿O soy una mariposa que ahora sueña que es un hombre? Zhuangzi dejó la pregunta abierta, para que nosotros también la pensemos."}],"game":{"items":[["Zhuangzi soñó que era una mariposa.",true,"sí: revoloteaba aquí y allá, ligera y feliz."],["la mariposa voló al jardín de un palacio.",false,"no está: el sueño solo muestra una mariposa feliz que revolotea."],["al despertar, Zhuangzi se preguntó quién soñaba a quién.",true,"sí: ¿era un hombre que soñaba con una mariposa, o al revés?"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, ligero como una mariposa. suelta el aire con suavidad, y deja descansar tus alas. cuatro respiraciones lentas, como un aleteo.","carry":"puedo disfrutar una gran pregunta sin apurarme a responder.","grownups":{"source":"Del Zhuangzi, capítulo 2, un libro chino de historias y dichos que lleva el nombre del pensador Zhuangzi (también escrito Chuang Tzu). Lo contamos con nuestras palabras; las traducciones dicen el final de distintas maneras.","ask":"Si soñaras que eres un animal, ¿cuál serías, y qué te preguntarías?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Zhuangzi, chapter 2 (Qiwulun, 'The Adjustment of Controversies' in Legge)","work":"Zhuangzi","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":13,"key":"town-and-country-mouse","game":"match","en":{"title":"the town mouse and the country mouse","word":"contentment","means":"feeling happy with what you have","story":[{"head":"a simple supper in the country","text":"A country mouse invited his cousin from the town to visit. He served what he had: plain food from the fields, some seeds and a little grain, in his quiet nest under a bush."},{"head":"the town mouse turns up his nose","text":"The town mouse nibbled politely, then said, “Cousin, how can you eat such plain food? Come to the town with me. I'll show you how to really live!”"},{"head":"a feast, and a big barking dog","text":"In the town house they found a table full of cakes, jelly and cheese. But just as they began to eat, the door burst open and a big dog came barking in. The mice ran to hide!"},{"head":"home to the quiet nest","text":"When it was quiet again, the country mouse said, “Thank you, cousin, but I'm going home. I'd rather have plain food in peace than a feast with a fright.”"}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the country mouse","served plain food"],["the town mouse","invited his cousin to town"],["the big dog","came barking in"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a little mouse curled up in a quiet nest. breathe out, safe and cozy. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll notice one simple thing I'm glad to have.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the animal stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words.","ask":"What simple thing at home makes you feel cozy and glad?"}},"es":{"title":"el ratón de ciudad y el ratón de campo","word":"contento","means":"sentirte feliz con lo que tienes","story":[{"head":"una cena sencilla en el campo","text":"Un ratón de campo invitó a su primo de la ciudad a visitarlo. Le sirvió lo que tenía: comida sencilla del campo, unas semillas y algo de grano, en su nido tranquilo bajo un arbusto."},{"head":"el ratón de ciudad hace una mueca","text":"El ratón de ciudad mordisqueó con cortesía y luego dijo: «Primo, ¿cómo puedes comer algo tan simple? Ven conmigo a la ciudad. ¡Te enseñaré lo que es vivir de verdad!»."},{"head":"un banquete, y un perro que ladra","text":"En la casa de la ciudad encontraron una mesa llena de pasteles, mermelada y queso. Pero justo cuando empezaron a comer, la puerta se abrió de golpe y entró un perro grande ladrando. ¡Los ratones corrieron a esconderse!"},{"head":"de vuelta al nido tranquilo","text":"Cuando todo volvió a estar en calma, el ratón de campo dijo: «Gracias, primo, pero me voy a casa. Prefiero comida sencilla en paz que un banquete con sustos»."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el ratón de campo","sirvió comida sencilla"],["el ratón de ciudad","invitó a su primo a la ciudad"],["el perro grande","entró ladrando"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un ratoncito acurrucado en un nido tranquilo. suelta el aire, a salvo y calientito. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"me fijo en algo sencillo que me alegra tener.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias de animales que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Qué cosa sencilla de la casa te hace sentir a gusto y contento?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Town Mouse and the Country Mouse”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":14,"key":"confucius-one-word","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Confucius and one word for a whole life","word":"reciprocity","means":"treating others the way you'd want them to treat you","story":[{"head":"a student with a big question","text":"Long ago in China, a student named Zigong came to his teacher, Confucius, with a big question. He asked if there was one word a person could live by, all life long."},{"head":"one word from Confucius","text":"Confucius taught about family, friendship and learning. But for this question he chose just one word. In James Legge's old English translation, the word is “reciprocity.”"},{"head":"what the word means","text":"Reciprocity means treating people the way you would want them to treat you, like a ball you toss gently, so it comes back to you gently."},{"head":"how Confucius explains it","text":"Then Confucius explained it, in Legge's words: “What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others.” His sayings were gathered into a book called the Analects, and people still read it today."}],"game":{"items":[["Zigong asked if there was one word to live by.",true,"yes: one word for a whole life, he asked."],["Confucius answered with a long list of rules.",false,"not in it: he chose just one word, reciprocity."],["Confucius said not to do to others what you don't want done to yourself.",true,"yes: that is how Legge's translation gives his answer."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like catching a ball gently. breathe out slowly, like tossing it back just as gently. four slow breaths.","carry":"today I'll give the kindness I'd like to get.","grownups":{"source":"The Analects, a collection of sayings of Confucius, book 15. The quotation is from James Legge's 1861 translation, where it is numbered 15:23; some newer editions number it 15:24.","ask":"If you could choose one word to live by, what would it be, and why?"}},"es":{"title":"Confucio y una palabra para toda la vida","word":"reciprocidad","means":"tratar a otros como quieres que te traten a ti","story":[{"head":"un alumno con una gran pregunta","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, en China, un alumno llamado Zigong fue a ver a su maestro, Confucio, con una gran pregunta. Le preguntó si había una sola palabra para vivir según ella toda la vida."},{"head":"una palabra de Confucio","text":"Confucio enseñaba sobre la familia, la amistad y el aprendizaje. Pero para esta pregunta eligió una sola palabra. En la vieja traducción inglesa de James Legge, la palabra es «reciprocity»: reciprocidad."},{"head":"lo que significa la palabra","text":"Reciprocidad quiere decir tratar a las personas como te gustaría que te trataran a ti, como una pelota que lanzas con suavidad para que vuelva a ti con suavidad."},{"head":"cómo lo explica Confucio","text":"Luego Confucio lo explicó: lo que no quieres que te hagan a ti, no se lo hagas a los demás. Sus dichos se reunieron en un libro llamado las Analectas, y la gente todavía lo lee hoy."}],"game":{"items":[["Zigong preguntó si había una sola palabra para vivir.",true,"sí: una palabra para toda la vida, preguntó."],["Confucio respondió con una lista larga de reglas.",false,"no está: eligió una sola palabra, reciprocidad."],["Confucio dijo que no hagas a otros lo que no quieres que te hagan a ti.",true,"sí: así da su respuesta la traducción de Legge."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si atraparas una pelota con suavidad. suelta el aire despacio, como si la lanzaras de vuelta igual de suave. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"hoy doy la amabilidad que me gustaría recibir.","grownups":{"source":"Las Analectas, una colección de dichos de Confucio, libro 15. La traducción inglesa de James Legge (1861) lo numera 15:23; algunas ediciones más nuevas lo numeran 15:24. Aquí lo contamos con nuestras palabras.","ask":"Si pudieras elegir una palabra para vivir, ¿cuál sería, y por qué?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Analects XV.23 (Legge's numbering; 15:24 in some editions)","work":"The Analects of Confucius","translation":"Legge 1861","quoted":"Is not RECIPROCITY such a word? What you do not want done to yourself, do not do to others."}]},{"day":15,"key":"androcles-and-lion","game":"match","en":{"title":"Androcles and the lion","word":"loyal","means":"sticking by a friend and remembering their kindness","story":[{"head":"a lion with a sore paw","text":"Androcles was a man who was not free. One day he ran away and hid in a forest. There he met a lion, moaning softly and holding up a swollen paw."},{"head":"the thorn comes out","text":"Androcles was nervous, but he came closer. A big thorn was stuck in the paw! Carefully, gently, he pulled it out. The lion sighed and laid his head on Androcles' knee."},{"head":"the lion shares his cave","text":"After that, the lion and Androcles were friends. The lion led him to his cave and shared his food with him, and Androcles stayed there safely for a while."},{"head":"the lion remembers his friend","text":"Later, both were caught and brought to a great arena. A lion came bounding toward Androcles, and the crowd gasped. But it was his old friend! The lion licked his hand like a happy dog, and both were set free."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["Androcles","pulled out the thorn"],["the lion","licked his friend's hand"],["the crowd","gasped in the arena"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a lion resting its head after the thorn is gone. breathe out, calm and easy. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll remember someone who was kind to me and thank them.","grownups":{"source":"A fable told under Aesop's name; George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation includes it. The Roman writer Aulus Gellius also tells a version. We retell the gentlest telling in our own words.","ask":"Who has been a loyal friend to you, and how did you know?"}},"es":{"title":"Androcles y el león","word":"leal","means":"quedarte con un amigo y recordar lo bueno que hizo por ti","story":[{"head":"un león con una pata herida","text":"Androcles era un hombre que no era libre. Un día huyó y se escondió en un bosque. Allí encontró a un león que gemía bajito y levantaba una pata hinchada."},{"head":"sale la espina","text":"Androcles estaba nervioso, pero se acercó. ¡Tenía una espina grande clavada en la pata! Con cuidado, muy suave, se la sacó. El león suspiró y apoyó la cabeza en la rodilla de Androcles."},{"head":"el león comparte su cueva","text":"Desde entonces, el león y Androcles fueron amigos. El león lo llevó a su cueva y compartió su comida con él, y Androcles se quedó allí a salvo por un tiempo."},{"head":"el león recuerda a su amigo","text":"Tiempo después, a los dos los atraparon y los llevaron a una gran arena. Un león corrió hacia Androcles, y la gente se asustó. ¡Pero era su viejo amigo! El león le lamió la mano como un perro feliz, y a los dos los dejaron libres."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["Androcles","sacó la espina"],["el león","le lamió la mano a su amigo"],["la gente","se asustó en la arena"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un león que descansa la cabeza cuando ya no tiene la espina. suelta el aire, tranquilo y en calma. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"recuerdo a alguien que fue amable conmigo y le doy las gracias.","grownups":{"source":"Una fábula que se cuenta con el nombre de Esopo; la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867) la incluye. El escritor romano Aulo Gelio también cuenta una versión. La contamos de la forma más suave, con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Quién ha sido un amigo leal contigo, y cómo te diste cuenta?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“Androcles”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Attic Nights 5.14","work":"Aulus Gellius, Attic Nights","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":16,"key":"tao-water","game":"truth","en":{"title":"water, the soft strong one","word":"Tao","means":"the Way: in Taoism, the natural flow of all things","story":[{"head":"an old book called the Tao Te Ching","text":"There is an old Chinese book called the Tao Te Ching. Tradition says a wise teacher named Laozi wrote it before he rode away on an ox, far off to the west."},{"head":"water helps everything grow","text":"The book says the very best way to be is like water. Water helps every living thing: grass, trees, birds, people. It gives and gives, and it doesn't fight or boast."},{"head":"water goes to the low places","text":"Water flows down to the low places that nobody wants. It doesn't push to be first or highest. In the book, that is part of what makes water so good."},{"head":"soft water shapes hard stone","text":"The book says something more: nothing is softer or weaker than water, yet nothing is better at wearing away what is hard. Drip by drip, a soft stream can smooth a rock."}],"game":{"items":[["the book says the best way to be is like water.",true,"yes: water helps every living thing and doesn't fight."],["Laozi built a dam to keep the water in one place.",false,"not in it: tradition says he wrote the book and rode away on an ox."],["water flows to the low places nobody wants.",true,"yes: it doesn't push to be first or highest."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like a stream filling a pool. breathe out, flowing gently downhill. four slow breaths, soft as water.","carry":"today I'll be like water: soft, helpful and patient.","grownups":{"source":"The Tao Te Ching, chapters 8 and 78, the old Chinese book at the heart of Taoism, retold in our own words (James Legge's English translation is from 1891). The story of Laozi and the ox is a tradition, not part of the book.","ask":"Where have you seen soft water change something hard, like a stone or the sand?"}},"es":{"title":"el agua, la suave y fuerte","word":"Tao","means":"el Camino: en el taoísmo, el fluir natural de todas las cosas","story":[{"head":"un libro antiguo, el Tao Te Ching","text":"Hay un libro chino antiguo llamado Tao Te Ching. La tradición dice que un sabio maestro llamado Laozi lo escribió antes de irse montado en un buey, muy lejos, hacia el oeste."},{"head":"el agua ayuda a todo a crecer","text":"El libro dice que la mejor manera de ser es como el agua. El agua ayuda a todo lo que vive: la hierba, los árboles, los pájaros, la gente. Da y da, y no pelea ni presume."},{"head":"el agua va a los lugares bajos","text":"El agua corre hacia los lugares bajos que nadie quiere. No empuja para ser la primera ni la más alta. En el libro, eso es parte de lo que hace tan buena al agua."},{"head":"el agua suave moldea la piedra","text":"El libro dice algo más: nada es más suave ni más débil que el agua, pero nada es mejor para desgastar lo que es duro. Gota a gota, un arroyo suave puede alisar una roca."}],"game":{"items":[["el libro dice que la mejor manera de ser es como el agua.",true,"sí: el agua ayuda a todo lo que vive y no pelea."],["Laozi construyó una presa para guardar el agua en un lugar.",false,"no está: la tradición dice que escribió el libro y se fue en un buey."],["el agua corre hacia los lugares bajos que nadie quiere.",true,"sí: no empuja para ser la primera ni la más alta."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como un arroyo que llena un estanque. suelta el aire, corriendo suave cuesta abajo. cuatro respiraciones lentas, suaves como el agua.","carry":"hoy soy como el agua: suave, servicial y paciente.","grownups":{"source":"El Tao Te Ching, capítulos 8 y 78, el antiguo libro chino en el corazón del taoísmo, contado con nuestras palabras (la traducción inglesa de James Legge es de 1891). La historia de Laozi y el buey es una tradición, no parte del libro.","ask":"¿Dónde has visto que el agua suave cambie algo duro, como una piedra o la arena?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Tao Te Ching, chapter 8","work":"Tao Te Ching (Laozi)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Tao Te Ching, chapter 78","work":"Tao Te Ching (Laozi)","translation":null,"quoted":null},{"ref":"Shiji, chapter 63 (the account of Laozi leaving through the pass)","work":"Sima Qian, Records of the Grand Historian","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":17,"key":"boy-who-cried-wolf","game":"match","en":{"title":"the shepherd boy who cried wolf","word":"honesty","means":"telling the truth, so people can trust what you say","story":[{"head":"a bored shepherd boy","text":"A shepherd boy watched his sheep on a hill near the village. The days were long and quiet, and he was bored. So he had an idea for a little excitement."},{"head":"the boy shouts “wolf!” for fun","text":"“Wolf! Wolf!” he shouted. The villagers dropped their work and ran up the hill to help. But there was no wolf. The boy laughed. He did it again another day, and again they came running."},{"head":"a real wolf comes","text":"Then one evening, a real wolf came out of the woods. “Wolf! Wolf!” cried the boy, as loud as he could. But this time the villagers thought, “He's only joking again,” and nobody came."},{"head":"the sheep scatter","text":"The wolf chased the sheep, and they scattered all over the hills. The boy learned something that day: when you tell the truth every time, people believe you when it matters most."}],"game":{"prompt":"who did what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the shepherd boy","shouted “wolf!” as a joke"],["the villagers","ran up the hill to help"],["the real wolf","chased the sheep"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like the quiet evening on the hill. breathe out, calm and true. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll tell the truth, even about small things.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words and gently.","ask":"Why does it matter that people can believe what we say?"}},"es":{"title":"el pastorcito y el lobo","word":"honestidad","means":"decir la verdad, para que la gente pueda confiar en ti","story":[{"head":"un pastorcito aburrido","text":"Un pastorcito cuidaba sus ovejas en una colina cerca del pueblo. Los días eran largos y tranquilos, y él se aburría. Así que tuvo una idea para divertirse un poco."},{"head":"el niño grita «¡lobo!» de broma","text":"«¡Lobo! ¡Lobo!», gritó. La gente del pueblo dejó su trabajo y subió corriendo la colina para ayudar. Pero no había ningún lobo. El niño se rio. Otro día lo volvió a hacer, y otra vez llegaron corriendo."},{"head":"llega un lobo de verdad","text":"Una tarde, salió del bosque un lobo de verdad. «¡Lobo! ¡Lobo!», gritó el niño lo más fuerte que pudo. Pero esta vez la gente pensó: «Otra vez está bromeando», y nadie vino."},{"head":"las ovejas se dispersan","text":"El lobo persiguió a las ovejas, y ellas se dispersaron por todas las colinas. Ese día el niño aprendió algo: si dices la verdad siempre, la gente te cree cuando más importa."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién hizo qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["el pastorcito","gritó «¡lobo!» de broma"],["la gente del pueblo","subió a ayudar"],["el lobo de verdad","persiguió a las ovejas"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como la tarde tranquila en la colina. suelta el aire, en calma y con verdad. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"digo la verdad, incluso en las cosas pequeñas.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras y con suavidad.","ask":"¿Por qué importa que la gente pueda creer lo que decimos?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Shepherd's Boy and the Wolf”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":18,"key":"thoreau-walden","game":"truth","en":{"title":"Thoreau listens at Walden Pond","word":"noticing","means":"paying close attention to what is around you","story":[{"head":"a little house by the pond","text":"A man named Henry David Thoreau built a small wooden house by a pond called Walden, in the woods of Massachusetts. He wanted to live simply and pay attention."},{"head":"why he went to the woods","text":"In his book Walden, he wrote: “I went to the woods because I wished to live deliberately.” Deliberately means slowly and on purpose, noticing what really matters."},{"head":"a morning in the doorway","text":"Some summer mornings, Thoreau just sat in his sunny doorway from sunrise till noon. He listened to the birds singing among the pine trees and watched the light move, quiet and happy."},{"head":"a mouse eats from his hand","text":"A little wild mouse lived under his house and came out at lunchtime for crumbs. One day Thoreau held a piece of cheese between his fingers, and the mouse sat in his hand, nibbled it, and then cleaned its face and paws."}],"game":{"items":[["Thoreau sat in his sunny doorway and listened to the birds.",true,"yes: some summer mornings, from sunrise till noon."],["Thoreau kept a pet dog in his little house.",false,"not in it: his visitor was a little wild mouse."],["a wild mouse nibbled cheese while sitting in his hand.",true,"yes: and then it cleaned its face and paws."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, like sitting in a sunny doorway. breathe out, and listen for one sound nearby. four slow, listening breaths.","carry":"today I'll stop and notice one small, living thing.","grownups":{"source":"From Walden (1854) by the American writer Henry David Thoreau, about his time living by Walden Pond in Massachusetts: the chapters “Where I Lived, and What I Lived For,” “Sounds” and “Brute Neighbors.” The quotation is his own words.","ask":"If we sat still outside for five minutes, what do you think we would notice?"}},"es":{"title":"Thoreau escucha en la laguna de Walden","word":"observar","means":"poner mucha atención a lo que hay a tu alrededor","story":[{"head":"una casita junto a la laguna","text":"Un hombre llamado Henry David Thoreau construyó una casita de madera junto a una laguna llamada Walden, en los bosques de Massachusetts. Quería vivir con sencillez y poner atención."},{"head":"por qué se fue al bosque","text":"En su libro Walden, escribió que se fue al bosque porque quería vivir de forma deliberada. Eso quiere decir con calma y a propósito, fijándose en lo que de verdad importa."},{"head":"una mañana en la puerta","text":"Algunas mañanas de verano, Thoreau se sentaba en la puerta soleada de su casa desde el amanecer hasta el mediodía. Escuchaba a los pájaros cantar entre los pinos y miraba moverse la luz, tranquilo y feliz."},{"head":"un ratón come de su mano","text":"Un ratoncito silvestre vivía debajo de su casa y salía a la hora del almuerzo a buscar migajas. Un día, Thoreau sostuvo un pedacito de queso entre los dedos, y el ratón se sentó en su mano, lo mordisqueó y luego se limpió la cara y las patitas."}],"game":{"items":[["Thoreau se sentaba en la puerta soleada a escuchar a los pájaros.",true,"sí: algunas mañanas de verano, desde el amanecer hasta el mediodía."],["Thoreau tenía un perro en su casita.",false,"no está: su visita era un ratoncito silvestre."],["un ratón silvestre mordisqueó queso sentado en su mano.",true,"sí: y luego se limpió la cara y las patitas."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, como si estuvieras sentado en una puerta con sol. suelta el aire, y escucha un sonido cercano. cuatro respiraciones lentas, escuchando.","carry":"hoy me detengo a observar algo pequeño y vivo.","grownups":{"source":"De Walden (1854), del escritor estadounidense Henry David Thoreau, sobre el tiempo que vivió junto a la laguna de Walden, en Massachusetts: los capítulos «Dónde viví y para qué», «Sonidos» y «Vecinos animales». Aquí lo contamos con nuestras palabras.","ask":"Si nos quedáramos quietos afuera cinco minutos, ¿qué crees que notaríamos?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Walden, “Where I Lived, and What I Lived For”","work":"Henry David Thoreau, Walden (1854)","translation":"Thoreau (original English, 1854)","quoted":"I went to the woods because I wished to live deliberately"},{"ref":"Walden, “Sounds” and “Brute Neighbors”","work":"Henry David Thoreau, Walden (1854)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":19,"key":"fox-and-grapes","game":"match","en":{"title":"the fox and the grapes","word":"sour grapes","means":"pretending you didn't want something you couldn't get","story":[{"head":"grapes high on a vine","text":"One hot afternoon, a hungry fox spotted a bunch of grapes hanging from a vine high above him. They were purple, round and shiny. “Just what I need,” he said."},{"head":"the fox jumps and jumps","text":"He backed up, ran, and jumped. Not high enough. He jumped again, and again, stretching as far as he could. But the grapes were always just out of reach."},{"head":"the fox gives up","text":"At last, tired and hot, the fox gave up. He sat down, looked up at the grapes one more time, and sniffed."},{"head":"the fox calls the grapes sour","text":"“I didn't want them anyway,” he said as he walked off. “They're probably sour.” Sometimes, when we can't have something, we pretend we never wanted it at all."}],"game":{"prompt":"what goes with what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["the vine","held the grapes up high"],["the grapes","looked purple and shiny"],["the fox","said they were probably sour"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, reaching up tall like the fox. breathe out, and let your arms float down. four slow breaths, no need to grab anything.","carry":"it's okay to say: I really wanted that.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the animal stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words. It gave English the saying “sour grapes.”","ask":"Have you ever said you didn't want something, when really you did?"}},"es":{"title":"la zorra y las uvas","word":"están verdes","means":"fingir que no querías algo que no pudiste tener","story":[{"head":"uvas en lo alto de una parra","text":"Una tarde calurosa, una zorra con hambre vio un racimo de uvas colgando de una parra, muy por encima de ella. Eran moradas, redondas y brillantes. «Justo lo que necesito», dijo."},{"head":"la zorra salta y salta","text":"Retrocedió, corrió y saltó. No llegó. Saltó otra vez, y otra, estirándose lo más que podía. Pero las uvas siempre quedaban un poquito fuera de su alcance."},{"head":"la zorra se rinde","text":"Por fin, cansada y acalorada, la zorra se rindió. Se sentó, miró las uvas una vez más y resopló con fuerza."},{"head":"la zorra dice que están verdes","text":"«De todos modos no las quería», dijo mientras se iba. «Seguro están verdes». A veces, cuando no podemos tener algo, fingimos que nunca lo quisimos."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿qué va con qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["la parra","sostenía las uvas en lo alto"],["las uvas","se veían moradas y brillantes"],["la zorra","dijo que estaban verdes"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, estirándote hacia arriba como la zorra. suelta el aire, y deja que tus brazos bajen flotando. cuatro respiraciones lentas, sin necesidad de agarrar nada.","carry":"está bien decir: de verdad lo quería.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias de animales que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras. De ella viene el dicho «están verdes».","ask":"¿Alguna vez dijiste que no querías algo, cuando en realidad sí lo querías?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Fox and the Grapes”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":20,"key":"milkmaid-and-pail","game":"truth","en":{"title":"the milkmaid and her pail","word":"daydream","means":"happy pictures in your head while you're awake","story":[{"head":"a pail of milk on her head","text":"A farmer's daughter was walking to market with a pail of fresh milk balanced on her head. As she walked, she began to dream about the money the milk would bring."},{"head":"eggs, then chicks, then a dress","text":"“I'll sell the milk and buy eggs,” she thought. “The eggs will hatch into chicks. I'll sell the chickens and buy a beautiful new dress to wear to the fair.”"},{"head":"she tosses her head proudly","text":"“Everyone will look at me,” she dreamed. “And I'll toss my head like this!” And she tossed her head, right there on the road."},{"head":"the milk splashes on the road","text":"Splash! Down fell the pail, and all the milk spilled onto the dusty road. No milk, no eggs, no chicks, no dress. She went home and told her mother, who said: don't count your chickens before they hatch."}],"game":{"items":[["the milkmaid carried the pail on her head.",true,"yes: balanced on her head, all the way down the road."],["she sold the milk and bought a new dress.",false,"not in it: the pail fell before she reached the market."],["she dreamed of eggs hatching into chicks.",true,"yes: eggs, then chicks, then a dress for the fair."]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, sitting tall, as if balancing a pail on your head. breathe out, steady and careful. four slow breaths.","carry":"dreams are fun. today I'll take one real step.","grownups":{"source":"One of Aesop's fables, the stories told under the name of Aesop, a storyteller of ancient Greece. We follow George Fyler Townsend's 1867 English translation, retold in our own words.","ask":"What's something you're dreaming about, and what's one real step toward it?"}},"es":{"title":"la lechera y su cántaro","word":"fantasía","means":"imágenes felices que imaginas sin estar dormido","story":[{"head":"un cántaro de leche en la cabeza","text":"La hija de un granjero caminaba al mercado con un cántaro de leche fresca en equilibrio sobre la cabeza. Mientras caminaba, empezó a soñar con el dinero que le daría la leche."},{"head":"huevos, luego pollitos, luego un vestido","text":"«Venderé la leche y compraré huevos», pensó. «De los huevos saldrán pollitos. Venderé los pollos y compraré un vestido nuevo y hermoso para ir a la feria»."},{"head":"mueve la cabeza con orgullo","text":"«Todos me van a mirar», soñaba. «¡Y yo moveré la cabeza así!». Y movió la cabeza con orgullo, ahí mismo en el camino."},{"head":"la leche cae al camino","text":"¡Plaf! Se cayó el cántaro, y toda la leche se derramó en el camino polvoriento. Sin leche, sin huevos, sin pollitos, sin vestido. Volvió a casa y se lo contó a su madre, que le dijo: no cuentes los pollitos antes de que nazcan."}],"game":{"items":[["la lechera llevaba el cántaro sobre la cabeza.",true,"sí: en equilibrio sobre la cabeza, por todo el camino."],["vendió la leche y se compró un vestido nuevo.",false,"no está: el cántaro se cayó antes de que llegara al mercado."],["soñó con huevos de los que salían pollitos.",true,"sí: huevos, luego pollitos, luego un vestido para la feria."]]},"breath":"respira despacio, sentado derecho, como si llevaras un cántaro en la cabeza. suelta el aire, firme y con cuidado. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"soñar es divertido. hoy doy un paso de verdad.","grownups":{"source":"Una de las fábulas de Esopo, las historias que se cuentan con el nombre de Esopo, un narrador de la antigua Grecia. Seguimos la traducción inglesa de George Fyler Townsend (1867), contada con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Con qué estás soñando, y cuál sería un paso de verdad hacia eso?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"“The Milk-Woman and Her Pail”","work":"Aesop's Fables (Townsend 1867, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]},{"day":21,"key":"marcus-aurelius-thanks","game":"match","en":{"title":"Marcus Aurelius says thank you","word":"Meditations","means":"Marcus Aurelius's notebook of thoughts, written for himself","story":[{"head":"an emperor with a notebook","text":"Long ago, Marcus Aurelius was an emperor of Rome. He had a great deal to do, but he kept a notebook of thoughts, written just for himself. Today we call it the Meditations."},{"head":"thank you, grandfather","text":"He began his notebook by thanking the people who had taught him. From his grandfather, he wrote, he learned to be good and to keep his temper calm."},{"head":"thank you, mother","text":"From his mother, he learned to be kind and generous, and to live simply, without wanting the fancy things that very rich people have."},{"head":"thank you, teacher","text":"From his teacher, the man who looked after him as a boy, he learned to work hard, to want little, and to do things with his own hands. Page after page, he kept saying thank you."}],"game":{"prompt":"who taught him what? tap the pairs.","pairs":[["his grandfather","taught him to keep calm"],["his mother","taught him to live simply"],["his teacher","taught him to work hard"]]},"breath":"breathe in slowly, and think of someone who taught you something good. breathe out, and quietly think: thank you. four slow breaths.","carry":"I'll thank someone who taught me something.","grownups":{"source":"The Meditations of Marcus Aurelius, book 1, written in Greek. Book 1 is a list of thanks to the people who taught him. George Long translated it into English in 1862; we retell it in our own words.","ask":"Who has taught you something good, and what did they teach you?"}},"es":{"title":"Marco Aurelio da las gracias","word":"Meditaciones","means":"el cuaderno de pensamientos de Marco Aurelio, escrito para sí mismo","story":[{"head":"un emperador con un cuaderno","text":"Hace mucho tiempo, Marco Aurelio fue emperador de Roma. Tenía muchísimo que hacer, pero llevaba un cuaderno de pensamientos escritos solo para él. Hoy lo llamamos las Meditaciones."},{"head":"gracias, abuelo","text":"Empezó su cuaderno dando las gracias a quienes le habían enseñado. De su abuelo, escribió, aprendió a ser bueno y a mantener la calma cuando se enojaba."},{"head":"gracias, mamá","text":"De su madre aprendió a ser bondadoso y generoso, y a vivir con sencillez, sin desear las cosas lujosas que tienen las personas muy ricas."},{"head":"gracias, maestro","text":"De su maestro, el hombre que lo cuidó de niño, aprendió a trabajar duro, a necesitar poco y a hacer las cosas con sus propias manos. Página tras página, siguió dando las gracias."}],"game":{"prompt":"¿quién le enseñó qué? toca las parejas.","pairs":[["su abuelo","le enseñó a mantener la calma"],["su madre","le enseñó a vivir con sencillez"],["su maestro","le enseñó a trabajar duro"]]},"breath":"respira despacio, y piensa en alguien que te enseñó algo bueno. suelta el aire, y piensa en silencio: gracias. cuatro respiraciones lentas.","carry":"le doy las gracias a alguien que me enseñó algo.","grownups":{"source":"Las Meditaciones de Marco Aurelio, libro 1, escrito en griego. El libro 1 es una lista de agradecimientos a las personas que le enseñaron. George Long lo tradujo al inglés en 1862; lo contamos con nuestras palabras.","ask":"¿Quién te ha enseñado algo bueno, y qué te enseñó?"}},"sources":[{"ref":"Meditations 1.1, 1.3, 1.5","work":"Marcus Aurelius, Meditations (Long 1862, paraphrased)","translation":null,"quoted":null}]}]}};
